proc:CM-1-3-2-3-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20002" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20002"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Establish Consumer Value Proposition Principles and Objectives"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-2-3 ;
    skos:definition "Establish the principles and objectives of the consumer value proposition — what the consumer promise must achieve and the principles any candidate proposition must satisfy."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.2.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Establish CVP principles and objectives"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers principle and objective setting for the consumer proposition: translating commercial and brand strategy into consumer-proposition objectives; setting the principles (economic, brand, experience, privacy-by-design) candidates must satisfy; setting the cycle's decision calendar. First step of the consumer VP lifecycle. Excludes commercial strategy (its owner); alternatives and testing (CM-1-3-2-3-2/-1); proposition strategy development and approval (CM-1-3-2-3-4).
In scope: Set consumer-proposition objectives | set the principles candidates must satisfy, including privacy-by-design | set the decision calendar
Out of scope: Commercial strategy (its owner) | alternatives and testing (CM-1-3-2-3-2/-1) | proposition strategy development and approval (CM-1-3-2-3-4)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Commercial and brand direction | governed insight (CM-1-3-1-6) | prior cycle results"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Consumer-proposition principles, objectives, and decision calendar per cycle"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A consumer promise that knows what it must achieve before it is designed."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "strategic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Formulate and Evaluate Strategic Operating Alternatives (CVP) | precedes: Test Customer value proposition | governed-by: Consumer Value Proposition"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 22 (2026-09-20). First step of the consumer VP lifecycle; consumer-directional normalized label required by review, queued. APQC 'Establish channel objectives' is a WEAK adjacent match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-2-3-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11095" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11095"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Develop and Update Consumer Value Proposition Strategy"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-2-3 ;
    skos:definition "Develop or update the consumer value-proposition strategy from the tested elements and evaluated alternatives, secure the applicable approvals, and issue the approved proposition to offer definition, experience strategy, and consumer-facing planning."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.2.3.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop/Update Strategy (CVP)"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers consumer-proposition strategy development, approval, and maintenance: consolidating tested elements and the recommended delivery model into the proposition; developing, recommending, and maintaining the approved promise by consumer segment (marketing lens, batch 19/21) under the applicable delegation of authority; updating as cycles complete. Output feeds Customer Offer Definition (CM-1-3-3-1), Customer Experience Strategy (CM-1-3-2-5), and consumer-facing planning (CM-1-3-5). Develops and maintains the approved promise, not the offers, networks, or campaigns. Excludes offer definition (CM-1-3-3-1); network design (CM-1-3-3-4); campaign planning/execution (CM-1-3-5/6).
In scope: Develop, recommend, and maintain the approved consumer proposition by segment | issue the approved proposition to offer, experience, and planning processes | update as cycles complete
Out of scope: Offer definition (CM-1-3-3-1) | network design (CM-1-3-3-4) | campaign planning/execution (CM-1-3-5/6)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Tested elements (CM-1-3-2-3-1) | evaluated alternatives (CM-1-3-2-3-2) | brand direction (CM-1-3-2-1)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The approved consumer value-proposition strategy"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One approved consumer promise the offer and experience processes can build on."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "strategic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Test Customer value proposition | enables: Customer Offer Definition | enables: Customer Experience Strategy"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 22 (2026-09-20). Develop-and-maintain step of the consumer VP lifecycle; governance-safe wording per review; consumer-directional normalized label required by review, queued. Same FALSE-match caution as CM-1-3-2-2-4: APQC 'Develop KM strategy' is a different 'strategy' sense; comparative only."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-2-4-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11466" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11466"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Conduct Channel-of-Trade Diagnostics"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-2-4 ;
    skos:definition "Diagnose the performance and economics of each channel of trade the company participates in — volumes, margins, cost-to-serve, share, counterparty health, and structural trends — producing the evidenced channel picture the channel strategy is built on."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.2.4.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Conduct Channel of trade diagnostics"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers channel diagnostics: assembling channel-level volume, margin, and share evidence; consuming cost-to-serve assessments from CM-1-2-6-1-3 (batch 19) and counterparty findings from CM-1-3-1-4 as governed inputs; assessing channel structural trends with external statistical context (EIA PMM categories, within their limitations); and stating findings with assumptions and confidence. Diagnostics inform; the strategy hypothesis and the approved strategy sit at CM-1-3-2-4-2 and the L4. Excludes cost-to-serve production (CM-1-2-6-1-3); the subject analyses (CM-1-3-1); the strategy hypothesis and approved strategy (CM-1-3-2-4-2, CM-1-3-2-4).
In scope: Assemble channel-level performance and economics evidence | consume governed cost-to-serve and counterparty findings | assess structural channel trends | state findings with confidence
Out of scope: Cost-to-serve production (CM-1-2-6-1-3) | the analyses (CM-1-3-1) | strategy hypothesis and approved strategy (CM-1-3-2-4-2, CM-1-3-2-4)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Channel volume/margin/share data | cost-to-serve (CM-1-2-6-1-3) | counterparty findings (CM-1-3-1-4) | EIA channel statistics (context)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Evidenced channel-of-trade diagnostics"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "An honest channel-by-channel picture before the channel bets are made."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "strategic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-PMM-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Establish operating Objectives and develop Channel Strategy Hypothesis | governed-by: Sales & Distribution Channel Strategy | uses-input: Assess Cost-to-Serve Performance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 22 (2026-09-20). Diagnostic step of the channel-strategy lifecycle; hyphenation/capitalization normalized in the queued label. EIA channel categories are external statistical context only (b21 limitation). APQC 'Conduct planning activities for major trade customers' is a WEAK false-direction match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-2-4-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20002" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20002"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Establish Channel Operating Objectives and Channel Strategy Hypothesis"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-2-4 ;
    skos:definition "Establish the channel operating objectives and develop the channel-strategy hypothesis — the proposed channel participation, roles, and target mix with the beliefs it depends on stated testably — for evaluation and approval at the channel-strategy level."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.2.4.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Establish operating Objectives and develop Channel Strategy Hypothesis"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers objective-setting and hypothesis development for channel strategy: translating commercial, brand, and value-proposition direction into channel operating objectives; developing the channel-strategy hypothesis from the diagnostics (CM-1-3-2-4-1); stating dependencies and risks, including — where arrangements meet the franchise criteria — the applicable PMPA and state-franchise constraints on changes to existing branded arrangements (cluster PMPA statement at CM-1-3-2-4); and submitting the hypothesis for development and approval of the strategy at CM-1-3-2-4. Excludes the diagnostics (CM-1-3-2-4-1); strategy development and approval (CM-1-3-2-4); channel design (CM-1-3-3-3); franchise legal assessment (Legal, locked rule).
In scope: Set channel operating objectives from strategy direction | develop the channel-strategy hypothesis from diagnostics | state dependencies, risks, and applicable franchise constraints | submit for strategy development and approval
Out of scope: Diagnostics (CM-1-3-2-4-1) | strategy development and approval (CM-1-3-2-4) | channel design (CM-1-3-3-3) | franchise legal assessment (Legal)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Channel diagnostics (CM-1-3-2-4-1) | value propositions (CM-1-3-2-2/-3) | brand direction (CM-1-3-2-1)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Channel operating objectives and the channel-strategy hypothesis"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A channel strategy proposed as a testable bet, not an assumption."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "strategic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001 | SRC-PMPA-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Conduct Channel of trade diagnostics | governed-by: Sales & Distribution Channel Strategy"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 22 (2026-09-20). Hypothesis step of the channel-strategy lifecycle; review's normalized label queued. APQC 'Establish channel objectives' STRONG, no validator — genuine comparative anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-2-5-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19960" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19960"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-2-5 ;
    skos:definition "Identify the customer-experience aspiration — the target experience the company commits to across its consumer and customer interactions — from the brand strategy, value propositions, and governed experience evidence."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.2.5.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Identify Customer Experience Aspiration"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers aspiration setting: synthesizing what consumers and customers value in their interactions (from CM-1-3-1-3/-4 findings) with brand and proposition direction; articulating the target experience and its ambition level relative to competitive and category benchmarks; and framing the aspiration for the principles step (CM-1-3-2-5-2). First step of the experience-strategy lifecycle; the consolidated strategy is developed and maintained at CM-1-3-2-5. Excludes the analyses (CM-1-3-1-3/-4); the principles and pillars (CM-1-3-2-5-2/-3); experience delivery (CM-1-3-6, CM-1-3-9).
In scope: Synthesize experience evidence with brand and proposition direction | articulate the target experience and ambition level | frame the aspiration for principles and pillars
Out of scope: The analyses (CM-1-3-1-3/-4) | principles and pillars (CM-1-3-2-5-2/-3) | experience delivery (CM-1-3-6/9)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Experience evidence (CM-1-3-1-3/-4) | brand and proposition direction | competitive benchmarks (legitimate sources)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The customer-experience aspiration"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A stated ambition for how interactions should feel, grounded in evidence."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "strategic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Define Customer Experience Guiding Principles | governed-by: Customer Experience Strategy"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 22 (2026-09-20). First step of the experience-strategy lifecycle. APQC 'Assess customer experience' is a WEAK measurement-side match (measurement is CM-1-3-9-3/CM-1-3-5-5)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-2-5-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19967" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19967"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-2-5 ;
    skos:definition "Define the guiding principles that translate the experience aspiration into decision rules — the consistent behaviors, standards of treatment, and design tenets every channel, offer, and service decision must satisfy."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.2.5.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Customer Experience Guiding Principles"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers principle definition: converting the aspiration (CM-1-3-2-5-1) into explicit, decision-usable principles; reconciling them with brand strategy, value propositions, and operational and regulatory constraints; and issuing them for the pillars step (CM-1-3-2-5-3) and for use by design, planning, and execution processes. Principles guide decisions; they are not service procedures (CM-1-3-9) or brand standards (CM-1-3-6-7). Excludes the aspiration (CM-1-3-2-5-1); the pillars (CM-1-3-2-5-3); service procedures (CM-1-3-9); brand standard management (CM-1-3-6-7).
In scope: Convert the aspiration into decision-usable principles | reconcile with brand, proposition, and constraint context | issue principles to strategy, design, and execution consumers
Out of scope: The aspiration (CM-1-3-2-5-1) | pillars (CM-1-3-2-5-3) | service procedures (CM-1-3-9) | brand standards (CM-1-3-6-7)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "The aspiration (CM-1-3-2-5-1) | brand and proposition direction | operational and regulatory constraints"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Customer-experience guiding principles"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The experience promise written as rules people can decide with."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "strategic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Identify Customer Experience Aspiration | precedes: Establish Customer Experience Strategic Pillars"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 22 (2026-09-20). Second step of the experience-strategy lifecycle. APQC 'Define a vision for the customer experience' STRONG, no validator — genuine comparative anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-2-5-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10020" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10020"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-2-5 ;
    skos:definition "Establish the strategic pillars of the customer-experience strategy — the small set of experience priorities the company will invest in and be known for — with their intended outcomes and the evidence basis for each."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.2.5.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Establish Customer Experience Strategic Pillars"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers pillar establishment: selecting the experience priorities from the aspiration and principles; stating each pillar's intended customer outcome and evidence basis; aligning pillars with channel strategy, value propositions, and service direction; and issuing the completed pillar set for consolidation into the approved strategy at CM-1-3-2-5 and use by planning and execution. Pillars direct investment and design focus; delivery programs and their measurement belong to CM-1-3-5/6/9. Excludes strategy consolidation and maintenance (CM-1-3-2-5); delivery programs (CM-1-3-5/6/9); enterprise KPI definition (KPI workstream).
In scope: Select experience priorities with intended outcomes | state each pillar's evidence basis | align pillars with channel, proposition, and service direction | issue the pillar set for strategy consolidation
Out of scope: Strategy consolidation and maintenance (CM-1-3-2-5) | delivery programs (CM-1-3-5/6/9) | KPI definition (KPI workstream)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Aspiration and principles (CM-1-3-2-5-1/-2) | channel and proposition direction | experience evidence"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The customer-experience strategic pillars"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A few experience priorities the company actually invests behind."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "strategic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Define Customer Experience Guiding Principles | governed-by: Customer Experience Strategy"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 22 (2026-09-20). Third step of the experience-strategy lifecycle; the consolidated strategy is developed and maintained at the L4 per review. Experience-pillar measures follow the batch 21 KPI Store routing: evidence-layer measures unless separately governed as enterprise KPIs. APQC 'Establish strategic vision' is a WEAK generic match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10160" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10160"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-1 ;
    skos:definition "Define the customer groups each offer targets — selecting and prioritizing segments from the governed segmentation evidence — so offers are designed for identified groups rather than everyone."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Target Customer Groups"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers targeting: selecting target groups for offers from the marketing-lens segmentation evidence of Consumer and Customer/Marketer Analysis (CM-1-3-1-3/-4); prioritizing groups against the approved propositions and channel strategy; and recording targeting rationale. Targeting may use personal, loyalty, account, and behavioral data only for approved purposes and within applicable privacy, consent, suppression, fairness, and non-discrimination controls. The design instance of the batch 19 four-lens rule: targeting uses the marketing lens; supply-economics, credit, and party-hierarchy segmentation stay with their lenses. Excludes the segmentation analyses (CM-1-3-1-3-3, CM-1-3-1-4-3); other segmentation lenses (their owners); offer content definition (CM-1-3-3-1-2); privacy and fairness framework ownership (accountable governance owners).
In scope: Select and prioritize target groups from governed segmentation evidence | apply privacy, consent, suppression, fairness, and non-discrimination controls | align targeting with propositions and channel strategy | record targeting rationale
Out of scope: Segmentation analysis production (CM-1-3-1-3/-4) | other segmentation lenses (four-lens rule) | offer definition (CM-1-3-3-1-2) | privacy/fairness framework ownership (governance owners)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Segmentation findings (CM-1-3-1-3/-4) | approved propositions | channel direction (CM-1-3-2-4)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Prioritized target customer groups per offer with rationale"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Offers aimed at chosen groups, chosen on evidence, within the rules."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Define Customer Offer | governed-by: Customer Offer Definition"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Targeting step; four-lens rule applied (batch 19/21); review privacy/eligibility caveat verbatim in scope note — material for retail/loyalty offers and credit-sensitive segments. APQC 'Define target audience' is a WEAK adjacent match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19967" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19967"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-1 ;
    skos:definition "Define the content of each customer offer — the product, service, program, and experience elements assembled for the target group, with the commercial logic connecting them — implementing the approved value proposition as a concrete, specifiable offer."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Customer Offer"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers offer definition: assembling product, service, program, and experience elements into the offer for each target group; specifying the offer precisely enough to validate, price, plan, and execute; and stating the commercial logic. Offer definition identifies the required pricing, product, channel, service, legal, tax, operational, and technology dependencies; it does not itself approve those dependent designs. Price-term design is coordinated with Pricing Strategy Management (CM-1-3-3-2), which governs price architecture and rules. The offer implements the approved proposition; it does not redefine it. Excludes price architecture, rules, and levels (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3); new product/service development (CM-1-3-4-3); the validation (CM-1-3-3-1-3); proposition strategy (CM-1-3-2-2/-3); approval of dependent designs (their owners).
In scope: Assemble offer elements for each target group | specify offers precisely for validation, pricing, planning, and execution | state the commercial logic | identify dependent designs without approving them
Out of scope: Price architecture/rules/levels (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3) | product development (CM-1-3-4-3) | validation (CM-1-3-3-1-3) | proposition strategy (CM-1-3-2-2/-3) | dependent-design approval (their owners)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Target groups (CM-1-3-3-1-1) | approved propositions | available products, services, and programs"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Specified customer offers with commercial logic and dependencies"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "An offer specified well enough to validate, price, and sell."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Define Target Customer Groups | precedes: Validate Customer Offer"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Definition step of the offer lifecycle; implements, never redefines, the approved propositions (batch 22 boundary); dependency-identification rule per review. APQC 'Define a vision for the customer experience' is a WEAK false-direction match (experience strategy is CM-1-3-2-5)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19968" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19968"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-1 ;
    skos:definition "Validate defined offers against evidence before release — customer and consumer response, commercial viability, operational deliverability, and consistency with the approved proposition — so offers reach planning and execution already tested."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Validate Customer Offer"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers offer validation: testing offer response with governed evidence and authorized research or concept tests (under the batch 21 privacy controls and aggregate/de-identified default); validating commercial viability with the pricing processes and cost-to-serve evidence (CM-1-2-6-1-3); confirming operational deliverability with the affected operating processes; and checking proposition consistency. Produces validation evidence and a recommendation—proceed, revise, defer, or stop—for the applicable approval and launch-planning process. Excludes in-market pilot execution (CM-1-3-6); the analyses (CM-1-3-1); launch approval and release decisions (the applicable approval and launch-planning process, CM-1-3-5).
In scope: Test offer response with governed evidence and authorized tests | validate commercial viability and operational deliverability | check proposition consistency | produce validation evidence with a proceed/revise/defer/stop recommendation
Out of scope: In-market execution (CM-1-3-6) | the analyses (CM-1-3-1) | launch approval and release decisions (approval process, CM-1-3-5)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Specified offers (CM-1-3-3-1-2) | governed findings and research | cost-to-serve (CM-1-2-6-1-3)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Validation evidence with a proceed/revise/defer/stop recommendation"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "No offer reaches the market untested."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Define Customer Offer | precedes: Define Offer Measurement Framework (CM-1-3-3-1-4)"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Validation step; go/no-go distinction per review — this process recommends, the applicable approval and launch-planning process decides. Privacy controls and publication default inherited (batch 21); consumes batch 19 cost-to-serve as governed input. APQC 'Validate with customers' is a WEAK generic match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-1-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19521" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19521"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-1 ;
    skos:definition "Define the measurement framework for customer offers — the metric definitions, baselines, targets, and measurement responsibilities by which offer performance will be managed — as evidence-layer measures with governed routing toward enterprise KPIs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.1.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Offer Measurement Framework"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers offer measurement design. Defines domain-level measures, baselines, targets, measurement responsibilities, data sources, and review cadence needed to evaluate offer, pricing, or channel design outcomes. The framework may nominate measures as candidate KPIs but does not approve, publish, version, calculate, certify, or govern enterprise KPIs. Those activities follow the Enterprise Measurement Catalog, Candidate KPI Register, Enterprise KPI Catalog, and KPI Store governance process. Offer measures span uptake, mix, margin contribution, target-group penetration, and retention effect, aligned with the marketing-analysis metrics stewarded at CM-1-3-1-6. Excludes enterprise KPI definition and KPI Store governance (KPI workstream); performance measurement execution (CM-1-3-5-5); the metrics stewardship capability (CM-1-3-1-6).
In scope: Define offer measures, baselines, targets, responsibilities, data sources, and review cadence | align with stewarded marketing-analysis metrics | nominate candidate KPIs into KPI Store governance
Out of scope: Enterprise KPI approval, publication, and governance (KPI Store workstream) | measurement execution (CM-1-3-5-5) | metrics stewardship (CM-1-3-1-6)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Validated offers (CM-1-3-3-1-3) | stewarded metric definitions (CM-1-3-1-6) | KPI Store classification rules"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The offer measurement framework (evidence-layer, KPI Store-routed)"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every offer launched with its scorecard already defined."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Validate Customer Offer | uses-input: Insight and Metrics"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Template row (sibling rows CM-1-3-3-2-5 and CM-1-3-3-3-5 by design). Review terminology decision: this is a domain measurement framework, not KPI governance — 'KPI Framework' in the locked name is easy to misread as KPI approval authority; preferred label 'Define Offer Measurement Framework' queued prominently for the naming pass. Standard measurement-framework statement verbatim in scope note; routing via Enterprise Measurement Catalog, Candidate KPI Register, Enterprise KPI Catalog, and KPI Store governance. APQC 'Define objectives' is a WEAK generic match. Step 3d naming pass (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Define KPI Framework' to 'Define Offer Measurement Framework' — critical semantic-collision resolution. Prior label retired from ontology aliases (a shared generic altLabel would recreate the collision); scoped historical alias 'Define KPI Framework (Offer)' recorded in the identity/migration map."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-2-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19949" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19949"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-2 ;
    skos:definition "Define the strategic pricing approach for downstream marketing — the pricing postures (value, cost-plus, index-linked, market-following, premium) by product, channel, and customer group, consistent with the strategic role of price set by the strategy branch."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Strategic Pricing Approach"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers pricing-approach definition: translating the strategic role of price (batch 22 boundary) into pricing postures by product, channel, and customer group; setting the principles the integrated strategy must satisfy, including the legal guardrails (price-discrimination risk, below-cost statutes, competition-information rule); and framing the pricing-strategy cycle. Excludes the integrated strategy itself (CM-1-3-3-2-2); price parameters and rules (CM-1-3-3-2-2); price levels (execution); legal assessment (Legal, Compliance).
In scope: Translate the strategic price role into pricing postures | set principles including legal guardrails | frame the pricing-strategy cycle
Out of scope: The integrated strategy (CM-1-3-3-2-2) | price parameters/rules (CM-1-3-3-2-2) | price levels (execution) | legal assessment (Legal, Compliance)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Strategic price role (CM-1-3-2) | market findings (CM-1-3-1-1) | legal guardrail advice (Legal)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The strategic pricing approach by product, channel, and customer group"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A stated pricing posture before any price is set."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "strategic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FTC-RP-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Develop Integrated Pricing Strategy | governed-by: Pricing Strategy Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). First step of the pricing lifecycle. APQC 'Define the strategic vision' is a WEAK generic match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11095" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11095"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-2 ;
    skos:definition "Develop the integrated pricing strategy — the approved price architecture, differential logic, index-linkage principles, corridors and guardrails, and pricing rules that connect postures across products, channels, and customer groups into one coherent, legally defensible system — issued as governed direction to the pricing execution processes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Integrated Pricing Strategy"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers integrated pricing-strategy development. Develops and maintains approved price architecture, pricing rules, differential logic, index-linkage principles, discount/terms guardrails, and exception criteria under the applicable commercial, Finance, Legal, Compliance, and delegation-of-authority approvals. Includes price corridors, floors, and ceilings where governed; price-index and timing conventions where applicable; discount/terms guardrails and exception criteria — not rebate administration, posted prices, or contract rates. Ensures cross-channel coherence so branded, unbranded, and direct channels are priced on defensible, documented logic — material where competing purchasers buy at different prices; defenses such as cost justification and meeting competition are Legal's assessment. Maintains the strategy from pilot and performance feedback (the lifecycle's feedback loop). Sets architecture, parameters, and rules, never transaction prices (CM-1-3-6-3, CM-1-3-7-2). Excludes transaction price setting (CM-1-3-6-3, CM-1-3-7-2); rebate administration (CM-1-3-6-4); legal defense assessment (Legal); the pilots and performance analysis (CM-1-3-3-2-4/-3).
In scope: Develop approved price architecture, differential logic, and index-linkage principles | set corridors, floors, ceilings, and discount/terms guardrails with exception criteria under the applicable approvals | keep cross-channel pricing coherent and documented | maintain the strategy from pilot and performance feedback
Out of scope: Transaction prices (CM-1-3-6-3, CM-1-3-7-2) | rebate administration (CM-1-3-6-4) | legal defense assessment (Legal) | pilots and performance analysis (CM-1-3-3-2-4/-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Pricing approach (CM-1-3-3-2-1) | cost-to-serve and channel economics | pilot and performance evidence (CM-1-3-3-2-4/-3)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The integrated pricing strategy: approved architecture, parameters, guardrails, and rules"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One coherent, documented price system instead of a thousand unconnected prices."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "strategic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FTC-RP-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Define Strategic Pricing Approach | precedes: Define Pricing Measurement Framework (CM-1-3-3-2-5) | informed-by: Conduct Pricing Pilots | informed-by: Analyze Pricing Performance | enables: Sales Pricing Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Core development step of the pricing lifecycle; review governance wording verbatim in scope note. Feedback relations: pilots and performance analysis inform this process — Hamid's relation rule ('informs: Develop Integrated Pricing Strategy' from pilots) is vocabulary-missing, applied as informed-by on this receiving row per the batch 10 conditional-relation precedent. APQC 'Develop KM strategy' is a FALSE lexical match (third occurrence of the knowledge-management 'strategy' confusion — carried forward); comparative only."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-2-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16500" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16500"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-2 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze the performance of deployed pricing — realization against architecture, margin and volume effects, competitive position, and rule adherence — producing the recurring evidence that keeps the pricing strategy honest."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Analyze Pricing Performance"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers pricing performance analysis: measuring price realization against the intended architecture; analyzing margin, volume, and mix effects by product, channel, and customer group; assessing competitive price position from legitimate sources only (competition-information rule at CM-1-3-3-2; EIA statistics as external context); monitoring execution's adherence to pricing rules as analytical findings; and feeding findings into the integrated strategy (the lifecycle's feedback loop). Findings concerning potential legal, competition, discriminatory-pricing, below-cost, or unfair-sales issues must be escalated to Legal/Compliance; this process does not make legal determinations. Excludes price correction execution (CM-1-3-6-3); improper price intelligence (prohibited); legal determinations (Legal/Compliance); enterprise performance reporting (its owners); the strategy updates themselves (CM-1-3-3-2-2).
In scope: Measure realization against the intended architecture | analyze margin, volume, and mix effects | assess competitive position from legitimate sources | report rule-adherence findings, escalate potential legal issues, and feed the strategy loop
Out of scope: Price corrections (CM-1-3-6-3) | improper price intelligence (prohibited) | legal determinations (Legal/Compliance) | enterprise reporting (its owners) | strategy updates (CM-1-3-3-2-2)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Transaction and realization data | measurement framework (CM-1-3-3-2-5) | legitimate competitive price data | EIA price statistics (context)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Pricing performance findings feeding the next strategy cycle"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Pricing strategy corrected by evidence, not anecdote."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-PMM-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Define Pricing Measurement Framework (CM-1-3-3-2-5) | informed-by: Sales Pricing Management | informed-by: Conduct Pricing Pilots | enables: Develop Integrated Pricing Strategy"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). The recurring feedback loop of the pricing lifecycle; legal-escalation boundary per review verbatim in scope note. Pilots also inform this analysis (vocabulary-missing 'informs', applied as informed-by here per the batch 10 precedent). APQC 'Analyze performance' scored 0.8 STRONG — genuine match. EIA PMM price statistics are aggregate external context within their b21 limitations."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-2-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "13169" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 13169"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-2 ;
    skos:definition "Conduct authorized pricing pilots — controlled, time-bounded tests of pricing changes in defined markets or channels — measuring effects before strategy-wide deployment, within the same legal guardrails as deployed pricing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.2.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Conduct Pricing Pilots"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers pricing pilots: designing controlled tests of proposed pricing changes; coordinating pilot execution through the pricing execution processes (CM-1-3-6-3 executes; this process designs and evaluates); measuring effects against the defined measures; and recommending adoption, adjustment, or abandonment to the integrated strategy (CM-1-3-3-2-2). A pricing pilot requires documented scope, eligible population/channel, time period, success/failure thresholds, legal/compliance review where required, approval authority, customer/partner communication requirements, data collection plan, rollback conditions, and post-pilot decision owner. Pilots operate under the same legal guardrails as deployed pricing; pilot pricing is still real pricing. Excludes pilot price execution (CM-1-3-6-3); the adoption decision (CM-1-3-3-2-2 with the applicable approvals); legal assessment (Legal).
In scope: Design controlled pricing tests with the required documented controls | coordinate execution through pricing execution processes | measure effects and recommend adopt/adjust/abandon
Out of scope: Pilot price execution (CM-1-3-6-3) | adoption decisions (CM-1-3-3-2-2) | legal assessment (Legal)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Proposed pricing changes (CM-1-3-3-2-2) | pilot design parameters and controls | pilot market data"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Pilot results with an adopt/adjust/abandon recommendation"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Price changes tested small before they are deployed large."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FTC-RP-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Develop Integrated Pricing Strategy | uses-input: Sales Pricing Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Evidence-source step of the pricing feedback loop; pilot-control safeguard per review verbatim in scope note. Relation rule (Hamid, review): pilots 'inform' the integrated strategy and performance analysis rather than strictly preceding them — 'informs' is not in the relationship vocabulary, so it is recorded as informed-by on the receiving rows (CM-1-3-3-2-2, CM-1-3-3-2-3) per the batch 10 conditional-relation precedent, with this rule recorded verbatim: pilots may run before or after limited deployment depending on the control model, may generate immediate results, and post-deployment performance analysis may continue long after pilots end. APQC 'Conduct pricing analysis' STRONG, no validator — comparative anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-2-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19521" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19521"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-2 ;
    skos:definition "Define the measurement framework for pricing — realization, margin, differential-integrity, and competitive-position measures with their definitions, baselines, targets, and measurement responsibilities — as evidence-layer measures with governed routing toward enterprise KPIs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.2.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Pricing Measurement Framework"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers pricing measurement design. Defines domain-level measures, baselines, targets, measurement responsibilities, data sources, and review cadence needed to evaluate offer, pricing, or channel design outcomes. The framework may nominate measures as candidate KPIs but does not approve, publish, version, calculate, certify, or govern enterprise KPIs. Those activities follow the Enterprise Measurement Catalog, Candidate KPI Register, Enterprise KPI Catalog, and KPI Store governance process. Pricing measures span price realization, margin capture, differential integrity, discount leakage, and competitive position, supplying the framework Analyze Pricing Performance measures against and aligning with the metrics stewarded at CM-1-3-1-6. Excludes enterprise KPI definition and KPI Store governance (KPI workstream); the measurement itself (CM-1-3-3-2-3); metrics stewardship (CM-1-3-1-6).
In scope: Define pricing measures, baselines, targets, responsibilities, data sources, and review cadence | supply the framework for pricing performance analysis | nominate candidate KPIs into KPI Store governance
Out of scope: Enterprise KPI approval, publication, and governance (KPI Store workstream) | performance measurement (CM-1-3-3-2-3) | metrics stewardship (CM-1-3-1-6)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Integrated pricing strategy (CM-1-3-3-2-2) | stewarded metric definitions (CM-1-3-1-6) | KPI Store classification rules"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The pricing measurement framework (evidence-layer, KPI Store-routed)"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Pricing measured against a framework, not against opinions."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Develop Integrated Pricing Strategy | precedes: Analyze Pricing Performance | uses-input: Insight and Metrics"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Template row (see CM-1-3-3-1-4); review terminology decision: domain measurement framework, not KPI governance — preferred label 'Define Pricing Measurement Framework' queued prominently. Standard measurement-framework statement verbatim in scope note. APQC 'Define objectives' is a WEAK generic match. Step 3d naming pass (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Define KPI Framework' to 'Define Pricing Measurement Framework' — critical semantic-collision resolution. Prior label retired from ontology aliases (a shared generic altLabel would recreate the collision); scoped historical alias 'Define KPI Framework (Pricing)' recorded in the identity/migration map."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-3-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11168" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11168"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-3 ;
    skos:definition "Create the channel-specific offerings and value propositions — what each channel of trade receives from the company and offers onward — implementing the approved customer, marketer, and consumer propositions in each channel's terms."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Create Channel Offering & Value Proposition"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers channel-offering creation: specializing the approved value propositions (CM-1-3-2-2/-3) into channel-model propositions and standardized channel offer components (supply terms shape, brand and program access, support and service elements) per channel segment; keeping channel offerings consistent with each other and with the propositions; and coordinating price-term structure with Pricing Strategy Management. This process designs channel-model propositions and standardized channel offer components; it does not configure customer-specific offers, negotiate individual partner terms, or establish individual contract prices. The design object here is the channel operating proposition — what a channel participant receives, carries, or commits to as part of the channel model; concrete offers for target customer groups are Customer Offer Definition's (CM-1-3-3-1). Excludes proposition strategy (CM-1-3-2-2/-3); customer-specific offer configuration (CM-1-3-3-1); individual partner negotiation (CM-1-3-6-1, CM-1-3-7-2); price architecture and rules (CM-1-3-3-2); contract prices (execution).
In scope: Specialize approved propositions into channel-model propositions and standardized offer components | keep offerings consistent across channels and with the propositions | coordinate price-term structure with pricing processes
Out of scope: Proposition strategy (CM-1-3-2-2/-3) | customer-specific offers (CM-1-3-3-1) | partner negotiation (CM-1-3-6-1, CM-1-3-7-2) | price architecture/rules (CM-1-3-3-2) | contract prices (execution)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved propositions (CM-1-3-2-2/-3) | channel segments (CM-1-3-3-3-3) | channel portfolio (CM-1-3-3-3-2)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Channel-model propositions and standardized channel offer components"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Each channel knowing exactly what it gets and what it carries."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Define Channel Portfolio | precedes: Develop Channel Structure, Size & Engagement Model | governed-by: Sales & Distribution Channel Design"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Implements, never redefines, the approved propositions (batch 22 boundary); review distinction verbatim in scope note — channel-model proposition here, customer-group offer at CM-1-3-3-1. APQC 'Define offering and customer value proposition' STRONG, no validator — comparative anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-3-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16403" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16403"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-3 ;
    skos:definition "Define the channel portfolio — which channel segments the company serves through which channel types, with what portfolio balance — implementing the approved channel participation and target mix as a designed portfolio."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Channel Portfolio"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers portfolio definition: translating the approved channel participation, roles, and target mix (CM-1-3-2-4) into the designed channel portfolio; balancing coverage, economics, and risk across channel types; identifying portfolio gaps and overlaps; and recording the portfolio design rationale. Portfolio design is validated against supply, logistics, terminal/fleet, retail-network, channel-partner, technology, and service-delivery capacity through the accountable operating and design processes. Portfolio changes affecting existing branded arrangements inherit the batch 22 fact-dependent PMPA statement (franchise legal analysis with Legal). Excludes channel strategy and mix approval (CM-1-3-2-4 and its approvals); individual partner selection (CM-1-3-6-1); operating-capacity ownership (the accountable operating processes); franchise legal analysis (Legal).
In scope: Translate approved participation and mix into the designed portfolio | balance coverage, economics, and risk | validate against operating and delivery capacity through the accountable processes | identify gaps and overlaps with rationale
Out of scope: Strategy and mix approval (CM-1-3-2-4) | partner selection (CM-1-3-6-1) | operating-capacity ownership (accountable processes) | franchise legal analysis (Legal)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved channel strategy (CM-1-3-2-4) | channel segments (CM-1-3-3-3-3) | channel economics | operating-capacity validation inputs"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The designed, capacity-validated channel portfolio with rationale"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The approved channel mix made concrete as a feasible, designed portfolio."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-PMPA-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Segment Channels | precedes: Create Channel Offering & Value Proposition | governed-by: Sales & Distribution Channel Design"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Portfolio step of the channel-design lifecycle; capacity-validation caveat per review verbatim in scope note — a strategically approved portfolio may be operationally infeasible. APQC 'Define portfolio governance' is a WEAK partial match (business-portfolio governance, different scope)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-3-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20001" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20001"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-3 ;
    skos:definition "Segment the channel landscape for design purposes — grouping channels and channel participants by role, economics, capability, and service needs — producing the segmentation the portfolio and engagement models are designed against."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Segment Channels"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers channel segmentation: grouping channels of trade and participants within them by strategic role, economics, capability, and service requirements, using governed counterparty findings (CM-1-3-1-4) and cost-to-serve evidence (CM-1-2-6-1-3); maintaining segment definitions for design use. The design instance of the four-lens discipline (batch 19/21): channel segmentation for design here; marketing-analysis, supply-economics, credit, and party-hierarchy segmentation stay with their lenses; the enterprise's approved channel taxonomy governs classification (batch 22 caveat). Excludes the underlying analyses (CM-1-3-1-4, CM-1-2-6-1); enterprise taxonomy ownership (its owner); portfolio decisions (CM-1-3-3-3-2).
In scope: Group channels and participants by role, economics, capability, and service needs | use governed counterparty and cost-to-serve evidence | maintain design-segment definitions
Out of scope: The analyses (CM-1-3-1-4, CM-1-2-6-1) | enterprise channel taxonomy ownership (its owner) | portfolio definition (CM-1-3-3-3-2)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Counterparty findings (CM-1-3-1-4) | cost-to-serve (CM-1-2-6-1-3) | channel diagnostics (CM-1-3-2-4-1)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Channel design segments with definitions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Channels grouped so design decisions fit real differences."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Define Channel Portfolio | governed-by: Sales & Distribution Channel Design | uses-input: Assess Cost-to-Serve Performance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). First step of the channel-design lifecycle; four-lens rule applied — design-lens segmentation only; enterprise taxonomy governs (batch 22 caveat). APQC 'Determine channels to be supported' is a WEAK adjacent match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-3-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16592" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16592"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-3 ;
    skos:definition "Develop the structure, size, and engagement model for each channel segment — coverage and capacity, roles and responsibilities between company and channel, and the engagement cadence and support model — completing the designed channel system."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.3.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Channel Structure, Size & Engagement Model"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers structure and engagement design: sizing coverage and capacity per channel segment against the portfolio; defining company-versus-channel roles and responsibilities; designing the engagement model (account coverage, support cadence, escalation, program delivery) the partner-management processes will execute; and maintaining the design as the portfolio evolves. The model can define desired roles, coverage, support, service cadence, and capacity assumptions. It does not set HR headcount, operating organization, or individual account assignments; those are addressed through Sales Planning, Operating Model Design, HR, and Channel/Partner Management. Organizational/delivery-model neutrality applies (b10/15/16/20). Structural changes to existing branded arrangements inherit the batch 22 fact-dependent PMPA statement. Excludes partner management execution (CM-1-3-6-1); sales force planning (CM-1-3-5-1); operating model and organizational design (CM-1-3-3-6, HR); individual account assignments (CM-1-3-6-1); franchise legal analysis (Legal).
In scope: Size coverage and capacity per segment | define company-versus-channel roles | design the engagement and support model for execution | maintain the design as the portfolio evolves
Out of scope: Partner management execution (CM-1-3-6-1) | sales force planning (CM-1-3-5-1) | operating model/organizational design and headcount (CM-1-3-3-6, HR) | individual account assignments (CM-1-3-6-1) | franchise legal analysis (Legal)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Channel portfolio and offerings (CM-1-3-3-3-2/-1) | segment definitions (CM-1-3-3-3-3) | engagement performance evidence"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Channel structure, sizing, and engagement models"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every channel sized and engaged by design, not by history."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Create Channel Offering & Value Proposition | precedes: Define Channel Measurement Framework (CM-1-3-3-3-5) | enables: Channel & Partner Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Structure step of the channel-design lifecycle; review headcount/organization boundary verbatim in scope note — protects batch 24 Operating Model Design and future Sales Planning; organizational neutrality per b10/15/16/20. APQC 'Develop omni-channel policies and procedures' is a WEAK adjacent match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-3-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19521" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19521"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-3 ;
    skos:definition "Define the measurement framework for the channel system — coverage, throughput, economics, and engagement measures with their definitions, baselines, targets, and measurement responsibilities — as evidence-layer measures with governed routing toward enterprise KPIs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.3.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Channel Measurement Framework"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers channel measurement design. Defines domain-level measures, baselines, targets, measurement responsibilities, data sources, and review cadence needed to evaluate offer, pricing, or channel design outcomes. The framework may nominate measures as candidate KPIs but does not approve, publish, version, calculate, certify, or govern enterprise KPIs. Those activities follow the Enterprise Measurement Catalog, Candidate KPI Register, Enterprise KPI Catalog, and KPI Store governance process. Channel measures span coverage, volume and share by segment, channel economics and cost-to-serve trends, and engagement and partner health, aligned with the metrics stewarded at CM-1-3-1-6 and the diagnostics at CM-1-3-2-4-1. Excludes enterprise KPI definition and KPI Store governance (KPI workstream); measurement execution (CM-1-3-5-5, CM-1-3-6-1); metrics stewardship (CM-1-3-1-6).
In scope: Define channel measures, baselines, targets, responsibilities, data sources, and review cadence | align with stewarded metrics and channel diagnostics | nominate candidate KPIs into KPI Store governance
Out of scope: Enterprise KPI approval, publication, and governance (KPI Store workstream) | measurement execution (CM-1-3-5-5, CM-1-3-6-1) | metrics stewardship (CM-1-3-1-6)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Channel design outputs (CM-1-3-3-3-1/-2/-4) | stewarded metric definitions (CM-1-3-1-6) | KPI Store classification rules"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The channel measurement framework (evidence-layer, KPI Store-routed)"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The channel system measured against its design intent."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Develop Channel Structure, Size & Engagement Model | uses-input: Insight and Metrics"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Template row (see CM-1-3-3-1-4); review terminology decision: domain measurement framework, not KPI governance — preferred label 'Define Channel Measurement Framework' queued prominently. Standard measurement-framework statement verbatim in scope note. APQC 'Define objectives' is a WEAK generic match. Step 3d naming pass (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Define KPI Framework' to 'Define Channel Measurement Framework' — critical semantic-collision resolution. Prior label retired from ontology aliases (a shared generic altLabel would recreate the collision); scoped historical alias 'Define KPI Framework (Channel)' recorded in the identity/migration map."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-4-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10160" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10160"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-4 ;
    skos:definition "Define the target locations for the retail network — trade-area criteria, location profiles, and prioritized location targets — from consumer, market, and network evidence, producing the location targets the investment and real-estate processes execute against."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.4.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Target Location"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers location targeting: defining trade-area and site-selection criteria (demand, traffic, competition, format fit, supply logistics); profiling and prioritizing target locations against the network operating model and target size; and maintaining the location target list with rationale. Location targets are hypotheses and priority criteria for investment/real-estate diligence, not approved acquisition decisions or guarantees of site availability, profitability, permitting, zoning, environmental suitability, or construction feasibility. Location evidence feeds back into size and format assumptions (iterative with CM-1-3-3-4-3). Uses consumer and market findings under their batch 21 controls. Excludes site acquisition/divestment execution and diligence decisions (investment and real-estate processes); site development and engineering (their owners); network size decisions (CM-1-3-3-4-3).
In scope: Define trade-area and site-selection criteria | profile and prioritize target locations | maintain the location target list with rationale | feed location evidence back into size and format assumptions
Out of scope: Site transactions, diligence decisions, and approvals (investment/real-estate processes) | site development/engineering (their owners) | network sizing (CM-1-3-3-4-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Network size and model (CM-1-3-3-4-3/-2) | consumer and market findings (CM-1-3-1-1/-3) | trade-area data (with source limitations)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Prioritized location targets with criteria and rationale, as diligence hypotheses"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The next site chosen by criteria, not by accident."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Define Target Network Size | precedes: Define Network Measurement Framework (CM-1-3-3-4-4) | governed-by: Network Design (Retail)"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 24 (2026-09-20). Location step of the network-design lifecycle; review safeguard verbatim in scope note — targets are hypotheses for diligence, never acquisition decisions; iterates with Define Target Network Size per review. APQC 'Define target audience' STRONG, no validator — false-direction lexical match (audience targeting, not location targeting); comparative only."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-4-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10058" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10058"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Formulate and Evaluate Strategic Operating Alternatives (Network)"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-4 ;
    skos:definition "Formulate the strategic operating alternatives for the retail network — company-operated, dealer-operated, and mixed network models within the approved channel mix — and evaluate them against network objectives, economics, and constraints."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.4.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Formulate And Evaluate Strategic Operating Alternatives (network Strategy)"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers network-alternative formulation and evaluation: defining candidate network operating models consistent with the approved channel-of-trade mix (CM-1-3-2-4); evaluating against network objectives, retail economics, capability requirements, and — where arrangements meet the franchise criteria — the applicable franchise constraints (batch 22 PMPA statement); recommending the preferred model with evidence. Evaluation within the approved mix — it does not re-open channel strategy. Excludes channel strategy and mix (CM-1-3-2-4); the network decisions (L4 with its approvals); franchise legal assessment (Legal); operating model design beyond the network (CM-1-3-3-6).
In scope: Define candidate network operating models within the approved mix | evaluate against objectives, economics, and applicable constraints | recommend with evidence
Out of scope: Channel strategy/mix (CM-1-3-2-4) | network decisions and approvals (L4) | franchise legal assessment (Legal) | wider operating model design (CM-1-3-3-6)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved channel mix (CM-1-3-2-4) | retail economics | network performance evidence"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Evaluated network operating alternatives with a recommendation"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The network model chosen from real alternatives, not inherited."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-PMPA-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Define Target Network Size | governed-by: Network Design (Retail)"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 24 (2026-09-20). First step of the network-design lifecycle; capitalization of '(network Strategy)' normalized in the queued label. APQC 'Evaluate strategic initiatives' is a WEAK generic match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-4-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10160" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10160"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-4 ;
    skos:definition "Define the target size of the retail network — outlet counts or ranges, formats, and coverage by market and segment — consistent with the chosen network model, the approved channel mix, and validated capacity and economics."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.4.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Target Network Size"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers network sizing: translating the chosen network operating model and approved channel mix into target outlet counts, formats, and market coverage; validating size targets against demand evidence, retail economics, and supply, logistics, and service capacity through the accountable operating processes (batch 23 capacity-validation discipline); and maintaining size targets with rationale. Target size may be expressed as ranges, formats, coverage objectives, or scenario-based capacity rather than a single fixed outlet count. Size targets iterate with location evidence (CM-1-3-3-4-1) and are recommendations into the applicable investment approvals — never acquisition commitments. Excludes investment commitment and approval (delegated approvals); location targeting (CM-1-3-3-4-1); channel mix (CM-1-3-2-4).
In scope: Set target outlet counts or ranges, formats, and coverage | validate against demand, economics, and operating capacity | maintain size targets with rationale, iterating with location evidence
Out of scope: Investment commitments and approvals (delegated approvals) | location targeting (CM-1-3-3-4-1) | channel mix (CM-1-3-2-4)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Network model (CM-1-3-3-4-2) | demand and market evidence | capacity validation inputs | location evidence (CM-1-3-3-4-1)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Target network size, formats, and coverage with rationale"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A network sized to evidence, capacity, and economics."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Formulate And Evaluate Strategic Operating Alternatives (network Strategy) | precedes: Define Target Location | governed-by: Network Design (Retail)"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 24 (2026-09-20). Sizing step of the network-design lifecycle; review addition verbatim — ranges, formats, coverage objectives, or scenario-based capacity, not a single fixed count; iterates with Define Target Location per review; capacity validation per the batch 23 discipline. APQC 'Define target audience' is a WEAK false-direction match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-4-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19521" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19521"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-4 ;
    skos:definition "Define the measurement framework for the retail network — coverage, throughput, unit economics, and network-health measures with their definitions, baselines, targets, and measurement responsibilities — as evidence-layer measures with governed routing toward enterprise KPIs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.4.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Network Measurement Framework"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers network measurement design. Defines domain-level measures, baselines, targets, measurement responsibilities, data sources, and review cadence needed to evaluate retail-network design outcomes. The framework may nominate measures as candidate KPIs but does not approve, publish, version, calculate, certify, or govern enterprise KPIs. Those activities follow the Enterprise Measurement Catalog, Candidate KPI Register, Enterprise KPI Catalog, and KPI Store governance process. Network measures span coverage against targets, per-site and network unit economics, format performance, and network health, aligned with the metrics stewarded at CM-1-3-1-6. Excludes enterprise KPI definition and KPI Store governance (KPI workstream); measurement execution (CM-1-3-5-5, operating processes); metrics stewardship (CM-1-3-1-6).
In scope: Define network measures, baselines, targets, responsibilities, data sources, and review cadence | align with stewarded metrics | nominate candidate KPIs into KPI Store governance
Out of scope: Enterprise KPI approval, publication, and governance (KPI Store workstream) | measurement execution (CM-1-3-5-5, operating processes) | metrics stewardship (CM-1-3-1-6)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Network design outputs (CM-1-3-3-4-1/-2/-3) | stewarded metric definitions (CM-1-3-1-6) | KPI Store classification rules"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The network measurement framework (evidence-layer, KPI Store-routed)"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The network measured against its design intent."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Define Target Location | uses-input: Insight and Metrics"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 24 (2026-09-20). Template row (batch 23 treatment); preferred label 'Define Network Measurement Framework' queued prominently; full measurement-framework routing statement in scope note per review. APQC 'Define objectives' is a WEAK generic match. Step 3d naming pass (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Define KPI Framework' to 'Define Network Measurement Framework' — critical semantic-collision resolution. Prior label retired from ontology aliases (a shared generic altLabel would recreate the collision); scoped historical alias 'Define KPI Framework (Network)' recorded in the identity/migration map."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-5-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16854" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16854"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-5 ;
    skos:definition "Define the media strategy — which paid, owned, and earned media channels carry the communications strategy, with what roles, weights, and principles — so media planning buys against deliberate strategic direction."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.5.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Media Strategy"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers media strategy: setting media channel roles and mix principles across broadcast, print, out-of-home, and digital media in their strategic roles; setting media investment principles and measurement expectations; and maintaining the strategy from performance evidence. Owned media, earned media, paid media, retail media, and partner/channel media roles may be defined here, while paid-media procurement/buying and partner placement execution follow Procurement/Marketing Execution controls. Strategy only: media planning and buying are planning/execution (CM-1-3-5-2, CM-1-3-6-5); media vendor and agency selection follows procurement processes. Excludes media planning and buying (CM-1-3-5-2, CM-1-3-6-5); vendor/agency selection (procurement); digital channel operations (execution).
In scope: Set media channel roles across owned, earned, paid, retail, and partner media | set mix and investment principles | set measurement expectations | maintain the strategy from evidence
Out of scope: Media planning/buying (CM-1-3-5-2, CM-1-3-6-5) | vendor/agency selection (procurement) | channel operations (execution)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Integrated communications direction (CM-1-3-3-5) | audience findings | media performance evidence"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The media strategy: roles, mix, and investment principles"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Media money spent against a strategy, not a habit."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Marketing Communications Strategy | enables: Integrated Marketing Planning (Advertising)"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 24 (2026-09-20). Parallel variant, media instance; review media-role coverage verbatim in scope note. APQC 'Define new media communication strategy' 0.75 STRONG — genuine comparative anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-5-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16851" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16851"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-5 ;
    skos:definition "Define the direct marketing strategy — how the company contacts identified customers and prospects one-to-one across mail, email, phone, and messaging — within consent, privacy, and marketing-communication law, so direct outreach is deliberate, targeted, and lawful."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.5.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Direct Marketing Strategy"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers direct marketing strategy: setting the role, audiences, contact channels, and frequency/consent principles for one-to-one outreach; requiring lawful contactability (consent/opt-out status, suppression lists, CAN-SPAM for email, the Telemarketing Sales Rule and related consent regimes for calls and texts — fact- and jurisdiction-dependent, Legal owns interpretation); and setting measurement expectations. This process consumes approved consent, lawful-basis, preference, suppression, identity, and contactability data from accountable systems/processes. It does not create, override, or retain consent status. Personal, loyalty, account, and behavioral data are used only within the batch 21 shared governance statement and the batch 23 targeting controls (privacy, consent, suppression, fairness, non-discrimination). Strategy only: campaign design and execution sit at CM-1-3-5/6. Excludes campaign planning and execution (CM-1-3-5, CM-1-3-6-5); consent, preference, and suppression data creation and management (accountable systems and processes); privacy framework ownership (governance owners); legal interpretation (Legal).
In scope: Set direct marketing roles, audiences, channels, and frequency/consent principles | require lawful contactability and suppression discipline | consume approved contactability data without creating or overriding it | set measurement expectations within privacy controls
Out of scope: Campaign planning/execution (CM-1-3-5, CM-1-3-6-5) | consent/preference/suppression data ownership (accountable systems) | privacy framework ownership (governance owners) | legal interpretation (Legal)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Audience findings (CM-1-3-1-3/-4) | approved consent and suppression status (accountable systems) | direct performance evidence"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The direct marketing strategy with consent and contactability principles"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One-to-one outreach that is purpose-limited, permissioned or otherwise legally authorized, appropriately targeted, and compliant with applicable contact-preference and suppression controls."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FTC-ADM-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Marketing Communications Strategy | enables: Marketing Communications Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 24 (2026-09-20). Parallel variant, direct instance; review contactability rule verbatim in scope note — consumes, never creates, overrides, or retains consent status; b21 privacy statement and b23 targeting controls inherited; CAN-SPAM/TSR context via SRC-FTC-ADM-001, Legal owns interpretation. APQC 'Define direct marketing strategy' scored 1.0 — the tree's first perfect APQC match; comparative anchor (description not copied)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-5-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10123" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10123"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-5 ;
    skos:definition "Define the advertising strategy — the strategic role, audiences, claims territory, and creative principles of paid advertising — within truth-in-advertising and substantiation requirements, so advertising builds the brand on claims the company can prove."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.5.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Advertising Strategy"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers advertising strategy: setting advertising's strategic role and audiences; defining the claims territory (what the company will say about products and offers) with the substantiation discipline — claims must be truthful, non-deceptive, and evidence-backed, with clear-and-conspicuous disclosures and endorsement-relationship disclosure where used. Claims must be supported before dissemination under the applicable substantiation standard; Legal/Compliance determines whether evidence is adequate for the specific claim, audience, medium, jurisdiction, and disclosure context. Fuel-related claims carry their own regulatory regimes and are flagged for Legal per campaign — non-exhaustive examples: fuel quality/specification, octane/cetane, additives, performance/economy, environmental/carbon, renewable-content, and loyalty and price claims; this process does not own those regulatory interpretations. Sets creative and brand-consistency principles under the brand strategy and measurement expectations. Excludes campaign creative development and execution (CM-1-3-5/6); claim and disclosure approval (Legal, Compliance); brand standards enforcement (CM-1-3-6-7).
In scope: Set advertising roles, audiences, and claims territory with substantiation discipline | set creative and brand-consistency principles | flag regulated fuel claims for Legal | set measurement expectations
Out of scope: Creative development/execution (CM-1-3-5/6) | claim and disclosure approval (Legal, Compliance) | fuel-claim regulatory interpretation (Legal, regulatory owners) | brand standards enforcement (CM-1-3-6-7)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Brand strategy (CM-1-3-2-1) | claims substantiation evidence | advertising performance evidence"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The advertising strategy: role, audiences, claims territory, and principles"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Advertising that can prove what it promises."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FTC-ADM-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Marketing Communications Strategy | enables: Integrated Marketing Planning (Advertising)"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 24 (2026-09-20). Parallel variant, advertising instance; review substantiation rule verbatim in scope note — Legal/Compliance determines evidentiary adequacy per claim, audience, medium, jurisdiction, and disclosure context; fuel-claim examples non-exhaustive, ownership stays with Legal/regulatory functions. APQC candidate 'Define pricing strategy' is a FALSE mapping (wrong process entirely); comparative value nil."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-5-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16855" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16855"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-5 ;
    skos:definition "Define the point-of-sale communication strategy — how the brand and offers are communicated at forecourts, stores, and other points of sale — coordinating brand, offer, and pricing communication where the customer actually decides."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.5.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define POS Communication Strategy"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers POS communication strategy: setting the strategic framework for on-site communications (signage roles, offer and price communication principles, promotional display principles) consistent with brand standards direction and the consumer proposition; coordinating with pricing display and regulatory signage requirements (jurisdiction-dependent); and setting measurement expectations. Excludes fuel-price setting, pump-sign configuration, legal metrology, product labeling, and price-display execution, which belong to the applicable pricing, site-operations, EHS/regulatory, and execution processes. Strategy only: site execution and merchandising are execution (CM-1-3-6-5/-7). Excludes site execution and merchandising (CM-1-3-6-5/-7); fuel-price setting and price-display execution (pricing and site-operations processes); pump-sign configuration, legal metrology, and product labeling (site-operations, EHS/regulatory processes).
In scope: Set POS communication frameworks for signage, offers, and promotional display | keep POS communication consistent with brand and proposition | set measurement expectations
Out of scope: Site execution/merchandising (CM-1-3-6-5/-7) | fuel-price setting and price-display execution (pricing, site operations) | pump-sign configuration, metrology, and labeling (site operations, EHS/regulatory)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Brand and proposition direction | offer definitions (CM-1-3-3-1) | site communication performance evidence"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The POS communication strategy"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The message at the pump and shelf chosen as deliberately as the media buy."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Marketing Communications Strategy | enables: Marketing Plan Execution"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 24 (2026-09-20). Parallel variant, POS instance; review exclusion verbatim in scope note — pump, pole-sign, shelf, foodservice, and forecourt displays carry product, price, tax, labeling, and safety implications owned by their processes. APQC 'Define point of sale (POS) communication strategy' 0.8 STRONG — genuine comparative anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-5-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16851" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16851"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-5 ;
    skos:definition "Define the digital marketing strategy — the strategic role of digital channels, platforms, and data-driven techniques across the communications mix — within privacy and online-advertising requirements, so digital investment follows deliberate direction rather than platform momentum."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.5.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Digital Marketing Strategy"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers digital marketing strategy: setting the strategic role of digital channels (search, social, programmatic, apps, retail media) and their relationship to media, direct, and POS strategies; setting principles for data-driven targeting and personalization within the batch 21 shared governance statement and batch 23 targeting controls; requiring online-disclosure discipline (clear-and-conspicuous, native-advertising and endorsement disclosure) with Legal owning interpretation; and setting measurement expectations. Three domains are distinguished per review: digital marketing strategy (channel roles, audience principles, personalization standards, measurement — this row); digital product and customer experience (apps, web, portals, commerce, loyalty systems, product roadmaps — their owners); and technology operation (platform selection, data pipelines, ad-tech, identity resolution, security — technology and procurement processes). This row must not become an application or product-management domain. Excludes digital campaign planning and execution (CM-1-3-5, CM-1-3-6-5); digital product/experience ownership (their owners); platform and ad-tech selection/operation (technology, procurement); consent operations (their owners); legal interpretation (Legal).
In scope: Set digital channel roles and their relationship to the other communication strategies | set data-driven targeting and personalization principles within privacy controls | require online-disclosure discipline | set measurement expectations
Out of scope: Digital planning/execution (CM-1-3-5, CM-1-3-6-5) | digital product/experience ownership (their owners) | platform and ad-tech selection/operation (technology, procurement) | consent operations (their owners) | legal interpretation (Legal)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Communications direction (CM-1-3-3-5) | audience findings within controls | digital performance evidence"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The digital marketing strategy"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Digital spend directed by strategy, disciplined by privacy."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FTC-ADM-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Marketing Communications Strategy | enables: Marketing Communications Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 24 (2026-09-20). Parallel variant, digital instance; review three-way distinction recorded in scope note — strategy here, digital product/experience and technology operation with their owners; b21 privacy statement and b23 targeting controls inherited; online-disclosure context via SRC-FTC-ADM-001. APQC 'Define direct marketing strategy' 0.75 is an adjacent match (digital, not direct)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-5-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16852" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16852"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-5 ;
    skos:definition "Developing a program to promote the objectives, values, products and services of the company to its employees by treating them as potential customers, in order to extend company client base, increase employee engagement or to foster brand advocacy."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.5.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Internal Marketing Communications Strategy"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Covers setting the strategy for communicating brand, offer, and marketing messages to the organization's own employees as an internal audience: audience and channel choice, message and tone direction, and the intended engagement or advocacy outcome. Strategy only. Excludes executing internal communications, owned by Implement Internal Communications (CM-1-3-5-3-9), and excludes campaign-level planning, owned by Marketing Communications Planning (CM-1-3-5-3). Excludes external, media, public, investor, and government communications, which the locked Legal & Corp Comm boundary places outside this branch. Excludes employee relations, terms of employment, and HR policy communication."@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "ADOPTED"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 02 (2026-09-18), bundled fix. Row was approved in the #29 pre-intake pass with an empty scope_note and has been the single BLOCKING finding of step3c-workbook-validate.py since #30. Only the missing scope_note was added; the approved definition, status, and all other fields are unchanged. Boundaries asserted here are drawn from existing taxonomy siblings and the locked Legal & Corp Comm decision, not from new research. Phase 1 fields remain empty, consistent with this row staying on the PRE_INTAKE_APPROVED allowlist for the later backfill pass."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-5-7 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19521" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19521"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-5 ;
    skos:definition "Define the measurement framework for marketing communications — reach, engagement, effectiveness, and efficiency measures across the communication channels with their definitions, baselines, targets, and measurement responsibilities — as evidence-layer measures with governed routing toward enterprise KPIs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.5.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Marketing Communications Measurement Framework"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers communications measurement design. Defines domain-level measures, baselines, targets, measurement responsibilities, data sources, and review cadence needed to evaluate marketing-communications outcomes. The framework may nominate measures as candidate KPIs but does not approve, publish, version, calculate, certify, or govern enterprise KPIs. Those activities follow the Enterprise Measurement Catalog, Candidate KPI Register, Enterprise KPI Catalog, and KPI Store governance process. Communications measures span reach and audience delivery, engagement, message effectiveness, and cost-efficiency by channel, aligned with the metrics stewarded at CM-1-3-1-6 and consumed by Marketing Performance Analysis (CM-1-3-5-5). Excludes enterprise KPI definition and KPI Store governance (KPI workstream); measurement execution (CM-1-3-5-5); metrics stewardship (CM-1-3-1-6).
In scope: Define communications measures, baselines, targets, responsibilities, data sources, and review cadence | align with stewarded metrics | nominate candidate KPIs into KPI Store governance
Out of scope: Enterprise KPI approval, publication, and governance (KPI Store workstream) | measurement execution (CM-1-3-5-5) | metrics stewardship (CM-1-3-1-6)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "The channel-communication strategies (CM-1-3-3-5-1..-6) | stewarded metric definitions (CM-1-3-1-6) | KPI Store classification rules"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The communications measurement framework (evidence-layer, KPI Store-routed)"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every channel's voice measured on the same evidence terms."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Marketing Communications Strategy | uses-input: Insight and Metrics"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 24 (2026-09-20). Template row (batch 23 treatment); preferred label queued prominently; full measurement-framework routing statement in scope note per review. APQC 'Define objectives' is a WEAK generic match. Step 3d naming pass (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Define KPI Framework' to 'Define Marketing Communications Measurement Framework' — sixth and final 'Define KPI Framework' collision resolved (Hamid's follow-up decision, discovered after the 8-rename Phase 1 execution). Prior label retired from ontology aliases (a shared generic altLabel would recreate the collision); scoped historical alias 'Define KPI Framework (Marketing Communications)' recorded in the identity/migration map."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-6-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10015" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10015"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-6 ;
    skos:definition "Develop the business process blueprint for the marketing operating model — the end-to-end processes, handoffs, and control points through which the marketing strategies and designs operate — at blueprint level, for the operating processes to implement."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.6.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Business Process Blueprint"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers process blueprinting: mapping the marketing operating processes end-to-end against the approved strategies and designs; defining handoffs, control points, and decision rights at blueprint level (consistent with this taxonomy's decision-rights architecture); identifying process gaps and redundancies; and maintaining the blueprint as designs evolve. Co-developed iteratively with the organizational blueprint (CM-1-3-3-6-2) per review. Blueprint, not implementation: process implementation, technology enablement, and continuous improvement belong to the operating processes and their owners. Excludes process implementation (operating processes); technology design (its owners); organizational blueprint content (CM-1-3-3-6-2); enterprise process governance (its owner).
In scope: Map end-to-end marketing operating processes against approved designs | define handoffs, control points, and decision rights at blueprint level | identify gaps and redundancies | maintain the blueprint
Out of scope: Process implementation (operating processes) | technology design (its owners) | organizational blueprint (CM-1-3-3-6-2) | enterprise process governance (its owner)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Operating-model requirements (CM-1-3-3-6-3) | approved designs (CM-1-3-3) | operating performance evidence"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The marketing business process blueprint"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The marketing machine drawn before it is built."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Determine Channel Of Trade Mix | precedes: Develop Organizational Blueprint | governed-by: Operating Model Design"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 24 (2026-09-20). Process-blueprint step; co-developed iteratively with the organizational blueprint (review note — the listed order is a logical dependency, not a rigid sequence). APQC 'Develop business strategy' is a WEAK false-direction match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-6-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10042" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10042"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-6 ;
    skos:definition "Develop the organizational blueprint for the marketing operating model — the capabilities, roles, and organizational constructs the process blueprint requires — at blueprint level, without setting headcount or making appointments."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.6.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Organizational Blueprint"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers organizational blueprinting: deriving required capabilities and role types from the process blueprint; defining organizational constructs (teams, capability groupings, interfaces to Commercial, Finance, Legal, and operations) at blueprint level; stating capacity assumptions; and maintaining the blueprint, co-developed iteratively with the process blueprint (CM-1-3-3-6-1). The batch 23 boundary governs: the blueprint defines desired roles, coverage, and capacity assumptions — it does not set HR headcount, job grading, compensation, operating-organization decisions, or individual appointments, which follow HR, management, and the applicable approval processes; organizational/delivery-model neutrality (b10/15/16/20) — the blueprint describes what the model requires, not the org chart. Excludes headcount approval, grading, compensation, and appointments (HR, management, approvals); the process blueprint (CM-1-3-3-6-1).
In scope: Derive required capabilities and role types from the process blueprint | define organizational constructs and interfaces at blueprint level | state capacity assumptions | maintain the blueprint iteratively with the process blueprint
Out of scope: Headcount, grading, compensation, and appointments (HR, management) | process blueprint (CM-1-3-3-6-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Process blueprint (CM-1-3-3-6-1) | capability assessments | capacity assumptions"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The marketing organizational blueprint"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The organization the process needs, described without naming names."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Develop Business Process Blueprint | precedes: Define Procedural Framework & Guidelines | governed-by: Operating Model Design"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 24 (2026-09-20). Organizational-blueprint step; batch 23 headcount boundary is this row's governing rule; organizational neutrality per b10/15/16/20; co-developed iteratively with the process blueprint (review). APQC 'Develop and set organizational goals' is a WEAK false-direction match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-6-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10127" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10127"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Translate Channel-of-Trade Mix into Operating Model Requirements"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-6 ;
    skos:definition "Translate the approved channel-of-trade mix and channel-design outputs into the capabilities, processes, service models, role types, control requirements, technology needs, and organizational interfaces required to operate each channel — without deciding, revising, approving, or reopening the channel mix."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.6.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Determine Channel Of Trade Mix"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers mix translation: taking the approved channel-of-trade mix (CM-1-3-2-4) and channel-design outputs (CM-1-3-3-3) and determining what each channel requires of the operating model — capabilities, processes, service and support models, role types, control requirements, technology needs, and organizational interfaces; identifying operating-model implications of mix changes; and feeding requirements to the process and organizational blueprints (CM-1-3-3-6-1/-2). Determine Channel Of Trade Mix within Operating Model Design translates the approved channel-of-trade mix and channel-design outputs into the capabilities, processes, service models, role types, control requirements, technology needs, and organizational interfaces required to operate each channel. It does not decide, revise, approve, or reopen the channel mix. Mix decisions belong to Sales & Distribution Channel Strategy (CM-1-3-2-4) under its approvals. Excludes channel mix decisions (CM-1-3-2-4); channel system design (CM-1-3-3-3); partner management (CM-1-3-6-1).
In scope: Determine operating-model requirements per channel of trade from the approved mix and channel design | identify operating-model implications of mix changes | feed requirements to the blueprints
Out of scope: Channel mix decisions (CM-1-3-2-4) | channel system design (CM-1-3-3-3) | partner management (CM-1-3-6-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved channel mix (CM-1-3-2-4) | channel design outputs (CM-1-3-3-3) | operating capability evidence"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Operating-model requirements per channel of trade"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every channel in the approved mix operationally provided for."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Sales & Distribution Channel Strategy | uses-input: Sales & Distribution Channel Design | enables: Develop Business Process Blueprint | enables: Develop Organizational Blueprint | governed-by: Operating Model Design"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 24 (2026-09-20). Q3 decision (review: 'the most important boundary in the package'): translation, never re-decision — the locked name reads as a mix decision but the mix is owned at CM-1-3-2-4 (batch 22); the canonical interpretation is recorded verbatim in the scope note and the preferred label 'Translate Channel-of-Trade Mix into Operating Model Requirements' is PROMOTED in the naming-normalization backlog per review (high-value correction). Relations per review: uses-input from Channel Strategy and Channel Design (information dependency, not lifecycle sequence — translation iterates as approved strategy evolves), enables toward both blueprints. APQC 'Determine channel role and fit with target segments' is a WEAK adjacent match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-6-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "18627" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 18627"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-6 ;
    skos:definition "Define the procedural frameworks and guidelines under which the marketing operating model runs — the standard procedures, working guidelines, and escalation paths that implement enterprise policies in marketing's operations — within, never in place of, the policies owned by their accountable functions."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.6.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Procedural Framework & Guidelines"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers procedural-framework definition: converting enterprise policies (commercial, pricing governance, privacy, compliance, brand) into marketing operating procedures, guidelines, and escalation paths; keeping procedures consistent with the process blueprint and decision-rights architecture; and maintaining them as policies and designs change. Procedures are controlled documents with owner, version, effective date, approval, review trigger, evidence requirements, and escalation path. The batch 15 procedures-not-policy discipline governs: this process writes procedures within policies — policy ownership stays with the accountable functions (Finance, Legal, Compliance, Data Governance, HR, and the batch 16 compliance architecture); formal Legal/Compliance interpretation is never this process's. Excludes policy ownership (accountable functions); compliance framework ownership (batch 16 architecture); procedure execution and adherence monitoring (operating processes, CM-1-3-6-8); the process blueprint (CM-1-3-3-6-1).
In scope: Convert enterprise policies into controlled marketing procedures, guidelines, and escalation paths | keep procedures consistent with the blueprint and decision rights | maintain procedures as controlled documents as policies and designs change
Out of scope: Policy ownership (accountable functions) | compliance framework ownership (batch 16 architecture) | formal Legal/Compliance interpretation (Legal, Compliance) | procedure execution/adherence monitoring (operating processes, CM-1-3-6-8) | process blueprint (CM-1-3-3-6-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Enterprise policies (accountable functions) | process blueprint (CM-1-3-3-6-1) | operating feedback"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Controlled marketing procedural frameworks and guidelines"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Policies made usable at the working level, without being rewritten."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Develop Organizational Blueprint | precedes: Define Operating Model Measurement Framework (CM-1-3-3-6-5) | governed-by: Operating Model Design"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 24 (2026-09-20). Procedural step; b15 procedures-not-policy discipline is the governing rule; review controlled-document requirement verbatim in scope note (owner, version, effective date, approval, review trigger, evidence requirements, escalation path) — supports the project's artifact-control principles. APQC 'Define communication guidelines and mechanisms' is a WEAK adjacent match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-6-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19521" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19521"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3-6 ;
    skos:definition "Define the measurement framework for the marketing operating model — process performance, capability health, and operating-efficiency measures with their definitions, baselines, targets, and measurement responsibilities — as evidence-layer measures with governed routing toward enterprise KPIs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.6.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Operating Model Measurement Framework"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers operating-model measurement design. Defines domain-level measures, baselines, targets, measurement responsibilities, data sources, and review cadence needed to evaluate operating-model design outcomes. The framework may nominate measures as candidate KPIs but does not approve, publish, version, calculate, certify, or govern enterprise KPIs. Those activities follow the Enterprise Measurement Catalog, Candidate KPI Register, Enterprise KPI Catalog, and KPI Store governance process. Operating-model measures span process cycle and quality performance against the blueprint, capability health, and operating efficiency, aligned with the metrics stewarded at CM-1-3-1-6. Excludes enterprise KPI definition and KPI Store governance (KPI workstream); measurement execution (operating processes, CM-1-3-5-5); metrics stewardship (CM-1-3-1-6).
In scope: Define operating-model measures, baselines, targets, responsibilities, data sources, and review cadence | align with stewarded metrics | nominate candidate KPIs into KPI Store governance
Out of scope: Enterprise KPI approval, publication, and governance (KPI Store workstream) | measurement execution (operating processes, CM-1-3-5-5) | metrics stewardship (CM-1-3-1-6)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Blueprints and frameworks (CM-1-3-3-6-1/-2/-4) | stewarded metric definitions (CM-1-3-1-6) | KPI Store classification rules"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The operating-model measurement framework (evidence-layer, KPI Store-routed)"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The operating model measured against its blueprint."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Define Procedural Framework & Guidelines | uses-input: Insight and Metrics"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 24 (2026-09-20). Template row (batch 23 treatment); preferred label 'Define Operating Model Measurement Framework' queued prominently per review; full measurement-framework routing statement in scope note. APQC 'Define objectives' is a WEAK generic match. Step 3d naming pass (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Define KPI Framework' to 'Define Operating Model Measurement Framework' — critical semantic-collision resolution. Prior label retired from ontology aliases (a shared generic altLabel would recreate the collision); scoped historical alias 'Define KPI Framework (Operating Model)' recorded in the identity/migration map."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10109" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10109"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Identify the market needs the innovation program should serve — unmet and emerging customer, consumer, and channel needs read from the governed evidence base on the innovation-opportunity lens — prioritized as innovation opportunities with their evidence."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Identify Market Needs"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers needs identification: reading governed findings (CM-1-3-1-1/-3/-4) and sensing signals (CM-1-3-4-1-2) for unmet and emerging needs; framing and prioritizing needs as innovation opportunities with evidence and confidence; and feeding prioritized needs to R&D planning (CM-1-3-4-1-3) and ideation (CM-1-3-4-2-1). Prioritizes needs for exploration and concept generation; delegated stage-gate and investment authorities select concepts for funded development. The innovation-opportunity lens of the Q2 rule: consumes the analyses, never re-performs them; personal data only through the governed findings and batch 21 controls. Excludes the subject analyses (CM-1-3-1); demand forecasting (CM-1-1-1-1); concept selection for funded development (delegated stage-gate and investment authorities).
In scope: Read governed findings and signals for unmet and emerging needs | frame and prioritize needs as innovation opportunities with evidence | feed prioritized needs to planning and ideation
Out of scope: The analyses (CM-1-3-1) | demand forecasting (CM-1-1-1-1) | concept selection for funded development (delegated authorities)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Governed findings (CM-1-3-1-1/-3/-4) | sensing signals (CM-1-3-4-1-2) | prior opportunity results"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Prioritized market needs as innovation opportunities for exploration"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The needs worth innovating for, found in the evidence."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Market Sensing | precedes: Develop R&D Implementation Plans | governed-by: Visioning And Implementation Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 25 (2026-09-20). Needs step of the visioning lifecycle; opportunity-selection wording per review — prioritizes for exploration, never selects for funding; continuously informed by Market Sensing. APQC 'Identify market segments' STRONG, no validator — false-direction match (segmentation ≠ needs identification); comparative only."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11424" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11424"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Continuously sense the market, technology, regulatory, and competitive signals relevant to downstream product and service innovation — a standing scan that feeds needs identification and planning with early, sourced, reliability-rated signals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Market Sensing"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers innovation sensing: maintaining a standing scan of technology developments (fuels, energy transition, convenience, digital), regulatory trajectories, competitive moves (legitimate sources only, batch 21 prohibition), and market signals. Market Sensing records sourced, reliability-rated signals, emerging hypotheses, and watch items. Signals become governed findings only after appropriate validation through Concept Research, Marketing Analysis, or another accountable analysis process. Signals are recorded by source class — primary/public authority, licensed market/technology intelligence, partner/customer signal, internal operating signal, hypothesis/watch item — with reliability rating and provenance. Sensing complements, never duplicates, the recurring subject analyses (Q2 rule): a forward scan for innovation, not a governed market analysis. Excludes the subject analyses (CM-1-3-1); improper competitive intelligence (prohibited, batch 21); regulatory interpretation (Legal, Regulatory).
In scope: Maintain the standing innovation scan across technology, regulatory, competitive, and market signals | record signals by source class with reliability rating and provenance | feed signals to needs identification and planning as hypotheses, not established facts
Out of scope: The analyses (CM-1-3-1) | improper competitive intelligence (prohibited) | regulatory interpretation (Legal, Regulatory) | publishing signals as governed findings (validation processes)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Technology and industry signals | regulatory trajectories | legitimate competitive observations | governed findings (context)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Sourced, reliability-rated innovation signals, hypotheses, and watch items"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Early signals reaching innovation before they become obvious — labeled as signals, not facts."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Identify Market Needs | governed-by: Visioning And Implementation Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 25 (2026-09-20). Sensing step; horizon continuous — a standing scan, not a cycle. Review classification verbatim in scope note: outputs are signals/hypotheses/watch items, never established market facts — they become governed findings only after validation through Concept Research, Marketing Analysis, or another accountable analysis process. Source classes per review recorded for traceability. Q2 rule and batch 21 competitor-information prohibition apply. APQC 'Review market performance' is a WEAK false-direction match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20624" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20624"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Develop the R&D implementation plans that mobilize the innovation vision — programs, priorities, capability and partner needs, and resource requirements — as approval-ready recommendations into the applicable delegated authorities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop R&D Implementation Plans"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers R&D planning: translating the innovation vision and prioritized needs into implementation plans. Develops approval-ready implementation plans with resource estimates, capability needs, external-collaboration needs, dependencies, risks, and proposed sequencing. Delegated investment, portfolio, staffing, procurement, and partnership authorities decide commitments. Portfolio management of the resulting R&D is Lifecycle Management's (CM-1-3-4-5, batch 26); collaboration execution is R&D Collaboration's (CM-1-3-4-6, batch 26). Excludes investment, staffing, procurement, and partnership commitments (delegated authorities); portfolio management (CM-1-3-4-5); development execution (CM-1-3-4-3); collaboration execution (CM-1-3-4-6).
In scope: Translate vision and needs into approval-ready R&D implementation plans | state resource estimates, capability and collaboration needs, dependencies, risks, and sequencing | submit plans to the applicable delegated authorities
Out of scope: Investment, staffing, procurement, and partnership commitments (delegated authorities) | portfolio management (CM-1-3-4-5) | development execution (CM-1-3-4-3) | collaboration execution (CM-1-3-4-6)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Innovation vision (CM-1-3-4-1) | prioritized needs (CM-1-3-4-1-1) | capability and partner assessments"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Approval-ready R&D implementation plans"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "R&D effort mobilized against the vision, with the right approvals."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Identify Market Needs | enables: Concept & Feasibility Study | governed-by: Visioning And Implementation Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 25 (2026-09-20). Planning step of the visioning lifecycle; review investment distinction verbatim in scope note — plans recommend, delegated authorities commit people, capital, and budget. APQC 'Develop IT transformation plans' is a FALSE lexical match (IT plans, not R&D plans)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-2-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16824" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16824"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-2 ;
    skos:definition "Generate and shape product and service ideas from the prioritized needs, sensing signals, and collaboration inputs — a managed idea pipeline with capture, enrichment, and screening — feeding candidate concepts to research and feasibility."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ideation"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers ideation: running idea generation against prioritized needs (CM-1-3-4-1-1) and signals (CM-1-3-4-1-2); capturing ideas from employees, customers and partners (through the CM-1-3-4-6 collaboration channels and their controls), and market evidence; enriching and screening ideas against the innovation vision; and promoting screened ideas as candidate concepts to research (CM-1-3-4-2-2). Ideas from external parties, customers, suppliers, inventors, and partners must be accepted only through approved intake channels and confidentiality/IP controls. The process must not create implied compensation, ownership, confidentiality, or evaluation obligations outside approved Legal/IP procedures. Excludes concept research (CM-1-3-4-2-2); development selection (stage gates); IP framework and unsolicited-idea procedure ownership (CM-1-3-4-4, Legal).
In scope: Generate and capture ideas against needs and signals | accept external ideas only through approved intake channels and confidentiality/IP controls | enrich and screen ideas against the vision | promote screened ideas as candidate concepts
Out of scope: Concept research (CM-1-3-4-2-2) | development selection (stage gates) | IP framework and unsolicited-idea procedures (CM-1-3-4-4, Legal)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Prioritized needs (CM-1-3-4-1-1) | sensing signals (CM-1-3-4-1-2) | collaboration inputs through approved channels (CM-1-3-4-6)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Screened candidate concepts"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A pipeline of ideas worth researching, captured cleanly."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Conduct Research | governed-by: Concept & Feasibility Study"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 25 (2026-09-20). Pipeline step; periodic — a standing idea pipeline with screening cycles. Review external-idea boundary verbatim in scope note — no implied compensation, ownership, confidentiality, or evaluation obligations outside approved Legal/IP procedures; anticipates the batch 26 licensing/IP cluster. APQC candidate is a WEAK broad match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10108" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10108"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-2 ;
    skos:definition "Conduct the research that tests candidate concepts — customer and consumer concept research, technical investigation, and market and commercial assessment — producing feasibility evidence with stated confidence for the concept recommendation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Conduct Research"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers concept research execution: designing and running concept tests and studies within the batch 21 privacy controls (consumer research through governed controls, aggregate/de-identified publication default); technical investigation of formulation, service, and delivery feasibility; market and commercial assessment using governed findings and cost evidence; early regulatory screening against the Q3 gate dimensions; and stating results with confidence. Research plans must define intended decision use, methodology, participant/data controls, evidence standard, reliability/confidence, and reproducibility or documentation requirements proportionate to the decision. Research on candidate fuel products flags the potential Part 79 registration pathway as a feasibility factor — applicability, classification, and execution stay with the accountable Regulatory, Legal, Quality, and Product Stewardship functions. Excludes the subject analyses (CM-1-3-1); research program governance (CM-1-3-4-2-3); regulatory classification (Regulatory, Legal); development (CM-1-3-4-3).
In scope: Design and run concept tests within privacy controls and research-plan standards | investigate technical and delivery feasibility | assess market and commercial feasibility from governed evidence | flag potential regulatory-gate factors with stated confidence
Out of scope: The analyses (CM-1-3-1) | research governance (CM-1-3-4-2-3) | regulatory classification (Regulatory, Legal) | development (CM-1-3-4-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Candidate concepts (CM-1-3-4-2-1) | governed findings and research capacity | technical and regulatory screening inputs"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Concept feasibility evidence with confidence"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Concept verdicts earned with evidence, not enthusiasm."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EPA-FF-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Ideation | governed-by: Concept & Feasibility Study"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 25 (2026-09-20). Research step; review research-plan quality control verbatim in scope note (decision use, methodology, participant/data controls, evidence standard, reliability, documentation proportionate to the decision); b21 privacy controls inherited; Q3 gate screening as a feasibility factor only. APQC 'Conduct customer and market research' STRONG, no validator — genuine comparative anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-2-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-2 ;
    skos:definition "Manage the concept-research activity — commissioning, standards, quality, and completion of the studies the concept lifecycle runs — so research is governed, comparable, and compliant without this process performing the research itself."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Research"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers research management as the concept lifecycle's governance overlay: commissioning and prioritizing concept studies against the pipeline; setting study standards (method quality, documentation, evidence lineage per the batch 21 Document Findings discipline); ensuring studies operate within the privacy, consent, and competitive-information controls; tracking completion and spend against the research plan; and maintaining the concept-research record. Research procurement, supplier contracting, data licensing, and external-study execution follow Procurement, Legal, Privacy, and applicable delegated authorities. Manages concept/feasibility studies only: the governed subject analyses belong to CM-1-3-1, and R&D portfolio management to CM-1-3-4-5 (batch 26). Excludes performing research (CM-1-3-4-2-2); the subject analyses (CM-1-3-1); R&D portfolio management (CM-1-3-4-5); procurement, contracting, and data licensing (Procurement, Legal, Privacy, delegated authorities); privacy/ethics framework ownership (governance owners).
In scope: Commission and prioritize concept studies | set study standards with evidence lineage | ensure privacy and competitive-information compliance | track completion and spend
Out of scope: Research execution (CM-1-3-4-2-2) | the analyses (CM-1-3-1) | R&D portfolio (CM-1-3-4-5) | procurement/contracting/data licensing (Procurement, Legal, Privacy) | privacy/ethics frameworks (governance owners)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Concept pipeline (CM-1-3-4-2-1) | study standards and controls | research plan and budget"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The governed concept-research program and record"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Research that is governed and comparable across every concept."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Concept & Feasibility Study | assures: Conduct Research"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 25 (2026-09-20). Governance overlay of the concept lifecycle (review: not a strict third sequential step) — manages studies, never performs them; procurement/contracting boundary verbatim in scope note; scoped away from batch 26's Manage R&D Portfolios. APQC 'Manage routings' is a FALSE match (manufacturing routings)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-3-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19696" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19696"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-3 ;
    skos:definition "Coordinate the product and service development program — evidence-based stage-gate reviews, development standards, cross-functional coordination, and program-level tracking — recording gate outcomes under the applicable delegation of authority."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Product/Service Development"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers development governance: coordinating evidence-based stage-gate reviews (concept entry, development, validation, pre-launch readiness) and recording outcomes under the applicable delegation of authority; setting development standards (documentation, specification control, testing evidence); convening the functions each gate requires (Regulatory, Quality, Legal, Product Stewardship, supply, operations, Finance); and tracking the program. Gate outcomes never substitute for regulatory, quality, investment, launch, or commercial approval by their accountable owners; where the Q3 gate applies, its requirements are built into gate criteria with readiness determined by the accountable functions. Excludes development execution (CM-1-3-4-3-2); investment, regulatory, launch, and commercial approval (their accountable authorities); regulatory and quality clearance (their owners); portfolio prioritization (CM-1-3-4-5).
In scope: Coordinate evidence-based stage-gate reviews with the required functions | record gate outcomes under delegated authority | set development standards and specification control | track the development program
Out of scope: Development execution (CM-1-3-4-3-2) | investment/regulatory/launch/commercial approval (accountable authorities) | regulatory/quality clearance (their owners) | portfolio prioritization (CM-1-3-4-5)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved concepts (CM-1-3-4-2) | gate criteria and standards | function readiness inputs"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Recorded stage-gate outcomes and program status under delegated authority"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every development effort gated on evidence, with the right people at the gate."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Conduct Product Development | governed-by: Product & Service Development"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 25 (2026-09-20). Governance child; review authority qualification verbatim — coordinates reviews and records outcomes under delegated authority, grants no approval itself. APQC 'Govern and manage product/service development program' 0.8 STRONG — genuine anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-3-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11428" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11428"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-3 ;
    skos:definition "Conduct the development of each product or service — specification, formulation or service design, testing and validation, and pre-launch readiness evidence — from gate entry to development completion under the program's standards."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Conduct Product Development"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers per-product development execution: developing specifications and formulations (fuels, additives, lubricants, blend components, packaged products) or service designs (commercial programs, convenience/retail, digital, loyalty, channel/customer services); planning and executing testing and validation, including testing and evidence generation that may be required for an applicable fuel/additive registration or other regulatory pathway, coordinated with the accountable Regulatory, Quality, Product Stewardship, and Legal functions; assembling pre-launch readiness evidence for the gates; and documenting the developed product under specification control. Where the Q3 gate applies, the regulated party's registration precedes introduction into US commerce — applicability and readiness are the accountable functions' determinations. Excludes gate coordination and outcomes (CM-1-3-4-3-1); registration submission and regulatory clearance (Regulatory, Legal, Product Stewardship); quality operations (batch 18 processes); launch (CM-1-3-5/6); offer assembly (CM-1-3-3-1).
In scope: Develop specifications, formulations, and service designs | plan and execute testing and evidence generation coordinated with the accountable functions | assemble pre-launch readiness evidence | document under specification control
Out of scope: Gate coordination (CM-1-3-4-3-1) | registration submission/regulatory clearance (Regulatory, Legal, Product Stewardship) | quality operations (batch 18) | launch (CM-1-3-5/6) | offer assembly (CM-1-3-3-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Gate-entered concepts (CM-1-3-4-3-1) | development standards | testing and validation capacity"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Developed products and services with validation evidence under specification control"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Each product developed to spec, tested, and ready for its gates."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EPA-FF-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Manage Product/Service Development | governed-by: Product & Service Development"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 25 (2026-09-20). Execution child — the b03/08 per-variant lifecycle; review test/clearance distinction verbatim — development generates evidence for, and coordinates with, the accountable functions; it owns no testing classification or submission. Change control applies during late development, validation, and after release (see CM-1-3-4-3-3 relation rule). APQC 'Conduct new product development process assessment' STRONG, no validator — adjacent (assessment vs execution); comparative only."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-3-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10061" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10061"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-3 ;
    skos:definition "Manage controlled change to existing products and services by assessing specification, formulation, service-design, regulatory, quality, supply, customer, pricing, tax, data, offer, channel, inventory, and operational impacts; obtaining required approvals; and coordinating effective-dated implementation and transition."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Product/Service Change"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers change control: capturing and classifying proposed product and service changes; assessing impacts across specification, formulation, and service design; regulatory and quality; supply, refining, inventory, terminal, and distribution; customer and contract commitments; pricing and taxation; labels, claims, and communications; product master data; offers and channels; digital and service support; and existing-stock, run-out, and transition management. Changes to composition, concentration, intended use, manufacturing source, specification, or other regulated characteristics may require regulatory-impact assessment, amended registration, further testing, revised labeling, or other approvals. Regulatory and Legal determine the applicable action. Routes changes through the applicable approvals and coordinates effective-dated implementation with the affected processes (offers CM-1-3-3-1, master data CM-1-3-7-1, quality processes, supply, channels). Change control is the development-side discipline and applies during late development, validation, post-release maintenance, and service evolution; portfolio decisions to retire or reposition products are Lifecycle Management's (CM-1-3-4-5, batch 26). Excludes portfolio/retirement decisions (CM-1-3-4-5); regulatory action execution (Regulatory, Legal); quality operations (batch 18); master data maintenance (CM-1-3-7-1).
In scope: Capture and classify product and service changes | assess the full impact domains including customer, pricing, tax, data, inventory, and transition | obtain required approvals | coordinate effective-dated implementation and transition
Out of scope: Portfolio/retirement decisions (CM-1-3-4-5) | regulatory action execution (Regulatory, Legal) | quality operations (batch 18) | master data maintenance (CM-1-3-7-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Change proposals | impact assessment inputs from the affected functions | approval requirements"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Controlled, approved, effective-dated product and service changes with transition plans"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "No change sneaks past the gates the product already earned."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EPA-FF-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Product & Service Development | triggers: Manage Master Data (Customer, Price, Product, Tax, Location)"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 25 (2026-09-20). Change-control child; review definition adopted verbatim with the broadened impact domains and effective-dated transition. Regulated-characteristic changes are potential triggers — Regulatory and Legal determine the applicable action (review wording verbatim in scope note). Relation rule (Hamid, review): change control 'governs-change-to' development outputs and 'may-follow' release rather than strictly following Conduct Product Development — these relation types are vocabulary-missing, so the strict precedes/follows chain is omitted per the batch 10 conditional-relation precedent and this rule is recorded verbatim: change control can apply during late development, validation, post-release maintenance, or service evolution. APQC 'Manage product and service portfolio' STRONG, no validator — adjacent match (portfolio is CM-1-3-4-5's); comparative only."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-4-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19696" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19696"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-4 ;
    skos:definition "Establish and maintain the intellectual-asset framework — asset categories, identification and disclosure procedures, ownership and decision-rights principles, and handling rules — operationalizing approved Legal IP policy, so intellectual assets are recognized and handled consistently from creation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.4.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Set up Intellectual Asset Framework"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers framework setup: defining asset categories (patentable inventions, marks, trade secrets/know-how, copyrights, data assets); establishing identification, disclosure/intake, and external idea/submission procedures; setting classification, handling, confidentiality and trade-secret handling, documentation/provenance, retention and access principles, and brand/trademark use guidance; setting business decision rights under the applicable delegation of authority; and defining escalation paths to Legal. The framework operationalizes approved Legal IP policy and business procedures — it does not set legal IP policy (batch 15 procedures-not-policy discipline). External-party intake follows the batch 25 idea-intake boundary. Excludes IP legal policy and its interpretation (Legal); the file/protect/exploit executions (sibling processes); HR and contractual IP terms (Legal, HR).
In scope: Define asset categories and disclosure/intake procedures including external submissions | set classification, handling, provenance, retention, and access principles | set business decision rights under delegated authority | define escalation paths to Legal
Out of scope: IP legal policy (Legal) | registration/protection/exploitation execution (siblings) | employment and contractual IP terms (Legal, HR)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Legal IP policies | development and collaboration activity | disclosure submissions"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The maintained intellectual-asset framework and procedures"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every valuable idea recognized and handled correctly from the day it exists."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "strategic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Manage Licensing & IP | enables: Register Intellectual Property | enables: Protect Intellectual Assets"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Cross-functional (Legal & Corporate Communications / Commercial & Marketing)"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 26 (2026-09-20). Framework function of the IP capability; review element list adopted (categories, disclosure/intake, decision rights, classification/handling, confidentiality/trade-secret, external submissions, brand/trademark use guidance, documentation/provenance, retention/access, Legal escalation); operationalizes Legal policy, never sets it (b15 discipline); capitalization normalized in the queued label. APQC candidate is a WEAK false-direction match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-4-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11049" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11049"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-4 ;
    skos:definition "Coordinate the registration of registrable intellectual property — identifying registrable assets, preparing business input, and developing business recommendations for Legal-executed filings and maintenance — never filing, prosecuting, or making legal determinations itself."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.4.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Register Intellectual Property"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers registration coordination: identifying registrable assets from disclosures and development outputs; preparing business input (commercial significance, markets, use evidence for trademarks). Develop and submit business recommendations regarding filing, trade-secret treatment, defensive publication, maintenance, or abandonment under the applicable framework and delegated authority; Legal and IP counsel determine legal viability, filing strategy, and execution. Coordinates filing, prosecution responses, and maintenance actions through Legal and its outside counsel, in the US (USPTO) and other jurisdictions per the business footprint. Registration effects and refusal risks (e.g., likelihood of confusion for marks) are Legal's assessment; the owner's enforcement responsibility is exercised through CM-1-3-4-4-4 and Legal. Excludes filing, prosecution, and maintenance execution (Legal, locked rule); legal viability and filing strategy (Legal, IP counsel); enforcement (CM-1-3-4-4-4 coordination, Legal execution); trade-secret handling (CM-1-3-4-4-4).
In scope: Identify registrable assets | prepare business input for filings | develop and submit file/secret/publish/maintain/abandon recommendations under delegated authority | coordinate Legal-executed filings and maintenance
Out of scope: Filing/prosecution/maintenance execution (Legal) | legal viability and filing strategy (Legal, IP counsel) | enforcement (CM-1-3-4-4-4, Legal) | trade-secret handling (CM-1-3-4-4-4)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Disclosures and development outputs | framework decision rights (CM-1-3-4-4-1) | Legal advice"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Registration recommendations and Legal-executed filings status"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The right assets recommended for registration, through Legal, in the right markets."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-USPTO-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Set up Intellectual Asset Framework | governed-by: Manage Licensing & IP"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Cross-functional (Legal & Corporate Communications / Commercial & Marketing)"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 26 (2026-09-20). Registration coordination on the b14 pattern; review decision-right qualification verbatim — business recommendations under the framework and delegated authority, with Legal and IP counsel determining legal viability, filing strategy, and execution. APQC 'Protect intellectual property' STRONG, no validator — adjacent (protection sibling); comparative only."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-4-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19696" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19696"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-4 ;
    skos:definition "Assess the commercial value of intellectual assets — their contribution, market relevance, and licensing potential — as decision evidence for protection, exploitation, and portfolio choices, without owning financial valuation policy."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.4.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Value Intellectual Assets"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers commercial valuation for decisions: assessing asset contribution to products, brands, and channels; market relevance and remaining life; licensing potential and comparable evidence; and supplying value assessments to exploitation (CM-1-3-4-4-5) and portfolio stewardship (CM-1-3-4-4-6), stated with assumptions and confidence (b21 evidence discipline). The commercial assessment may inform licensing, product, partnership, or portfolio choices. It does not establish transfer prices, tax value, royalty rates, license fees, accounting value, impairment, capitalization, or financial-reporting treatment. The Finance lock governs accounting treatment of intangibles (batch 15 architecture). Excludes transfer prices, tax value, royalty rates, and license fees (their accountable processes and approvals); accounting valuation and impairment (Finance); the portfolio decisions themselves (CM-1-3-4-4-6, CM-1-3-4-5).
In scope: Assess commercial contribution, relevance, and licensing potential | state assumptions and confidence | supply value evidence to exploitation and portfolio decisions
Out of scope: Transfer prices, tax value, royalty rates, license fees (accountable processes) | accounting valuation/impairment (Finance) | portfolio decisions (CM-1-3-4-4-6, CM-1-3-4-5)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Asset portfolio (CM-1-3-4-4-6) | market and licensing evidence | usage in products and brands"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Commercial value assessments with assumptions and confidence"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Knowing what the assets are commercially worth before deciding what to do with them."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Exploit Intellectual Assets | enables: Manage Intellectual Asset Portfolios | governed-by: Manage Licensing & IP"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Cross-functional (Legal & Corporate Communications / Commercial & Marketing)"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 26 (2026-09-20). Valuation function; review separation verbatim — commercial decision evidence only, never transfer pricing, tax, royalty, license-fee, or accounting determinations; Finance valuation lock applies. APQC candidate is a WEAK false-direction match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-4-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11049" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11049"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-4 ;
    skos:definition "Protect intellectual assets operationally — confidentiality and trade-secret practice, misuse monitoring, and coordination of enforcement through Legal — continuously, across everything the framework classifies as worth protecting."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.4.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Protect Intellectual Assets"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers operational protection: applying the framework's handling rules (marking, access discipline, need-to-know, secure collaboration practice per CM-1-3-4-6 channels); maintaining trade-secret discipline; monitoring for potential misuse, infringement, and unauthorized disclosure from legitimate sources; and preparing and coordinating enforcement responses through Legal — cease-and-desist, opposition, litigation, and settlement are Legal's to execute and the applicable authorities' to approve. This process applies business handling discipline and escalates potential misuse, unauthorized disclosure, or infringement. Security incident response, cybersecurity controls, access provisioning, forensic investigation, and data-governance enforcement remain with their accountable owners. Excludes enforcement execution and litigation (Legal); security incident response, cybersecurity controls, access provisioning, and forensic investigation (Security, Data Governance); registration (CM-1-3-4-4-2); confidentiality agreement drafting (Legal).
In scope: Apply handling and trade-secret discipline | monitor for misuse and infringement from legitimate sources | escalate and coordinate enforcement through Legal
Out of scope: Enforcement execution/litigation (Legal) | security incident response, cyber controls, access provisioning, forensics (Security, Data Governance) | registration (CM-1-3-4-4-2) | agreement drafting (Legal)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Framework handling rules (CM-1-3-4-4-1) | monitoring signals | Legal advice"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Operational protection and coordinated enforcement responses"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Assets that stay protectable because they were handled as if they mattered."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-USPTO-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Set up Intellectual Asset Framework | governed-by: Manage Licensing & IP"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Cross-functional (Legal & Corporate Communications / Commercial & Marketing)"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 26 (2026-09-20). Protection function; continuous; enforcement on the b14 coordinate-never-execute pattern; review Security boundary verbatim — business handling discipline and escalation here, security frameworks and enforcement with their accountable owners; monitoring under the b21 legitimate-sources discipline. APQC 'Protect intellectual property' STRONG, no validator — genuine comparative anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-4-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19696" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19696"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-4 ;
    skos:definition "Exploit intellectual assets commercially — licensing out, brand and technology licensing arrangements, and contribution to products and partnerships — negotiating business terms within delegated authority while Legal drafts and executes every agreement."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.4.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Exploit Intellectual Assets"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers commercial exploitation: identifying exploitation opportunities from value assessments (CM-1-3-4-4-3); shaping licensing and partnership structures (brand licensing consistent with Brand Strategy CM-1-3-2-1 and brand standards CM-1-3-6-7; technology licensing consistent with R&D collaboration commitments); and negotiating business terms within delegated authority. Commercial proposals must be consistent with approved pricing, brand, product, franchise, competition-law, tax, export-control, and delegated-authority requirements. Legal drafts, reviews, negotiates legal terms, and executes agreements through authorized signatories. Where brand licensing touches franchise arrangements, the batch 22 fact-dependent PMPA statement applies. Excludes agreement drafting, legal-term negotiation, and execution (Legal, authorized signatories); approvals beyond delegated authority (commercial approvals); brand standards enforcement (CM-1-3-6-7); royalty accounting and collection (Finance, settlement processes).
In scope: Identify exploitation opportunities from value evidence | shape licensing and partnership structures consistent with brand and collaboration commitments | negotiate business terms within delegated authority and the applicable requirement set | coordinate agreements through Legal
Out of scope: Agreement drafting/legal terms/execution (Legal, authorized signatories) | approvals beyond delegated authority (commercial approvals) | brand standards enforcement (CM-1-3-6-7) | royalty accounting/collection (Finance, settlements)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Value assessments (CM-1-3-4-4-3) | brand and collaboration commitments | Legal and commercial approvals"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Negotiated, Legal-executed licensing and exploitation arrangements"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Assets earning their keep, on terms Legal wrote."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Value Intellectual Assets | governed-by: Manage Licensing & IP | informed-by: Brand Strategy"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Cross-functional (Legal & Corporate Communications / Commercial & Marketing)"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 26 (2026-09-20). Exploitation function; review commercial-terms boundary verbatim — proposals consistent with pricing, brand, product, franchise, competition-law, tax, export-control, and delegated-authority requirements; b20 Legal-coordinated execution and b22 PMPA fact-dependence where franchise arrangements are touched. APQC candidate is a WEAK false-direction match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-4-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19224" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19224"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-4 ;
    skos:definition "Steward the intellectual-asset portfolio — the maintained inventory of assets and registrations, their cost and value balance, and recommendations to maintain, renew, license, or prune — coordinated through Legal for every registration action."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.4.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Intellectual Asset Portfolios"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers portfolio stewardship: maintaining the asset and registration inventory with status, markets, and renewal calendar; reviewing value-versus-cost using CM-1-3-4-4-3 assessments; recommending maintain/renew/license/abandon actions under the framework's decision rights and delegated authority; and coordinating resulting registration actions through Legal. Recommendations to abandon, lapse, or cease maintaining an asset must consider active contracts, product claims, channel/franchise commitments, regulatory registrations, customer commitments, and ongoing enforcement or defense matters. Portfolio here is the intellectual-asset portfolio; product, service, and R&D portfolios belong to Lifecycle Management (CM-1-3-4-5). Excludes renewal and abandonment filings (Legal); product/service/R&D portfolio management (CM-1-3-4-5); accounting for intangibles (Finance).
In scope: Maintain the asset and registration inventory with renewal calendar | review value versus cost | recommend maintain/renew/license/abandon under delegated authority with discontinuance risks assessed | coordinate registration actions through Legal
Out of scope: Renewal/abandonment filings (Legal) | product/service/R&D portfolios (CM-1-3-4-5) | intangibles accounting (Finance)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Registration status (Legal) | value assessments (CM-1-3-4-4-3) | renewal costs"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The stewarded intellectual-asset portfolio with maintain/renew/prune recommendations"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A portfolio kept deliberately — nothing renewed by inertia, nothing lost by neglect."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-USPTO-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Value Intellectual Assets | governed-by: Manage Licensing & IP"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Cross-functional (Legal & Corporate Communications / Commercial & Marketing)"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 26 (2026-09-20). Portfolio function of the IP capability; review discontinuance-risk statement verbatim — abandonment recommendations consider contracts, claims, channel/franchise commitments, registrations, customer commitments, and live enforcement/defense matters; scoped against CM-1-3-4-5's portfolios. APQC 'Manage asset construction' is a FALSE match (construction assets)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-5-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16793" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16793"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-5 ;
    skos:definition "Identify the technology needs and opportunities the product and R&D portfolios imply — capability gaps, emerging technologies worth evaluating, and renewal needs — feeding portfolio valuation and R&D planning."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.5.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Identify Technology Needs/Opportunities"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers technology needs identification on the portfolio lens: reading portfolio assessments, development experience, sensing signals (CM-1-3-4-1-2, as signals not facts, per the batch 25 classification), and collaboration inputs (CM-1-3-4-6) for technology gaps and opportunities; framing them with evidence for valuation (CM-1-3-4-5-2) and R&D planning (CM-1-3-4-1-3). Technology needs are expressed as business capability/research needs, not solution architecture, product selection, infrastructure standards, or implementation decisions. Distinct from Market Sensing (forward scan) and from technology-architecture ownership (technology owners, batch 24 boundary). Excludes solution architecture, product selection, infrastructure standards, and implementation decisions (technology owners); R&D plan development (CM-1-3-4-1-3); the valuation (CM-1-3-4-5-2).
In scope: Read portfolio, development, sensing, and collaboration evidence for technology gaps and opportunities | express needs as business capability/research needs with evidence
Out of scope: Solution architecture, product selection, infrastructure standards, implementation (technology owners) | R&D planning (CM-1-3-4-1-3) | valuation (CM-1-3-4-5-2)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Portfolio assessments (CM-1-3-4-5-3) | sensing signals (CM-1-3-4-1-2) | collaboration inputs (CM-1-3-4-6)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Evidenced technology needs and opportunities as business capability/research needs"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The portfolio's technology gaps named before they become surprises."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Value Projects/Portfolios | governed-by: Lifecycle Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 26 (2026-09-20). First step of the portfolio cycle; review boundary verbatim — business capability/research needs, never architecture or implementation (Process Excellence & IT ownership consistent, b24 line). APQC 'Identify restructuring opportunities' is a FALSE lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-5-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20664" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20664"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-5 ;
    skos:definition "Value R&D projects and the product and service portfolios for prioritization — commercial value, strategic fit, risk, resource demand, and option value with stated assumptions and confidence — as decision evidence, not financial accounting."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.5.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Value Projects/Portfolios"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers portfolio valuation. Assess commercial value, strategic fit, risk, resource demand, and option value for portfolio prioritization, using stated assumptions and confidence. It does not determine accounting fair value, intangible-asset valuation, impairment, capitalization, or financial-reporting treatment. Valuation states evidence, commercial assumptions, dependencies, uncertainty, option value, resource demand, and downside risk, with methods kept consistent and comparable across the portfolio (b21 evidence discipline). Investment decisions are the delegated authorities'. Excludes accounting fair value, intangible valuation, impairment, capitalization, and financial reporting (Finance); investment decisions (delegated authorities); portfolio assessment against composition targets (CM-1-3-4-5-3).
In scope: Value projects and portfolio elements on commercial value, fit, risk, resource demand, and option value | state evidence, assumptions, dependencies, uncertainty, and downside risk | keep methods consistent and comparable
Out of scope: Accounting valuation/impairment/capitalization (Finance) | investment decisions (delegated authorities) | composition assessment (CM-1-3-4-5-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Technology needs (CM-1-3-4-5-1) | project and market evidence | resource data"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Comparable commercial valuations with uncertainty, option value, and downside risk"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every project's worth stated the same way, so priorities compare honestly."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Identify Technology Needs/Opportunities | precedes: Assess Portfolios | governed-by: Lifecycle Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 26 (2026-09-20). Valuation step; review commercial-valuation statement and optionality/uncertainty dimensions verbatim; Finance valuation lock continued. APQC 'Prioritize IT projects' is a FALSE lexical match (IT projects)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-5-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20819" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20819"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-5 ;
    skos:definition "Assess the product, service, and R&D portfolios against strategy — composition, balance, readiness, performance, and lifecycle position — producing the evidenced portfolio picture the recommendation step works from."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.5.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Assess Portfolios"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers portfolio assessment: analyzing composition and balance against the innovation vision and approved strategies (horizon mix, risk mix, market coverage); assessing performance and lifecycle position of in-market products and services using governed evidence; identifying candidates for stage, stop, accelerate, extend, reposition, or retire recommendations; and stating findings with confidence. Portfolio assessment may consider technical maturity, regulatory readiness, supply readiness, customer evidence, channel fit, brand fit, capability readiness, and lifecycle performance—not only financial performance. Assessment informs; recommendations are the manage step's (CM-1-3-4-5-4) and decisions the delegated authorities'. Excludes the recommendations (CM-1-3-4-5-4); decisions (delegated authorities); performance measurement execution (CM-1-3-5-5, operating processes).
In scope: Analyze portfolio composition and balance against strategy | assess technical, regulatory, supply, customer, channel, brand, and capability readiness alongside lifecycle performance | identify decision candidates with confidence
Out of scope: Recommendations (CM-1-3-4-5-4) | decisions (delegated authorities) | measurement execution (CM-1-3-5-5, operating processes)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Valuations (CM-1-3-4-5-2) | market and performance evidence | strategy direction (CM-1-3-2, CM-1-3-4-1)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Evidenced portfolio assessments with decision candidates"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "An honest picture of what the portfolio is, before deciding what it should be."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Value Projects/Portfolios | precedes: Manage R&D Portfolios | governed-by: Lifecycle Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 26 (2026-09-20). Assessment step; review readiness dimensions verbatim — maturity and readiness across the non-financial dimensions weigh alongside financial performance. APQC 'Assess IT remediation' is a FALSE lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-5-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Product, Service, and R&D Portfolios"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-5 ;
    skos:definition "Develop the portfolio recommendations and track delegated decisions — consolidating assessments into stage, stop, accelerate, extend, reposition, and retirement recommendations across the product, service, and R&D portfolios, submitting them to the applicable delegated authorities, and tracking execution of approved decisions through the accountable processes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.5.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage R&D Portfolios"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers portfolio management: consolidating valuations and assessments into portfolio recommendations; submitting recommendations through the applicable delegation of authority; recording approved decisions; and tracking their execution — development changes via the stage gates (CM-1-3-4-3-1), product retirement, extension, and repositioning via Manage Product/Service Change (CM-1-3-4-3-3) and the transition planning of the accountable processes (per the L4 transition statement). Scope spans the product, service, and R&D portfolios — the locked name understates this (preferred label queued). Recommends and tracks; never approves investments (delegated authorities) or executes changes (accountable processes). Excludes investment and portfolio approval (delegated authorities); change execution (CM-1-3-4-3-3, operating processes); development governance (CM-1-3-4-3-1); IP portfolio stewardship (CM-1-3-4-4-6).
In scope: Consolidate assessments into portfolio recommendations across product, service, and R&D portfolios | submit through delegated authority and record decisions | track execution through the accountable processes
Out of scope: Investment/portfolio approval (delegated authorities) | change execution (CM-1-3-4-3-3, operating processes) | development governance (CM-1-3-4-3-1) | IP portfolios (CM-1-3-4-4-6)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Portfolio assessments (CM-1-3-4-5-3) | delegated-authority requirements | execution status from accountable processes"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Portfolio recommendations, recorded decisions, and execution tracking"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Portfolio decisions made by the right authority, executed by the right process, tracked to done."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Assess Portfolios | enables: Manage Product/Service Change | governed-by: Lifecycle Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 26 (2026-09-20). Manage step of the portfolio cycle — conceptually 'Develop Portfolio Recommendations and Track Decisions' per review; the locked name 'Manage R&D Portfolios' is too narrow for its product/service/R&D scope, so the review's preferred label is queued prominently for the naming pass. Governance-safe wording (recommend/record/track, b22 pattern); execution routes through the b25 change-control discipline. APQC 'Manage routings' is a FALSE match (manufacturing routings — second occurrence)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-6-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16819" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16819"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-6 ;
    skos:definition "Collaborate with refining, blending, and manufacturing on product innovation — developability, blend and formulation feasibility, scale-up, and trial production — informing development without directing any manufacturing operation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.6.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Collaborate with Manufacturing"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the manufacturing collaboration channel: engaging refining, blending, lubricants, and packaging operations on candidate-product developability and cost; blend and formulation feasibility within operational constraints; scale-up planning and trial coordination; and feeding manufacturing insight into concepts and development. Trial production, scale-up, blend trials, plant trials, and operational experiments require the owning operating function's safety, quality, engineering, production-planning, scheduling, and change-control approvals. The locked boundaries govern: refining, blending, terminal, and manufacturing operations are directed by their owners (Refining, Midstream locks, batch 20 architecture) — collaboration informs and requests, never directs. Excludes manufacturing/refining operational direction (their owners, locked rules); trial approvals (owning operating functions); production scheduling (operations); quality operations (batch 18); development decisions (CM-1-3-4-3).
In scope: Engage operations on developability, feasibility, and cost | coordinate scale-up and trials under the owning function's approvals | feed manufacturing insight into concepts and development
Out of scope: Operational direction (Refining/Midstream locks) | trial approvals (owning operating functions) | production scheduling (operations) | quality operations (batch 18) | development decisions (CM-1-3-4-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Candidate products and concepts | operational constraints and cost data | trial capacity (operations)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Manufacturing feasibility insight and coordinated, operations-approved trials"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Products designed with the plant, not thrown over its wall."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: R&D Collaboration | enables: Conduct Product Development"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 26 (2026-09-20). Manufacturing channel; informs-and-requests, never directs — locked operational boundaries; review trial-approval statement verbatim (safety, quality, engineering, production-planning, scheduling, change-control approvals of the owning function). APQC 'Design for manufacturing' is a WEAK adjacent match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-6-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20044" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20044"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-6 ;
    skos:definition "Collaborate with customers and technical service on product innovation — performance needs, field experience, and field trials — under the applicable privacy, confidentiality, and intake controls, feeding real-use evidence into concepts and development."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.6.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Collaborate with Customers/Technical Service"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the customer collaboration channel: engaging customers, marketers, and technical service on product performance needs and field experience; coordinating field trials with customer consent and clear IP/confidentiality terms (through approved intake channels and Legal-executed agreements — batch 25 rule); and feeding validated field evidence into concepts (CM-1-3-4-2) and development (CM-1-3-4-3). Customer or field trials require appropriate product suitability, safety, insurance/liability, privacy/confidentiality, product stewardship, consent, technical-service, and contractual controls before use. Customer data and feedback operate under the batch 21 privacy and counterparty-confidentiality controls; competitively sensitive information about other parties is never solicited (batch 21 prohibition). Technical-service delivery itself is Service & Support's (CM-1-3-9). Excludes technical-service delivery (CM-1-3-9); account management (CM-1-3-6); agreement drafting (Legal); trial-readiness control ownership (accountable functions); the analyses (CM-1-3-1-4).
In scope: Engage customers and technical service on performance needs and field experience | coordinate consented field trials only after the required readiness controls are satisfied | feed validated field evidence into concepts and development
Out of scope: Technical-service delivery (CM-1-3-9) | account management (CM-1-3-6) | agreement drafting (Legal) | trial-readiness control ownership (accountable functions) | the analyses (CM-1-3-1-4)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Customer and technical-service relationships | field trial capacity | consented field data"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Validated field evidence for concepts and development"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Products proven where they are actually used, safely and by consent."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: R&D Collaboration | enables: Conduct Research | enables: Conduct Product Development"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 26 (2026-09-20). Customer channel; review field-trial readiness statement verbatim (suitability, safety, insurance/liability, privacy/confidentiality, product stewardship, consent, technical-service, contractual controls before use) — material for fuels, additives, lubricants, and services; b21 privacy/competitive controls and b25 intake rule govern. APQC 'Collaborate with customers' STRONG, no validator — genuine comparative anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-6-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10092" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10092"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-6 ;
    skos:definition "Collaborate with external research partners — universities, institutes, suppliers, and technology providers — on research and development of mutual interest, under agreements Legal executes and the IP framework governs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.6.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Collaborate with Research Partners"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the research-partner channel: identifying and engaging partners against the technology needs (CM-1-3-4-5-1) and R&D plans (CM-1-3-4-1-3); shaping collaboration scope, contributions, and IP positions within the intellectual-asset framework (CM-1-3-4-4-1); operating joint work under Legal-executed agreements sourced through Procurement (batch 25 boundary); and integrating partner results into concepts and development. Partner results must have defined provenance, permitted-use rights, confidentiality classification, IP allocation assumptions, publication/review controls, and data-sharing restrictions before integration into concepts or development. Partner selection and contracting follow Procurement and Legal; IP allocation positions are business input to Legal's drafting. Excludes partner contracting and agreement execution (Procurement, Legal); IP legal terms (Legal); the research/development work governance (CM-1-3-4-2-3, CM-1-3-4-3-1).
In scope: Engage partners against technology needs and plans | shape scope, contributions, and IP positions as business input | operate joint work under executed agreements | integrate results only with defined provenance, rights, and restrictions
Out of scope: Contracting/agreement execution (Procurement, Legal) | IP legal terms (Legal) | research/development governance (CM-1-3-4-2-3, CM-1-3-4-3-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Technology needs (CM-1-3-4-5-1) | partner capabilities | executed agreements (Legal, Procurement)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed partner collaborations and integrated results with provenance and rights"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Outside brains on inside problems, on paper Legal wrote."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: R&D Collaboration | enables: Conduct Research | uses-input: Identify Technology Needs/Opportunities"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 26 (2026-09-20). Partner channel; review provenance requirement verbatim (provenance, permitted-use rights, confidentiality classification, IP allocation assumptions, publication/review controls, data-sharing restrictions before integration) — extends the b21 evidence-lineage discipline; b25 procurement/Legal boundary governs. APQC 'Collaborate on design with suppliers and external partners' is a WEAK adjacent match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-6-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19553" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19553"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4-6 ;
    skos:definition "Collaborate with the marketing strategy, design, and planning functions on product and service innovation — carrying market-facing requirements into R&D and innovation results back into offers, communications, and launch preparation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.6.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Collaborate with marketing"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the internal marketing channel: carrying proposition, offer, channel, and experience requirements (CM-1-3-2, CM-1-3-3) into concepts and development; returning innovation results, constraints, and timing into offer definition (CM-1-3-3-1), communications strategy (CM-1-3-3-5), and planning (CM-1-3-5); and keeping innovation and marketing calendars coherent. The collaboration interface does not approve offers, claims, campaigns, pricing, channel changes, launch readiness, or market release. It provides inputs, timing, constraints, and readiness evidence to those accountable processes. An internal collaboration interface: the counterpart processes own their decisions (three-layer rule, batch 22); launch remains Planning/Execution's (batch 25 boundary). Excludes the counterpart processes' decisions (their owners); offer, claim, campaign, pricing, channel, and launch approvals (accountable processes); offer definition (CM-1-3-3-1); launch planning/execution (CM-1-3-5/6); development decisions (CM-1-3-4-3).
In scope: Carry market-facing requirements into concepts and development | return innovation results, constraints, timing, and readiness evidence to offers, communications, and planning | keep innovation and marketing calendars coherent
Out of scope: Counterpart decisions and approvals (their owners) | offer definition (CM-1-3-3-1) | launch (CM-1-3-5/6) | development decisions (CM-1-3-4-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Strategy and design requirements (CM-1-3-2/3) | development status (CM-1-3-4-3) | planning calendars (CM-1-3-5)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Coherent innovation-to-marketing requirements, timing, and readiness flow"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Innovation and marketing moving on one calendar, not surprising each other."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: R&D Collaboration | informed-by: Customer Offer Definition | enables: Marketing Communications Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 26 (2026-09-20). Internal marketing channel; review authority statement verbatim — provides inputs, timing, constraints, and readiness evidence; approves nothing the accountable processes own. Lowercase 'marketing' in the locked name — capitalization normalized in the queued label. APQC 'Collaborate with regulators' is a FALSE mapping (wrong counterpart entirely)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12900" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12900"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-1 ;
    skos:definition "Determine and allocate sales targets — volume, margin, and mix commitments by segment, channel, account group, and period — from approved plans and the governed forecast, as managerial commitments under the applicable approvals, never as a substitute forecast."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Determine & Allocate Sales Targets"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers target setting: deriving target envelopes from approved commercial plans and the governed demand forecast (consumed, never adjusted); allocating targets by segment, channel of trade, account group, and period; reconciling allocations with account plans (CM-1-3-5-1-3); and maintaining targets under the applicable delegation of authority. Target allocation is validated against approved supply, inventory, channel, sales-capacity, offer, pricing, and promotion assumptions; it does not create supply commitments or override allocation decisions. Targets are accountability constructs distinct from the forecast and from quotas (glossary at the L4); quota setting and target-based incentive design belong to the performance-management and compensation processes (HR). Excludes the forecast (CM-1-1-1-1); quota and incentive/compensation design (HR, performance management); supply commitments and allocation overrides (accountable operating processes); target approval beyond delegated authority (management authorities); account-level execution (CM-1-3-6).
In scope: Derive target envelopes from approved plans and the consumed forecast | allocate by segment, channel, account group, and period | validate against approved supply, capacity, offer, pricing, and promotion assumptions | maintain under delegated authority
Out of scope: The forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) | quotas and incentive design (HR, performance management) | supply commitments/allocation overrides (operating processes) | approvals beyond delegated authority (management) | execution (CM-1-3-6)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved plans (CM-1-3-2/5) | governed forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) | account, capacity, and promotion assumptions"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Allocated, feasibility-validated sales targets under delegated authority"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Commitments people can be held to, derived from evidence they can see."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Perform Account Planning | governed-by: Sales Strategy & Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Target step; review validation statement verbatim; target-versus-quota distinction per the L4 glossary. APQC 'Determine inventory targets' is a FALSE lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10235" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10235"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-1 ;
    skos:definition "Develop and agree the sales-side input process into the governed demand forecast — which sales intelligence, account plans, and pipeline evidence feed Produce Demand Forecast, in what form, cadence, and quality — never producing or adjusting the forecast itself."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop & Agree Demand Forecasting Process"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the forecast-input operating agreement: agreeing with Produce Demand Forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) what sales-side inputs the governed forecast consumes (account intelligence, pipeline evidence, promotional and campaign calendars, channel signals); defining input form, cadence, quality expectations, and accountabilities on the sales side; and maintaining the agreement as planning evolves — an enduring operating agreement rather than a recurring annual phase (review). The enterprise demand forecast is produced, published, changed, versioned, and governed only by Produce Demand Forecast (CM-1-1-1-1). The sales organization supplies agreed sales intelligence, account plans, pipeline evidence, promotion calendars, channel signals, and other approved inputs to that process. Sales targets are management-accountability commitments allocated from approved plans, not forecasts; they must never be relabeled, substituted for, or used to overwrite the governed demand forecast. The forecast process itself, its methods, and its publication are Produce Demand Forecast's; disagreements about forecast content follow that process's governance. Excludes forecast production, methods, publication, and adjustment (CM-1-1-1-1, locked); forecast-process ownership (CM-1-1-1-1); the inputs' downstream use (CM-1-1-1-1).
In scope: Agree the sales-input set, form, cadence, and quality with Produce Demand Forecast | define sales-side input accountabilities | maintain the enduring input agreement
Out of scope: Forecast production/methods/publication/adjustment (CM-1-1-1-1, locked) | forecast-process ownership (CM-1-1-1-1) | downstream input use (CM-1-1-1-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Produce Demand Forecast's input requirements (CM-1-1-1-1) | sales planning outputs | promotional calendars (CM-1-3-5-4)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The agreed, maintained sales-input process into the governed forecast"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The forecast fed well by sales, and never fought by sales."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Produce Demand Forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) | enables: Determine & Allocate Sales Targets | enables: Perform Account Planning | governed-by: Sales Strategy & Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). The batch's sharpest boundary row: the locked name could read as owning the forecasting process — the canonical rule (verbatim in scope note) fixes the reading: sales-side input process only, agreed with the forecast owner. Relation decision (review): an enduring operating agreement, so it enables targets and account planning rather than strictly preceding them each cycle. APQC 'Develop baseline demand forecasts' is a false-direction match (forecast production, exactly what this row must not do)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10728" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10728"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-1 ;
    skos:definition "Perform account planning for the accounts and account groups the sales plan prioritizes — objectives, opportunity maps, action plans, and resource assumptions per account — feeding execution and the forecast-input process with account-level evidence."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Account Planning"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers account planning: building account plans (objectives, opportunity and risk maps, planned actions, service and support assumptions) for prioritized accounts and marketer relationships using governed counterparty evidence (CM-1-3-1-4) and cost-to-serve input (CM-1-2-6-1-3); aligning plans with targets and channel engagement models (CM-1-3-3-3-4); and feeding account intelligence into the forecast-input process (CM-1-3-5-1-2). Account plans may use customer, relationship, commercial, and performance data only through approved access and privacy/confidentiality controls. They must not incorporate improperly obtained competitor, supplier, or customer-confidential information. Planning, not execution: account interventions, negotiations, and relationship execution belong to Channel & Partner Management and Sales Execution (CM-1-3-6-1/-2); credit decisions to the credit process (b11 boundary); individual account assignments follow the management processes (b23 boundary). Excludes account execution (CM-1-3-6-1/-2); credit decisions (b11 boundary); account assignments (management processes, b23); pricing and quotes (CM-1-3-6-3, CM-1-3-7-2).
In scope: Build account plans from governed evidence under approved access controls | align with targets and engagement models | feed account intelligence into the forecast-input process
Out of scope: Account execution (CM-1-3-6-1/-2) | credit decisions (b11 boundary) | account assignments (management, b23) | pricing/quotes (CM-1-3-6-3, CM-1-3-7-2)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Counterparty findings (CM-1-3-1-4) | targets (CM-1-3-5-1-1) | engagement models (CM-1-3-3-3-4) | cost-to-serve (CM-1-2-6-1-3)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Account plans with objectives, actions, and evidence"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every priority account approached with a plan, not a habit."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Determine & Allocate Sales Targets | enables: Sales Execution | enables: Channel & Partner Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Account-planning step; review data restriction verbatim — approved access and privacy/confidentiality controls only, no improperly obtained competitor, supplier, or customer-confidential information (b21 discipline); b23 assignment boundary inherited. APQC 'Perform planning and management accounting' is a FALSE lexical match (accounting)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-1-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10025" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10025"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Analyze Gaps Between Forecast, Plan, Targets, and Actual Margin Performance"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-1 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze the gaps between the demand forecast, sales plan, targets, and actual margin performance — where assumptions, plans, and commitments diverged from governed actuals, and why — producing the evidence the next planning cycle corrects from."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.1.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Analyze Gaps Between Forecast & Actual Margins"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers margin-gap analysis: comparing governed actual volume, mix, and margin (Finance and settlement data — consumed, never produced) against the demand forecast and sales-plan assumptions, targets, and margin expectations; building the margin bridge and decomposing variance drivers by segment, channel, account, and cause; distinguishing plan quality, input quality, execution, and market causes; and feeding findings to the next cycle, the forecast-input process, and Marketing Performance Analysis (CM-1-3-5-5). Terminology (review): the locked name compares objects of different kinds — the process compares demand forecast and sales-plan assumptions, targets, actual volume/mix/margin, and the margin bridge/variance drivers; the preferred label is queued. Analysis, not correction: pricing corrections follow the pricing processes; forecast-method questions follow Produce Demand Forecast's governance; findings with potential legal or compliance implications escalate to Legal/Compliance (b23 pattern). Excludes financial reporting production (Finance); forecast adjustment (CM-1-1-1-1); pricing corrections (CM-1-3-6-3); target changes (CM-1-3-5-1-1 with approvals); legal determinations (Legal/Compliance).
In scope: Compare governed actuals to forecast assumptions, plans, and targets | build the margin bridge and decompose variance drivers | distinguish plan, input, execution, and market causes | feed findings to the next cycle and performance analysis
Out of scope: Financial reporting (Finance) | forecast adjustment (CM-1-1-1-1) | pricing corrections (CM-1-3-6-3) | target changes (CM-1-3-5-1-1) | legal determinations (Legal/Compliance)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Governed actuals (Finance, settlements) | forecast and plan assumptions | targets (CM-1-3-5-1-1)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Margin-gap findings with the bridge and causes, feeding the next planning cycle"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Next year's plan built on why this year's plan missed."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Perform Account Planning | enables: Sales Strategy & Planning | informed-by: Marketing Performance Analysis"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Feedback step of the sales-planning cycle; review terminology correction — forecast (demand/volume) and margin (financial expectation) are different objects, so the comparison set is spelled out and the corrected label queued; governed-actuals discipline (Finance data consumed, never produced). APQC returned NONE — 'Analyze demographics' is a false match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11222" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11222"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5 ;
    skos:definition "Integrate the advertising and media plans across campaigns, brands, and channels into one coherent plan — a single calendar, budget envelope, and priority set implementing the approved media and advertising strategies — so buying and execution work from one integrated plan rather than competing campaign plans."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Integrated Marketing Planning (Advertising)"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers integrated advertising planning: consolidating campaign-level advertising and media needs (from CM-1-3-5-3) into one integrated plan; maintaining the integrated advertising calendar and budget envelope under the applicable delegation of authority; prioritizing and resolving conflicts across campaigns, brands, and channels; and issuing the integrated plan to media buying and execution through the procurement and execution controls (b24 media boundary — buying and placement follow Procurement/Marketing Execution controls). The integrated plan may prioritize campaigns within an approved envelope but cannot create new funding, approve media commitments beyond authority, or override Legal/Compliance claim/disclosure approval. Implements the approved media strategy (CM-1-3-3-5-1) and advertising strategy (CM-1-3-3-5-3); claims and creative approvals follow the b24 substantiation controls. Excludes media and advertising strategy (CM-1-3-3-5-1/-3); campaign development (CM-1-3-5-3-4); media buying and placement execution (Procurement, CM-1-3-6-5); claim approval (Legal, Compliance); budget approval and new funding (Finance, management authorities).
In scope: Consolidate campaign advertising and media needs into one integrated plan | maintain the integrated calendar and budget envelope under delegated authority | prioritize and resolve cross-campaign conflicts within the approved envelope | issue the plan through procurement and execution controls
Out of scope: Media/advertising strategy (CM-1-3-3-5-1/-3) | campaign development (CM-1-3-5-3-4) | buying/placement execution (Procurement, CM-1-3-6-5) | claim approval (Legal, Compliance) | budget approval/new funding (Finance, management)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved media and advertising strategies (CM-1-3-3-5-1/-3) | campaign plans and needs (CM-1-3-5-3) | budget envelope (delegated authority)"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The integrated advertising plan, calendar, and budget envelope"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One advertising plan, one calendar, one envelope — not a bidding war between campaigns."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FTC-ADM-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Define Media Strategy | informed-by: Marketing Communications Planning | enables: Marketing Plan Execution"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — an integration process (childless L4; the b24 integration pattern applied to plans); tactical. Review funding-authority statement verbatim — prioritizes within the envelope, creates no funding, approves no commitments beyond authority, overrides no Legal/Compliance approvals. APQC returned NONE — no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-3-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10418" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10418"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-3 ;
    skos:definition "Identify the marketing communications needs each planning cycle must serve — which offers, propositions, launches, and channel priorities need communications support, with what urgency and evidence."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Identify Marketing Needs"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers needs identification: reading the approved strategies, offer pipeline (CM-1-3-3-1), innovation timing (CM-1-3-4-6-4 interface), channel priorities, and performance findings (CM-1-3-5-5) for communications needs; framing and prioritizing needs with evidence for the mix model and plan. Excludes strategy (CM-1-3-3-5); the mix and plan decisions (CM-1-3-5-3-3/-6); campaign development (CM-1-3-5-3-4).
In scope: Read strategies, offers, launches, and performance evidence for communications needs | frame and prioritize needs with evidence
Out of scope: Strategy (CM-1-3-3-5) | mix and plan decisions (CM-1-3-5-3-3/-6) | campaign development (CM-1-3-5-3-4)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved strategies and offers | innovation and launch timing | performance findings (CM-1-3-5-5)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Prioritized communications needs per cycle"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Communications money pointed at the needs that exist."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Identify Target Groups | governed-by: Marketing Communications Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Needs step; performance findings feed this step (lifecycle feedback per review). APQC 'Identify strategic HR needs' is a FALSE lexical match (HR)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-3-10 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11136" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11136"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Prepare and Release Brand Advertising and Communications Deliverables"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-3 ;
    skos:definition "Coordinate production, approval readiness, controlled release, and handoff of brand advertising and communications deliverables — brand-building campaign materials from approved packages to execution — recording readiness while the accountable approvers authorize release."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.3.10" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Implement Brand Advertising / Communications"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers brand-deliverable preparation and release coordination: coordinating production of brand campaign materials from campaign packages (CM-1-3-5-3-4), consistent with the brand strategy (CM-1-3-2-1) and standards (via CM-1-3-5-3-7); confirming approval readiness across the control gates, including environmental and brand claims per the b24 fuel-claim discipline; coordinating placement scheduling through the media-buying and procurement controls; and handing released deliverables to execution (CM-1-3-6-5). Release coordination confirms that required Legal, Compliance, Brand, Privacy, Procurement, channel/site, and delegated commercial approvals are complete. The accountable approver authorizes release; this process records readiness and coordinates the handoff. Excludes in-channel execution (CM-1-3-6-5); release authorization (accountable approvers); claim approval (Legal, Compliance); media buying commitments (Procurement); brand strategy and standards (CM-1-3-2-1, CM-1-3-6-7).
In scope: Coordinate production of brand deliverables | confirm approval readiness including claims and brand gates | coordinate placement scheduling through buying controls | record readiness and hand off to execution
Out of scope: Execution (CM-1-3-6-5) | release authorization (accountable approvers) | claim approval (Legal, Compliance) | buying commitments (Procurement) | brand strategy/standards (CM-1-3-2-1, CM-1-3-6-7)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Campaign packages (CM-1-3-5-3-4) | brand direction and standards (CM-1-3-2-1, via -3-7) | approval status (accountable approvers)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Release-ready brand deliverables handed to execution with recorded readiness"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The brand's voice produced and released exactly as approved."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FTC-ADM-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Develop / Update Campaigns | enables: Marketing Plan Execution | governed-by: Marketing Communications Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Release-preparation child per the review narrowing; release-authority statement verbatim; b24 substantiation and fuel-claim disciplines inherited; preferred label queued. APQC returned NONE."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-3-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20143" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20143"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-3 ;
    skos:definition "Identify the target groups each communications need addresses — selecting audiences from the governed segmentation and targeting evidence within the privacy, fairness, and contactability controls."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Identify Target Groups"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers communications targeting: selecting audiences per need from the marketing-lens segmentation evidence (CM-1-3-1-3/-4) and the offer target groups (CM-1-3-3-1-1); applying the batch 23 targeting controls (approved purposes, privacy, consent, suppression, fairness, non-discrimination) and the b24 contactability discipline for direct channels (consume, never create consent status); and recording targeting rationale for the plan. Excludes the segmentation analyses (CM-1-3-1); consent/suppression data ownership (accountable systems); offer targeting decisions (CM-1-3-3-1-1); the plan (CM-1-3-5-3-6).
In scope: Select audiences per need from governed evidence | apply privacy, fairness, and contactability controls | record targeting rationale
Out of scope: The analyses (CM-1-3-1) | consent/suppression ownership (accountable systems) | offer targeting (CM-1-3-3-1-1) | the plan (CM-1-3-5-3-6)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Communications needs (CM-1-3-5-3-1) | segmentation evidence (CM-1-3-1-3/-4) | contactability status (accountable systems)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Target groups per communications need with rationale"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The right audiences, chosen lawfully, documented."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Identify Marketing Needs | precedes: Develop / Update Marketing Mix Model | governed-by: Marketing Communications Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Targeting step; b23 targeting controls and b24 contactability rule inherited. APQC 'Identify impacted groups' is a FALSE lexical match (risk management)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-3-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10102" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10102"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Develop and Update Marketing Mix Model"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-3 ;
    skos:definition "Develop and update the marketing mix model — how communications effort and spend distribute across channels, campaigns, and audiences for best effect — using performance evidence and the strategy's channel roles."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop / Update Marketing Mix Model"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers mix modeling: allocating communications effort across the channel roles set by strategy (media, direct, POS, digital, internal — CM-1-3-3-5) per need and target group; using performance evidence (CM-1-3-5-5) and measurement-framework metrics (b24) to update the mix; and stating assumptions and confidence (b21 discipline). The mix model informs budgets and the plan; channel-role strategy stays with CM-1-3-3-5. Excludes channel-role strategy (CM-1-3-3-5); budget approval (delegated authority); analytic model/technology ownership (technology owners); the plan (CM-1-3-5-3-6).
In scope: Allocate effort across strategy-set channel roles per need and audience | update the mix from performance evidence | state assumptions and confidence
Out of scope: Channel-role strategy (CM-1-3-3-5) | budget approval (delegated authority) | model/technology ownership (technology owners) | the plan (CM-1-3-5-3-6)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Needs and target groups (CM-1-3-5-3-1/-2) | performance evidence (CM-1-3-5-5) | strategy channel roles (CM-1-3-3-5)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The current marketing mix model with assumptions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Spend distributed by evidence, not by last year's split."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Identify Target Groups | precedes: Define Resource Allocation / Budgets | governed-by: Marketing Communications Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Mix step; normalized label queued. APQC 'Develop marketing strategy' is a WEAK broad match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-3-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10428" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10428"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Develop and Update Campaigns"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-3 ;
    skos:definition "Develop and update campaigns — objectives, audiences, messages, channel plans, creative briefs, and measurement hooks per campaign — within the communications plan and the claims and brand controls, assembled as approval-routed campaign packages."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.3.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop / Update Campaigns"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers campaign development: building each campaign from the plan (objectives, target groups, messages within the approved claims territory, channel plan from the mix model, budget from the allocation, measurement hooks from the communications measurement framework — b24); developing creative briefs and coordinating creative development; and routing claims, disclosures, and brand use through their approvals (Legal/Compliance for substantiation; CM-1-3-6-7 standards via CM-1-3-5-3-7) before release preparation. A campaign is ready for release preparation only when its approval and readiness artifacts are complete — a creative brief alone is not readiness (review). Excludes claim/disclosure approval (Legal, Compliance); brand standards (CM-1-3-6-7); release preparation and handoff (CM-1-3-5-3-8/-9/-10); in-channel execution (CM-1-3-6-5); media buying (Procurement, execution).
In scope: Build campaigns from the plan with measurement hooks | develop creative briefs and coordinate creative | route claims and brand use through the accountable approvals | assemble approval-routed campaign packages
Out of scope: Claim approval (Legal, Compliance) | brand standards (CM-1-3-6-7) | release preparation (CM-1-3-5-3-8/-9/-10) | execution (CM-1-3-6-5) | media buying (Procurement)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "The communications plan (CM-1-3-5-3-6) | mix and budgets (CM-1-3-5-3-3/-5) | claims territory (CM-1-3-3-5-3) | measurement framework (b24)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Approval-routed campaign packages with creative, channel, measurement, compliance, brand, and execution-readiness artifacts"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Campaigns built approved, measurable, and on-brief."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FTC-ADM-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Define Communications Plan | precedes: Implement Promotional Advertising | precedes: Implement Brand Advertising / Communications | governed-by: Marketing Communications Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Campaign step; review output correction — approval-routed campaign packages with creative, channel, measurement, compliance, brand, and execution-readiness artifacts; b24 substantiation-before-dissemination inherited; normalized label queued. APQC 'Develop other HR programs' is a FALSE lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-3-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20688" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20688"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Define Resource Allocation and Budgets"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-3 ;
    skos:definition "Define the communications resource allocation and budgets — distributing the approved envelope across needs, campaigns, and channels per the mix model — as allocations under the applicable delegation of authority."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.3.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Resource Allocation / Budgets"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers allocation: distributing the approved communications budget envelope across needs, campaigns, and channels per the mix model; maintaining allocations as plans change; and tracking committed versus available within the planning cycle. Allocations operate under the applicable delegation of authority; budget approval and financial control are Finance's and the management authorities'; procurement commitments follow Procurement. Excludes budget approval (Finance, management authorities); financial accounting and control (Finance); procurement commitments (Procurement); spend-effectiveness analysis (CM-1-3-5-5-1).
In scope: Distribute the approved envelope per the mix model | maintain allocations as plans change | track committed versus available
Out of scope: Budget approval (Finance, management) | financial control (Finance) | procurement commitments (Procurement) | spend analysis (CM-1-3-5-5-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved envelope (delegated authority) | mix model (CM-1-3-5-3-3) | campaign needs"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Communications allocations under delegated authority"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every campaign funded on purpose from one envelope."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Develop / Update Marketing Mix Model | precedes: Define Communications Plan | governed-by: Marketing Communications Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Allocation step; normalized label queued. APQC 'Optimize IT resource allocation' is a FALSE lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-3-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "17477" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 17477"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-3 ;
    skos:definition "Define the communications plan — the consolidated plan of campaigns, channels, audiences, timings, budgets, and responsibilities for the cycle — the single planning artifact the release preparation and execution work from."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.3.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Communications Plan"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers plan definition: consolidating needs, target groups, mix, and allocations into the communications plan with campaign list, channel plans, timings, responsibilities, and measurement expectations; synchronizing with the integrated advertising plan (CM-1-3-5-2), promotions calendars (CM-1-3-5-4), and launch timings (innovation interface); and maintaining the plan through the cycle. Excludes the campaign builds (CM-1-3-5-3-4); release preparation (CM-1-3-5-3-8/-9/-10); execution (CM-1-3-6-5); strategy (CM-1-3-3-5).
In scope: Consolidate the cycle's communications plan with timings and responsibilities | synchronize with advertising, promotions, and launch calendars | maintain the plan through the cycle
Out of scope: Campaign builds (CM-1-3-5-3-4) | release preparation (CM-1-3-5-3-8/-9/-10) | execution (CM-1-3-6-5) | strategy (CM-1-3-3-5)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Needs, groups, mix, allocations (CM-1-3-5-3-1/-2/-3/-5) | calendars (CM-1-3-5-2/-4) | launch timings"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The maintained communications plan"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One plan everyone implements from."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Define Resource Allocation / Budgets | precedes: Develop / Update Campaigns | governed-by: Marketing Communications Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Plan step. APQC 'Define sampling plan' is a FALSE lexical match (quality sampling)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-3-7 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-3 ;
    skos:definition "Manage the use of the brand in communications — a planning and material-review control applying the standards owned by Brand Standard Management within planned communications materials, release packages, and placements — escalating deviations to the accountable owners."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.3.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Use Of Brand"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers brand-use management in communications: applying and coordinating use of standards owned by Brand Standard Management (CM-1-3-6-7) within planned communications materials, release packages, and placements; maintaining usage guidance for the planning and release-preparation children; and escalating deviations, exceptions, and trademark/legal questions to the accountable standards and Legal owners. This process coordinates review and obtains the required approval or exception decision from the accountable Brand Standard Management and Legal owners; it does not itself grant exceptions. Trademark legal questions go to Legal (b26 IP architecture); licensed brand use follows the licensing arrangements (CM-1-3-4-4-5). Excludes brand standards ownership and exception granting (CM-1-3-6-7); brand strategy (CM-1-3-2-1); trademark legal work (Legal); licensing arrangements (CM-1-3-4-4-5).
In scope: Apply the owned standards to materials, release packages, and placements | maintain usage guidance | coordinate review and obtain approval or exception decisions from the accountable owners | escalate deviations and legal questions
Out of scope: Standards ownership and exception granting (CM-1-3-6-7) | brand strategy (CM-1-3-2-1) | trademark legal work (Legal) | licensing (CM-1-3-4-4-5)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Brand standards (CM-1-3-6-7) | campaign materials and release packages (CM-1-3-5-3-4/-8/-10) | licensing terms where applicable"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Standards-conformant brand use across communications, with owner-decided exceptions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The brand used right in everything the plan produces."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Marketing Communications Planning | uses-input: Brand Standard Management | assures: Develop / Update Campaigns"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Continuous planning/material-review control; review wording — applies and coordinates, obtains decisions from the accountable owners, grants nothing itself. APQC 'Manage routings' is a FALSE match (third occurrence)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-3-8 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11495" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11495"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Prepare and Release Promotional Advertising Deliverables"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-3 ;
    skos:definition "Coordinate production, approval readiness, controlled release, and handoff of promotional advertising deliverables — from approved campaign packages to execution — recording readiness while the accountable approvers authorize release."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.3.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Implement Promotional Advertising"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers promotional-deliverable preparation and release coordination: coordinating production of promotional campaign materials from campaign packages (CM-1-3-5-3-4); confirming approval readiness across the control gates; coordinating placement scheduling through the media-buying and procurement controls (commitments are Procurement's and the authorized buying process's); and handing released deliverables to in-channel and in-site execution (CM-1-3-6-5) synchronized with the promotions calendars (CM-1-3-5-4). Release coordination confirms that required Legal, Compliance, Brand, Privacy, Procurement, channel/site, and delegated commercial approvals are complete. The accountable approver authorizes release; this process records readiness and coordinates the handoff. Promotional pricing content follows the pricing processes (b23/24 boundaries). Excludes in-channel/in-site execution and activation (CM-1-3-6-5); release authorization (accountable approvers); claim approval (Legal, Compliance); media buying commitments (Procurement, authorized process); promotional pricing (pricing processes); promotions planning (CM-1-3-5-4).
In scope: Coordinate production of promotional deliverables | confirm approval readiness across the control gates | coordinate placement scheduling through buying controls | record readiness and hand off to execution on calendar
Out of scope: Execution/activation (CM-1-3-6-5) | release authorization (accountable approvers) | claim approval (Legal, Compliance) | buying commitments (Procurement) | promotional pricing (pricing processes) | promotions planning (CM-1-3-5-4)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Campaign packages (CM-1-3-5-3-4) | promotions calendars (CM-1-3-5-4) | approval status (accountable approvers)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Release-ready promotional deliverables handed to execution with recorded readiness"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Promotional creative that ships approved, scheduled, and on time."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FTC-ADM-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Develop / Update Campaigns | enables: Marketing Plan Execution | governed-by: Marketing Communications Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Release-preparation child per the review narrowing — coordinate production, approval readiness, controlled release, and handoff; never placement, delivery, channel execution, customer contact, or release approval. Release-authority statement verbatim in scope note; preferred label queued prominently. APQC 'Implement promotional pricing programs' is a false-direction match (pricing)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-3-9 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11136" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11136"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Prepare and Release Internal Marketing Communications"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-3 ;
    skos:definition "Coordinate production, approval readiness, controlled release, and handoff of internal marketing communications — the campaigns and materials that promote the company's products, objectives, and marketing programs to employees — within the internal-communications boundary shared with Corporate Communications and HR."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.3.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Implement Internal Communications"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers internal marketing communications preparation and release per the approved internal strategy (CM-1-3-3-5-6): coordinating production of internal campaign materials (product launches, program awareness, sales enablement, employee brand engagement); confirming approval readiness and brand use (CM-1-3-5-3-7); coordinating channels with the owners of internal communication platforms; and handing released materials to delivery. Release coordination confirms that required Legal, Compliance, Brand, Privacy, Procurement, channel/site, and delegated commercial approvals are complete. The accountable approver authorizes release; this process records readiness and coordinates the handoff. Internal marketing communications must not be used to issue corporate policy, regulatory instructions, safety notices, employment terms, labor-relations communications, or official crisis communications unless the accountable Corporate Communications, HR, Legal, EHS, or Compliance owner has approved and owns the message. Excludes corporate communications (Legal & Corporate Communications, locked rule); HR/employee-relations communications (HR); platform ownership (their owners); release authorization (accountable approvers); the internal strategy (CM-1-3-3-5-6).
In scope: Coordinate production of internal marketing materials per the approved strategy | confirm approval readiness and brand use | coordinate channels with platform owners | record readiness and hand off for delivery
Out of scope: Corporate communications (locked rule) | HR communications (HR) | platform ownership (owners) | release authorization (accountable approvers) | internal strategy (CM-1-3-3-5-6)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Internal strategy (CM-1-3-3-5-6) | campaigns and launches | internal channels (platform owners)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Release-ready internal marketing communications with recorded readiness"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Employees hearing about the products before the customers do."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Develop / Update Campaigns | governed-by: Marketing Communications Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Release-preparation child per the review narrowing; release-authority statement and internal-communications prohibition verbatim in scope note — never corporate policy, regulatory instructions, safety notices, employment terms, labor-relations, or crisis communications without the accountable owner owning the message; preferred label queued. APQC 'Implement change' is a WEAK generic match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-4-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "17477" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 17477"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-4 ;
    skos:definition "Define the promotional plan — which promotions run, for which offers, audiences, channels, and periods, with what mechanics, budgets, and success measures — within the pricing and claims guardrails."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.4.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Promotional Plan"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers promotional-plan definition: selecting and scheduling promotions against offers (CM-1-3-3-1), audiences, and channel priorities; defining promotion mechanics within the pricing guardrails (promotional price content designed with and governed by the pricing processes — b23 discipline); setting budgets within the allocation (CM-1-3-5-3-5 coordination) and success measures from the measurement frameworks; and routing claims through their approvals. The L4's conditional readiness framework applies before any promotion enters a calendar. Excludes promotional price setting (pricing processes); rebate design (CM-1-3-6-4); claim approval (Legal); the calendars (CM-1-3-5-4-2/-3/-4); execution (CM-1-3-6-5).
In scope: Select and schedule promotions with mechanics inside the pricing guardrails | set budgets and success measures | route claims through approvals
Out of scope: Promotional prices (pricing processes) | rebates (CM-1-3-6-4) | claim approval (Legal) | calendars (CM-1-3-5-4-2/-3/-4) | execution (CM-1-3-6-5)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Offers (CM-1-3-3-1) | pricing guardrails (CM-1-3-3-2) | budgets and measures"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The promotional plan with mechanics, budgets, and measures"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Promotions chosen for effect, priced by the price system."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Create Promotional Event Calendar | governed-by: Promotions Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Plan step; pricing-guardrail discipline per b23; L4 readiness framework applies. APQC 'Define sampling plan' is a FALSE lexical match (second occurrence)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-4-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10147" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10147"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-4 ;
    skos:definition "Create and maintain the promotional event calendar — the scheduled promotional events across channels and periods — synchronized with the communications plan, supply and inventory readiness, and the forecast-input process."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.4.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Create Promotional Event Calendar"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the event calendar: scheduling promotional events from the plan once the L4 readiness framework is confirmed; synchronizing iteratively with the communications plan (CM-1-3-5-3-6), advertising calendar (CM-1-3-5-2), supply and inventory readiness (through the accountable operating processes), and the forecast-input process (CM-1-3-5-1-2 — promotions are a forecast input, per the batch 03 architecture); and maintaining the calendar as events change. Excludes event execution (CM-1-3-6-5); supply commitments (operating processes); the plan decisions (CM-1-3-5-4-1).
In scope: Schedule readiness-confirmed promotional events | synchronize iteratively with communications, advertising, supply, and forecast inputs | maintain the calendar
Out of scope: Event execution (CM-1-3-6-5) | supply commitments (operating processes) | plan decisions (CM-1-3-5-4-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Promotional plan (CM-1-3-5-4-1) | communications and advertising calendars | supply and inventory readiness inputs"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The maintained promotional event calendar"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every promotion on a calendar supply can see coming."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Define Promotional Plan | governed-by: Promotions Planning | enables: Develop & Agree Demand Forecasting Process"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Calendar child; promotions feed the forecast as inputs (b03); iterative readiness synchronization per review. APQC returned NONE."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-4-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10295" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10295"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Create Point-of-Purchase (POP) Promotional Calendar"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-4 ;
    skos:definition "Create and maintain the point-of-purchase promotional calendar — the scheduled POP promotional activity at forecourts, stores, and other points of sale — within the POS communication and regulatory-display boundaries."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.4.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Create Point Of Purchase (Pop) Promotional Calendar"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the POP calendar: scheduling POP promotional activity per site cluster and period from the promotional plan, consistent with the POS communication strategy (CM-1-3-3-5-4); coordinating material and display readiness with release preparation (CM-1-3-5-3-8) and execution (CM-1-3-6-5); and maintaining the calendar. The batch 24 POS exclusions apply verbatim by reference: no fuel-price setting, pump-sign configuration, legal metrology, product labeling, or price-display execution — those belong to pricing, site-operations, and EHS/regulatory processes. Excludes POS execution and merchandising (CM-1-3-6-5/-7); price display and regulatory signage (b24 owners); the plan decisions (CM-1-3-5-4-1).
In scope: Schedule POP promotional activity by site cluster and period | coordinate material and display readiness | maintain the calendar within the POS boundaries
Out of scope: POS execution/merchandising (CM-1-3-6-5/-7) | price display and regulatory signage (b24 owners) | plan decisions (CM-1-3-5-4-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Promotional plan (CM-1-3-5-4-1) | POS strategy (CM-1-3-3-5-4) | site readiness inputs"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The maintained POP promotional calendar"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The right promotion at the right pump and shelf, on schedule."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Define Promotional Plan | governed-by: Promotions Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Calendar child; b24 POS exclusions inherited by reference; '(Pop)' casing in the locked name — normalized label queued. APQC 'Create/Distribute purchase orders' is a FALSE lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-4-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10157" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10157"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-4 ;
    skos:definition "Create and maintain the site-level marketing calendar — the consolidated marketing activity view per site or site cluster — so sites see one coherent calendar instead of competing programs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.4.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Create Site Level Marketing Calendar"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the site calendar: consolidating promotional events, POP activity, campaign touchpoints, and program activity into per-site/cluster calendars; deconflicting overlapping activity; coordinating with site operations for feasibility (site operating constraints owned by the operating functions, b20/24 architecture); and maintaining the calendar for execution (CM-1-3-6-5). Excludes site operations and execution (operating functions, CM-1-3-6-5); network design (CM-1-3-3-4); the underlying plans (CM-1-3-5-3/-4).
In scope: Consolidate marketing activity into per-site calendars | deconflict overlapping activity | coordinate feasibility with site operations | maintain for execution
Out of scope: Site operations/execution (operating functions, CM-1-3-6-5) | network design (CM-1-3-3-4) | underlying plans (CM-1-3-5-3/-4)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Event and POP calendars (CM-1-3-5-4-2/-3) | campaign plans (CM-1-3-5-3) | site feasibility inputs"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The maintained site-level marketing calendar"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One calendar per site, not five programs colliding at the till."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Define Promotional Plan | governed-by: Promotions Planning | enables: Marketing Plan Execution"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Calendar child; site operating constraints owned by operations (b20/24). APQC 'Create marketing budget' is a WEAK adjacent match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-5-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10102" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10102"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-5 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze marketing spend — where the money went versus the allocations, with what efficiency across channels, campaigns, and programs — from governed financial data on a stated cost basis, as evidence for allocation and mix decisions."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.5.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Marketing Spend Analysis"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers spend analysis: analyzing actual marketing spend against allocations (CM-1-3-5-3-5) and the integrated advertising envelope (CM-1-3-5-2) from governed Finance and procurement data (consumed, never produced); stating the cost basis used (committed, invoiced, paid, or accrued) and the cost types covered (media, production, agency, trade marketing, promotion, loyalty/program) and reconciling to Finance-controlled records — this process does not define Finance's accounting taxonomy; computing efficiency measures per the communications measurement framework (b24); identifying leakage, timing, and mix variances; and feeding findings to allocation, mix-model, and planning updates. Excludes financial accounting and reporting (Finance); accounting taxonomy definition (Finance); procurement performance management (Procurement); allocation decisions (CM-1-3-5-3-5); ROI attribution (CM-1-3-5-5-2).
In scope: Analyze actual spend versus allocations from governed data on a stated, reconciled cost basis | compute efficiency per the measurement framework | identify variances and feed planning updates
Out of scope: Financial accounting/reporting and taxonomy (Finance) | procurement management (Procurement) | allocation decisions (CM-1-3-5-3-5) | ROI attribution (CM-1-3-5-5-2)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Governed spend data (Finance, Procurement) | allocations (CM-1-3-5-3-5) | measurement framework (b24)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Spend findings with stated cost basis, variances, and efficiency evidence"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Knowing where the money actually went before allocating more."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Marketing Performance Analysis | enables: Define Resource Allocation / Budgets"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Spend child; review cost-basis note — the analysis states its basis (committed/invoiced/paid/accrued) and cost types and reconciles to Finance-controlled records without defining Finance's taxonomy. APQC returned NONE."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-5-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "13169" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 13169"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-5 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze campaign returns — outcomes against the measurement hooks each campaign carried, attribution within stated methodological limits, and return on spend — producing campaign-level evidence with assumptions and confidence stated."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.5.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Campaign ROI Analysis"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers campaign ROI analysis: measuring campaign outcomes against the measurement hooks defined at development (CM-1-3-5-3-4) and the communications measurement framework (b24); performing attribution within stated methodological limits; computing return on spend using governed cost data. ROI and attribution findings should identify the methodology, attribution window, control/baseline approach, data completeness, assumptions, uncertainty, incremental-versus-correlated effects, and known limitations. Where experimental, quasi-experimental, or control-group methods are unavailable, findings must be described as association, contribution, or directional evidence—not as proven causal incremental return. Feeds findings to mix, plan, and strategy updates. Excludes campaign execution (CM-1-3-6-5); financial reporting (Finance); mix and plan decisions (CM-1-3-5-3-3/-6); enterprise KPI publication (KPI Store).
In scope: Measure outcomes against campaign measurement hooks | attribute within stated limits, identifying methodology, window, baseline, completeness, and uncertainty | compute return on spend from governed data | feed mix, plan, and strategy updates
Out of scope: Execution (CM-1-3-6-5) | financial reporting (Finance) | mix/plan decisions (CM-1-3-5-3-3/-6) | KPI publication (KPI Store)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Campaign measurement hooks (CM-1-3-5-3-4) | outcome data (execution processes) | governed cost data"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Campaign ROI findings with methodology, assumptions, and confidence"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Campaign returns stated with the honesty the attribution deserves."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Marketing Performance Analysis | enables: Develop / Update Marketing Mix Model"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). ROI child; review methodology and no-causality-overclaim rules verbatim in scope note — without experimental or control-group methods, findings are association, contribution, or directional evidence, never proven causal incremental return (b21 evidence discipline extended). APQC returned NONE."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-5-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20007" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20007"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5-5 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze customer programs — loyalty, card, and customer program performance against their design intents — within the privacy controls, as evidence for program design and planning decisions, never as program operation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.5.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Customer Program Analysis"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers customer program analysis: analyzing loyalty and cards programs (operated at CM-1-3-6-6), offers-in-program performance, and customer program economics against their design intents and the measurement frameworks. Customer Program Analysis may analyze program performance using authorized, privacy-controlled, aggregate or de-identified data. It does not make customer eligibility, reward, account, credit, fraud, consent, or loyalty-operational decisions. Findings may inform program, offer, proposition, and experience decisions, but the process does not redefine the value proposition, offer, loyalty mechanics, or customer experience strategy. Uses personal, loyalty, and transaction data only under the batch 21 shared governance statement and the aggregate/de-identified publication default. Excludes loyalty and cards operation (CM-1-3-6-6); customer eligibility, reward, account, credit, fraud, and consent decisions (their accountable processes); program, offer, proposition, and experience redefinition (their owners); the consumer analyses (CM-1-3-1-3); privacy framework ownership (governance owners).
In scope: Analyze program performance and economics against design intent from authorized, privacy-controlled data | operate within the publication default | feed program, offer, and planning decisions without making them
Out of scope: Loyalty/cards operation (CM-1-3-6-6) | eligibility, reward, account, credit, fraud, consent decisions (accountable processes) | program/offer/proposition/experience redefinition (their owners) | consumer analyses (CM-1-3-1-3) | privacy frameworks (governance owners)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Program data within consent (CM-1-3-6-6) | design intents | measurement frameworks"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Customer program findings within privacy controls"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Programs judged by evidence, run by their operators."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Marketing Performance Analysis | informed-by: Loyalty And Cards Management - Marketing"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Program child; review boundaries verbatim — analysis never drifts into operation, customer-service, fraud, privacy, or credit scope, and never redefines propositions, offers, mechanics, or experience strategy; b21 privacy statement and publication default inherited. APQC 'Define customer loyalty program' is a false-direction match (program design)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Franchisee Relationships Under Franchise Agreements"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-1 ;
    skos:definition "Manage the ongoing relationships with franchisees under their franchise agreements — engagement, support, performance dialogue, and issue resolution within the agreement's terms — with every change to the legal relationship handled through Legal."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Relationship with Franchise agreement"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers franchisee relationship management: running the engagement cadence per the designed model; supporting franchisees with programs, offers, and standards guidance (standards owned at CM-1-3-6-7); conducting performance dialogue from the reported evidence (CM-1-3-6-1-3); resolving operational issues within the agreement's terms; and preparing business input for agreement changes. Commercial relationship issues that may alter a legal agreement, franchise right, supply obligation, lease relationship, or statutory notice must be referred to Legal and the appropriate approval authority. Where an arrangement meets the applicable statutory and contractual criteria for a motor-fuel franchise, changes involving trademark, supply, lease, renewal, termination, or non-renewal may be subject to PMPA and state-law constraints. Legal & Corporate Communications determines applicability, notice requirements, legal analysis, documentation, and formal legal action. This process never issues franchise notices, alters legal terms, or makes franchise classification decisions. Excludes franchise legal work of every kind (Legal, locked rule); agreement drafting/amendment execution (Legal); brand standards ownership (CM-1-3-6-7); rebates and program funds administration (CM-1-3-6-4); site operations (operating functions).
In scope: Run the franchisee engagement cadence | support with programs and standards guidance | conduct performance dialogue | resolve operational issues within agreement terms | refer legal-touching issues to Legal and the approval authority
Out of scope: Franchise legal work, notices, and determinations (Legal, locked rule) | agreement changes (Legal) | brand standards ownership (CM-1-3-6-7) | rebates (CM-1-3-6-4) | site operations (operating functions)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Franchise agreements (Legal-executed) | engagement model (CM-1-3-3-3-4) | performance reporting (CM-1-3-6-1-3)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Managed franchisee relationships within agreement terms"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Franchisees supported and held to the deal, with Legal holding the law."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-PMPA-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Channel & Partner Management | uses-input: Report Franchise Performance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 28 (2026-09-20). Continuous relationship channel; relationship management, not agreement administration or legal franchise control — the review referral rule verbatim in scope note; preferred label per review queued (the locked name reads as a relationship with an agreement). APQC 'Manage routings' is a FALSE match (fourth occurrence)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "14209" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 14209"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-1 ;
    skos:definition "Manage the ongoing relationships with licensed-brand providers — the parties whose brands the company licenses in, or to whom it licenses out — operating the commercial relationship within the Legal-executed licensing arrangements."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Relationship with License Brand Providers"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers licensed-brand relationship management: operating the commercial relationship under the licensing arrangements shaped at CM-1-3-4-4-5 and executed by Legal; coordinating brand-use obligations both ways (standards owned at CM-1-3-6-7 for own brands; the licensor's requirements per the agreement for licensed brands); monitoring relationship performance against the arrangement; and preparing business input for renewals and changes (negotiated per CM-1-3-4-4-5, executed by Legal). The process manages commercial relationship performance and operating coordination. It does not determine trademark validity, trademark-use legal sufficiency, licensing legal terms, royalty accounting, or brand-standard policy. Royalty accounting and payments follow Finance and settlement processes (b26 boundary). Excludes licensing negotiation and structuring (CM-1-3-4-4-5); agreement drafting/execution and licensing legal terms (Legal); trademark validity and use-sufficiency determinations (Legal); royalty accounting/payment (Finance, settlements); brand-standard policy (CM-1-3-6-7).
In scope: Operate licensed-brand relationships under executed arrangements | coordinate brand-use obligations both ways | monitor relationship performance | prepare business input for renewals and changes
Out of scope: Licensing negotiation/structuring (CM-1-3-4-4-5) | agreement execution and licensing legal terms (Legal) | trademark determinations (Legal) | royalty accounting/payment (Finance, settlements) | brand-standard policy (CM-1-3-6-7)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Licensing arrangements (CM-1-3-4-4-5, Legal-executed) | brand standards and licensor requirements | relationship performance data"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Managed licensed-brand relationships within their arrangements"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Licensed brands working as the arrangements intended, both directions."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Channel & Partner Management | informed-by: Exploit Intellectual Assets"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 28 (2026-09-20). Continuous relationship channel; review non-determination rule verbatim — commercial performance and operating coordination only; b26 exploitation architecture governs. APQC returned NONE."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16574" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16574"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-1 ;
    skos:definition "Report franchise and partner performance — volumes, standards conformance, program participation, and relationship health against the measurement frameworks — producing the evidence the relationship management and planning processes act from."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Report Franchise Performance"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers partner performance reporting: assembling franchisee and partner performance evidence (volumes and mix from governed sources, standards conformance, program participation, service levels) against the channel measurement framework (CM-1-3-3-3-5) and stewarded metrics; producing per-partner and network views for relationship dialogue (CM-1-3-6-1-1), planning (CM-1-3-5), and performance analysis (CM-1-3-5-5); and maintaining reporting cadence. Standards-conformance results are consumed from Brand Standard Management and relevant operating/EHS processes; this process reports them in the partner-performance view without independently certifying standards compliance. Evidence-layer reporting with KPI Store routing (b21/23 rule); commercially sensitive partner data handled per classification (b21); financial reporting is Finance's; relationship health is an analytical signal (b21 discipline). Excludes standards assessment and certification (CM-1-3-6-7, operating/EHS processes); measurement-framework definition (CM-1-3-3-3-5); financial reporting (Finance); performance dialogue and interventions (CM-1-3-6-1-1); enterprise KPI publication (KPI Store).
In scope: Assemble partner performance evidence against the channel measurement framework | consume standards-conformance results without certifying them | produce per-partner and network views for dialogue, planning, and analysis | maintain reporting cadence within data classifications
Out of scope: Standards assessment/certification (CM-1-3-6-7, operating/EHS) | framework definition (CM-1-3-3-3-5) | financial reporting (Finance) | interventions (CM-1-3-6-1-1) | KPI publication (KPI Store)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Governed volume and program data | standards conformance (CM-1-3-6-7, operating/EHS) | channel measurement framework (CM-1-3-3-3-5)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Per-partner and network performance reporting"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every partner conversation starting from the same evidence."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Channel & Partner Management | enables: Manage Relationship with Franchise agreement | uses-input: Insight and Metrics"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 28 (2026-09-20). Reporting child; review consumed-not-certified rule verbatim; evidence-layer with KPI Store routing. APQC 'Monitor and report performance' STRONG, no validator — genuine comparative anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-1-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10289" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10289"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Train and Certify Franchise Site Managers"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-1 ;
    skos:definition "Train and certify franchise site managers against the brand and operating standards — the structured training and certification program that qualifies partner site management to run branded operations — with the standards and mandatory-training regimes owned by their accountable processes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.1.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Train and Certify AMPM Manager"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers franchise-manager training and certification. Operate the commercial/brand/operating-standard training and certification program for franchise or branded-site managers. It does not replace legally required, EHS, food-safety, hazardous-materials, employment, transportation, regulatory, or technical training owned by the relevant accountable function. Operates the training program for partner site managers against the brand standards (CM-1-3-6-7), the applicable operating procedures (CM-1-3-3-6-4 framework), and program requirements; administers certification and recertification with records (validity, expiry, suspension, and transfer conditions per the program's rules); and coordinates training obligations that arise from franchise agreements (obligations set in the Legal-executed agreements). Generic reading (review): the locked name carries a specific franchise brand ('ampm'); the row is defined generically as franchise site-manager training and certification, with the brand-specific origin recorded here and the preferred label a high-priority naming-normalization item. Excludes legally required, EHS, food-safety, hazardous-materials, employment, transportation, regulatory, and technical training (accountable owners); standards ownership (CM-1-3-6-7, procedure owners); company-staff HR training (HR); agreement training obligations' legal terms (Legal).
In scope: Operate franchise site-manager training against the owned standards | administer certification and recertification with records | coordinate agreement-driven training obligations
Out of scope: Mandatory EHS/safety/regulatory/technical training (accountable owners) | standards ownership (CM-1-3-6-7, procedure owners) | company-staff HR training (HR) | legal terms of training obligations (Legal)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Brand and operating standards (CM-1-3-6-7, CM-1-3-3-6-4) | agreement training obligations | certification records"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Trained, certified franchise site managers with maintained records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every branded site run by someone certified to run it."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Channel & Partner Management | uses-input: Brand Standard Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 28 (2026-09-20). Review decision: brand-specific 'AMPM' in the locked name — defined generically; brand origin recorded; preferred label 'Train and Certify Franchise Site Managers' is a HIGH-PRIORITY naming-normalization item (the taxonomy should not hard-code one brand). Review boundary verbatim — a commercial/brand certification program, never a substitute for mandatory training owned elsewhere. APQC 'Certify and validate suppliers' is a FALSE match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-2-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20011" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20011"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-2 ;
    skos:definition "Manage the sales pipeline — the maintained, staged view of prospects and opportunities across segments and channels — keeping pipeline evidence honest, current, and feeding the forecast-input process."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Sales Pipeline"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers pipeline management: maintaining the staged pipeline with entry/exit discipline and evidence standards; balancing pipeline against targets and coverage; feeding pipeline evidence into the forecast-input process (CM-1-3-5-1-2, b27 agreement) and planning; and reviewing pipeline health. Pipeline stage, probability, value, timing, and close-date assumptions must be evidence-based, versioned where material, and governed by the agreed sales-input process. Pipeline optimism must not be substituted for the enterprise demand forecast. Pipeline data uses customer information under approved access and privacy controls (b27 account-data restriction). Excludes opportunity pursuit (CM-1-3-6-2-3); target setting (CM-1-3-5-1-1); the forecast (CM-1-1-1-1, locked); CRM platform ownership (technology owners).
In scope: Maintain the staged pipeline with evidence-based, versioned assumptions | balance pipeline against targets and coverage | feed pipeline evidence to the forecast-input process | review pipeline health
Out of scope: Opportunity pursuit (CM-1-3-6-2-3) | targets (CM-1-3-5-1-1) | the forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) | CRM ownership (technology owners)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Opportunities (CM-1-3-6-2-3) | targets (CM-1-3-5-1-1) | account plans (CM-1-3-5-1-3)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The maintained, evidence-disciplined sales pipeline"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A pipeline you can plan from because it tells the truth."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Manage Opportunity | governed-by: Sales Execution | enables: Develop & Agree Demand Forecasting Process"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 28 (2026-09-20). Pipeline overlay of the pursuit lifecycle; review forecast-integrity safeguard verbatim — pipeline optimism never substitutes for the governed forecast (b27 reinforced). APQC 'Manage opportunity pipeline' STRONG, no validator — genuine anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10183" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10183"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-2 ;
    skos:definition "Manage the key account relationships — executing the account plans for the accounts that matter most, coordinating the company's face to each account, and keeping relationship commitments within the approved commercial frameworks."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Key Accounts"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers key-account management: executing account plans (CM-1-3-5-1-3) for designated key accounts and marketer relationships; coordinating cross-functional touchpoints (service, credit, supply, technical) so the account experiences one company; conducting account reviews from governed evidence; and maintaining relationship commitments within the approved offer, pricing, and contract frameworks. Key-account coordination may use approved account information, but must not override customer master data, credit hierarchy, contract records, consent status, service-case records, or delegated account assignment controls. Account designation and assignments follow the management processes (b23/27 boundary); commitments requiring new terms route through quotes/contracts and their approvals; credit is the credit process's (b11); relationship health remains an analytical signal (b21). Excludes account-plan creation (CM-1-3-5-1-3); account assignments (management processes); new-term approvals (quote/contract, delegated authorities); credit (b11 boundary); service delivery (CM-1-3-9); master data, consent, and case-record overrides (their accountable owners).
In scope: Execute account plans for key accounts | coordinate the cross-functional account experience | conduct account reviews from governed evidence | keep commitments within approved frameworks and data controls
Out of scope: Account-plan creation (CM-1-3-5-1-3) | assignments (management processes) | new-term approvals (quote/contract, delegated authorities) | credit (b11) | service delivery (CM-1-3-9) | master data/consent/case-record overrides (accountable owners)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Account plans (CM-1-3-5-1-3) | governed account evidence | cross-functional touchpoint inputs"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Executed account plans and coordinated key-account relationships"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Key accounts experiencing one company, on plan."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Sales Execution | uses-input: Perform Account Planning | enables: Manage Opportunity"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 28 (2026-09-20). Continuous overlay across the full pursuit and relationship lifecycle (review); data boundary verbatim — coordination never overrides master data, credit hierarchy, contract records, consent, service cases, or assignment controls. APQC 'Manage customers and accounts' STRONG, no validator — genuine anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-2-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20011" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20011"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-2 ;
    skos:definition "Manage each sales opportunity from qualification through pursuit — pursuit strategy, stakeholder engagement, and solution shaping within the approved offers — driving the commercial pursuit to a qualified proceed/no-proceed or handoff outcome."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Opportunity"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers opportunity management: qualifying opportunities into pursuit with evidence; running pursuit strategy and stakeholder engagement per the account plan; shaping the proposed solution within the approved offers (CM-1-3-3-1) and channel offerings (CM-1-3-3-3-1) — never inventing unapproved products, terms, or claims; and driving the commercial pursuit to a qualified proceed/no-proceed or handoff outcome, with proceeding opportunities handed to quote development (CM-1-3-6-2-4). Competitive intelligence in pursuit follows the b21 legitimate-sources prohibition; pricing content comes from Sales Pricing (CM-1-3-6-3) within the approved architecture. Excludes offer definition (CM-1-3-3-1); price determination (CM-1-3-6-3); quote development (CM-1-3-6-2-4); contract terms and transaction closing (quote/contract processes, Legal, delegated authorities); improper competitive intelligence (prohibited, b21).
In scope: Qualify opportunities with evidence | run pursuit strategy and engagement per the account plan | shape solutions within approved offers | drive to a qualified proceed/no-proceed or handoff outcome
Out of scope: Offer definition (CM-1-3-3-1) | price determination (CM-1-3-6-3) | quote development (CM-1-3-6-2-4) | contract terms and closing (quote/contract, Legal, authorities) | improper competitive intelligence (prohibited)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Qualified pipeline (CM-1-3-6-2-1) | account plans and key-account coordination | approved offers (CM-1-3-3-1)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Qualified proceed/no-proceed outcomes with proceeding opportunities handed to quoting"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Opportunities pursued on strategy and handed off on approved terms."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Manage Sales Pipeline | precedes: Develop Quote | governed-by: Sales Execution"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 28 (2026-09-20). Per-opportunity pursuit step; review outcome wording — a qualified proceed/no-proceed or handoff outcome, never a unilateral close (transactions complete in the controlled processes). APQC 'Manage opportunity pipeline' 0.8 STRONG — genuine anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-2-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10428" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10428"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-2 ;
    skos:definition "Develop the commercial quote for a proceeding opportunity — assembling the offer, volume, service, and price content within the approved frameworks — for processing through the quote-and-sale transaction process, creating no binding commitment itself."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.2.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Quote"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers quote development on the commercial side: assembling quote content from the approved offers (CM-1-3-3-1), channel offerings, and applied pricing (CM-1-3-6-3, within the approved architecture, corridors, and delegated authority — exceptions per the b23 criteria); validating deliverability with the affected operating processes; and submitting the quote through the quote-and-sale transaction process (CM-1-3-7-2). The commercial quote content must identify the intended offer, product/service, quantity, location, delivery basis, price basis, validity period, conditions, and required approvals. It is submitted for controlled transactional processing; it does not itself create an executed agreement. A commercial quote package is not a binding offer, contract, price commitment, credit approval, or order until it has completed the applicable transaction, pricing, Legal, Credit, Contract, and delegated-authority controls. The partition: commercial quote content here; transactional processing, configuration, and the controlled quote/contract record in O2C. Contract legal terms are Legal's; credit clearance the credit process's (b11). Excludes quote/sale transaction processing (CM-1-3-7-2); price determination and exceptions beyond delegated authority (CM-1-3-6-3, approvals); contract legal terms (Legal); credit clearance (b11 boundary); order capture (CM-1-3-7-3).
In scope: Assemble quote content identifying offer, product, quantity, location, delivery basis, price basis, validity, conditions, and required approvals | validate deliverability with operating processes | submit through the quote-and-sale transaction process
Out of scope: Transaction processing (CM-1-3-7-2) | price determination/exceptions beyond authority (CM-1-3-6-3, approvals) | contract legal terms (Legal) | credit (b11) | order capture (CM-1-3-7-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Proceeding opportunities (CM-1-3-6-2-3) | approved offers (CM-1-3-3-1) | applied prices (CM-1-3-6-3)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Complete commercial quote content submitted to the transaction process"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Quotes that are sellable, priceable, and deliverable before they are sent — and binding only after the controls."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Manage Opportunity | precedes: Manage Contract & Order | enables: Manage Pricing & Contracting (Manage Quote & Sale)"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 28 (2026-09-20). Commercial quote step of the partition — content here, transaction at CM-1-3-7-2; review non-binding statement and content specification verbatim in scope note. APQC 'Develop other HR programs' is a FALSE lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-2-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10332" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10332"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Commercial Contract and Order Relationship"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-2 ;
    skos:definition "Manage the customer's contract-and-order relationship commercially — from accepted quote through contract setup coordination and ongoing order-relationship stewardship — with the transactional contract, order, and fulfillment records owned by the order-to-cash processes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.2.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Contract & Order"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers commercial contract-and-order relationship management: coordinating contract setup from accepted quotes (legal drafting and execution Legal's; transactional contract pricing and records at CM-1-3-7-2); stewarding the ongoing order relationship commercially (volume performance against commitments, issue resolution routing, change coordination); and keeping account plans and pipeline current from contract-and-order reality. The partition: commercial stewardship here; order capture, fulfillment management, and the transactional records at CM-1-3-7-3; invoicing and disputes at CM-1-3-8 (five-quadrant dispute architecture, b10/17); physical delivery locked to CM-1-1-3-8. Excludes contract legal work (Legal, locked rule); transactional contract/pricing records (CM-1-3-7-2); order capture and fulfillment (CM-1-3-7-3); invoicing and disputes (CM-1-3-8, b10/17 architecture); physical delivery (CM-1-1-3-8, locked).
In scope: Coordinate contract setup from accepted quotes | steward the order relationship commercially | route issues to their owning processes | keep plans current from contract reality
Out of scope: Contract legal work (Legal) | transactional records (CM-1-3-7-2/-3) | invoicing/disputes (CM-1-3-8) | physical delivery (CM-1-1-3-8, locked)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Accepted quotes (via CM-1-3-7-2) | contract commitments | order and fulfillment status (CM-1-3-7-3)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Commercially stewarded contract-and-order relationships"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The commercial relationship behind every contract kept alive and honest."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Develop Quote | precedes: Serve to Customer (Non-Retail) | enables: Capture And Manage Order Fulfillment"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 28 (2026-09-20). Commercial stewardship step of the partition; review preferred label queued — makes the commercial-stewardship role explicit against O2C transactional ownership. APQC 'Manage service order fulfillment' is an adjacent O2C-side match (fulfillment is CM-1-3-7-3's)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-2-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-2 ;
    skos:definition "Serve non-retail customers commercially — the standing commercial serving relationship for commercial, industrial, and wholesale customers — maintaining relationship cadence and coordinating resolution while the owning processes execute delivery, service, cases, and billing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.2.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Serve to Customer (Non-Retail)"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers commercial serving of non-retail customers. Maintain a commercial relationship cadence; monitor commitments and satisfaction; coordinate cross-functional resolution; and refer specific requests, incidents, service cases, billing issues, delivery exceptions, or technical inquiries to the processes that own case handling and execution. Coordinates across the executing processes — physical delivery by Secondary Distribution (CM-1-1-3-8, locked rule), terminal loading per the terminal processes, service and support by CM-1-3-9, case handling by CM-1-3-7-5, invoicing by CM-1-3-8-1 — so the customer's experience matches the commitments, and feeds serving evidence to account management and performance reporting. Coordinates and refers, never executes or duplicates case handling: the b19/20 fulfillment-coordination discipline applies and this process is not a shadow service desk. Excludes physical delivery and scheduling (CM-1-1-3-8, locked); terminal operations (their processes); service delivery (CM-1-3-9); case handling (CM-1-3-7-5); invoicing (CM-1-3-8-1); retail site serving (retail operations).
In scope: Maintain the commercial relationship cadence for non-retail customers | monitor commitments and satisfaction | coordinate cross-functional resolution | refer requests, incidents, cases, billing, delivery, and technical matters to their owning processes
Out of scope: Physical delivery/scheduling (CM-1-1-3-8, locked) | terminal operations (their processes) | service delivery (CM-1-3-9) | case handling (CM-1-3-7-5) | invoicing (CM-1-3-8-1) | retail serving (retail operations)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Contract commitments (CM-1-3-6-2-5) | delivery and service status (owning processes) | customer communications"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Coordinated commercial serving against commitments, with referred cases in their owning processes"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Non-retail customers served as promised, by the processes that own the work."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Manage Contract & Order | governed-by: Sales Execution | uses-input: Manage Customer Requests and Inquiries"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 28 (2026-09-20). Continuous serving relationship; review referral wording verbatim — cadence, monitoring, coordination, and referral; no shadow service desk (case handling is CM-1-3-7-5's). APQC returned NONE — 'Invoice customer' is a false match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-2-7 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16395" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16395"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Measure and Report Sales Performance"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-2 ;
    skos:definition "Measure and report sales-execution performance — executing approved sales-performance measures under the relevant measurement frameworks, producing evidence-layer views and variance findings for the planning feedback loops."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.2.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Measure & report"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers sales measurement and reporting. Execute approved sales-performance measures under the relevant measurement frameworks; produce evidence-layer views and variance findings; and route candidate KPI proposals through the KPI Store rather than publishing enterprise KPIs. Measures execution against targets, account plans, and the relevant measurement frameworks; produces pursuit, conversion, account, and serving views for sales management, planning (CM-1-3-5-1-4 margin-gap analysis), and Marketing Performance Analysis (CM-1-3-5-5); and maintains reporting cadence. Governed actuals from Finance consumed, never produced (b27); quota and incentive measurement belong to the performance-management processes (b27 glossary). Excludes measurement-framework definition (b23/24 rows); financial reporting (Finance); quota/incentive administration (HR, performance management); plan corrections (CM-1-3-5-1 with approvals); enterprise KPI publication (KPI Store).
In scope: Execute approved sales-performance measures under the frameworks | produce evidence-layer pursuit, conversion, account, and serving views with variance findings | route candidate KPI proposals through the KPI Store
Out of scope: Framework definition (b23/24) | financial reporting (Finance) | quota/incentive administration (HR) | plan corrections (CM-1-3-5-1) | KPI publication (KPI Store)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Governed actuals (Finance) | targets and plans (CM-1-3-5-1) | pipeline and account evidence"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Evidence-layer sales performance views and variance findings"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Sales execution seeing its own scoreboard weekly, honestly."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Sales Execution | enables: Analyze Gaps Between Forecast & Actual Margins | enables: Marketing Performance Analysis"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 28 (2026-09-20). Periodic feedback loop to planning, pipeline, account plans, offers, and pricing strategy; review metric qualifier verbatim; lowercase locked name — expanded normalized label queued. APQC 'Measure and report process performance' STRONG, no validator — genuine anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-3-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10209" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10209"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-3 ;
    skos:definition "Determine posted and transaction prices by applying approved price architecture, effective-dated price rules, differentials, indices, corridors, floors, ceilings, customer/channel eligibility, and delegated exception criteria to current permitted inputs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Determine sales price"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers price determination, distinguishing (review): rule-driven price calculation; delegated price override within authority; price exception request beyond authority; legal-risk escalation; and price-effectivity timing. Computes posted and transaction prices per the approved architecture from current permitted inputs; keeps determinations within the approved corridors, floors, ceilings, and eligibility; and records determinations with effectivity for deployment (CM-1-3-6-3-2) and realization analysis (CM-1-3-3-2-3). Determinations outside approved authority, corridor, eligibility, or legal-risk thresholds are routed for approval; the process does not approve its own exception beyond delegated authority. Pricing decisions with price-discrimination, below-cost, or unfair-sales risk escalate per the b23 fact-dependent statement — Legal and Compliance determine applicability and sufficiency; competitor price inputs only from legitimate sources (b23 rule). Excludes architecture, corridor, and eligibility changes (CM-1-3-3-2); exception approval beyond delegated authority (the authorities); quote/contract price configuration (CM-1-3-7-2); legal determinations (Legal, Compliance).
In scope: Compute prices per the approved architecture and effective-dated rules from permitted inputs | keep within corridors, ceilings, floors, and eligibility | route overrides and exceptions per delegated criteria with rationale | escalate legal-risk situations | record determinations with effectivity
Out of scope: Architecture/corridor/eligibility changes (CM-1-3-3-2) | exception approval beyond authority (authorities) | quote/contract configuration (CM-1-3-7-2) | legal determinations (Legal, Compliance)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved architecture and effective-dated rules (CM-1-3-3-2) | permitted market and cost inputs | exception criteria"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Determined prices with effectivity, within the architecture, exceptions documented"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Today's prices computed from the approved system, not improvised."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FTC-RP-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Implement sales price | governed-by: Sales Pricing Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 28 (2026-09-20). Determination step of the daily cycle; review wording adopted verbatim as the definition; the five distinctions (calculation, override, exception, escalation, effectivity) recorded; no-self-exception rule verbatim; lowercase locked name — capitalization queued. APQC 'Determine sales resource allocation' is a FALSE lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-3-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11497" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11497"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-3 ;
    skos:definition "Implement determined sales prices — submitting approved price changes to the accountable Price Master Data process and coordinating deployment to approved pricing systems, channels, customer communications, and downstream consumers — verifying effective-date, completeness, and audit-trail status."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Implement sales price"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers price implementation. Submit approved price changes to the accountable Price Master Data process; coordinate deployment to approved pricing systems, channels, customer communications, and downstream consumers; verify effective-date, completeness, and audit-trail status. Coordinates channel and site price data (physical price display, pump-sign configuration, and legal metrology remain with site operations and EHS/regulatory processes, b24), customer price communications per contractual notice terms, and the downstream consumers (quotes CM-1-3-7-2, invoicing CM-1-3-8-1); maintains the price-change audit trail for realization analysis (CM-1-3-3-2-3). Excludes price determination (CM-1-3-6-3-1); price master-data ownership and record changes (CM-1-3-7-1, the accountable process); physical display and metrology (site operations, EHS/regulatory, b24); invoicing (CM-1-3-8-1); customer contract notice terms (contracts, Legal).
In scope: Submit approved price changes to the Price Master Data process | coordinate deployment to approved systems, channels, and communications per notice terms | verify effective-date, completeness, and audit-trail status
Out of scope: Price determination (CM-1-3-6-3-1) | price master-data ownership (CM-1-3-7-1) | physical display/metrology (site operations, b24) | invoicing (CM-1-3-8-1) | notice terms (contracts, Legal)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Determined prices with effectivity (CM-1-3-6-3-1) | Price Master Data process (CM-1-3-7-1) | deployment channels and notice terms"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Deployed prices with verified effectivity and a complete audit trail"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The determined price live everywhere it should be, provably."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Determine sales price | governed-by: Sales Pricing Management | enables: Manage Customer Invoicing and Billing"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 28 (2026-09-20). Implementation step of the daily cycle; review master-data correction adopted verbatim as the operative wording — submits to, never deploys into, the accountable Price Master Data process; the b24 price-display split maintained; lowercase locked name — capitalization queued. APQC 'Communicate and implement price changes' is a genuine adjacent match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-4-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10464" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10464"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-4 ;
    skos:definition "Negotiate the terms of an individual rebate arrangement with a customer or channel partner — eligibility, performance conditions, rates or tiers, measurement basis, period, and settlement method — within the approved rebate architecture and delegations of authority, obtaining the approvals required before commitment."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.4.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Negotiate Rebate"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Negotiation operates only within: the approved rebate architecture; approved customer/channel eligibility; delegated commercial authority; Legal/Compliance guardrails; pricing corridors and exception criteria; and contract and master-data controls. No individual arrangement may create a rebate structure, treatment, eligibility rule, payment term, customer classification, or price effect outside the approved architecture and delegation. Terms outside approved structures or thresholds require approval through Pricing Strategy Management governance and the applicable delegations before any commitment. Fact-dependent statement: where a rebate, discount, promotional allowance, or service/payment arrangement differs among purchasers that may compete, US price-discrimination or promotional-allowance requirements may be implicated depending on commodity comparability, purchaser status, competitive effect, available defenses, and other facts. Legal/Compliance determines applicability and sufficiency. Contractual formalization of agreed terms executes through the contract-management processes, not within this process.
In scope: Prepare negotiation positions from approved structures and verified customer data | negotiate terms within architecture, eligibility, and delegation | secure required approvals | document agreed terms for implementation
Out of scope: Creating rebate structures, treatments, eligibility rules, payment terms, customer classifications, or price effects outside the approved architecture | contract execution (Manage Contract & Order; Establish Commercial Terms & Contracts) | legal determinations (Legal/Compliance) | settlement"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved architecture and pricing corridors | customer performance data | delegations of authority | Legal/Compliance guidance"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Approved, documented rebate terms ready for implementation"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Approvable rebate terms reached inside the governed architecture and delegation."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-FTC-RP-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Pricing Strategy Management | precedes: Implement Rebate"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Review negotiation-constraint list and no-new-structure rule adopted verbatim; refined Robinson-Patman statement per review; contract formalization routed to contract management."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-4-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11136" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11136"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-4 ;
    skos:definition "Set up an agreed rebate arrangement in the systems and procedures that administer it — configuring terms, measurement and accrual rules, and validation controls — so earned rebates accrue and calculate accurately from the arrangement's effective date."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.4.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Implement Rebate"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Implements agreed terms exactly as approved; discrepancies discovered during setup route back through Negotiate Rebate and its approvals rather than being resolved by configuration. Where rebate conditions reside in governed pricing or discount master data, this process submits validated changes through Maintain Price & Discount Master Data and its controls — it never writes directly into governed master data. Accrual setup coordinates with Finance (Accruals Analysis And Calculation).
In scope: Configure agreed terms in rebate-administration systems | set up measurement and accrual rules | validate and test calculation logic | effective-dating | implementation records
Out of scope: Changing agreed terms (Negotiate Rebate) | master-data writes outside governed submission (CM-1-3-7-1) | accrual accounting policy (Finance)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved documented terms (Negotiate Rebate) | Price & Discount Master Data submission controls (CM-1-3-7-1) | accrual rules from Finance"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Operational rebate arrangement accruing correctly from its effective date"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Agreed rebate arrangements operational, accurate, and effective on time."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Negotiate Rebate | precedes: Settle Rebate | uses-input: Maintain Price & Discount Master Data"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Master-data submission rule carried from the b28 Implement sales price correction: submits to, never deploys into, governed master data."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-4-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "18641" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 18641"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-4 ;
    skos:definition "Calculate, validate, and settle earned rebates at agreed settlement points: verify performance against the arrangement's conditions, compute amounts due, resolve calculation discrepancies with the customer or partner, and prepare the appropriate authorized settlement request in the agreed form."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.4.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settle Rebate"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Settlement distinguishes rebate calculation, rebate validation, accrual coordination, credit memo request, cash payment request, and offset/netting request from accounting and funds movement, which remain with Finance/Treasury. Prepare the appropriate authorized credit, offset, accrual-release, or payment request through the accountable accounting/treasury processes. Credit-memo settlements execute through Manage Customer Invoicing and Billing controls (Create Credit Memo); accrual releases coordinate with Accruals Analysis And Calculation. Discrepancies over earned amounts under an arrangement are resolved here; disputes and collection of unrelated receivables remain with Manage Collection and Disputes.
In scope: Settlement calculations against verified performance | customer/partner validation and discrepancy resolution on earned amounts | accrual coordination | preparation of authorized credit, offset, accrual-release, or payment requests | settlement records
Out of scope: Funds movement and payment execution (Finance/Treasury) | invoice and credit-memo issuance outside CM-1-3-8-1 controls | collection strategy (CM-1-3-8-3) | write-offs and accounting treatment (Finance)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Operational arrangement terms | verified sales performance | accrual balances (Finance) | delegations of authority"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Settled rebates with validated calculations and a complete settlement record"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Earned rebates settled accurately, on time, through authorized instruments."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Implement Rebate | uses-input: Manage Cash Application, A/R & Revenue Accounting | enables: Accruals Analysis And Calculation"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Review settlement-request wording adopted verbatim (authorized credit, offset, accrual-release, or payment request through accountable accounting/treasury processes); b14 payment-authority rule; settlement forms enumerated per review."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-5-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-5 ;
    skos:definition "Execute released advertising: traffic approved creative to booked media, coordinate and monitor agency and vendor execution within approved media commitments, monitor placement quality and delivery, and capture spend and delivery actuals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.5.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Advertising"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Substantiation before dissemination, inherited from communications planning: only creative that has completed the claim-substantiation and approval controls in planning is trafficked; execution verifies release status and never approves claims. Media placement may be executed by agencies or procurement-managed vendors; this process coordinates and monitors that execution under approved commitments entered through procurement and contract controls.
In scope: Creative trafficking of released assets | coordination and monitoring of agency/vendor execution | placement and delivery monitoring | makegoods and delivery exception handling within commitments | spend and delivery actuals
Out of scope: Creative development or approval | claim substantiation (planning; SRC-FTC-ADM-001 controls) | media strategy and budget approval | new media commitments beyond approvals (Procurement)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Released creative and media plans | approved media commitments | brand standards"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Delivered advertising with verified placement, delivery, and spend actuals"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Released advertising delivered accurately across booked media."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-FTC-ADM-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Marketing Communications Planning | enables: Campaign ROI Analysis"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Coordinate-and-monitor reading per review — agency/vendor placement responsibility preserved; substantiation-before-dissemination inherited from b24 advertising controls."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-5-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16408" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16408"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Promotions and Events Execution"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-5 ;
    skos:definition "Execute released promotions and events: coordinate site and channel readiness, deploy promotional materials, deliver events, coordinate participating partners, and close out each promotion or event with captured actuals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.5.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Promotions/Events Execution"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """The promotion-readiness conditional framework applies. Site, supply, inventory, price, promotional mechanics, legal terms, partner, EHS, POS, staffing, technology, and measurement readiness are confirmed by their accountable owners before activation. Marketing Plan Execution coordinates these confirmations; it does not certify the conditions itself. Price-affecting promotional mechanics are implemented only through Sales Pricing Management and governed Price & Discount Master Data submission; execution never configures prices or price displays directly (b24 POS price-display exclusion; b28 no-self-exception rule). Partner participation operates under executed agreements only.
In scope: Coordinate readiness confirmation with accountable owners | promotional material deployment | event delivery and staffing coordination | partner execution coordination under executed agreements | teardown, closeout, and actuals capture
Out of scope: Promotion design and approval (planning) | certifying readiness conditions (their accountable owners) | price setting or POS price configuration (CM-1-3-6-3; site operations) | legal terms drafting (Legal) | inventory ownership"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Released promotion plans and calendars | readiness confirmations from accountable owners | promotional materials | executed partner agreements"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Executed promotions and events with closeout records and actuals"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Released promotions and events delivered completely and compliantly."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Marketing Communications Planning | informed-by: Implement sales price"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Review readiness-owner list adopted verbatim; coordinates-but-does-not-certify qualifier per review; b27 promotion-readiness framework and b28 pricing rules inherited. Normalized label 'Manage Promotions and Events Execution' recorded as alt label and queued for the naming pass."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-5-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16851" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16851"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-5 ;
    skos:definition "Execute released direct-marketing activity: build audience executions from permissioned contactability data, dispatch messages through approved channels, honor preferences and opt-outs, and capture responses for measurement."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.5.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Direct Marketing"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Contactability rule: this process consumes permissioned contactability data under the enterprise consent framework and never creates or presumes consent. Opt-out/suppression updates must be processed without undue delay through the accountable consent/preference system, and execution must stop or suppress future contact as required by the applicable channel, law, and system control. Personal data handled here is governed under the enterprise personal-data governance framework, with collection, use, retention, and disclosure limited to what the framework and applicable law permit. Response data flows to Marketing Performance Analysis and Customer Program Analysis for evaluation.
In scope: Audience execution from permissioned data | message dispatch through approved channels | preference and opt-out honoring with suppression processed without undue delay through the accountable consent/preference system | response capture and handoff to measurement
Out of scope: Consent creation or consent-framework ownership | creative and offer approval (planning) | audience strategy | effectiveness evaluation (CM-1-3-5-5)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Released campaigns | permissioned contactability data | suppression lists | approved channel capabilities"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Dispatched direct-marketing activity with response and suppression records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Released direct marketing executed lawfully to permissioned audiences."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-FTC-ADM-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Marketing Communications Planning | enables: Customer Program Analysis"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Review suppression wording adopted verbatim — 'without undue delay through the accountable consent/preference system' replaces 'immediately' to avoid an unvalidated SLA promise; b24 contactability rule and b21 personal-data governance statement apply."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-6-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "18641" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 18641"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Set Up Prospect"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-6 ;
    skos:definition "Coordinate prospective program-member/cardholder onboarding by capturing applications, validating completeness, and initiating required Credit, KYC, Compliance, Master Data, contract, and program-eligibility controls. Request establishment of governed customer, member, and card-account records through their owning master-data controls, and confirm activation readiness."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.6.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Setup Prospect"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Onboarding coordination model (b28 precedent): setup coordinates commercial qualification and activation readiness through the owning controls — credit through Establish Credit Limit & Risk Code, identity through KYC Process, customer records through Maintain Customer Master Data — and each control retains its owner. Setup never grants credit, bypasses KYC, or writes directly into governed master data: it requests governed record creation and consumes the outcomes. Personal data handled here is governed under the enterprise personal-data governance framework, with collection, use, retention, and disclosure limited to what the framework and applicable law permit.
In scope: Application capture and completeness validation | initiation of Credit, KYC, Compliance, Master Data, contract, and program-eligibility controls | requests for governed record creation | activation readiness confirmation
Out of scope: Credit decisions and limit setting (CM-1-3-7-4) | identity/KYC determinations (their owners) | master-data record ownership and writes outside governed submission (CM-1-3-7-1) | marketing acquisition campaigns"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Applications | credit and KYC outcomes (CM-1-3-7-4) | customer master-data controls (CM-1-3-7-1) | program terms"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Activation-ready member/cardholder records established through governed controls"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Qualified applicants converted into activation-ready members/cardholders through the owning controls."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "requires: KYC Process | requires: Establish Credit Limit & Risk Code | uses-input: Maintain Customer Master Data"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Review onboarding wording adopted verbatim; records are requested through governed controls, never owned here. Normalized label 'Set Up Prospect' recorded as alt label and queued for the naming pass."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-6-10 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10954" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10954"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-6 ;
    skos:definition "Handle cards securely through issuance: coordinate personalization and fulfillment, hold cards in secure custody, deliver cards to customers, and manage return, destruction, and exceptions. Coordinate and evidence quality acceptance, secure custody, delivery, return, destruction, and exception handling according to Security-defined and vendor/scheme requirements."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.6.10" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Physical Card Handling"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Chain-of-custody and secure-handling controls are defined by Security and applied without exception. Quality checks may be performed by the card manufacturer or personalization vendor; this process coordinates and evidences quality acceptance rather than necessarily performing inspection itself. Card-data security follows the fact-dependent PCI DSS scope statement and Security-defined controls stated at Loyalty And Cards Management - Marketing (CM-1-3-6-6). Delivery exceptions route back through card administration.
In scope: Personalization and fulfillment coordination | quality acceptance coordination and evidence | secure storage and chain of custody | distribution and delivery | return, destruction, and exception handling with records
Out of scope: Security control definition (Security) | card production runs (CM-1-3-6-6-13) | design compliance (CM-1-3-6-6-8) | vendor contracting (Procurement)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Produced cards (CM-1-3-6-6-13) | Security handling controls | vendor/scheme requirements | delivery instructions"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Delivered (or destroyed) cards with complete custody and acceptance records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every card moved from production to customer — or to destruction — under unbroken, evidenced secure custody."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-PCI-DSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Manage Card Production & Monthly Reissue | governed-by: Ensure Card Design Compliance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Review coordinate-and-evidence wording adopted verbatim — vendor quality inspection responsibility preserved."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-6-11 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10768" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10768"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-6 ;
    skos:definition "Detect and respond to suspected fraudulent card activity at program level: monitor transaction alerts, investigate suspected misuse within program authority, block cards and accounts, initiate dispute and chargeback workflows where applicable, and escalate per the enterprise fraud framework."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.6.11" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Fraudulent Transactions"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Escalation boundary: enterprise fraud investigation, forensics, and law-enforcement engagement are owned by Security and Legal; this process's authority is program-level detection, immediate servicing response — block, hold, customer contact — and escalation with complete evidence. It makes no legal determinations. Chargeback boundary: Initiates and coordinates card-program disputes, fraud claims, and chargeback requests through the applicable acquirer, issuer, processor, O2C, or legal processes; it does not determine scheme adjudication or execute financial recovery. Customer credits follow the billing-coordination and reimbursement controls.
In scope: Fraud alert monitoring and triage | program-level investigation and customer verification | immediate card/account response | dispute, fraud-claim, and chargeback initiation and coordination through the applicable acquirer, issuer, processor, O2C, or legal processes | escalation to Security/Legal with evidence | fraud-pattern feedback to transaction analysis
Out of scope: Enterprise investigation and forensics (Security) | legal determinations and law-enforcement engagement (Legal) | scheme adjudication and financial recovery execution (acquirer/issuer/processor; O2C; Finance) | write-offs (Finance)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Transaction alerts and anomaly reports (CM-1-3-6-6-7) | Security fraud framework | customer verification data"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Contained fraud incidents with servicing actions, initiated dispute/chargeback workflows, and escalation records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Fraud losses stopped fast at program level with clean escalation and initiated recovery workflows."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-PCI-DSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Perform Transaction Analysis Reporting & Confirmation | uses-input: Manage Credit Card Transactions"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Review chargeback-boundary statement adopted verbatim; program-servicing-versus-Security/Legal escalation split maintained."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-6-12 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10003" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10003"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Additional Card Services"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-6 ;
    skos:definition "Manage supplementary services offered through the card program, such as on-road services (breakdown assistance, tolls, parking) and partner services: administer service enrollments, coordinate service-partner delivery, and handle service transactions within the program."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.6.12" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Additional Card Services (On Road Services)"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Service-partner arrangements are contracted through the contract-management controls and create no obligations beyond executed agreements (b26 rule). Service transactions bill through the same program billing-coordination boundary as card transactions. Service scope offered to customers follows approved program terms; new service types are approved through program governance before offering.
In scope: Service enrollment administration | partner delivery coordination under executed agreements | service transaction capture and routing to billing coordination | service-level issue resolution at program level
Out of scope: Partner contracting terms (contract management; Legal) | invoicing execution (CM-1-3-8-1) | new service approval (program governance) | the partner's own service delivery"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved program service terms | executed partner agreements | enrollments | service transaction data"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Administered service enrollments and transactions delivered per agreement"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Reliable supplementary services adding card-program value under executed agreements."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage Contract & Order | enables: Manage Invoicing"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). b26 no-implied-obligations rule applied to service partners. Normalized label 'Manage Additional Card Services' recorded as alt label and queued — parenthetical examples belong in the definition."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-6-13 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10224" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10224"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-6 ;
    skos:definition "Manage card production runs and periodic reissue cycles: schedule production and personalization with vendors, manage the monthly reissue of expiring cards, and hand produced cards to physical handling for distribution."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.6.13" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Card Production & Monthly Reissue"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Production and personalization vendors operate under procurement and contract controls and under the applicable Security-defined and scheme secure-production requirements. Card-data security follows the fact-dependent PCI DSS scope statement and Security-defined controls stated at Loyalty And Cards Management - Marketing (CM-1-3-6-6). Production releases only designs verified by Ensure Card Design Compliance. Reissue scheduling coordinates with card-media inventory monitoring so cycles are supplied.
In scope: Production and personalization scheduling | monthly reissue cycle management | vendor run coordination within contracts | handoff to physical handling | production records
Out of scope: Vendor contracting (Procurement) | design compliance (CM-1-3-6-6-8) | physical distribution and custody (CM-1-3-6-6-10) | card-media purchasing"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Compliance-verified card designs (CM-1-3-6-6-8) | card orders and expiry schedules | card media (CM-1-3-6-6-9) | vendor contracts"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Produced and reissued cards handed to secure physical handling"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Cards produced and reissued on schedule from compliant designs and supplied media."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Ensure Card Design Compliance | uses-input: Analyze Consumption Planning & Monitor Stock | precedes: Perform Physical Card Handling"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Secure-production requirements per Security and applicable schemes; compliant-design gate from CM-1-3-6-6-8."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-6-14 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20933" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20933"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-6 ;
    skos:definition "Manage acceptance-side payment-card transaction operations where assigned: monitor approved processor/acquirer transaction flows, reconcile transaction submissions and settlement files, prepare exceptions, and coordinate resolution with Treasury, processors, O2C, and Security."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.6.14" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Credit Card Transactions"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Coordinate, monitor, and reconcile acceptance-side transaction processing, including submission or transmission through approved processors/acquirers where that activity is assigned to the program. Submission to acquirers may be performed automatically by processors, gateways, POS systems, or merchant-services platforms; this process coordinates, monitors, and reconciles rather than assuming it performs acquiring, transmission, or settlement in every architecture. Acquirer, processor, issuer, gateway, and banking relationships are owned by Finance/Treasury; funds movement is never performed here — reconciliation and exception preparation only, coordinating with Perform Reconciliations & Settlements. Card-data security follows the fact-dependent PCI DSS scope statement and Security-defined controls stated at Loyalty And Cards Management - Marketing (CM-1-3-6-6).
In scope: Monitoring of approved processor/acquirer transaction flows | reconciliation of transaction submissions and settlement files | exception identification, preparation, and routing | coordination of resolution with Treasury, processors, O2C, and Security | processing records
Out of scope: Acquirer/processor/issuer/gateway/banking relationships (Treasury) | funds movement and settlement execution | interchange and scheme fee terms (Treasury; networks) | cash application (CM-1-3-8-2)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Processor/acquirer transaction and settlement files | approved processing arrangements (Treasury) | Security-defined controls"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Reconciled daily transaction cycles with prepared, routed exceptions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Card acceptance operations monitored and reconciled accurately every day."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-PCI-DSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Perform Reconciliations & Settlements | enables: Perform Transaction Analysis Reporting & Confirmation"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Review payment-architecture wording adopted verbatim — 'where assigned' coordination role covers both centralized and outsourced processing models; second use of the daily horizon (after b28 Sales Pricing) for daily submission/settlement cycles."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-6-15 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16405" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16405"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-6 ;
    skos:definition "Operate the company's brand loyalty programs: administer member accounts and tiers, operate earn and redemption mechanics according to approved program rules, manage rewards fulfillment and partner benefits, and service member inquiries at program level."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.6.15" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Brand Loyalty Programs"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Operation-versus-analysis boundary: program performance evaluation and member analytics are performed by Customer Program Analysis under the marketing analysis durable rule — this process operates and records; it does not evaluate effectiveness or redesign the program, and rule changes are approved through program governance before implementation. The program maintains operational earn, burn, expiration, adjustment, partner-benefit, and fulfillment records. Finance determines loyalty liability accounting, accrual method, breakage assumptions, financial reporting, and impairment/valuation treatment. Personal data handled here is governed under the enterprise personal-data governance framework, with collection, use, retention, and disclosure limited to what the framework and applicable law permit. Program communications consume permissioned contactability data and never create consent.
In scope: Member account and tier administration | earn/redemption operation per approved rules | rewards fulfillment and partner benefit coordination under executed agreements | member servicing at program level | operational earn, burn, expiration, adjustment, partner-benefit, and fulfillment records for Finance and analysis
Out of scope: Program design and rule approval (governance/planning) | performance evaluation (CM-1-3-5-5-3) | loyalty liability accounting, accrual method, breakage assumptions, financial reporting, and impairment/valuation treatment (Finance) | consent creation | partner contracting terms"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved program rules | member activity data | permissioned contactability data | executed partner agreements"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Operating loyalty programs with complete member, earn/redemption, and fulfillment records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Loyalty programs run faithfully to approved rules with governed member data and complete records."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-PCI-DSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Customer Program Analysis | uses-input: Manage Direct Marketing"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Review loyalty-liability distinction adopted verbatim (operational records here; accounting determinations with Finance); b21 privacy statement and b24 contactability consumption apply."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-6-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Card Program Billing Coordination"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-6 ;
    skos:definition "Coordinate card-program billing readiness and program-specific statement requirements; provide validated transaction, account, and presentation data to enterprise invoicing. Manage program-specific presentation such as consolidated statements and itemization, and resolve program-level invoice inquiries."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.6.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Invoicing"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Boundary statement: invoice creation, taxability determination, distribution, corrections, reversals, and credit memos execute under Manage Customer Invoicing and Billing (CM-1-3-8-1) and its controls; this process supplies validated transaction, account, and presentation data and program statement requirements and never issues, corrects, or reverses invoices outside those controls. Receivables, cash application, collections, and payment execution remain with their O2C, Finance, and Treasury owners. Transaction completeness for each cycle is confirmed by Perform Transaction Analysis Reporting & Confirmation.
In scope: Billing-cycle readiness coordination | validated transaction, account, and presentation data to enterprise invoicing | program statement and itemization requirements | program-level inquiry resolution and routing
Out of scope: Invoice creation, distribution, correction, reversal, credit memos (CM-1-3-8-1) | taxability (its owners) | cash application and A/R (CM-1-3-8-2) | collections and disputes (CM-1-3-8-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Confirmed transaction data (CM-1-3-6-6-7) | program billing terms | enterprise invoicing calendar (CM-1-3-8-1)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Validated billing-cycle data and statement requirements delivered to enterprise invoicing"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Accurate, complete card-program billing delivered through enterprise invoicing controls."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Perform Transaction Analysis Reporting & Confirmation | enables: Manage Customer Invoicing and Billing"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Review billing-coordination wording adopted verbatim; preferred label 'Manage Card Program Billing Coordination' recorded as alt label and queued for the naming pass — the locked name 'Manage Invoicing' understates the coordination-only role and collides semantically with CM-1-3-8-1."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-6-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-6 ;
    skos:definition "Administer reimbursements owed under the card program — such as to accepting sites and partners for card transactions or to customers for program credits — validating claims against program terms. Prepare, validate, and submit authorized reimbursement requests or settlement instructions; track status and reconcile program records."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.6.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Reimbursement"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Payment-authority rule: this process prepares, validates, and submits authorized instructions; it never moves funds, which remains with Finance/Treasury execution. Prepare, validate, and submit authorized reimbursement requests or settlement instructions; track status and reconcile program records. Customer credits that take invoice form execute through Manage Customer Invoicing and Billing controls. Claim disputes are resolved at program level against program terms; unresolved disputes escalate per the applicable agreements.
In scope: Claim receipt and validation against program terms | reimbursement calculation | preparation, validation, and submission of authorized reimbursement requests or settlement instructions | status tracking and reconciliation of program records
Out of scope: Funds movement and payment execution (Finance/Treasury) | invoice and credit-memo issuance outside CM-1-3-8-1 controls | program term setting | partner agreement terms (contract management)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Claims | program terms and executed agreements | confirmed transaction data (CM-1-3-6-6-7) | delegations of authority"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Validated, submitted reimbursement requests with tracked status and reconciled program records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Valid program claims reimbursed accurately through authorized instruments."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Perform Transaction Analysis Reporting & Confirmation | follows: Manage Credit Card Transactions"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Review reimbursement wording adopted verbatim — prepares/validates/submits requests and reconciles records; the program never 'settles' or 'pays'; b14 payment-authority rule."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-6-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11074" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11074"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Card Administration"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-6 ;
    skos:definition "Administer card accounts day to day: process new-card orders, changes to card data such as vehicles, drivers, and purchase restrictions, renewals and replacements, and activations and blocks on customer instruction."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.6.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Cards Administration (Order New Cards, Change Card Data)"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Changes to card, driver, vehicle, contact, product restriction, and delivery data require authenticated instructions, authorization checks, audit logging, effective-dating, and applicable master-data synchronization. Changes to data held in governed master data are submitted through the owning master-data controls. Card-data security follows the fact-dependent PCI DSS scope statement and Security-defined controls stated at Loyalty And Cards Management - Marketing (CM-1-3-6-6).
In scope: New-card order processing | card data changes on authenticated, authorized instruction with audit logging and effective-dating | renewals, replacements, activations, blocks | master-data synchronization through governed submission | administration records
Out of scope: Credit and limit decisions (CM-1-3-7-4; Handle Card Limits within envelope) | physical production and logistics (CM-1-3-6-6-10/-13) | master-data writes outside governed submission | security control definition (Security)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Authenticated customer instructions | program rules | master-data controls (CM-1-3-7-1)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Accurately administered card accounts and orders with complete audit records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Card accounts kept accurate and serviced on authenticated customer instruction."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-PCI-DSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "triggers: Manage Card Production & Monthly Reissue | uses-input: Maintain Customer Master Data"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Review authentication/authorization/audit rule adopted verbatim. Normalized label 'Manage Card Administration' recorded as alt label and queued — parenthetical examples belong in the definition, not the label."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-6-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20930" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20930"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-6 ;
    skos:definition "Administer card-level spending and product controls within a customer's approved credit envelope: set and adjust card, driver, or vehicle-level limits and purchase restrictions at customer request or per program rules."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.6.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Handle Card Limits"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Credit boundary (b11 precedent): customer-level credit limits and risk codes are established by Establish Credit Limit & Risk Code and monitored by Monitor Credit Against Limit under Manage Credit & Risk. Card, driver, vehicle, product, transaction, merchant-category, geographic, time-of-day, and velocity restrictions may be administered here only within the approved customer credit envelope and program rules. Requests that would exceed the approved envelope route to Credit for decision; this process implements the outcome.
In scope: Card, driver, vehicle, product, transaction, merchant-category, geographic, time-of-day, and velocity restrictions within the approved envelope and program rules | limit-change processing on authenticated instruction | routing envelope-exceeding requests to Credit
Out of scope: Customer credit limits and risk codes (CM-1-3-7-4) | credit monitoring | collections responses (CM-1-3-8-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved credit envelope and risk codes (CM-1-3-7-4) | authenticated customer requests | program rules"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Card-level limits and restrictions consistent with the approved credit envelope"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Granular card-level control for customers without breaching credit governance."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "constrained-by: Establish Credit Limit & Risk Code | informed-by: Monitor Credit Against Limit"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Review restriction-granularity sentence adopted verbatim; within-envelope rule — card-level limits never increase the credit envelope."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-6-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10761" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10761"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Card Delinquency and Collections Referral"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-6 ;
    skos:definition "Manage the card program's exposure to non-paying accounts at program level: monitor delinquency signals on card accounts, apply program-level servicing responses such as card blocking, transaction holds, and limit suspension, refer delinquent accounts to collections, and supply program data for bad-debt assessment."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.6.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Bad Debt"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Boundary statement: bad-debt allowance, impairment, write-off decisions, and their financial treatment remain with Finance (Bad Debt Allowance); collection strategy, disputed debt, and recovery operations remain with Manage Collection and Disputes. This process's authority is program-level servicing action — block, suspend, hold, notify, refer, and reinstate after resolution — and data supply; it never decides write-offs, sets allowances, or conducts collections.
In scope: Delinquency-signal monitoring on card accounts | program-level blocks, holds, and suspensions per policy | referral to collections with complete account data | program data for bad-debt assessment | reinstatement servicing after resolution
Out of scope: Bad-debt allowance, impairment, write-offs, and financial treatment (Finance; CM-1-3-8-3) | collection strategy and recovery operations (CM-1-3-8-3) | credit limit decisions (CM-1-3-7-4)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Delinquency and payment data | credit monitoring outputs (CM-1-3-7-4) | program policy"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Contained exposure with referred accounts and complete referral records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Program credit exposure contained through timely servicing action and clean referral."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Monitor Credit Against Limit | triggers: Collection Management | enables: Bad Debt Allowance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Finance valuation/write-off lock continued; review program-action list (block/suspend/hold/notify/refer/reinstate) adopted. Normalized label 'Manage Card Delinquency and Collections Referral' recorded as alt label and queued — the locked name 'Manage Bad Debt' misleadingly implies bad-debt accounting ownership."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-6-7 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11783" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11783"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Analyze and Confirm Card Transactions"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-6 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze, report, and confirm card transaction activity: validate captured transactions for completeness and accuracy against expected patterns and program terms, confirm transaction sets for billing cycles, and produce transaction reports for customers and internal stakeholders."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.6.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Transaction Analysis Reporting & Confirmation"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Measurement qualifier: metrics produced here are evidence-layer transaction measures; any nomination toward enterprise KPIs routes through the KPI Store per the enterprise measurement framework — this process approves and publishes no KPIs. Anomalies suggesting fraud route to Manage Fraudulent Transactions; confirmed transaction sets feed billing coordination and reimbursement.
In scope: Transaction validation and completeness confirmation | billing-cycle transaction confirmation | customer and internal transaction reporting | anomaly identification and routing
Out of scope: Fraud investigation (CM-1-3-6-6-11; Security) | invoicing execution (CM-1-3-8-1) | KPI approval or publication (enterprise measurement governance) | pricing analysis"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Captured transaction data | program terms | expected-pattern baselines"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Confirmed transaction sets and transaction reports"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Transaction completeness and accuracy assured as the evidence base for billing, reimbursement, and fraud response."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Manage Invoicing | enables: Manage Reimbursement | triggers: Manage Fraudulent Transactions"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). b21/b23 measurement-framework rule applied — evidence-layer metrics, candidate KPIs only via the KPI Store. Normalized label 'Analyze and Confirm Card Transactions' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-6-8 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11047" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11047"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-6 ;
    skos:definition "Ensure physical and digital card designs comply with applicable requirements: card scheme and network specifications where applicable, enterprise brand standards, and Security-defined technical and card-media specifications."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.6.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ensure Card Design Compliance"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """This process applies standards; it sets none. Brand standards are governed by Brand Standard Management (CM-1-3-6-7); card scheme specifications are external constraints from the applicable networks; security features and card-media requirements are defined by Security. Design compliance includes visual brand use, scheme/card-media requirements, and Security-defined technical requirements. It does not approve product claims, customer communications, price display, or physical production. Use of brand assets in card designs operates under the enterprise brand governance framework — rights are obtained through it, never granted by this process.
In scope: Design compliance review against scheme, brand, and Security-defined technical requirements | compliance sign-off before production release | specification-change monitoring and design updates
Out of scope: Brand standards setting (CM-1-3-6-7) | scheme rule-making (networks) | security specification setting (Security) | product claims, customer communications, and price display approval | physical production (CM-1-3-6-6-13)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Brand standards (CM-1-3-6-7) | scheme specifications | Security requirements | proposed designs"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Compliance-verified card designs released for production"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Only compliant card designs released into production."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-PCI-DSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Brand Standard Management | precedes: Manage Card Production & Monthly Reissue"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Review design-compliance distinction adopted verbatim; b26 obtains-never-grants brand-rights pattern applied."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-6-9 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20685" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20685"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Plan Card Stock Consumption and Monitor Inventory"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-6 ;
    skos:definition "Plan consumption of card issuance materials and monitor their inventory. Manage inventory of blank cards, secure card media, carriers, mailers, and issuance materials according to Security-defined inventory and custody controls. Keep production and issuance runs continuously supplied without excess or insecure holdings."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.6.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Analyze Consumption Planning & Monitor Stock"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """'Stock' here means card media and issuance materials — blank cards, secure card media, carriers, mailers — not petroleum inventory; secure-inventory terminology used to avoid collision with hydrocarbon stock concepts elsewhere in the taxonomy. Consumption forecasts are operating forecasts of material usage, not commitments; replenishment purchasing executes through procurement and its contracts. Card media are held under the Security-defined inventory and custody controls.
In scope: Consumption analysis and card-media forecasting | inventory monitoring and replenishment triggering through procurement | card-media reconciliation | Security-defined inventory and custody control compliance
Out of scope: Purchasing and vendor contracting (Procurement) | production scheduling (CM-1-3-6-6-13) | security requirement setting (Security) | petroleum/product inventory management"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Issuance and reissue volumes (CM-1-3-6-6-13) | production schedules | card-media inventory records | Security custody controls"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Card-media plans and replenishment triggers keeping issuance supplied"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Card issuance continuously supplied under secure custody, without excess or shortage."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Manage Card Production & Monthly Reissue | informed-by: Manage Cards Administration (Order New Cards, Change Card Data)"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Review secure-inventory terminology adopted verbatim; explicit disambiguation from petroleum 'stock'. Normalized label 'Plan Card Stock Consumption and Monitor Inventory' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-7-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10530" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10530"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Brand Standards and Inspections"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-7 ;
    skos:definition "Define, maintain, and publish the brand standards that govern brand presentation and customer experience at branded sites and channels, and verify conformance through inspection programs: plan and conduct inspections where authorized, document findings, agree and track corrective-action plans with the accountable relationship owners, and escalate unresolved or material issues."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.7.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Brand Standards and Perform Inspection"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Standards change through governed change control, with Legal/IP review where standards touch protected marks. Inspection access, scope, records access, site-entry rules, and corrective-action requirements must follow the applicable agreement, operating authority, security rules, and safety requirements. Inspections produce findings and corrective-action coordination; remedies under franchise, dealer, and license agreements route through the relationship processes. Fact-dependent statement: where a remedy would affect a franchise relationship governed by the Petroleum Marketing Practices Act, the determination is reserved to Legal. Inspection evidence feeds Report Franchise Performance. Licensed-brand providers' own inspections of their standards are the provider's right under the license agreements; this process coordinates and consumes their outcomes. Where standards conflict or appear ambiguous, the process escalates interpretation to the licensor and Legal; it does not certify provider-standard compliance or modify provider standards.
In scope: Standards definition, maintenance, publication, and change control | inspection planning and conduct where authorized | findings documentation | corrective-action plans agreed and tracked with relationship owners | escalation of unresolved or material issues | conformance reporting
Out of scope: Statutory or legal notices, material-breach determinations, termination or nonrenewal, financial penalties, and litigation/arbitration (relationship management; Legal) | overriding operating or EHS authority | provider standards certification | imaging execution (CM-1-3-6-7-2) | training delivery (CM-1-3-6-1-4)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Brand governance framework | license agreements and operating authorities | site/network data | prior findings"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Published standards and inspection findings with agreed, tracked corrective actions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Standards that are current, published, and verifiably met across the network."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-PMPA-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Report Franchise Performance | governed-by: Manage Licensing & IP"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 30 (2026-09-20). Review inspection-authority caveat and does-not-do list adopted verbatim; standards governance is continuous with periodic inspection cycles within it. Normalized label 'Manage Brand Standards and Inspections' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-7-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-7 ;
    skos:definition "Manage the brand imaging of sites and assets to approved brand standards: receive standards and network/site pipeline inputs, define and review imaging scope and specifications, coordinate business-case and supplier activity, verify approved work against brand standards, and record conformance and defects."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.7.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Brand Imaging"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Brand imaging plans may identify capital, maintenance, signage, reimage, equipment, digital-display, and site-work requirements. Investment authorities approve funding; Engineering/site operations/EHS execute physical work; Procurement contracts suppliers; Brand Standards verifies conformance. This process does not own physical work, capital authorization, permit applications, site shutdowns, safety planning, engineering design, or construction acceptance beyond brand-conformance verification. Trademark and trade-dress usage in imaging operates under the enterprise brand governance framework.
In scope: Imaging and reimaging program planning and coordination | imaging scope and specifications | business-case and supplier activity coordination within procurement contracts | brand-conformance verification of approved work at new, converted, and reimaged sites | conformance and defect records
Out of scope: Capital and funding approval (investment authorities, b24) | physical site works, permits, shutdowns, safety planning, engineering design, and construction acceptance (Engineering; site operations; EHS) | supplier contracting terms (Procurement) | standards definition (CM-1-3-6-7-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Brand standards (CM-1-3-6-7-1) | network plans and site pipeline | imaging specifications | procurement contracts"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Sites imaged and verified to brand standard with conformance and defect records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every branded site imaged and verified to brand standard, on program."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Manage Brand Standards and Perform Inspection | informed-by: Network Design"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 30 (2026-09-20). Review imaging lifecycle and boundary list adopted verbatim — coordinates and verifies brand conformance; investment, engineering, EHS, procurement, and physical work remain with their owners."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-8-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10194" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10194"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Conduct Commercial Audit to Validate Reported Sales"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6-8 ;
    skos:definition "Conduct agreement-based commercial reviews of sales reported by franchisees, dealers, licensees, marketers, and partners; examine supporting records within contractual audit rights; quantify discrepancies affecting contract-based fees, royalties, rebates, contributions, or commitments; and route findings for financial, relationship, legal, or corrective resolution."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.8.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Conduct Audit To Validate Reported Sales"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Audit rights and records access are exercised only as the executed agreements provide, within valid review periods. This is an agreement-rights commercial audit, not Internal Audit: it exercises specific rights under partner, franchise, and license agreements to validate the inputs to royalties, fees, rebates, and contract obligations, and routes discrepancies to Finance/O2C, relationship management, and Legal. Independent enterprise assurance under an audit charter, control-effectiveness assessment, and Audit Committee reporting remain with Internal Audit. Verify evidence of agreed corrective actions or corrected reporting, while Finance/O2C, Relationship Management, and Legal own financial adjustment, collection, remedy, and legal action. Sales and supporting records obtained during the review are commercially sensitive and must be accessed, retained, shared, and disposed of only in accordance with the agreement, confidentiality terms, legal holds, records-retention requirements, privacy obligations where applicable, and delegated audit protocols. Fact-dependent statement: where a remedy would affect a franchise relationship governed by the Petroleum Marketing Practices Act, the determination is reserved to Legal. Distinct from Report Franchise Performance, which reports operational performance: this process validates the accuracy of reported sales and reported obligation bases under the agreement's audit rights — a partner can perform well while reporting inaccurately, or report accurately while performing poorly.
In scope: Audit program planning within agreement rights and review periods | examination of reported sales against supporting records | discrepancy quantification for contract-based fees, royalties, rebates, contributions, and commitments | findings reports routed for financial, relationship, legal, or corrective resolution | verification of evidence of agreed corrective actions or corrected reporting
Out of scope: Financial adjustment, collection, remedy, and legal action (Finance/O2C; relationship management; Legal) | accounting determinations (CM-1-3-8-4) | Internal Audit and statutory audit | records access beyond agreement rights"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Executed agreements, audit rights, and review periods | reported sales data | supporting records per agreement access rights | royalty/fee bases (CM-1-3-8-4-7)"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Audit findings with quantified discrepancies routed for resolution"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Royalties, fees, rebates, and contributions computed from validated reported sales."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-PMPA-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Manage Collection of Royalty and Fees | informed-by: Report Franchise Performance | governed-by: Compliance Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 30 (2026-09-20). Review canonical commercial-audit definition, Internal Audit distinction, corrective-verification wording, and confidentiality rule adopted verbatim. Preferred label 'Conduct Commercial Audit to Validate Reported Sales' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10252" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10252"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Maintain Customer and Commercial Account Master Data"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-1 ;
    skos:definition "Maintain governed Party, Customer Account, account-role, hierarchy, address/location-reference, contact, commercial attribute, and relationship records as defined by the approved customer domain model. A customer master record may represent a party, account, role, relationship, or system-specific record according to the governed model; it must not collapse Sold-To, Bill-To, Payer, Ship-To, Loading-Authorized Party, dealer, distributor, or marketer meanings. Create, validate, update, deduplicate, and retire these records on authorized submissions, keeping them accurate, unique within the governed model, and consumable by sales, fulfillment, billing, credit, and service processes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Maintain Customer Master Data"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Who decides: commercial qualification, credit standing, and KYC outcomes are decided by their owning processes (prospect onboarding coordination, Manage Credit & Risk, KYC Process); this process records and maintains the governed result and never grants status. Record creation is requested through governed submission (the Setup Prospect rule, owner's side). Customer personal data is governed under the enterprise personal-data governance framework, with collection, use, retention, and disclosure limited to what the framework and applicable law permit. Contact details, communication preferences, suppression indicators, and consent/lawful-basis records may be linked to commercial master data where authorized, but the existence of an email, phone number, address, account relationship, or contactability attribute never creates marketing permission. The consent/preference framework remains separately governed.
In scope: Party, account, role, hierarchy, and relationship record creation on authorized request | validation, enrichment, deduplication within the governed customer domain model | change application with audit trails | quality monitoring and remediation | controlled retirement/closure recording per Perform Customer Closure & Reinstatement decisions
Out of scope: Credit, KYC, and qualification decisions (their owners) | consent creation or consent/preference framework ownership | customer relationship management | customer domain model definition (Data Governance) | privacy framework setting"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Authorized creation/change requests | approved customer domain model | KYC and credit outcomes | data quality standards | privacy framework"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Accurate, governed party/account/role records with complete audit trails"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Accurate, governed party and account records, distinct by role, consumable everywhere needed."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Setup Prospect | enables: Manage Customer Invoicing and Billing | informed-by: KYC Process"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 31 (2026-09-20). Review Party/Customer Account/role wording adopted verbatim — never collapses Sold-To, Bill-To, Payer, Ship-To, Loading-Authorized Party, dealer, distributor, or marketer meanings; strengthened consent separation adopted verbatim. Preferred label 'Maintain Customer and Commercial Account Master Data' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10252" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10252"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-1 ;
    skos:definition "Maintain governed price and discount records: validate and apply authorized price, discount, and rebate-condition submissions with correct effective-dating, maintain price structures and condition records, and assure the completeness, consistency, and auditability of the pricing data consumed by quoting, ordering, and billing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Maintain Price & Discount Master Data"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Receiving end of locked submission rules: Implement sales price and Implement Rebate submit validated, approved changes to this process — it validates authorization and content, applies with effective-dating, and returns confirmation; submitters never write directly, and this process never determines a price, discount, or rebate (Sales Pricing Management, pricing governance, and Rebate Management own those decisions under their approvals). Master-data validation must confirm source decision, approver, effective dates, eligibility scope, legal/commercial constraints, data completeness, and conflict with existing price/discount/rebate conditions before publication. Discrepancies between a submission and its approval route back to the submitting process — never resolved by master-data edit (the no-self-exception rule, owner's side).
In scope: Validation of source decision, approver, effective dates, eligibility scope, legal/commercial constraints, completeness, and condition conflicts before publication | effective-dated application | price structure and condition record maintenance | consistency and completeness assurance | audit trails and submission confirmations
Out of scope: Price, discount, and rebate determination (CM-1-3-6-3, CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-4) | direct writes by any process | price deployment to displays (b24 POS exclusion) | invoicing (CM-1-3-8-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Validated submissions (Implement sales price; Implement Rebate) | approval references and delegations | pricing structures"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed price and discount records with verified authorization lineage"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every price and condition consumed downstream is the approved one, effective-dated and provable."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Implement sales price | uses-input: Implement Rebate | enables: Manage Customer Invoicing and Billing"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 31 (2026-09-20). Owner's side of the b28/b29 submission rules; review validation-completeness sentence adopted verbatim."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10252" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10252"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Maintain Product and Service Master Data"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-1 ;
    skos:definition "Maintain governed product and service master records: create and update records, attributes, units of measure, classifications, and hierarchies on authorized submissions from the accountable product owners, keeping the catalog accurate and consumable by pricing, ordering, fulfillment, and billing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Maintain Product Master Data"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Product master data maintains the approved commercial/product catalog representation, including identifiers, product hierarchy, UoM, commercial attributes, tax/handling classifications, lifecycle status, and approved specification references. The authoritative technical specification, quality, regulatory, formulation, and manufacturing sources remain with their accountable owners. Who decides: product and service definitions, specifications, formulations, and lifecycle decisions (introduce, change, discontinue) belong to the accountable product management and innovation processes; this process records the decided catalog content with effective-dating and never defines, approves, or discontinues an offer.
In scope: Product and service record creation and maintenance on authorized submission | identifiers, hierarchy, UoM, commercial attributes, tax/handling classifications | effective-dated lifecycle status recording | approved specification references | quality monitoring and remediation | audit trails
Out of scope: Product/service definition, specification, and lifecycle decisions (product management/innovation) | authoritative technical specification, quality, regulatory, formulation, and manufacturing sources (their owners) | pricing (CM-1-3-7-1-2) | inventory (supply chain)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Authorized product/service submissions from accountable owners | classification standards | approved specification references"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed product and service master records consumed by commercial processes"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "An accurate governed catalog behind every quote, order, and invoice."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Capture & Validate Order | enables: Create & Distribute Bill"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 31 (2026-09-20). Review specification distinction adopted verbatim — catalog representation here, authoritative technical/quality/regulatory sources with their owners (material for fuels, additives, lubricants, and regulated products). Preferred label 'Maintain Product and Service Master Data' recorded as alt label and queued — scope includes services."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-1-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10929" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10929"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-1 ;
    skos:definition "Maintain governed tax master records as directed by the accountable tax owners, with effective-dating aligned to legal effectivity, so that taxability determination and tax calculation in transactions consume current, authorized tax data."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.1.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Maintain Tax Master Data"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Tax master data may include tax jurisdiction, tax code, product tax class, exemption treatment reference, rate version, effective date, filing/registration attributes where authorized, and certificate references. It does not determine legal taxability, interpret law, validate a customer's legal entitlement beyond Tax-owned controls, or make tax remittance/accounting decisions. Who decides: tax positions, taxability determinations, interpretations, and responses to law changes are made by the accountable Tax function and the Determine Taxability processes; this process maintains the records that encode those determinations. Statutory rate and rule changes are applied on the Tax function's direction; exemption certificates are recorded and tracked per Tax's validation.
In scope: Tax jurisdiction, code, product tax class, exemption treatment reference, rate version, and certificate reference maintenance on Tax direction | filing/registration attributes where authorized | effective-dating aligned to legal effectivity | certificate expiry tracking | audit trails | quality monitoring
Out of scope: Tax positions, determinations, interpretations, and legal-entitlement validation beyond Tax-owned controls (Tax function; Determine Taxability) | tax remittance and accounting decisions (CM-1-3-8-4; Finance) | tax policy"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Tax function directions | statutory changes as interpreted by Tax | exemption certificates"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed tax master records with legal-effectivity-aligned dating"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every transaction taxed from current, authorized tax records."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Determine Taxability | enables: Reconcile And Report Indirect Tax"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 31 (2026-09-20). Review tax-content sentence adopted verbatim; APQC 'Maintain tax master data' (score 1.0) supports the maintenance reading."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-1-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20950" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20950"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Maintain Commercial Workflow Configuration"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-1 ;
    skos:definition "Maintain the governed workflow configurations that control commercial transactions and master-data changes — approval chains, routing rules, thresholds, and escalation paths — keeping them aligned with approved delegations of authority and process designs, tested, versioned, and auditable."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.1.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Maintain Workflows"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Workflow configurations implement approved delegations of authority and process designs; they never create authority. A workflow that routes an approval differently from the approved delegation is a defect to correct, not a decision to make — changes to authority itself are approved through the delegation governance owners before any configuration change. The process owns business configuration requirements and validation of workflow behavior against approved authorities. IT owns platform administration, application security, code/configuration deployment controls, integration operation, monitoring, recovery, and technical change management.
In scope: Business configuration requirements and workflow-behavior validation against approved authorities | threshold, routing, and escalation rule administration | configuration testing and version control | periodic reconciliation of configurations against approved delegations | audit trails
Out of scope: Delegation of authority approval (DoA governance owners) | process design approval (accountable process owners) | platform administration, application security, deployment controls, integration operation, monitoring, recovery, and technical change management (IT / Process Excellence) | the approvals the workflows route (their accountable processes)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved delegations of authority | approved process designs | change requests from accountable owners"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Tested, versioned workflow configurations faithful to approved authority"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every approval routed exactly as the approved authority prescribes."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Manage Master Data (Customer, Price, Product, Tax, Location) | enables: Establish Commercial Terms & Contracts"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 31 (2026-09-20). Review technical-controls distinction adopted verbatim — business configuration here, platform controls with IT (low-code/ERP/CRM ambiguity resolved). Preferred label 'Maintain Commercial Workflow Configuration' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-1-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10771" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10771"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Maintain Commercial Policy Content and Parameters"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-1 ;
    skos:definition "Maintain commercial policy content and system-consumable policy parameters as governed reference content. Formal policy approval, legal review, corporate publication, retention schedule, and enterprise document-management controls remain with the accountable governance and document-management owners. Administer, publish, version, and distribute the commercial policy content and parameters that transactions and workflows reference — terms frameworks, condition catalogs, policy parameters — keeping the published version identical to the approved one."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.1.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Maintain Commercial Policies"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Who decides: policy approval authority remains with the accountable governance owners — commercial leadership, Finance, Legal, and the applicable delegations; this process administers and publishes approved policy content and never approves, amends, or waives a policy. Policy parameters consumed by systems are maintained on the owners' approved direction with effective-dating; waivers and exceptions are decisions of the policy owners under the delegations of authority, recorded — not granted — here. Formal enterprise policy documents remain under the accountable governance and document-management controls; this process holds the commercial reference content and system-consumable parameters, not enterprise document-management authority.
In scope: Commercial policy content administration, publication, versioning, distribution | system-consumable policy parameter maintenance on approved direction | effective-dating | retirement of superseded versions | audit trails
Out of scope: Policy approval, amendment, waiver, and exception decisions (governance owners; delegations of authority) | legal review (Legal) | corporate publication, retention schedules, and enterprise document-management controls (their owners) | the commercial decisions policies govern"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved policies and parameters from governance owners | delegations of authority | change directions"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Published, versioned commercial policy content and parameters identical to approved content"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The policy content everyone consumes is exactly the content that was approved."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Manage Master Data (Customer, Price, Product, Tax, Location) | enables: Establish Commercial Terms & Contracts"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 31 (2026-09-20). Review controlled-document distinction adopted verbatim — reference content and parameters here, enterprise policy-document authority with governance/document-management owners. Preferred label 'Maintain Commercial Policy Content and Parameters' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-2-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10290" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10290"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-2 ;
    skos:definition "Establish the durable commercial arrangements under which customers transact: negotiate and establish master agreements, terms frameworks, and customer-specific commercial terms within approved policies and delegations, coordinate legal review and execution, and have executed terms recorded into governed systems so downstream transactions apply them automatically."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Establish Commercial Terms & Contracts"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """The process coordinates commercial negotiation and approved customer-specific term setup. Legal drafts/reviews legal terms and manages signature formalities; Contract Management maintains legal contract records where applicable; Master Data/Policy content maintains system-consumable terms and conditions. Operates within the approved commercial policy content, pricing governance outputs, Credit-approved terms, and delegations of authority; terms outside approved frameworks require approval through the accountable governance owners before commitment. Deal-level contract and order handling within an established framework belongs to Manage Contract & Order (Sales Execution); rebate arrangements formalize here per the batch 29 routing. This capability establishes O2C commercial terms and customer-facing commercial frameworks. Enterprise/trading contract lifecycle administration, legal document control, counterparty-contract records, novation, termination, and master-agreement administration remain with Contract Management and Legal where applicable. Executed terms are submitted to governed master data and policy content under the Governed Submission and Stewardship Rule — never applied informally.
In scope: Master agreement and terms-framework negotiation within approved policies | customer-specific terms establishment | legal review and execution coordination | submission of executed terms to governed systems | coordinate commercial lifecycle changes, expiry/renewal review, and authorized amendments; Legal and Contract Management execute legal/document lifecycle actions
Out of scope: Policy approval (governance owners) | price determination (CM-1-3-6-3) | legal drafting, review standards, signature formalities, and legal contract records (Legal; Contract Management) | deal-level contracting and orders (CM-1-3-6-2-5) | credit terms decisions (CM-1-3-7-4)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved policy content and pricing outputs | credit decisions | delegations of authority | Legal and Contract Management support"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Executed commercial terms and frameworks recorded in governed systems"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Durable customer arrangements that downstream transactions apply without renegotiation."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Maintain Commercial Policies | enables: Manage Contract & Order | enables: Issue Quotes & Renewals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 32 (2026-09-20). Review framework-vs-deal split confirmed (Establish here; Manage Contract & Order transacts within it); Legal/Contract Management/Master Data role split adopted verbatim; commercial-vs-legal lifecycle wording adopted verbatim."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20098" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20098"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-2 ;
    skos:definition "Formally issue customer quotes and manage renewals through governed systems: produce quotes from approved prices, established terms, and the approved quote content requirements; manage validity periods, revisions, and versioning; and run contract-anchored renewal cycles so expiring arrangements are re-quoted and re-executed on time."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Issue Quotes & Renewals"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Quotes are issued with their approved legal status, validity period, conditions, disclaimers, and binding/non-binding treatment as determined by the applicable legal, contract, and commercial framework. A quote does not create a binding commitment unless the applicable approval, acceptance, and contract/order controls establish one. Quotes price exclusively from governed price and discount master data and established terms — never from ad-hoc values (no-self-exception). Quote composition for a specific opportunity is Develop Quote's (Sales Execution); this process issues the controlled document and owns its lifecycle evidence. Renewals follow the contract's renewal terms; changed terms route through Establish Commercial Terms & Contracts.
In scope: Quote production from governed prices and terms per approved content requirements | approved legal status, validity, conditions, and disclaimers applied per the legal/contract framework | revision and version management | renewal cycle management against contract terms | quote and renewal lifecycle records
Out of scope: Quote content composition in the sales motion (CM-1-3-6-2-4) | price determination (CM-1-3-6-3) | terms changes (CM-1-3-7-2-1) | contract execution and binding-status determination (Legal; delegations; contract/order controls) | order capture (CM-1-3-7-3-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Governed price/discount data | established terms | approved quote content requirements | contract renewal schedules"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Issued quotes and completed renewals with full version and status records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every issued quote and renewal is current, governed, and carries its approved legal status."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Develop Quote | uses-input: Maintain Price & Discount Master Data | enables: Capture & Validate Order"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 32 (2026-09-20). Review quote-status wording adopted verbatim, replacing the absolute non-binding phrasing — binding/non-binding treatment is determined by the applicable legal, contract, and commercial framework (b28 rule qualified, not weakened); develop-vs-issue split confirmed."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-2-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16501" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16501"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Develop and Monitor Commercial Revenue Plan"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-2 ;
    skos:definition "Develop the commercial revenue plan — the monetized operating outlook of the agreed sales plan by customer, channel, product, and period — and monitor actual revenue against it, analyzing deviations and routing findings to the accountable owners according to cause."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop & Monitor Revenue Plan"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """The plan uses approved sales plans, governed demand forecasts as inputs, commercial terms, price architecture, planned volume/mix, discount/rebate assumptions, and Finance-approved revenue-recognition assumptions where relevant. It does not establish the enterprise forecast, target/quota, statutory revenue forecast, financial budget, accounting revenue, or revenue-recognition policy. The forecast-versus-targets rule applies: a commercial revenue plan is neither a forecast (the unbiased expectation, owned by the forecasting processes), nor a target or quota (leadership's performance commitments), nor the financial budget (Finance's); it is the monetized operating plan used for monitoring and corrective conversation. Variance findings are routed to Sales Planning, Pricing, Offer, Channel, Supply, Finance, and other accountable owners according to cause; this process does not make price, target, forecast, supply, or accounting corrections. It is not a source of official financial reporting. Metrics produced are evidence-layer; candidate KPIs route via the KPI Store.
In scope: Commercial revenue plan development from approved sales plans, governed demand forecasts, terms, price architecture, volume/mix, and discount/rebate assumptions | periodic re-planning | actuals monitoring against plan | deviation analysis and cause-based routing to accountable owners
Out of scope: Enterprise demand forecasting (forecasting processes) | targets and quotas (leadership) | financial budgeting, accounting revenue, and revenue-recognition policy (Finance; CM-1-3-8-2-6) | price, target, forecast, supply, and accounting corrections (their owners)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved sales plans and governed demand forecasts | governed price, discount, and rebate data (CM-1-3-7-1-2) | Finance revenue actuals (Perform Revenue Accounting) | Finance-approved revenue-recognition assumptions where relevant"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Current commercial revenue plan with monitored deviations routed for action"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A monetized operating plan the business can steer commercial revenue against."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Manage Sales Pipeline | uses-input: Maintain Price & Discount Master Data | uses-input: Perform Revenue Accounting | enables: Analyze Customer Profitability"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 32 (2026-09-20). Review calculation statement and variance-routing sentence adopted verbatim under the b27 forecast/target/plan glossary. Review requested relations 'informs: Sales Planning' and 'informs: Pricing Strategy Management' — 'informs' is not in the relationship vocabulary (b10/b23 precedent): recorded here verbatim; nearest-vocabulary relations applied instead and receiving rows are already approved, so no informed-by could be added there. Preferred label 'Develop and Monitor Commercial Revenue Plan' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-2-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19521" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19521"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-2 ;
    skos:definition "Define the approved financing options, payment methods, payment-term menus, eligibility conditions, and customer-facing commercial rules that may be offered within Treasury-approved instruments, Credit-approved exposure rules, Tax/Legal requirements, and delegated authorities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.2.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Financing & Payment Methods"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Methods considered where applicable include invoice/bank transfer, ACH/direct debit, card, fleet/fuel-card billing, prepayment, deposit, credit terms, financing programs, electronic payment, cash where relevant, currency and cross-border constraints where applicable, and surcharges, discounts, fees, incentives, or payment-method conditions subject to law and policy. This process does not open bank accounts, select acquirers, manage merchant IDs, execute payment, extend credit, determine legal tax treatment, create financial products, or override card/network rules. A customer's eligibility for a method may depend on approved credit status, risk profile, contract, geography, tax, channel, and product rules; this process defines the menu and conditions, while Credit decides customer-specific exposure and terms. Defined methods take effect only as governed policy content and master data under the Governed Submission and Stewardship Rule; card acceptance operates under the Treasury/acquirer arrangements and card-program rules.
In scope: Definition of offered payment methods, financing options, payment-term menus, eligibility conditions, and customer-facing commercial rules | coordination of Treasury, Credit, Legal, and Tax review | submission of defined methods to governed policy content and master data | periodic review of method availability, performance, and cost
Out of scope: Bank accounts, acquirer selection, merchant IDs, and banking arrangements (Treasury) | customer-specific credit exposure and terms (CM-1-3-7-4) | payment execution and funds movement | legal tax treatment (Tax) | financial product creation | card/network rules (schemes; Treasury)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Treasury-approved instruments and arrangements | Credit-approved eligibility and exposure rules | Legal/Tax review requirements | commercial requirements"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed payment and financing method definitions consumed by contracting, quoting, and billing"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A governed, approved menu of payment and financing methods customers can actually be offered."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Maintain Credit Policies | enables: Establish Commercial Terms & Contracts | enables: Manage Customer Invoicing and Billing"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 32 (2026-09-20). Review commercial-offering definition, method inclusion list, exclusion list, and customer-specific credit distinction adopted verbatim; b14 payment-authority and b29 card-program boundaries inherited."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-2-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10176" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10176"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-2 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze the profitability of customers, accounts, channels, and segments: apply Finance-approved costing and allocation methods to revenue, cost-to-serve, terms, rebate, and credit data to produce profitability views and insights that inform pricing strategy, terms decisions, credit reviews, and account management."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.2.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Analyze Customer Profitability"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Profitability analysis must use Finance-approved revenue, cost, allocation, discount, rebate, credit-loss, service, channel, and cost-to-serve methods. Results should state attribution assumptions, allocation basis, exclusions, data-quality limitations, and confidence. They are commercial decision support, not financial reporting, transfer pricing, tax valuation, or customer-credit decisions. Customer profitability outputs are classified and distributed according to commercial-confidentiality, privacy, competition-law, and access-control requirements. Analysis may identify patterns but does not authorize differential treatment, pricing, credit, terms, or customer action. The analysis-and-inform durable rule applies: insights inform the accountable owners, who decide. Cost-to-serve calculations are consumed from Assess Cost-to-Serve Performance, not recreated here. Metrics produced are evidence-layer; candidate KPIs route only via the KPI Store. Customer personal data is governed under the enterprise personal-data governance framework.
In scope: Profitability analysis by customer, account, channel, segment using Finance-approved methods | consumption of approved cost-to-serve results | terms and rebate impact analysis | stated assumptions, allocation basis, exclusions, and confidence in all results | classified, access-controlled insight delivery to accountable owners
Out of scope: Pricing, terms, credit, and customer-action decisions (their owners) | costing method and allocation definition (Finance) | cost-to-serve calculation (CM-1-2-6-1-3) | financial reporting, transfer pricing, and tax valuation (Finance; Tax) | KPI approval/publication"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Finance revenue and margin actuals | Finance-approved costing and allocation methods | cost-to-serve results (CM-1-2-6-1-3) | terms, rebate, and credit data"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Classified profitability analyses and insights informing accountable owners"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Decision-makers see which customers and terms actually make money, and why — with stated assumptions."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Develop & Monitor Revenue Plan | uses-input: Assess Cost-to-Serve Performance | enables: Analyze Pricing Performance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 32 (2026-09-20). Review method/assumption statement and confidentiality/competition-law classification statement adopted verbatim; consumes Assess Cost-to-Serve Performance per review; b21 analysis rule and KPI Store boundary inherited."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-2-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20085" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20085"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-2 ;
    skos:definition "Operate the customer self-service portal as a governed commercial channel: manage portal content and functions, coordinate customer access and business entitlements through Security-defined services, and route every portal transaction and inquiry into its accountable process."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.2.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Customer Portal"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Channel-not-process rule: the portal presents governed data and routes transactions — prices from governed price master data, invoices and statements from Manage Customer Invoicing and Billing, orders into Capture & Validate Order, account changes through governed master-data submission — and is never an alternate path around any control. A portal transaction is valid only after the receiving process has completed its own validation, pricing, credit, compliance, terms, order, tax, and workflow controls. Portal submission is a channel entry point, not transaction approval. Coordinate customer-facing portal access and business entitlements through Security-defined identity, authentication, authorization, and access-control services. The portal process administers approved business-role/access requests and routes changes to the accountable identity, customer-master, credit, contract, and consent processes. Portal-submitted requests, complaints, disputes, or inquiries are captured and routed to the corresponding customer-request, billing, collections, credit, order, service, or technical-support process. The portal does not resolve them merely by displaying status. The portal may present service notices, transaction notices, and preference-management links under applicable rules; marketing communications require the separate consent/contactability framework. Platform administration, application security, deployment, integration operation, monitoring, and recovery are IT's (the batch 31 technical-controls distinction).
In scope: Portal content and function management | administration of approved business-role/access requests through Security-defined identity and access-control services | transaction and inquiry capture and routing to accountable processes | portal service quality monitoring | channel feedback to owning processes
Out of scope: The transactions themselves and their approval (owning processes) | identity, authentication, authorization, and access-control standards and services (Security) | platform administration and technical operations (IT) | consent creation (consent/contactability framework) | request and dispute resolution (CM-1-3-7-5; billing, collections, credit, order, service owners)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Governed master data and documents from owning processes | Security identity and access-control services | consent framework | IT platform services"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "An operating portal channel with all transactions and inquiries routed through governed processes"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Customers self-serve safely against governed data, with every action landing in the accountable process."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Maintain Price & Discount Master Data | enables: Capture & Validate Order | enables: Manage Customer Requests and Inquiries"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 32 (2026-09-20). Review identity/access wording, portal-transaction validity rule (the O2C no-self-exception equivalent), service-case routing boundary, and communications sentence adopted verbatim; b28 no-shadow-service-desk rule inherited."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-3-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10332" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10332"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-3 ;
    skos:definition "Capture customer orders from all approved channels — portal, EDI, phone, direct entry — and validate each against governed controls: resolve the correct party and account roles, confirm product, price, terms, and tax from governed master data, apply credit checks, verify loading and delivery authorizations, and accept, hold, or reject the order accordingly."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Capture & Validate Order"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Order validation applies the governed controls; it never waives, overrides, or substitutes any of them (no-self-exception at order level). The order process resolves applicable Party, Customer Account, account-role, location, contractual, and authorization relationships according to the approved customer-domain model. It does not collapse a location into a party role or treat one legal entity as automatically authorized for every commercial or physical action. Applies all applicable contract, product, channel, location, tax, credit, compliance, authorization, and operational eligibility controls for the transaction type. Detects duplicate, repeated, superseded, or conflicting submissions according to channel/order controls before accepting a valid order. A portal or EDI submission becomes a valid order only when this validation completes (the batch 32 portal-transaction rule's receiving end). Credit checks apply Credit's rules via Monitor Credit Against Limit; a credit hold is Credit's rule firing and its release is Credit's decision. Prices, terms, and tax resolve exclusively from governed master data; an order that cannot resolve them fails validation and routes for correction.
In scope: Multi-channel order capture with duplicate and conflict detection | party/account role resolution per the governed customer-domain model | product, price, terms, tax validation from governed data | credit check application and hold routing | loading/delivery and operational eligibility verification for the transaction type | accept/hold/reject with reasons and audit trail
Out of scope: Credit decisions and hold releases (CM-1-3-7-4) | price/terms/tax determination (their owners) | master data correction (governed submission) | allocation (CM-1-3-7-3-2) | customer-domain model definition (Data Governance)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Channel submissions | governed master data and customer-domain model | credit rules and statuses | authorization records"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Validated orders accepted into allocation, with holds and rejects routed with reasons"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Only valid, authorized, creditworthy orders enter the fulfillment pipeline."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage Customer Portal | uses-input: Maintain Customer Master Data | informed-by: Monitor Credit Against Limit | precedes: Allocate & Release Order"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 33 (2026-09-20). Review Party/Account/Role resolution sentence, applicable-eligibility-controls sentence, and duplicate-detection control adopted verbatim; b31 Party model and b32 portal rule receiving end."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-3-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20081" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20081"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-3 ;
    skos:definition "Allocate available product and supply capacity to validated orders and release allocated orders to the physical scheduling and fulfillment processes. Apply approved allocation rules, including contractual commitments, approved priority classes, fairness rules, regulatory/safety obligations, and authorized shortage/disruption procedures."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Allocate & Release Order"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Allocation rules are applied, not made: allocation policies, including shortage and disruption procedures, are approved by the accountable commercial and supply leadership under their own approvals, with Legal review where allocation touches contractual or regulatory obligations; this process applies the approved rules, documents allocation decisions, and escalates conflicts it cannot resolve within them. Allocation and release operate in daily/intraday operating cycles and may be re-run on supply, credit, order, contract, or disruption events. Release requires the applicable validation, allocation, credit, compliance, product-availability, authorization, and operational-readiness conditions to be satisfied. Where a condition is not met, the order remains held, partially released, substituted, or escalated under approved rules. Communicates hold, allocation, partial-release, substitution, or release status through the appropriate customer/service channel under approved communication rules. Release hands the order to the accountable scheduling/logistics processes — it does not schedule trucks, terminals, or deliveries.
In scope: Allocation of supply and capacity to validated orders per approved rules, including regulatory/safety obligations and authorized shortage/disruption procedures | release on satisfied conditions, with hold, partial release, substitution, or escalation under approved rules otherwise | allocation documentation and conflict escalation | reallocation on supply, credit, order, contract, or disruption events | status communication through approved channels
Out of scope: Allocation policy setting (commercial/supply leadership; Legal review) | physical scheduling and dispatch (logistics) | supply planning and inventory ownership (supply chain) | credit release decisions (CM-1-3-7-4)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Validated orders | supply and capacity availability from supply chain | approved allocation rules and shortage/disruption procedures | credit/compliance statuses"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Allocated, released orders in the physical fulfillment pipeline with documented allocation decisions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Available supply goes to the right orders, by the approved rules, provably."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Capture & Validate Order | precedes: Manage Fulfillment | governed-by: Manage Credit & Risk"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 33 (2026-09-20). Horizon set event-driven per review, with the daily/intraday cycle statement recorded verbatim; review allocation-rule wording (regulatory/safety obligations and authorized shortage/disruption procedures replace an absolute contract-first ordering), release-condition rule, and customer-communication sentence adopted verbatim."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-3-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10332" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10332"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Change or Cancel Order"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-3 ;
    skos:definition "Process authenticated order change and cancellation requests: validate the request against the order's status and the applicable cutoff rules, re-run affected validations (price, credit, allocation, tax) for changes, apply approved change and cancellation fees per policy, and propagate the outcome to allocation, fulfillment, billing, and the customer."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Change / Cancel Order"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Physical-status constraints govern feasibility: an order in loading or transit follows the physical processes' cutoff and return rules — this process never overrides a physical status. Changes re-validate through the same governed controls as capture (no-self-exception); repricing on change follows the applicable terms and governed price data. Fees apply per approved policy; waivers are decisions of the policy owners under the delegations of authority, recorded here. Change and cancellation handling preserves original order, revision, effective timestamp, approval, fee, allocation, credit, tax, and fulfillment evidence. It does not delete history. Cancellations after fulfillment route to returns authorization or billing correction, not deletion.
In scope: Authenticated change/cancel request processing | cutoff and status validation | re-validation of affected controls | approved fee application | propagation to allocation, fulfillment, billing, and the customer | complete preserved change history
Out of scope: Overriding physical status or logistics cutoffs (physical processes) | fee policy and waiver decisions (policy owners; DoA) | returns execution (CM-1-3-7-3-6) | invoicing corrections (CM-1-3-8-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Authenticated requests | order and physical status | cutoff rules | fee policies"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Correctly changed or cancelled orders with complete preserved history and propagation"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Order changes happen only when valid, priced right, fully propagated, and fully evidenced."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Capture & Validate Order | uses-input: Manage Fulfillment | enables: Manage Returns"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 33 (2026-09-20). Review history-preservation rule adopted verbatim. Normalized label 'Change or Cancel Order' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-3-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10332" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10332"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-3 ;
    skos:definition "Coordinate released orders through physical fulfillment to evidenced completion: hand orders to the accountable scheduling and logistics processes, monitor fulfillment progress, manage commercial exceptions — shortages, substitutions, partial deliveries — per approved rules, receive, link, validate for commercial completeness, and retain fulfillment evidence supplied by physical processes, and confirm completed fulfillments to billing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.3.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Fulfillment"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Coordination, not execution: scheduling, dispatch, terminal loading, transport, delivery, and custody transfer are executed by the accountable supply chain, terminal, and logistics processes; this process monitors and coordinates commercially and never directs equipment, drivers, or terminal operations. Custody-transfer evidence is produced under the applicable measurement, terminal, and logistics controls. API MPMS provides industry measurement context; the governing contract, operating procedure, applicable standard, and legal/regulatory regime determine the transaction-specific evidence and quantity basis. This process consumes that evidence as the fulfillment record and invoicing basis and never restates measured quantities. Commercial exceptions include substitution, partial fulfillment, shortage, non-delivery, backorder, hold, cancellation, return, quality exception, and delivery discrepancy. The process applies approved commercial rules and routes physical/quality/safety decisions to their owners. It coordinates customer-facing fulfillment-status communication through the appropriate commercial or service channels.
In scope: Fulfillment handoff and monitoring | commercial exception management per approved rules with routing of physical/quality/safety decisions to their owners | receipt, linkage, commercial-completeness validation, and retention of fulfillment evidence (BOL, delivery tickets, meter records) | completion confirmation to billing | coordination of customer-facing status communication through commercial/service channels
Out of scope: Physical execution and custody transfer (supply chain/terminal/logistics) | measurement and quantity determination (measurement processes under applicable standards) | invoicing (CM-1-3-8-1) | exception rules setting (their owners) | service resolution (CM-1-3-7-5)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Released orders | fulfillment status from physical processes | custody-transfer evidence | approved exception rules"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Confirmed fulfillments with validated, linked evidence handed to invoicing"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every released order reaches evidenced completion, with billing-grade evidence, or a managed exception."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-API-MPMS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Allocate & Release Order | enables: Create & Distribute Bill | enables: Track & Forecast Order"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 33 (2026-09-20). Review evidence wording adopted verbatim — received, linked, validated for commercial completeness, retained; never created or restated. API MPMS retained as contextual measurement/custody evidence only, with the review's qualifying note: the governing contract, operating procedure, applicable standard, and legal/regulatory regime determine transaction-specific evidence and quantity basis."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-3-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10332" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10332"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Track Orders and Forecast Order Book"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-3 ;
    skos:definition "Track the order book end to end — status, aging, exceptions, and completion across all open orders — and maintain the rolling short-horizon operational forecast of expected order volumes and timing that scheduling and supply processes plan against."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.3.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Track & Forecast Order"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """The operational order-book forecast is a short-horizon operational signal, not a supply commitment or forecast override; it does not automatically alter the enterprise demand forecast or approved allocation/replenishment decisions. Refreshes daily and on material order, supply, credit, allocation, fulfillment, or disruption events. It is none of the b27 glossary's other concepts: not the enterprise demand forecast (forecasting processes), not a target or quota (leadership), not the commercial revenue plan (CM-1-3-7-2-3). It may feed supply planning, terminal/rack planning, transportation scheduling, inventory awareness, and customer status communication. Order-status data is the governed record from the order processes; customer-facing presentation flows through the portal and service channels. Metrics produced are evidence-layer; candidate KPIs route via the KPI Store.
In scope: Order-book tracking, aging, and exception surfacing | daily operational order forecast, refreshed also on material order, supply, credit, allocation, fulfillment, or disruption events | status data provision to portal and service channels | evidence-layer order metrics
Out of scope: Enterprise demand forecasting, targets, and revenue planning (their owners) | supply commitments and allocation/replenishment decisions (their owners) | order status changes (owning order processes) | customer service resolution (CM-1-3-7-5) | KPI approval/publication"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Order and fulfillment status data | historical order patterns | supply calendars"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "A current order book and operational forecast consumed by scheduling and supply"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Operations always know what's in the pipeline and what's coming — without the signal becoming a commitment."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage Fulfillment | enables: Manage Customer Portal | informed-by: Capture & Validate Order"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 33 (2026-09-20). Review non-committal signal statement and event-refresh sentence adopted verbatim under the b27/b32 forecast/target/plan glossary. Normalized label 'Track Orders and Forecast Order Book' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-3-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Commercial Returns Authorization and Coordination"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-3 ;
    skos:definition "Manage commercial return authorization and coordination; it does not determine product disposition, direct hazardous-product transport, issue a credit memo, or authorize a refund. Validate return requests against policy and the original order, coordinate authorized physical return through the accountable logistics and quality processes, route product disposition to its owners, and hand financial credit to the billing controls."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.3.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Returns"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Downstream return taxonomy: bulk fuel/product returns are exceptional — off-spec, contamination, delivery error, tank-integrity concern, custody/quality dispute, or safety/EHS incident — with disposition (return to stock, downgrade, reprocess, dispose) a Quality/EHS/Operations determination; packaged lubricants/consumables return per commercial policy with warehouse/logistics and quality; damaged/defective and misdelivery/wrong-product recovery combine quality, logistics, and commercial coordination; containers, totes, cylinders, and similar assets follow the asset/container processes; cancellation before dispatch is Change / Cancel Order's; billing adjustment without physical return is the invoicing/credit-memo/dispute processes'. Return transport of hazardous product follows the applicable transport safety regulations and company procedures (FMCSA cited as transport-safety context only — PHMSA/HMR, state, maritime/rail, and site rules may also apply). Financial credit flows exclusively through Manage Customer Invoicing and Billing controls; the payment-authority rule applies to any refund instruction. Root causes feed quality and order-process improvement.
In scope: Return request validation against policy and original order | commercial return authorization | coordination of authorized physical return with logistics and quality owners | disposition routing to Quality/EHS/Operations owners | credit handoff to billing controls | return records and root-cause routing
Out of scope: Product disposition decisions (Quality/EHS/Operations) | physical return transport execution (logistics; applicable transport regulations) | container/asset recovery processes (their owners) | credit memo issuance (CM-1-3-8-1) | refund execution (Finance; payment-authority rule) | return policy setting (policy owners)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Return requests | original order and fulfillment evidence | return policies | quality/EHS determinations"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Completed authorized returns with correct disposition routing and credited outcomes through governed controls"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every return is authorized, safely handled by its owners, dispositioned correctly, and credited through governed controls."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-FMCSA-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Change / Cancel Order | enables: Create Credit Memo | uses-input: Manage Fulfillment"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 33 (2026-09-20). Review authorization-and-coordination wording and return-category taxonomy adopted verbatim; FMCSA retained as transport-safety context only per review. Preferred label 'Manage Commercial Returns Authorization and Coordination' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-4-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10789" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10789"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-4 ;
    skos:definition "Maintain the credit policies that govern customer credit: encode the Finance-approved risk appetite into credit standards, terms eligibility rules, security requirements, hold/release rules, and delegation thresholds; keep policies current with experience and conditions; and publish them through governed policy content for consistent application."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.4.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Maintain Credit Policies"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Policies encode an appetite decided above: enterprise credit risk appetite is Finance's, and policy changes are approved through the credit governance owners (credit committee, Finance, delegations of authority) — this process drafts, maintains, and operationalizes, and its changes take effect only after that approval. Credit Policy owns substantive credit criteria and business rules; Maintain Commercial Policies administers controlled publication and system-consumable policy content. Neither process changes Finance-approved risk appetite without the required governance approval. Fact-dependent legal obligations affecting credit policy (including U.S. ECOA/Regulation B where applicable) are determined by Legal/Compliance and incorporated as they direct.
In scope: Credit policy drafting and maintenance within approved appetite | credit standards, terms eligibility, security requirements, hold/release rules, delegation thresholds | policy performance review and revision proposals | publication through governed policy content | incorporation of Legal/Compliance-directed requirements
Out of scope: Risk appetite setting and policy approval (Finance; credit governance; DoA) | customer-level decisions (CM-1-3-7-4-2) | policy content platform stewardship (CM-1-3-7-1-6) | legal determinations (Legal/Compliance)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Finance-approved risk appetite | portfolio analysis findings | loss experience | Legal/Compliance directions"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Current, approved, published credit policies"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One current, approved credit rulebook every credit decision runs on."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-REG-B-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Analyze Portfolio Credit Risk | enables: Establish Credit Limit & Risk Code | uses-input: Maintain Commercial Policies"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 34 (2026-09-20). Review policy-document distinction adopted verbatim, protecting the b31 policy-content rule."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-4-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20709" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20709"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-4 ;
    skos:definition "Decide customer-level credit: assess creditworthiness from KYC outcomes, financial statements, bureau data, payment history, parent support and credit-support instruments, and industry, geography, product, and channel risk; establish credit limits, risk codes, terms eligibility, and required security (guarantees, letters of credit, prepayment, deposits) per policy and the delegations of authority; and record decisions to governed master data with effective dates and review schedules."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.4.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Establish Credit Limit & Risk Code"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Decisions apply policy — a limit outside policy or threshold requires approval through the credit governance owners before commitment. A customer may be approved only after all mandatory eligibility, KYC, sanctions, compliance, and credit criteria have been satisfied. A favorable financial assessment cannot override a Compliance/Legal restriction. Limits and risk codes attach at the level the governed customer-domain model defines and aggregate per the approved Party/Account/credit-group hierarchy — never assigned to a collapsed 'customer'. Fact-dependent statement: where U.S. credit is extended, including commercial credit, the Equal Credit Opportunity Act and Regulation B may impose nondiscrimination and adverse-action notice obligations depending on applicant type and circumstances; applicability and required procedures are Legal/Compliance determinations, and this process operates under the procedures they define. Decisions take effect only through governed master-data submission; consuming processes read the governed record.
In scope: Creditworthiness assessment across KYC, financial, bureau, payment-history, credit-support, and sector evidence | limit, risk-code, terms-eligibility, and security decisions per policy and DoA | periodic and event-triggered reviews | recording via governed submission with effective dates | escalation of out-of-policy cases
Out of scope: Policy setting (CM-1-3-7-4-1) | KYC execution (CM-1-3-7-4-6) | overriding Compliance/Legal restrictions | exposure monitoring (CM-1-3-7-4-3) | legal determinations (Legal/Compliance) | master-data writes outside governed submission"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "KYC outcomes | financial statements, bureau data, payment history, credit support | credit policies and DoA | customer-domain hierarchy"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed customer credit limits, risk codes, and security requirements with review schedules"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every customer's credit envelope is decided, evidenced, current, and governed."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-REG-B-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: KYC Process | governed-by: Maintain Credit Policies | enables: Monitor Credit Against Limit | enables: Setup Prospect"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 34 (2026-09-20). Review evidence hierarchy and mandatory-criteria rule adopted verbatim — a favorable financial assessment cannot override a Compliance/Legal restriction; fact-dependent ECOA/Regulation B statement retained with SRC-REG-B-001 registered for traceability per review."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-4-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11215" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11215"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-4 ;
    skos:definition "Monitor customer credit exposure against approved limits continuously: calculate exposure per approved policy, aggregate it across the customer's accounts per the governed credit hierarchy, apply and release holds under authorized rules and decisions, and route deterioration signals to limit review, servicing, and collections."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.4.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Monitor Credit Against Limit"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Exposure is calculated according to approved policy and may include open orders, allocated/released orders, unbilled fulfillments, unbilled card activity, open receivables, disputed balances, accrued charges, contingent obligations, security/collateral offsets, and other exposure elements specified by the policy. Exposure aggregates at the Party/Customer Account/credit-group level defined by the governed customer domain and approved credit policy. It does not assume each account is independently creditworthy or that all legal entities share exposure automatically. Holds may be automatically applied by approved system rules or manually applied by authorized Credit personnel. Hold release requires a documented authorized Credit decision or an approved automated rule; commercial order, card, billing, or service teams cannot bypass the hold. Holds fire in the consuming processes (order validation, release, card servicing apply them — the batch 33/29 receiving ends); deteriorating-exposure signals route to Establish Credit Limit & Risk Code for limit review and, at delinquency, to the servicing and collections processes. Sanctions holds are distinct from credit holds and follow the Compliance-owned framework.
In scope: Continuous policy-defined exposure calculation, including security/collateral offsets and policy-qualified disputed balances | aggregation across the governed Party/Account/credit-group hierarchy | limit utilization monitoring and alerting | automatic and authorized manual holds | documented hold-release decisions | deterioration signal routing
Out of scope: Limit and risk-code decisions (CM-1-3-7-4-2) | exposure policy definition (CM-1-3-7-4-1) | collections (CM-1-3-8-3) | order and card servicing actions (their processes, applying these decisions) | sanctions hold adjudication (Compliance) | AR accounting (CM-1-3-8-2)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved limits and risk codes | exposure data from orders, fulfillment, cards, and AR | policy exposure and hold rules | governed credit hierarchy"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Current exposure positions, applied holds, documented releases, and routed deterioration signals"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "No customer quietly exceeds the risk the business decided to take."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Establish Credit Limit & Risk Code | enables: Capture & Validate Order | enables: Manage Bad Debt | informed-by: Manage Cash Application, A/R & Revenue Accounting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 34 (2026-09-20). Review policy-qualified exposure model adopted verbatim (security/collateral offsets included; disputed balances per policy treatment, not all-in); hold automation/override rule adopted verbatim; sanctions-vs-credit hold distinction noted."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-4-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "14187" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 14187"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-4 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze credit risk at portfolio level: concentration by customer, sector, channel, and geography; aging and risk-code migration; loss experience and expected-loss signals; and stress indicators under changing market conditions — producing insights that inform credit policies, limit strategies, and Finance's reserve and allowance determinations."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.4.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Analyze Portfolio Credit Risk"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """The process may calculate risk signals, expected-loss indicators, concentration views, migration, and stress results according to Finance-approved methodologies. Finance determines accounting allowance, impairment, reserve, and disclosure treatment. The analysis-and-inform rule applies: policy changes are Maintain Credit Policies' to propose and the governance owners' to approve; limit changes are Establish Credit Limit & Risk Code's; reserve, allowance, impairment, and disclosure determinations are Finance's (the Finance lock, supplied from here with data, never made here). Metrics produced are evidence-layer; candidate KPIs route via the KPI Store.
In scope: Portfolio concentration, migration, aging, and loss analysis | expected-loss and stress signals per Finance-approved methodologies | insight delivery to policy, limits, and Finance allowance processes | evidence-layer credit metrics
Out of scope: Policy, limit, and closure decisions (their processes) | accounting allowance, impairment, reserve, and disclosure treatment (Finance; Bad Debt Allowance) | collections strategy (CM-1-3-8-3) | KPI approval/publication"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Exposure, aging, and loss data | risk codes and migration history | market and sector indicators | Finance-approved methodologies"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Portfolio risk insights informing policies, limits, and Finance determinations"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The credit book's shape and drift are visible before they become losses."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Monitor Credit Against Limit | enables: Maintain Credit Policies | enables: Bad Debt Allowance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 34 (2026-09-20). Review expected-loss vs accounting-provisioning distinction adopted verbatim; b21 analysis rule and Finance lock inherited."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-4-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10106" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10106"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Perform Credit-Driven Customer Closure and Reinstatement"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-4 ;
    skos:definition "Determine and coordinate credit-driven suspension, closure, or reinstatement of commercial eligibility under approved policy. It does not dissolve a legal entity, unilaterally terminate contracts, extinguish debt, delete master data, or prevent Legal/Commercial owners from managing non-credit exits. Coordinate the closure or reinstatement workflow through the owning processes so records, orders, cards, collections, and contracts land consistently."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.4.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Customer Closure & Reinstatement"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Decision here, execution through owners: master-data retirement or reactivation records through governed submission (Maintain Customer Master Data records per this process's decisions); open orders resolve through the order processes; card servicing through the card program; collections continue through Manage Collection and Disputes — closure never extinguishes a debt or a collection; contract termination executes through Legal and Contract Management under the agreements. Commercial exits for non-credit reasons are decisions of the accountable commercial owners; legal-entity closure, account closure, service shutdown, and regulatory deactivation have their own owners. Reinstatement requires the applicable credit reassessment (limits, KYC refresh) before reopening.
In scope: Credit-driven suspension, closure, and reinstatement decisions of commercial eligibility per policy and DoA | workflow coordination across master data, orders, cards, collections, and contracts | reinstatement reassessment gating | complete closure records
Out of scope: Non-credit commercial exits (commercial owners) | legal-entity dissolution, contract termination execution, and regulatory deactivation (Legal; Contract Management; their owners) | debt extinguishment and collections (CM-1-3-8-3) | master-data deletion or writes outside governed submission"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Credit deterioration and delinquency signals | policies and DoA | collections and Legal status | reassessment outcomes"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Decided, fully coordinated credit-eligibility closures and reinstatements with consistent records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Credit eligibility closes and reopens deliberately, completely, and on the record."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Monitor Credit Against Limit | enables: Maintain Customer Master Data | uses-input: Collection Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 34 (2026-09-20). Review credit-driven recast adopted verbatim — suspension/closure/reinstatement of commercial eligibility only, never entity dissolution, contract termination, debt extinguishment, or data deletion. Preferred label 'Perform Credit-Driven Customer Closure and Reinstatement' recorded as alt label and queued (more than punctuation: prevents conflict with general customer-closure processes)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-4-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20094" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20094"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Perform KYC Due Diligence"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-4 ;
    skos:definition "Execute know-your-customer due diligence: verify customer identity and legal existence, collect, verify, and maintain beneficial-ownership information where required by applicable policy, law, regulation, customer type, or risk rating, screen customers and counterparties against sanctions and watchlists, assess KYC risk per the enterprise framework, and refresh due diligence on the required cycles and trigger events."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.4.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "KYC Process"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Execution, not framework ownership: the sanctions compliance and financial-crime frameworks are owned by Compliance and Legal; this process executes them. Potential sanctions, watchlist, PEP, adverse-media, beneficial-ownership, identity, or document discrepancies are escalated for designated Compliance/Legal review. The KYC process does not clear a true/false match, grant exceptions, or determine permissibility of a relationship. Fact-dependent statement: U.S. sanctions administered by OFAC apply broadly to U.S. persons — customer and counterparty screening against the SDN and other OFAC lists is performed per the enterprise sanctions compliance program; anti-money-laundering program obligations are fact-dependent on the entity's regulatory status and activities; applicability and program design are Compliance/Legal determinations. Refresh triggers may be time-based, event-driven, risk-rating driven, ownership/control-change driven, negative-news or sanctions-driven, or transaction/activity-triggered. KYC outcomes gate onboarding (Setup Prospect requires them; customer master records them) and feed credit assessment. Personal data handled here is governed under the enterprise personal-data governance framework, with collection, use, retention, and disclosure limited to what the framework and applicable law permit.
In scope: Identity and legal-existence verification | collection, verification, and maintenance of beneficial-ownership information where required | sanctions and watchlist screening per the enterprise program | KYC risk assessment per framework | refresh on required cycles and trigger events | escalation of potential matches and discrepancies to designated Compliance/Legal review | KYC records
Out of scope: Sanctions/AML framework ownership, match clearing, exceptions, and relationship-permissibility determinations (Compliance; Legal) | credit decisions (CM-1-3-7-4-2) | onboarding coordination (Setup Prospect) | privacy framework setting"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Customer applications and documents | enterprise sanctions/AML framework | screening lists per the program | refresh triggers"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Completed, current KYC outcomes gating onboarding and credit, with screening and escalation evidence"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The business always knows who it is dealing with, and never deals with who it must not."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-OFAC-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Setup Prospect | enables: Establish Credit Limit & Risk Code | governed-by: Manage Credit & Risk"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 34 (2026-09-20). Review beneficial-ownership wording (collect/verify/maintain, never legally 'establish') and match-escalation rule adopted verbatim; refresh-trigger taxonomy per review. Preferred label 'Perform KYC Due Diligence' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-5-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10324" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10324"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Prioritize Customer Requests and Inquiries"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-5 ;
    skos:definition "Assign priority to each incoming customer request, inquiry, or complaint per the approved prioritization framework, and maintain that framework current with service experience. Priority is assigned using an approved framework that may include safety/regulatory escalation, customer impact, service commitment, financial/commercial impact, vulnerability/criticality, operational impact, recurrence, and required response time."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.5.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Establish Priority Assignment of Customer Request/Inquiry"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Safety first, outside the queue: Any request, complaint, or communication indicating a potential product-quality, safety, environmental, spill/release, contamination, security, or regulatory event is immediately captured and escalated to the accountable Quality, EHS, Security, and Operations processes under their incident protocols. The service capability does not assess severity, determine reportability, investigate the event, delay escalation pending normal prioritization, or close the incident; it tracks the customer-facing case and communications. Priority frameworks, tiers, and service-level commitments are approved through the accountable service governance; per-case assignment applies the approved framework (apply-not-make, the standing pattern). Customer-tier treatment follows approved commercial terms, never ad-hoc preference.
In scope: Per-case priority assignment per approved framework | immediate safety/quality/environmental/security escalation under incident protocols | framework maintenance proposals from service experience | priority-queue integrity
Out of scope: Framework, tier, and service-level approval (service governance) | safety/quality/security assessment and incident ownership (Quality; EHS; Security; Operations) | resolution execution (CM-1-3-7-5-3) | commercial terms decisions"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Incoming cases | approved prioritization framework and service levels | customer terms | incident escalation protocols"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Prioritized case queue with safety-relevant matters escalated immediately"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The right cases first — and safety-relevant cases instantly with the right owner."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Process Inquiries and Requests | governed-by: Manage Customer Requests and Inquiries"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 35 (2026-09-20). Review priority-taxonomy sentence adopted verbatim (framework-defined, not universally numerical); safety-escalation rule per the L4. Preferred label 'Prioritize Customer Requests and Inquiries' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-5-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10252" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10252"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Maintain Customer Request and Inquiry Records"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-5 ;
    skos:definition "Maintain governed operational case and interaction records for customer requests, inquiries, complaints, and service communications, including categorization, ownership, status, timestamps, routing, correspondence, evidence links, resolution outcome, and retention classification."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.5.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Maintain Requests and Inquiries Master Database"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Case records, not master data: these are operational service records, distinct from the enterprise master-data domains (batch 31) — nothing here creates or changes customer, price, product, or tax master data, which move only through governed submission. Case records carry: unique case identifier; channel/source; customer/account/party reference; request category/subcategory; priority; safety/compliance flag; owning process/queue; status; timestamps; SLA/commitment; escalation history; related order/invoice/contract/card/incident identifiers; resolution code; customer communication evidence; and retention/disposition classification. Case merge preserves original identifiers, source events, communications, audit history, and linked outcomes; it must not delete or obscure complaint/incident evidence. The service record references the incident/case identifier managed by the accountable process. It does not become the incident system of record. Platform administration, application security, and technical operations are IT's (the batch 31 technical-controls distinction). Personal data in case records is governed under the enterprise personal-data governance framework; retention, legal holds, and disposal follow the records-management policy.
In scope: Case record stewardship across channels and routed processes | categorization taxonomy maintenance | data quality and status integrity | duplicate merge preserving identifiers, communications, audit history, and linked outcomes | retention, legal-hold, and disposal handling per records policy | case reporting data provision
Out of scope: Master-data domains and changes (CM-1-3-7-1; governed submission) | incident system of record (accountable incident processes) | platform and technical operations (IT) | case resolution (CM-1-3-7-5-3) | records-management policy setting"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Case events from all channels and routed processes | categorization taxonomy | records-management policy and legal holds"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed, current, complete case records with categorization, linkage, and retention control"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One trustworthy, auditable record per request, wherever the request goes."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Process Inquiries and Requests | enables: Perform Customer Follow up | governed-by: Manage Customer Requests and Inquiries"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 35 (2026-09-20). Review refined definition, case-state control list, and merge safeguard adopted verbatim — merge never deletes or obscures complaint/incident evidence (matters for disputes, EHS complaints, regulated issues, legal holds). Preferred label 'Maintain Customer Request and Inquiry Records' recorded as alt label and queued — removes the incorrect 'master' implication."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-5-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "13482" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 13482"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-5 ;
    skos:definition "Work each customer request or inquiry to resolution: answer directly what governed data and approved content can answer — status, information, and documentation requests — route the substance of everything else to its owning process with the case attached, track routed items to outcome, and communicate resolution to the customer."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.5.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Process Inquiries and Requests"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """The resolution-routing rule operationalized: Direct answers must be drawn from governed records, approved knowledge content, approved documents, or confirmed owner-process outcomes. The service process must not infer, estimate, or promise a price, delivery, credit, tax, quality, safety, legal, or contract outcome. Substance resolution belongs to the owning processes (disputes, billing corrections, credit, orders, cards, technical/site matters route per the L4 rule), and this process tracks each routed item to outcome and owns the customer communication loop — the owning process decides, the service process closes the loop. Commitments to customers reflect owning-process confirmations, never independent promises. Escalation for aging or stuck cases follows the approved escalation paths.
In scope: Direct resolution from governed records, approved knowledge content, approved documents, and confirmed owner-process outcomes | substance routing with case linkage | routed-item tracking to outcome | customer resolution communication | aging and escalation management
Out of scope: Dispute adjudication (CM-1-3-8-3-1) | billing corrections and credits (CM-1-3-8-1) | credit decisions (CM-1-3-7-4) | order changes (CM-1-3-7-3-3) | card servicing (CM-1-3-6-6) | inferred, estimated, or promised price, delivery, credit, tax, quality, safety, legal, or contract outcomes"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Prioritized cases | governed status/document data and approved knowledge content | owning-process outcomes | escalation paths"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Resolved cases with routed substance, tracked outcomes, and completed customer communication"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every case resolves through the right owner, and the customer always hears back."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Establish Priority Assignment of Customer Request/Inquiry | enables: Dispute Management | enables: Perform Customer Follow up"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 35 (2026-09-20). Review information-source safeguard adopted verbatim — the no-shadow-decision rule's answer-side twin: governed content or confirmed owner outcomes only, no inference or promises."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-5-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10106" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10106"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-5 ;
    skos:definition "Follow up resolved cases with the customer: confirm the resolution held, verify commitments made during resolution were met, capture satisfaction and residual issues, and reopen or re-route where resolution failed."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.5.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Customer Follow up"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Follow-up communications are service communications tied to the customer's case — conducted through the customer's preferred service channels; marketing content requires the separate consent/contactability framework and never rides a service follow-up. Case-level follow-up feedback is operational service evidence. It does not replace governed Consumer Analysis, Customer/Marketer Analysis, formal survey design, loyalty analytics, or customer-experience measurement. Reopened cases re-enter prioritization; commitments verified are the owning processes' commitments, tracked here. Satisfaction signals and residual-issue patterns feed the service reviews and evidence-layer metrics (candidate KPIs via the KPI Store).
In scope: Post-resolution confirmation | commitment verification | satisfaction and residual-issue capture as operational service evidence | reopening and re-routing on failed resolution | signal provision to service reviews
Out of scope: Marketing communication (Marketing execution; consent/contactability framework) | formal survey design, consumer/customer analysis, loyalty analytics, and customer-experience measurement (Marketing Performance Analysis, CM-1-3-5-5) | re-resolution substance (owning processes) | KPI approval/publication (Enterprise measurement governance)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Resolved cases and commitments | customer channel preferences | satisfaction responses"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Verified resolutions, reopened exceptions, and satisfaction signals for improvement"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Resolution means resolved — verified with the customer, not assumed."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Process Inquiries and Requests | enables: Conduct Quarterly Review Meeting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 35 (2026-09-20). Review formal-research distinction adopted verbatim — case-level feedback is operational service evidence, never a substitute for governed analysis processes. Normalized label 'Perform Customer Follow-Up' is punctuation-only — queued for the naming pass, no alt label (validator lesson)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-5-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11427" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11427"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Conduct Customer Service Reviews"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-5 ;
    skos:definition "Conduct structured periodic service reviews with customers: review service performance against commitments, open items and their paths to closure, complaint and issue trends, and agreed improvement actions, with tracked follow-through. Review cadence may be quarterly by default and adjusted by customer tier, agreement, issue profile, regulatory obligation, or service model."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.5.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Conduct Quarterly Review Meeting"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Service review, not commercial negotiation: Service reviews may identify commercial opportunities, terms issues, pricing concerns, volume changes, contract questions, or strategic account topics, but those are recorded and referred to the appropriate commercial/account process rather than negotiated or decided in the service review. Review commitments entered are service commitments within existing authority; anything beyond routes for approval. Review outputs feed account management, service improvement, and the evidence layer.
In scope: Periodic service review preparation and conduct, quarterly by default and adjusted per tier, agreement, issue profile, regulatory obligation, or service model | performance-against-commitment review | open-item and trend review | improvement action agreement within authority and tracked follow-through | recording and referral of commercial topics to their owners
Out of scope: Commercial negotiation, terms, pricing, volumes, and contract decisions (Manage Key Accounts; commercial processes) | commitments beyond existing authority (approval paths) | account strategy (sales processes)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Service performance data | open cases and trends | prior actions | account context"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Conducted reviews with agreed, tracked improvement actions and referred commercial topics"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Service performance reviewed with the customer, on cadence, with actions that actually close."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Perform Customer Follow up | enables: Manage Key Accounts | governed-by: Manage Customer Requests and Inquiries"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 35 (2026-09-20). Review commercial-referral and cadence sentences adopted verbatim — quarterly is the default cadence, not the concept. Preferred label 'Conduct Customer Service Reviews' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-5-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20754" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20754"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7-5 ;
    skos:definition "Provide customers training on commercial tools, portals, ordering, documentation, card-program administration, and approved product/service interaction procedures. Where the program includes safe-handling orientation, the content is delivered exactly from approved Product Stewardship/EHS materials; it is not safety certification, regulatory training authority, or site-operator training unless a separate accountable framework expressly delegates that role. Develop delivery materials from approved content, deliver through appropriate formats, and track completion where required."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.5.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Provide Customer Training"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Delivery, not content authority: product-safety and regulatory content (safety data sheets, handling requirements) comes from the accountable Product Stewardship and EHS owners; commercial-process content from the accountable process owners; this process develops delivery materials from approved content and never authors safety, regulatory, or policy substance. Training completion evidence may be recorded where required by agreement, program rule, regulatory requirement, or customer request; certification authority remains with the accountable content framework. Distinct from franchise-operations training (Train and Certify AMPM Manager, CM-1-3-6-1-4): this row trains customers on commercial interactions, not franchise site operations.
In scope: Customer training needs identification | delivery material development from approved content | training delivery across formats | completion tracking and evidence where required by agreement, program rule, regulatory requirement, or customer request | training feedback to content owners
Out of scope: Safety, regulatory, and policy content authorship (Product Stewardship; EHS; process owners) | safety certification and regulatory training authority (accountable content frameworks) | franchise operations training (CM-1-3-6-1-4) | training obligations creation (contracts; programs)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved content from accountable owners | customer needs and program requirements | delivery channels"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Delivered customer training with completion evidence where required"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Customers use the commercial tools and processes correctly, safely, and confidently."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage Customer Portal | enables: Process Inquiries and Requests | governed-by: Manage Customer Requests and Inquiries"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 35 (2026-09-20). Review safety-training qualifier and completion-evidence note adopted verbatim — safe-handling orientation delivers approved Product Stewardship/EHS materials exactly; no certification or regulatory training authority unless expressly delegated."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10295" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10295"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-1 ;
    skos:definition "Create customer invoices from confirmed channel evidence and governed commercial data on the customer's billing cadence — per delivery, consolidated, statement, or cycle — and distribute them through the customer's channels in the formats and content the applicable requirements mandate."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Create & Distribute Bill"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Billing consumes the contractually applicable fulfillment, custody, service-completion, or transaction evidence for the channel. The evidence source, quantity basis, timing, and billable event are defined by the agreement, applicable operational process, and governing measurement/transaction rules. Channel evidence includes, as applicable: bills of lading, delivery tickets and proof of delivery, rack meter tickets, terminal automation load records, into-plane uplift tickets, bunker delivery notes, pipeline nomination/allocation/statements, retail POS/shift/settlement evidence, service completion or fee events, contractual self-bill statements, and approved manual exception evidence. Billing runs detect duplicate, missing, out-of-period, held, incomplete, or conflicting billable events before invoice creation and route exceptions for correction. Invoices compute from confirmed, linked evidence and governed price, terms, and tax data — never from restated quantities or ad-hoc values (no-self-exception, billing side). Card-program statements assemble per the card-program billing coordination (b29). Tax/Legal determine applicable jurisdictional e-invoicing, invoice-content, digital-signature, clearance, archival, government-platform, and reporting requirements. Billing implements approved format, channel, content, delivery, and evidence rules; it does not determine legal applicability. Portal presentation flows through the portal channel (b32).
In scope: Daily billing runs from confirmed channel evidence with duplicate, missing, out-of-period, held, incomplete, and conflict detection | per-delivery, consolidated, statement, and cycle billing per terms | invoice content assembly per governed data and Tax/Legal-directed mandates | multi-channel distribution including e-invoicing where directed | billing-run controls and completeness checks
Out of scope: Quantity determination (measurement/fulfillment evidence owners) | pricing (governed data) | e-invoicing legal applicability (Tax; Legal) | credit memos and corrections (CM-1-3-8-1-4/-5) | cash application (CM-1-3-8-2)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Confirmed channel evidence | governed price/terms/tax data | customer billing terms and channels | Tax/Legal distribution directions"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Accurate invoices distributed on cadence with complete run controls"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every confirmed billable event billed once, accurately, on cadence, in the required form."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage Fulfillment | uses-input: Determine Taxability | enables: Receive Cash Receipts / Payment"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 36 (2026-09-21). Review channel-evidence definition and billing-run control adopted verbatim — evidence basis is contract/channel-dependent, never assumed to be a physical delivery quantity; e-invoicing implementation boundary adopted verbatim; fourth use of the daily horizon. Normalized label 'Create and Distribute Bill' is punctuation-only — queued, no alt label."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10774" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10774"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Perform Self-Billing Accounting"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-1 ;
    skos:definition "Account for customer self-billing arrangements: receive customer-issued billing documents under executed self-billing agreements, validate each against confirmed fulfillment evidence and governed prices and terms, record the validated self-bill, and resolve differences with the customer per the agreement."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Accounting For Self-billing"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Self-billing operates only under executed agreements that meet the applicable tax-law conditions — fact-dependent by jurisdiction, determined by the Tax function; no self-billing without a qualifying agreement. The applicable agreement must specify the parties' billing responsibility, tax/document treatment, acceptance rules, evidence basis, data exchange, correction process, retention obligations, and settlement mechanism. A customer's self-bill never overrides governed evidence or prices: validation compares to confirmed fulfillment evidence and governed data, and differences resolve per the agreement's mechanism — through controlled correction documents, never by adopting the customer's figures unverified. Validated self-bill acceptance must preserve the customer-issued original, internal validation results, agreement reference, fulfillment evidence links, tax treatment, and correction chain. Validated self-bills flow to the accountable receivables, payables, revenue, tax, and accounting processes according to the applicable commercial and accounting model.
In scope: Self-bill receipt under executed agreements | validation against confirmed evidence and governed data | recording of validated self-bills with preserved originals, validation results, and linkage | difference resolution per agreement through controlled documents | routing to the accountable accounting processes per the applicable model
Out of scope: Self-billing agreement terms (contracting processes; Legal) | tax-law qualification of self-billing (Tax function) | evidence and price determination (their owners) | accounting treatment determination (Finance) | cash application (CM-1-3-8-2)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Customer self-bills | executed self-billing agreements | confirmed fulfillment evidence | governed price/terms data"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Validated, recorded self-bills with preserved originals, resolved differences, and complete records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Customer paper accepted only when it matches governed evidence — and reconciled when it doesn't."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage Fulfillment | governed-by: Establish Commercial Terms & Contracts | enables: Perform Revenue Accounting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 36 (2026-09-21). Review agreement-content requirement, preserved-original rule, and accounting-model routing adopted verbatim — no single assumed AR path; self-billing covers ERS-style and customer-issued document arrangements per the governing agreement. Preferred label 'Perform Self-Billing Accounting' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10196" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10196"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Determine Transaction Taxability"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-1 ;
    skos:definition "Determine the taxes applicable to each billed transaction. Apply tax determination rules through effective-dated tax master data, transaction attributes, product tax classes, origin/destination or jurisdiction logic, customer tax status, exemption evidence, and other Tax-approved rules. Produce the tax lines the invoice carries, including federal and state fuel excise treatment where applicable."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Determine Taxability"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Application, not position-taking (the batch 31 tax rule's receiving end): tax positions, interpretations, exemption-validation rules, and responses to law changes are the Tax function's; this process applies them per governed tax master data. If tax master data, exemption evidence, product classification, jurisdiction, or transaction facts cannot resolve taxability, the transaction is held or routed according to Tax-approved exception procedures; billing does not guess a tax result. Fuel excise context: federal excise and fee treatment for taxable fuels follows the Tax function's determinations (IRS Publication 510 registered as federal fuel-excise context only — not authority for state/local/non-US outcomes). Distinct from the compliance/reporting-side Determine Taxability (CM-1-3-8-4-5), which will carry assessment/reporting/filing/remittance determination — the transaction-time vs compliance-time split, with future normalized labels 'Determine Transaction Taxability' and 'Determine Taxability for Tax Compliance and Reporting' queued. Tax corrections flow through controlled correction documents, never in place.
In scope: Per-transaction tax determination through effective-dated tax master data, transaction attributes, product tax classes, origin/destination/jurisdiction logic, customer tax status, and exemption evidence | fuel excise and fee line application per Tax determinations | hold-or-route handling of unresolvable cases per Tax-approved exception procedures | tax line audit trails
Out of scope: Tax positions, interpretations, and law-change responses (Tax function) | tax master data maintenance (CM-1-3-7-1-4) | certificate validation rules (Tax) | tax remittance, filing, and reporting (CM-1-3-8-4) | guessed tax results"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Governed tax master data (rates, codes, classes, certificates) | transaction data | Tax function determinations and exception procedures"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Correct transaction tax lines with complete determination audit trails"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every invoice carries exactly the tax the Tax function's determinations require — or holds until it can."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-IRS-P510-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Maintain Tax Master Data | enables: Create & Distribute Bill | enables: Reconcile And Report Indirect Tax"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 36 (2026-09-21). Review tax-application wording and hold-don't-guess exception rule adopted verbatim; duplicate-name normalization queued per review ('Determine Transaction Taxability' here, recorded as alt label; compliance-side label reserved for CM-1-3-8-4-5); SRC-IRS-P510-001 scoped to federal fuel-excise context only."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-1-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10147" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10147"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-1 ;
    skos:definition "Create controlled credit memos on authorized instruction: validate the instructing process's authorization and documented basis, reference the original invoice, apply the applicable approvals per the delegations of authority, issue the credit memo, and record its complete linkage from instruction to settlement effect."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.1.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Create Credit Memo"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """The single controlled credit instrument: rebate settlements (b29), returns authorizations (b33), dispute resolutions, tax and pricing corrections, contractual allowances, and approved commercial adjustments all credit customers exclusively through this process. The issuing process validates that an authorized originating process has provided the correct reason code, original-document reference, evidence, approvals, tax treatment, amount, customer/account, and settlement/AR handling instructions. The credit memo uses governed customer, tax, price, and account data; any refund, offset, rebill, or AR application follows its own authorized downstream process. A credit memo is an accounting/billing document: it is not a discount mechanism (pricing and rebate decisions stay with their owners), it does not itself move cash (a refund requires a separate authorized payment request under the payment-authority rule and Finance/Treasury execution), and it does not authorize a write-off (Finance's, via Bad Debt Allowance). It is never self-initiated and never issued on a service-desk request without an owning-process instruction (b35). Originals are preserved and referenced — never altered.
In scope: Authorization and basis validation per instructing process, including reason code, original-document reference, evidence, approvals, tax treatment, amount, customer/account, and settlement/AR handling instructions | DoA approval application | credit memo issuance with tax treatment from governed data | instruction-to-effect linkage and records
Out of scope: Credit decisions and instructions (rebates, returns, disputes, corrections — their owners) | discounts and pricing (their owners) | refund execution (Finance/Treasury; payment-authority rule) | write-offs (Finance) | offset, rebill, and AR application execution (their authorized downstream processes)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Authorized instructions with documented basis and handling instructions | original invoices | delegations of authority | governed customer/tax/price/account data"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Issued credit memos with complete authorization and linkage records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every customer credit is instructed, evidenced, approved, and traceable end to end."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Settle Rebate | uses-input: Manage Returns | uses-input: Dispute Management | enables: Manage Cash Application, A/R & Revenue Accounting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 36 (2026-09-21). Review originating-process validation list and governed-data/downstream-process conditions adopted verbatim; credit-memo vs refund vs offset vs write-off distinctions per review. Review preferred an 'informs' relation toward cash application — 'informs' is not in the relationship vocabulary (b10/b23 precedent): recorded here verbatim, nearest vocabulary applied to the locked L4 name."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-1-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10147" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10147"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Invoice Exceptions, Reversals, and Rebilling"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-1 ;
    skos:definition "Correct billing through controlled document events: validate the correction's basis and authorization, and create the proper controlled document type under approved rules — full cancellation, partial correction, replacement/rebill, debit adjustment, tax-only correction, or exception invoice — with coded reasons and the applicable approvals, preserving the complete original-to-correction chain."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.1.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Create Exception & Reverse Invoice"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Corrections are controlled events, never edits: an incorrect invoice is corrected by a new controlled document that references and preserves the original — nothing edited in place, nothing deleted (the Billing Integrity Rule's correction arm, parallel to the b33 history rule). The document type follows approved rules for the correction case. Reason codes classify every correction; patterns feed root-cause analysis and, where they suggest control failures or abuse, route to the accountable control owners. Corrections affecting tax follow the determination controls; corrections affecting evidenced quantities route back through the fulfillment evidence owners — billing never fixes an evidence problem by adjusting the bill. Rebilling follows the corrected basis through the standard billing controls.
In scope: Correction basis and authorization validation | controlled document-type selection per approved rules (cancellation, partial correction, replacement/rebill, debit adjustment, tax-only correction, exception invoice) | issuance with coded reasons and DoA approvals | rebilling through standard billing controls | original-to-correction chain preservation | correction pattern reporting to control owners
Out of scope: Evidence corrections (fulfillment/measurement owners) | credit memos (CM-1-3-8-1-4) | dispute adjudication (CM-1-3-8-3-1) | root-cause fixes in other processes (their owners) | document deletion or in-place editing"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Correction requests with basis | affected invoices and evidence linkages | reason-code taxonomy and document-type rules | delegations of authority"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Issued controlled correction documents with preserved chains, completed rebills, and routed patterns"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Billing errors die by the right controlled correction, with the full history intact."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Create & Distribute Bill | uses-input: Process Inquiries and Requests | enables: Manage Cash Application, A/R & Revenue Accounting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 36 (2026-09-21). Review document-category taxonomy adopted verbatim; preferred label 'Manage Invoice Exceptions, Reversals, and Rebilling' recorded as alt label and queued. Review preferred an 'informs' relation toward receivables/revenue accounting pending batch 37/38 authoring — 'informs' is vocabulary-missing (b10/b23 precedent): recorded here verbatim, nearest vocabulary applied to the locked L4 name."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-2-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20104" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20104"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Record Customer Receipts and Payment Notifications"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-2 ;
    skos:definition "Record customer payment receipts daily: capture receipt notifications and settlement information from Treasury-managed banking, lockbox, card-acquirer, and electronic arrangements across the approved payment methods, validate completeness against the receipt-source reports, and stage receipts for application with their identifying data."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Receive Cash Receipts / Payment"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Recording, not moving (the payment-authority rule's receiving side): funds arrive through arrangements Treasury owns; this process records what arrived, where, and from whom — it operates no bank accounts and initiates no movements. Validates completeness against bank, lockbox, card processor, payment gateway, and other approved receipt-source reports. It does not determine whether a payment is final/irrevocable; Treasury/banking arrangements and payment method rules determine settlement finality. Payment methods follow the governed menu (Define Financing & Payment Methods); card-acquirer settlement amounts stage here for the reconciliation process. Receipts recorded but not yet identifiable route immediately to the unapplied-exception process — never held informally.
In scope: Daily receipt-notification and settlement-information capture across approved methods and channels | completeness validation against bank, lockbox, processor, and gateway reports | receipt staging with identifying data | immediate routing of unidentifiable receipts to exception management
Out of scope: Bank, lockbox, and acquirer arrangements and funds movement (Treasury) | settlement finality determination (Treasury; payment method rules) | application (CM-1-3-8-2-3) | payment-method definition (CM-1-3-7-2-4) | refunds (Finance/Treasury; payment-authority rule)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Bank, lockbox, processor, and gateway reports via Treasury arrangements | governed payment-method definitions"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Complete daily receipt records staged for application"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every arriving payment is captured completely, daily, and staged with what's known about it."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Create & Distribute Bill | precedes: Apply Receipts | governed-by: Manage Cash Application, A/R & Revenue Accounting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 37 (2026-09-21). Review recording/notification wording and settlement-finality boundary adopted verbatim. Preferred label 'Record Customer Receipts and Payment Notifications' recorded as alt label and queued — reflects what the process actually does."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10397" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10397"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-2 ;
    skos:definition "Capture and normalize customer remittance information from all formats — EDI, portal, email, paper, bank addenda — validate it for completeness and consistency, link it to the receipts it explains, and stage the combined evidence for application."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Receive Remittance Advice"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Remittance data is application evidence, not instruction to deviate: a remittance that directs application contrary to governed rules or claims deductions routes those elements to their owning processes (disputes, collections, credit instructions) rather than being applied as claimed. Remittance instructions, bank-detail changes, payment redirection requests, or suspicious messages are validated through approved controls and escalated to Security/Treasury/Legal as applicable; remittance information does not authorize change of bank, customer, invoice, or master-data records. Missing or ambiguous remittance triggers customer contact through the service channels and, unresolved, the exception process.
In scope: Multi-format remittance capture and normalization | completeness and consistency validation | receipt linkage | validation and Security/Treasury/Legal escalation of bank-detail changes, redirection requests, and suspicious messages | routing of claimed deductions and contrary instructions to owning processes | staging of combined evidence
Out of scope: Application decisions (CM-1-3-8-2-3) | deduction and dispute adjudication (CM-1-3-8-3) | bank, customer, invoice, or master-data changes (their governed processes) | remittance format mandates (terms; channels)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Remittance advices across formats | receipt records | customer terms | fraud-control procedures"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Normalized, linked, validated remittance evidence staged for application"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every receipt arrives at application with the best available, validated evidence of what it pays."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Receive Cash Receipts / Payment | precedes: Apply Receipts | uses-input: Manage Customer Portal"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 37 (2026-09-21). Review fraud/security caution adopted verbatim — remittance information never authorizes record changes; payment-redirection and bank-detail-change fraud controls escalate to Security/Treasury/Legal."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-2-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10801" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10801"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-2 ;
    skos:definition "Apply staged receipts against open governed receivables daily: match on remittance evidence and approved application rules, apply exact matches, resolve tolerable differences per approved tolerance rules, route short-pays, over-pays, and claimed deductions to their owning processes, and post applications with complete linkage."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Apply Receipts"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """The integrity rule operationalized: application follows remittance evidence and approved rules — including approved auto-application and tolerance rules — and never forces, nets, or absorbs differences. Application follows the approved customer/account, payer, bill-to, currency, entity, and remittance rules. Cross-account, cross-entity, cross-currency, or offset application requires approved authority and accounting treatment. A short-pay is a receivable still open plus a routed difference (dispute, collection, or credit-memo instruction per its nature); an over-pay is an exception item or, on instruction, a refund request through the payment-authority process — never netted silently. Credit memos apply per their settlement/AR handling instructions (b36). Application reversals are controlled events with reasons and approvals.
In scope: Daily matching and application per remittance evidence and approved rules | approved auto-application and tolerances | application per the approved customer/account, payer, bill-to, currency, and entity rules, with cross-account/entity/currency and offset application only under approved authority and accounting treatment | short-pay/over-pay/deduction routing with open-item integrity | credit-memo application per instructions | controlled application reversals
Out of scope: Difference adjudication (disputes, collections, credit owners) | tolerance and application rule approval (Finance) | write-offs (Finance) | refund execution (Finance/Treasury) | invoice changes (CM-1-3-8-1 controlled documents)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Staged receipts with remittance evidence | open governed receivables and the governed account hierarchy | approved application and tolerance rules | credit-memo instructions"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Posted applications with complete linkage, open differences routed, exceptions escalated"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Cash lands on exactly the receivables it pays — and differences stay visible until their owners resolve them."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Receive Remittance Advice | enables: Manage Unapplied Receipts / Exceptions | enables: Collection Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 37 (2026-09-21). Review account-hierarchy safeguard adopted verbatim — cross-account, cross-entity, cross-currency, and offset applications require approved authority and accounting treatment (material in complex downstream account structures)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-2-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20807" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20807"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Unapplied Receipts and Exceptions"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-2 ;
    skos:definition "Own the exception state for cash that cannot be applied: research unidentified and unapplied receipts, contact customers through the service channels, resolve each item within policy time bounds — application, refund request, or other authorized disposition — and maintain aged visibility of everything unresolved."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.2.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Unapplied Receipts / Exceptions"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """An explicit, aged, owned state: every unapplied or unidentified item carries an owner, aging, and a policy resolution clock — nothing is silently netted, absorbed, or left to age invisibly. Exception resolution, refund request, offset, transfer, or reclassification must preserve segregation of duties between investigation, approval, accounting posting, and Treasury execution. Resolutions follow their authorized paths: application on evidence, refund by authorized payment request under the payment-authority rule, offset per approved rules, or escalation. Unresolved credits, overpayments, uncashed refunds, unapplied customer funds, or other property interests may become subject to unclaimed-property reporting, due-diligence, and remittance obligations after applicable dormancy periods. Legal, Tax, Treasury, and Finance determine applicability, owner identification, dormancy, reporting, and remittance requirements; this process supplies complete, aged, auditable item records. Jurisdiction-specific applicability is an open item, not an assumed fact.
In scope: Unidentified and unapplied receipt research | customer contact through service channels | resolution within policy time bounds by authorized disposition, with segregation of duties across investigation, approval, posting, and Treasury execution | aged exception visibility and reporting | complete, aged, auditable item records for Legal/Tax/Treasury/Finance unclaimed-property determinations
Out of scope: Refund execution (Finance/Treasury) | write-offs (Finance) | unclaimed-property determinations, due diligence, and filings (Legal; Tax; Treasury; Finance) | application rules (Finance) | dispute adjudication (CM-1-3-8-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Unapplied and unidentified receipts | research sources and customer contact channels | resolution policies and SoD controls | Legal/Tax/Treasury/Finance directions"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Resolved exceptions through authorized dispositions, with complete aged records for what remains"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "No dollar sits unexplained — every exception has an owner, an age, and a clock."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Apply Receipts | uses-input: Process Inquiries and Requests | enables: AR Reconciliation / Compliance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 37 (2026-09-21). Review segregation-of-duties condition and the broadened unclaimed-property wording adopted verbatim; per review, jurisdiction-specific unclaimed-property applicability is recorded as an open issue — no source registered until target jurisdictions are defined. Preferred label 'Manage Unapplied Receipts and Exceptions' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-2-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10954" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10954"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Perform Cash, Acquirer, and Receivables Reconciliations"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-2 ;
    skos:definition "Reconcile the receivables and receipts positions daily: bank and lockbox reporting to recorded receipts, card-acquirer and processor settlement files to recorded transactions and receipts, subledger activity to applications, and provide the reconciled positions that feed general-ledger reconciliation and period-end close."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.2.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Reconciliations & Settlements"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Settlement in this node means reconciliation of bank/acquirer settlement files and receivable positions; it does not own Trading Settlement, physical commodity settlement, or funds movement. This row is the named receiving end of the card-program coordination (Manage Credit Card Transactions reconciles its daily cycles with this process, b29); acquirer fees and holdbacks reconcile per the Treasury-owned arrangements. Breaks are exception items with owners and aging (same discipline as unapplied cash); unresolvable breaks escalate per policy. Period-end GL reconciliation and close ownership sit with the accounting processes (CM-1-3-8-4); this row supplies reconciled daily positions.
In scope: Daily bank/lockbox-to-receipt reconciliation | acquirer/processor settlement-file reconciliation including the card-program cycles | subledger-to-application integrity checks | break identification, ownership, aging, and escalation | reconciled positions to GL reconciliation and close
Out of scope: Funds movement and banking arrangements (Treasury) | Trading Settlement and physical commodity settlement (their owning processes) | GL ownership and period-end close (CM-1-3-8-4) | acquirer fee terms (Treasury) | break adjudication outside policy (escalation owners)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Bank/lockbox reporting | acquirer and processor settlement files | receipt and application records | card-program transaction cycles"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Daily reconciled positions with owned, aged breaks"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "What the bank says, what the processors say, and what the books say agree — daily, or the break is owned."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage Credit Card Transactions | uses-input: Apply Receipts | enables: AR Reconciliation / Compliance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 37 (2026-09-21). Review terminology guard adopted verbatim — 'settlement' here means settlement-file and position reconciliation, never Trading Settlement (batch 14), physical commodity settlement, or funds movement. Preferred label 'Perform Cash, Acquirer, and Receivables Reconciliations' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-2-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10729" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10729"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-2 ;
    skos:definition "Execute revenue accounting under Finance-approved policy: recognize revenue as performance obligations are satisfied per the approved recognition model, account for deferred and unbilled revenue, coordinate revenue-affecting accruals with the accrual processes, enforce revenue cutoff, and deliver revenue accounting into period-end close."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.2.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Revenue Accounting"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Execution, not policy: revenue-recognition policy, method elections, contract-assessment judgments, and interpretations under the applicable standards are Finance's — this process applies the approved model. Revenue recognition is supported by the Finance-approved evidence model, which may include billing documents, fulfillment/service evidence, contract terms, satisfaction-of-performance-obligation evidence, accrual support, and other approved accounting evidence. Unbilled revenue requires an approved basis and complete audit trail; it is not recognized merely because an invoice is expected. Revenue-accounting entries, deferrals, unbilled receivables, contract liabilities, variable consideration, and cutoff adjustments are posted under Finance-approved accounting policy and accounting-period controls. The process does not approve the policy, decide accounting elections, or publish financial statements. US GAAP context: revenue from contracts with customers follows the Topic 606 five-step model — contract, performance obligations, transaction price, allocation, recognition on satisfaction — as Finance's approved policy applies it (SRC-FASB-ASC606-001 registered as context only). Variable consideration tied to rebates and allowances coordinates with Accruals Analysis And Calculation; loyalty and similar liabilities per the b29 Finance determinations.
In scope: Revenue recognition per the Finance-approved model and evidence framework | deferred revenue, unbilled receivables, and contract asset/liability accounting on approved bases with complete audit trails | variable-consideration coordination with accrual processes | revenue cutoff enforcement under accounting-period controls | revenue delivery into period-end close
Out of scope: Revenue-recognition policy, elections, judgments, and interpretations (Finance) | accrual calculation ownership (CM-1-3-8-4-3) | period-end close ownership (CM-1-3-8-4-1) | billing (CM-1-3-8-1) | financial statement publication and disclosure (Finance)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Billed and evidenced transactions | Finance-approved recognition policy and evidence model | accrual coordination | contract terms"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Accounted revenue with deferrals, cutoffs, and complete traceability into close"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Revenue on the books exactly when and as the approved model says, traceable to approved evidence."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-FASB-ASC606-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Create & Distribute Bill | uses-input: Perform Accounting For Self-billing | uses-input: Accruals Analysis And Calculation | enables: Period End Processing"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 37 (2026-09-21). Review revenue-evidence model adopted verbatim — approved unbilled bases are recognizable under Finance policy with audit trails; the earlier 'never-unbilled' phrasing was corrected as too restrictive for accrual accounting; posting-controls sentence adopted verbatim. APQC 'Perform revenue accounting' scored 1.0."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-3-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20682" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20682"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Receivables Disputes"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-3 ;
    skos:definition "Adjudicate disputed billed amounts: capture and classify disputes from all routes, investigate each against the evidence chain — billing documents, fulfillment and custody evidence, contracts and terms, tax determinations, credit memo and adjustment evidence, and applicable commercial policy — decide validity under the delegations of authority, and instruct the outcome: credit-memo or correction instruction for valid disputes, release to collection for invalid ones, with the customer informed through the service communication loop."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dispute Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Adjudication on evidence, not negotiation: validity is decided against the governed evidence chain (the Billing Integrity Rule's records are the dispute record). Where a customer request seeks a goodwill adjustment, commercial concession, price accommodation, fee waiver, settlement compromise, or relationship remedy beyond the evidenced bill, Dispute Management routes the request to the applicable Commercial, Credit, Finance, Legal, or delegated authority; it does not classify such concessions as a billing dispute outcome. During investigation the disputed portion is flagged per policy: Only the disputed component is held from collection where policy requires; undisputed amounts remain subject to collection. Credit exposure, availability, holds, and service treatment follow the applicable Credit policy and customer-specific decision. Valid outcomes execute only as instructions to billing's controlled documents (b36 credit-memo controls); this process issues nothing itself. Dispute categories and reason codes feed root-cause analysis; recurring disputes with a customer feed the credit and service reviews.
In scope: Dispute capture and classification from all routes | evidence-chain investigation | validity adjudication per DoA | credit-memo/correction instructions with documented basis | invalid-dispute release to collection | routing of concession requests to their authorities | customer communication through the service loop | dispute records and category data
Out of scope: Credit memo and correction issuance (CM-1-3-8-1) | goodwill adjustments, concessions, and settlement compromises (Commercial; Credit; Finance; Legal; delegated authorities) | collection execution (CM-1-3-8-3-2) | service-request intake (CM-1-3-7-5, which routes here) | evidence creation (their owners)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Routed disputes and deductions | the governed evidence chain | delegations of authority | dispute policies"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Adjudicated disputes with instructed outcomes, routed concessions, and complete decision records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every disputed dollar gets an evidence-based verdict, fast, with a controlled outcome."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Process Inquiries and Requests | enables: Create Credit Memo | enables: Collection Management | enables: Develop Root Cause Analysis/Action Plan"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 38 (2026-09-21). Review commercial-concession routing rule adopted verbatim — concessions are never classified as dispute outcomes; disputed-portion hold rule per the L4. Preferred label 'Manage Receivables Disputes' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-3-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20682" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20682"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Receivables Collections"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-3 ;
    skos:definition "Execute the approved collection strategy on undisputed, due receivables: run dunning and contact cycles by segment and aging, secure and track payment commitments, administer payment plans within pre-approved authority, escalate through the approved ladder to third-party agencies or legal referral, and keep the collection record complete."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Collection Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Recovery within everyone else's authority: Collection may present, administer, and monitor payment plans or settlement arrangements only within pre-approved Credit/Finance authority, standard terms, and delegated limits. Any concession, extension, interest waiver, fee waiver, reduced settlement, debt compromise, or non-standard plan requires the applicable Credit, Finance, Legal, or delegated approval. Disputed portions are held per policy while Dispute Management adjudicates; collected cash arrives only through the cash-application controls — never handled directly; write-offs are Finance's and do not stop collection unless Finance and Legal so decide; agency placement and legal action route through Legal under executed agreements, with agency conduct governed by contract and law. Delinquency signals flow to Credit monitoring, credit-driven closure, and the card program's servicing processes (b29/b34 receiving ends). Where collected accounts involve consumer or individual obligors, applicable consumer debt-collection, privacy, and communication-conduct requirements may apply. Legal/Compliance determine applicability and required procedures.
In scope: Segmented dunning and contact cycles | payment commitment tracking | payment plan presentation, administration, and monitoring within pre-approved Credit/Finance authority, standard terms, and delegated limits | escalation ladder execution | agency placement and legal referral through Legal under executed agreements | collection records and performance data
Out of scope: Dispute adjudication (CM-1-3-8-3-1) | bill adjustment and credits (CM-1-3-8-1, on instruction) | concessions, waivers, reduced settlements, and debt compromises beyond delegated limits (Credit; Finance; Legal; delegated approvals) | write-offs and allowances (CM-1-3-8-3-3; Finance) | cash handling and application (CM-1-3-8-2) | legal action conduct (Legal)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Overdue undisputed receivables and aging | approved collection strategy and escalation ladder | Credit/Finance authority bounds | Legal procedures and agency agreements"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Recovered receivables, tracked commitments, and clean escalations with complete records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Due money comes in — systematically, lawfully, and without anyone's authority being borrowed."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Apply Receipts | informed-by: Monitor Credit Against Limit | uses-input: Manage Bad Debt | enables: Bad Debt Allowance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 38 (2026-09-21). Review payment-plan vs concession refinement adopted verbatim; consumer-debt statement in the review's general form (no statute named — no source registered, per the unclaimed-property precedent; Legal/Compliance determine applicability). Preferred label 'Manage Receivables Collections' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-3-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10761" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10761"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Determine Bad Debt Allowance"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-3 ;
    skos:definition "Execute Finance-approved expected-credit-loss estimation and write-off processes for trade receivables, using approved methodology, portfolio inputs, collection status, and forward-looking assumptions; prepare and record allowance and write-off outcomes under the applicable Finance delegation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bad Debt Allowance"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """The Finance lock, home side: allowance methodology, estimates, impairment treatment, and write-off authority are Finance's — every earlier boundary that said so points here, and this process executes under that authority and the applicable Finance delegation. US GAAP context: expected credit losses on trade receivables follow the current expected credit loss model (Topic 326) as Finance's approved methodology applies it (SRC-FASB-ASC326-001 registered as context only). Inputs are supplied, not decided, by their owners: portfolio risk signals from Analyze Portfolio Credit Risk, program delinquency data from the card program, collection status from Collection Management. A write-off is an accounting event, not debt forgiveness. A write-off does not automatically release collateral, cancel contractual rights, close a customer, stop collections, suspend legal recovery, or forgive debt; those actions require their own authorized decision. Recoveries on written-off amounts record per the approved treatment. Allowance and write-off activity delivers into period-end close.
In scope: Expected-credit-loss estimation per Finance-approved methodology with approved forward-looking assumptions | allowance preparation and recording through close | write-off preparation, decision recording, and posting per the Finance delegation | recovery treatment per approved policy | methodology input data assembly from owning processes | audit-ready estimation records
Out of scope: Methodology setting, segmentation, model governance, and accounting elections (Finance governance) | collection and recovery execution (CM-1-3-8-3-2) | collateral release, contract cancellation, customer closure, collection cessation, and debt forgiveness (their own authorized decisions) | portfolio risk analysis (CM-1-3-7-4-4) | financial statement disclosure (Finance)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Aging and loss experience | portfolio and program risk data (b34/b29) | collection statuses | Finance-approved methodology and delegations"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Determined allowances and recorded write-offs, delivered through close with complete estimation evidence"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The receivables on the books are worth what the books say — provably, per the approved model."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-FASB-ASC326-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Analyze Portfolio Credit Risk | uses-input: Collection Management | enables: Period End Processing"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 38 (2026-09-21). Review Finance-authority definition adopted verbatim — this process executes Finance-approved estimation and write-off processes, it does not own the authority; write-off non-release clarification adopted verbatim. Preferred label 'Determine Bad Debt Allowance' recorded as alt label and queued (verb-led per the taxonomy convention)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-3-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12046" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12046"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Develop Root-Cause Analyses and Action Plans"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-3 ;
    skos:definition "Turn dispute, deduction, and collection patterns into prevention: analyze the reason-coded evidence the resolution processes produce, identify root causes, develop action plans with the accountable owning processes, and track agreed actions to verified closure."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.3.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Root Cause Analysis/Action Plan"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """The analysis-and-inform rule applied to receivables friction: this process classifies causes from governed reason-code data (billing correction codes, application exception codes, dispute categories, collection outcomes), quantifies impact, and develops action plans with the owners. Root-cause categories include billing/invoice data; master data; price/discount/rebate conditions; tax determination; fulfillment/custody evidence; order validation/allocation; contract/terms; card/payment processing; cash/remittance/application; collections/credit; customer/service communication; and system/integration/data-quality defects. Action plans identify the accountable owner, due date, control/change path, validation evidence, and closure criteria. This process tracks commitments but does not implement changes, waive controls, approve remediation on behalf of owners, or close actions without owner evidence. Billing errors are billing's to fix, evidence gaps fulfillment's, credit-policy gaps Credit's, pricing errors pricing's, service failures the service capability's. Metrics produced are evidence-layer; candidate KPIs route via the KPI Store. Systemic findings suggesting control failures or abuse escalate to the accountable control owners.
In scope: Cross-process pattern analysis from governed reason-code data | root-cause classification across the approved category taxonomy and impact quantification | owner-agreed action plans with accountable owner, due date, control/change path, validation evidence, and closure criteria | commitment tracking to owner-evidenced closure | systemic-finding escalation | evidence-layer prevention metrics
Out of scope: Implementing changes, waiving controls, approving remediation on owners' behalf, or closing actions without owner evidence (Owning processes and their change controls) | dispute adjudication and collection execution (Dispute Management; Collection Management) | KPI approval/publication (Enterprise measurement governance) | control ownership (Accountable control owners)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Reason-coded dispute, correction, exception, and collection data | owning-process context | impact data"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Root-cause findings and owner-agreed, owner-evidenced, tracked action plans"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The same dispute never happens twice for the same reason — because its owner fixed it."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Dispute Management | uses-input: Create Exception & Reverse Invoice | enables: Maintain Credit Policies"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 38 (2026-09-21). Review action-plan discipline and root-cause category taxonomy adopted verbatim — plans bind only through owners' agreement and close only on owner evidence. Preferred label 'Develop Root-Cause Analyses and Action Plans' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-4-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10822" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10822"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Perform Period-End Processing"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-4 ;
    skos:definition "Run O2C period-end close on the Finance-approved calendar: verify completeness and reconciliation evidence from every feeding process, process period-end adjustments under approved authority, enforce cutoff across billing, receipts, accruals, and corrections, and deliver the closed O2C position into Finance's consolidation with its evidence chain."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.4.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Period End Processing"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Close on evidence: the close verifies each feed — billing runs complete, applications and reconciliations current, allowances and write-offs recorded, accruals calculated, tax reconciled, intercompany issued. No period closes over a missing, unresolved, or unreconciled material feed without a documented Finance-approved exception, compensating control, owner, resolution plan, and aging/true-up requirement. Close-control artifacts include the close checklist, feed certifications, reconciliation status, the exception log, adjustment journal support, materiality assessment, owner sign-offs, true-up plans, and post-close review. Adjustments are controlled events under the delegations; cutoff enforcement follows the accounting-period controls. The Finance close calendar, formal sign-off, consolidation, and statement production are Finance's; this process delivers O2C's closed, evidenced position into them.
In scope: Close-calendar execution for O2C | feed completeness and reconciliation verification with certifications | period-end adjustments under approved authority with journal support | cutoff enforcement | documented Finance-approved exceptions with compensating controls, owners, resolution plans, and aging/true-up requirements | delivery of the closed position with evidence into consolidation | post-close review
Out of scope: Close calendar, policy, and formal sign-off ownership (Finance) | consolidation and statements (Finance) | the feeding processes' own work (Their owners) | undocumented closes over material gaps"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Finance close calendar and policies | feed evidence and certifications from all O2C financial processes | delegations of authority"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "A closed, evidenced, reconciled O2C period delivered into consolidation with a complete exception log"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The period closes when the evidence says it can — and exceptions are Finance's explicit, controlled choice."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Perform Revenue Accounting | uses-input: Bad Debt Allowance | uses-input: Accruals Analysis And Calculation | enables: AR Reconciliation / Compliance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 39 (2026-09-21). Review close-control artifact list and qualified exception wording adopted verbatim — exceptions need documentation, compensating control, owner, resolution plan, and aging/true-up. Preferred label 'Perform Period-End Processing' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-4-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "17467" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 17467"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Perform AR Reconciliation and Compliance"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-4 ;
    skos:definition "Reconcile the receivables subledger to the general ledger each period, resolve reconciling items through their owning processes, maintain the evidence that the O2C financial controls operated as designed, and support internal and external audit with governed records."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.4.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "AR Reconciliation / Compliance"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Compliance in this process denotes evidence that prescribed O2C accounting controls operated as designed. It does not own enterprise regulatory compliance, Tax compliance, SOX design, Internal Audit assurance, or control-framework policy. Period reconciliation on daily foundations: the daily reconciled positions (b37) roll into period AR-to-GL reconciliation here; reconciling items are exception items with owners and aging (the standing discipline), resolved through their owning processes — never by plug or unexplained adjustment. Control design and the control framework are Finance's; independent assurance is Internal Audit's (the batch 30 distinction). Audit support supplies governed records and evidence; it makes no representations beyond them.
In scope: Period AR subledger-to-GL reconciliation | reconciling-item ownership, aging, and resolution routing | O2C financial-control operation evidence assembly | internal/external audit support with governed records
Out of scope: Enterprise regulatory compliance, Tax compliance, SOX design, and control-framework policy (Finance; Accountable compliance functions) | independent assurance (Internal Audit) | GL ownership (Finance) | reconciling-item fixes in owning processes (Their controls) | unexplained adjustments"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Daily reconciled positions (b37) | GL balances | control operation records | audit requests"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Completed period reconciliations with resolved or owned items and assembled control-operation evidence"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The subledger, the ledger, and the control evidence agree — and every difference has an owner."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Perform Reconciliations & Settlements | uses-input: Manage Unapplied Receipts / Exceptions | enables: Period End Processing"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 39 (2026-09-21). Review compliance-label clarification adopted verbatim — 'compliance' here means O2C accounting-control operation evidence only, preventing collision with the enterprise and commercial-agreement compliance clusters. Preferred label 'Perform AR Reconciliation and Compliance' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-4-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16463" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16463"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Analyze and Calculate Accruals"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-4 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze and calculate the O2C accruals each period under Finance-approved methodologies: rebate and discount accruals, returns and allowances, loyalty liabilities, unbilled revenue and costs, variable consideration, promotional and brand-fund obligations, royalty and fee liabilities, and freight and service charges where applicable — delivering calculated, evidenced accruals and true-ups into close."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.4.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Accruals Analysis And Calculation"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Accrual calculation distinguishes operational estimates, contractual obligations, Finance-approved assumptions, and accounting true-ups. It does not establish methodology, approve estimates, or make revenue-recognition conclusions. Calculation under authority: methodologies, estimates policy, and accounting treatment are Finance's; this process applies them to governed data — rebate arrangements and settlement history, loyalty program records, revenue variable consideration (b37 coordination), billing and fulfillment evidence — and never invents a methodology or books an unapproved estimate. True-ups reconcile accruals to actual settlements through controlled adjustments with full audit trails; systematic true-up bias routes to methodology review with Finance. Every accrual traces to its arrangement, data, and methodology.
In scope: Rebate, discount, returns, allowance, loyalty, unbilled, variable-consideration, fund, royalty/fee, and applicable freight/service accrual calculation per approved methodologies | operational-estimate vs contractual-obligation vs true-up distinction | settlement true-ups through controlled adjustments | methodology-input evidence and bias routing | delivery into close
Out of scope: Methodology and estimates policy, estimate approval, and revenue-recognition conclusions (Finance) | revenue recognition execution (CM-1-3-8-2-6) | rebate and program decisions (Their owners) | settlement execution (Their processes)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Arrangement terms and performance data | Finance methodologies | settlement actuals | program records"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Calculated, evidenced accruals and true-ups delivered into close"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every obligation the business has earned into existence is measured, evidenced, and on the books."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Settle Rebate | uses-input: Manage Brand Loyalty Programs | enables: Perform Revenue Accounting | enables: Period End Processing"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 39 (2026-09-21). Review accrual scope list and distinction sentence adopted verbatim. Preferred label 'Analyze and Calculate Accruals' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-4-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10881" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10881"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-4 ;
    skos:definition "Reconcile O2C indirect taxes each period and prepare their reporting: reconcile transaction tax lines and governed tax data to the amounts reportable and remittable by tax type and jurisdiction — excise, sales and use, VAT/GST as applicable — prepare returns and reports per the Tax function's direction, and support remittance through the authorized payment process."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.4.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Reconcile And Report Indirect Tax"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Reconcile and prepare, never file on own authority: filing and remittance obligations, forms, cadences, and positions are jurisdiction-dependent and determined by the Tax function; this process reconciles transaction-level tax data (b36 tax lines, b31 governed tax master data) to reportable amounts, investigates and resolves differences through the owning processes' controlled corrections, and hands remittance to the authorized payment process (payment-authority rule). Tax reconciliation distinguishes tax collected, tax accrued, tax payable, tax recoverable, exempt, refunded, credited, self-billed, and adjusted amounts according to Tax-approved treatment. Prepared tax reports and return support preserve source transaction lineage, jurisdiction/rate version, exemption/certificate evidence, adjustments, reconciliation differences, preparer/reviewer evidence, and Tax-owner approval. Federal fuel-excise context per the registered IRS Publication 510 source — federal scope only. Exemption-certificate and treatment discrepancies route to the compliance-side taxability process and Tax.
In scope: Period indirect-tax reconciliation by type and jurisdiction, distinguishing collected, accrued, payable, recoverable, exempt, refunded, credited, self-billed, and adjusted amounts per Tax-approved treatment | difference investigation and routing to controlled corrections | return and report preparation per Tax direction with preserved lineage, evidence, and preparer/reviewer/Tax-owner approvals | remittance support through the authorized payment process | reconciliation records for audit
Out of scope: Tax positions, filings ownership, rulings, and interpretations (Tax function) | remittance execution (Finance/Treasury; payment-authority rule) | transaction tax determination (CM-1-3-8-1-3) | tax master data changes (Governed submission; CM-1-3-7-1-4)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Transaction tax lines (b36) | governed tax master data (b31) | Tax function directions and calendars | remittance processes"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Reconciled indirect-tax positions and prepared, evidenced, Tax-approved returns and reports"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every tax dollar collected or owed is reconciled, prepared, and evidenced exactly as Tax directs."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-IRS-P510-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Determine Taxability | uses-input: Maintain Tax Master Data | enables: Period End Processing"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 39 (2026-09-21). Review tax amount-category and lineage/approval requirements adopted verbatim; SRC-IRS-P510-001 scoped to federal fuel-excise context only."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-4-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10196" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10196"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Determine Taxability for Tax Compliance and Reporting"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-4 ;
    skos:definition "Assess and prepare taxability-treatment recommendations for Tax approval, including new or changed product, channel, jurisdiction, exemption, and exception classes; evaluate reporting/remittance implications; and coordinate approved treatment changes into governed tax data and procedures."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.4.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Determine Taxability"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """The compliance-side half of the batch 36 split: the transaction-time process (CM-1-3-8-1-3) applies determinations per transaction; this process assesses treatment correctness at class level, resolves exception classes the transaction process holds and routes, and prepares treatment recommendations — its output is a Tax-approved treatment record, never an autonomous determination. Tax positions, law interpretations, rulings requests, and filings are Tax's, and no treatment takes effect except through Tax approval and governed tax master data submission (the batch 31 rule) — the relation to tax master data means exactly that: Tax-approved treatment changes submitted through governed procedures, nothing else. Treatment reviews run on event triggers — new products or channels, law changes as Tax interprets them, exception-class accumulations, audit findings — with periodic class-level reviews within.
In scope: Class-level taxability treatment assessment against Tax positions | treatment recommendations for Tax approval covering new/changed products, channels, jurisdictions, exemption and exception classes | exception-class resolution routed from transaction determination | reporting and remittance implication assessment | coordination of Tax-approved treatment changes into governed tax data and procedures
Out of scope: Tax positions, interpretations, rulings, and filings (Tax function) | autonomous treatment determinations | per-transaction determination (CM-1-3-8-1-3) | tax master data stewardship (CM-1-3-7-1-4) | treatment effect outside Tax approval and governed submission"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Tax positions and interpretations | exception classes and audit findings | product/channel/jurisdiction changes | governed tax data"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Tax-approved taxability treatment records flowing into governed tax master data with assessment evidence"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The treatments transactions are taxed by are current, correct, and Tax-approved — before the transactions happen."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Maintain Tax Master Data | uses-input: Reconcile And Report Indirect Tax | governed-by: Manage Accounting, Regulatory Reporting and Compliance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 39 (2026-09-21). Review recast adopted verbatim — this process assesses and prepares recommendations for Tax approval; its output is a Tax-approved treatment record. The batch 36 reserved label 'Determine Taxability for Tax Compliance and Reporting' recorded as alt label, completing the duplicate-name normalization queue. Review preferred an 'informs'/'requires: Tax approval' relation — 'informs' is vocabulary-missing and 'Tax approval' is not a taxonomy concept (b10/b23 precedent): recorded here verbatim, with the scope note carrying the Tax-approval gate and the enables-relation meaning governed submission of Tax-approved changes only."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-4-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-4 ;
    skos:definition "Execute intercompany invoicing between legal entities: invoice intercompany product transfers, services, and charges per the approved transfer-pricing policies and executed intercompany agreements, on the approved cycles, with the evidence and documentation the policies require, and support Finance's reconciliation, elimination, and consolidation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.4.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Intercompany Invoicing"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Execution per approved basis: transfer-pricing policy, arm's-length methodology, and the tax positions behind them are Tax's and Finance's; intercompany agreements are executed through the contracting and Legal processes; this process invoices exactly per the approved basis and agreements, prices nothing, and interprets nothing — pricing or treatment questions route to Tax/Finance. Intercompany invoices identify the legal entities, transaction type, product/service, transfer-pricing basis, tax treatment, currency, accounting period, agreement reference, and supporting evidence required by the approved intercompany policy. Intercompany billing differences are reconciled under approved Finance intercompany procedures; material tax, transfer-pricing, agreement, or accounting disagreements escalate to Finance, Tax, Legal, or the designated intercompany governance owner. Fact-dependent statement: arm's-length and intercompany documentation requirements under applicable tax law are jurisdiction-dependent and determined by Tax and Legal; invoicing and documentation follow their direction. Eliminations and consolidation are performed by Finance; this process supports them with reconciled balances and evidence.
In scope: Intercompany invoicing per approved transfer-pricing basis and executed agreements, identifying entities, transaction type, product/service, basis, tax treatment, currency, period, agreement reference, and required evidence | required documentation per Tax/Legal direction | bilateral balance reconciliation on approved cadence under Finance intercompany procedures | escalation of material disagreements to Finance, Tax, Legal, or the intercompany governance owner | consolidation and elimination support
Out of scope: Transfer-pricing policy and arm's-length methodology (Tax; Finance) | intercompany agreement terms (Contracting; Legal) | eliminations and consolidation performance (Finance) | tax positions and documentation standards (Tax; Legal)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved transfer-pricing policies | executed intercompany agreements | intercompany activity data | Finance/Tax direction"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Issued intercompany invoices with required documentation and reconciled balances"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Entities bill each other exactly per the approved basis, evidenced, reconciled, and ready to eliminate."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Manage Accounting, Regulatory Reporting and Compliance | enables: Period End Processing | uses-input: Manage Fulfillment"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 39 (2026-09-21). Review intercompany invoice-content and dispute-routing statements adopted verbatim; supports consolidation, never performs it."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-4-7 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16463" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16463"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Administer Royalty, Brand Fee, and Contribution Streams"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8-4 ;
    skos:definition "Administer the royalty, brand-fee, and contribution streams owed under franchise, license, and partner agreements: compute amounts due from validated reported sales and agreement terms, invoice them through the billing controls, track and support their collection through the receivables processes, apply audit-finding adjustments, and account for administered funds per the agreements and Finance's treatment."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.4.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Collection of Royalty and Fees"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """The batch 30 audit's receiving end: royalty and fee bases are validated reported sales — computation uses the figures the commercial audit validates, and audit-finding discrepancies adjust through controlled corrections per the batch 30 routing. Royalty, brand-fee, contribution, and fund-administration records distinguish amount earned, billed, collected, credited, disputed, held, due to third parties, and available for approved use. Fund usage and disbursement require agreement-based governance and Finance-authorized treatment. Where royalty, fee, or contribution obligations are contested, the process routes the matter to Commercial Agreement Compliance, Relationship Management, Collections/Disputes, Legal, or Finance according to whether the issue concerns reported-sales integrity, billing, collection, agreement interpretation, remedy, or accounting. Invoicing executes through the billing controls (b36), collection through the receivables machinery (b37/b38) — this process administers the stream and never bills or collects outside those controls; the payment-authority rule applies throughout. Fact-dependent statement: where a remedy would affect a franchise relationship governed by the Petroleum Marketing Practices Act, the determination is reserved to Legal.
In scope: Royalty, fee, and contribution computation from validated reported sales and agreement terms | invoicing through billing controls | collection tracking and support through receivables processes | audit-finding adjustment application through controlled corrections | stream records distinguishing earned, billed, collected, credited, disputed, held, due-to-third-party, and available-for-approved-use amounts | administered-fund accounting per agreements and Finance-authorized treatment | contested-obligation routing per issue type
Out of scope: Reported-sales validation (CM-1-3-6-8-1) | agreement terms, interpretation, and enforcement (Relationship management; Legal; PMPA fact-dependence for franchise remedies) | billing and collection execution (CM-1-3-8-1/-2/-3) | fund usage and disbursement decisions (Agreement-based governance; Finance) | funds movement (Finance/Treasury)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Validated reported sales (b30) | executed agreements and terms | audit findings | Finance treatment directions"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Computed, invoiced, tracked royalty and fee streams with audit-adjusted, agreement-compliant fund accounting"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every royalty and fee the agreements earn is computed from validated figures, billed, collected, and accounted — to the letter of the agreements."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-PMPA-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Conduct Audit To Validate Reported Sales | uses-input: Report Franchise Performance | enables: Create & Distribute Bill | enables: Period End Processing"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 39 (2026-09-21). Review administered-funds record distinctions and contested-obligation routing adopted verbatim. Preferred label 'Administer Royalty, Brand Fee, and Contribution Streams' recorded as alt label and queued — removes the ambiguity with the collections process; administration here, collection execution in CM-1-3-8-3."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-9-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19551" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19551"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-9-1 ;
    skos:definition "Define and maintain the service operating model. The model defines service scope, channels, operating hours, service tiers, escalation paths, roles, service-level design assumptions, and knowledge/procedure requirements. Contractually committed service levels require commercial-terms approval and cannot be created solely by the operating model. Approved through the accountable governance and published as controlled content."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.9.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Operating Model"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Blueprint, not decree (the batch 24 operating-model pattern): the model is developed with the owning functions and approved through the accountable governance; procedures publish as controlled documents. Contractually committed service levels flow only through the commercial-terms processes. Technology implications route to Manage Technology and IT; people implications to HR frameworks. Changes are event-driven with periodic review.
In scope: Service scope, channels, hours, tiers, escalation paths, roles, service-level design assumptions, and knowledge/procedure requirements | governance approval coordination | controlled publication | periodic model review
Out of scope: Contractual service-level commitments (Commercial terms processes) | employment frameworks (HR) | platform decisions (IT) | the operation itself (CM-1-3-9-1-2)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Service strategy and demand patterns | owning-function input (HR, IT, Facilities, EHS) | governance approvals"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The approved, published service operating model and its controlled procedures"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One approved, published model the whole service operation runs on."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Manage Operations | governed-by: Service & Support Enablement"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 40 (2026-09-21). Review model-content and commercial-terms-approval sentences adopted verbatim."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-9-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10734" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10734"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Service Operations"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-9-1 ;
    skos:definition "Run the service operation day to day: plan and schedule capacity against demand, balance workload across teams and channels, monitor operational quality and service-level attainment in real time, and manage operational incidents and escalations within the operating model."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.9.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Operations"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Operates the model, never redefines it (changes route to Define Operating Model). Operational incidents are triaged according to the service operating model. Product-quality, safety, environmental, security, fraud, regulatory, or major continuity incidents follow immediate escalation protocols and are not resolved solely through service operations. Demand signals come from the request capability and order/portal channels; capacity actions on people route through Manage Service Employees within HR frameworks; technology incidents route to IT per the batch 31 split. Operational metrics are evidence-layer (KPI Store boundary).
In scope: Capacity planning and scheduling against demand | workload balancing across teams and channels | real-time quality and service-level monitoring | operational incident triage per the operating model with immediate escalation of quality/safety/security/fraud/regulatory/continuity incidents to their protocols
Out of scope: Operating-model changes (CM-1-3-9-1-1) | substantive incident resolution for escalated categories (Quality; EHS; Security; their protocols) | employment actions (HR) | technology operations (IT)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "The operating model | demand signals | workforce availability | operational telemetry"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "A running operation at service level with correctly routed incidents"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The service operation meets its levels every day, and the wrong incidents never stay in service hands."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Define Operating Model | uses-input: Manage Customer Requests and Inquiries | enables: Fulfill Service Event"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 40 (2026-09-21). Review incident-escalation sentence adopted verbatim. Preferred label 'Manage Service Operations' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-9-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20085" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20085"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-9-1 ;
    skos:definition "Manage the service workforce operationally: plan staffing against forecast demand, schedule and roster within applicable rules, maintain skills and readiness through training coordination, and support the workforce day to day — all within the HR-owned employment frameworks."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.9.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Service Employees"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Service workforce planning may identify workload, skill, coverage, shift, queue, capacity, and training needs. It does not establish employment status, job classification, compensation, individual performance rating, promotion, disciplinary action, employee relations treatment, or employment termination. Scheduling honors applicable working-time rules as HR/Legal direct. Training content follows the batch 35 content-authority rule (delivery from approved content). Where service is delivered by third parties, workforce oversight is limited to contractually authorized service-capability, qualification, and performance requirements; employment terms remain with the provider. Employee personal data is governed under the enterprise personal-data governance framework.
In scope: Staffing plans against forecast demand | scheduling and rostering within applicable rules | skills and readiness development from approved content | day-to-day workforce support | contractually authorized capability/qualification/performance oversight of third-party service workers
Out of scope: Employment status, classification, compensation, ratings, promotion, discipline, employee relations, termination (HR; line management under HR authority) | third-party employment terms (The provider) | safety/regulatory training authority (Accountable content frameworks)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Demand forecasts | HR frameworks and working-time rules | approved training content | provider contracts"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "A staffed, scheduled, ready service workforce within HR frameworks"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The right people, ready and scheduled — without ever borrowing HR's authority."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage Operations | governed-by: Service & Support Enablement"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 40 (2026-09-21). Review workforce-planning boundary and third-party worker condition adopted verbatim."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-9-1-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10949" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10949"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-9-1 ;
    skos:definition "Manage service locations from the occupant side: define space and workplace needs, coordinate readiness, upkeep requests, and moves with the corporate facilities owners, and coordinate safety and access compliance with EHS and Security at service sites."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.9.1.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Facilities"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Occupant, not landlord: Service Facilities management coordinates occupancy readiness, layout/workplace requirements, equipment needs, site-access requirements, and operational fitness. It does not approve leases, capital projects, construction, landlord contracts, building maintenance, or safety standards. Facility readiness findings that indicate safety, accessibility, security, environmental, or emergency concerns are escalated to the accountable EHS, Security, Facilities, or Operations owner. This process states needs, coordinates, and verifies fitness for service operations — it contracts and builds nothing.
In scope: Occupancy readiness coordination | layout/workplace and equipment requirements | site-access requirements coordination with Security | operational-fitness verification | escalation of safety, accessibility, security, environmental, and emergency findings to their owners
Out of scope: Leases, capital projects, construction, landlord contracts, building maintenance (Corporate facilities/Real Estate) | safety standards (EHS) | access-control standards (Security)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Operating model and capacity plans | corporate facilities services | EHS and Security requirements"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Service-ready locations with coordinated, escalated findings"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every service site fit for the operation — with the owners owning what's theirs."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Service & Support Enablement | enables: Manage Operations"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 40 (2026-09-21). Review occupant-side boundary and safety-escalation sentences adopted verbatim."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-9-1-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20607" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20607"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-9-1 ;
    skos:definition "Own the service operation's technology from the business side: define requirements and business configuration for service platforms (case, telephony, knowledge, workforce tools), drive adoption and fit-for-purpose use, and feed the service capability roadmap — with IT owning the platforms themselves."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.9.1.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Technology"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Service Enablement owns business requirements, approved business configuration, workflow/knowledge requirements, adoption, and service-roadmap input. IT owns application/platform administration, identity/security, integration operation, release/deployment, technical monitoring, recovery, backup, and technical incident management. Business requirements for knowledge, case, service, telephony, workforce, and self-service tools must comply with approved data, privacy, retention, security, and integration controls. Configuration changes follow the governed change controls; workflow-authority content follows the batch 31 workflow rule (configurations implement approved authority, never create it).
In scope: Business requirements and approved business configuration for service platforms | workflow/knowledge requirements under approved data, privacy, retention, security, and integration controls | adoption and fit-for-purpose management | service-roadmap input
Out of scope: Application/platform administration, identity/security, integration operation, release/deployment, technical monitoring, recovery, backup, technical incident management (IT) | authority creation via configuration (DoA governance owners) | data/privacy control standards (Their owners)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "The operating model | user and process needs | IT services and controls | governed change controls"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Fit-for-purpose, adopted service technology with governed business configuration"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Service technology that fits the operation — with IT's authority intact."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Define Operating Model | governed-by: Service & Support Enablement"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 40 (2026-09-21). Review Enablement/IT split and data-control compliance sentences adopted verbatim; batch 31 technical-controls distinction applied to service platforms."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-9-1-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20085" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20085"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-9-1 ;
    skos:definition "Operate the contact-center channel for commercial service: manage capacity, queues, and routing across voice and digital contact channels, run channel operations to the service levels, monitor interaction quality, and provide the channel through which the customer-request processes work."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.9.1.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Contact Center"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Channel, not case owner: the contact center is the channel the batch 35 request capability works through — the batch 32 channel rule applies (a channel entry point, never an alternate control path). Contact Center operations may manage workforce routing, queue configuration requirements, service-channel capacity, quality monitoring, and approved scripts/knowledge delivery. It does not own case decisions, credit decisions, refunds, order changes, complaint adjudication, consent, or legal determinations. Contact-center channel design may include accessibility, language, communication preference, emergency escalation, and vulnerable-customer requirements where approved by the accountable Legal/Compliance/Customer Experience owners. Telephony/platform operations are IT's per the batch 31 split. Fact-dependent statement: Where voice, chat, video, screen, or other customer interactions are recorded, monitored, transcribed, or analyzed, applicable notice, consent, retention, monitoring, biometric, labor, privacy, and jurisdictional requirements are determined by Legal, Privacy, HR, and Compliance. Contact-center operations implement the approved controls. Interaction personal data is governed under the enterprise personal-data governance framework.
In scope: Channel capacity, queue, and routing management | workforce routing and queue configuration requirements | channel operations to service levels | interaction quality monitoring | approved scripts and knowledge delivery | approved accessibility, language, preference, emergency, and vulnerable-customer requirements
Out of scope: Case decisions, credit decisions, refunds, order changes, complaint adjudication, consent, legal determinations (Their owning processes) | telephony/platform operations (IT) | recording/monitoring requirement determination (Legal; Privacy; HR; Compliance)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "The operating model and service levels | workforce schedules | approved scripts/knowledge | Legal/Privacy-approved recording controls"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "An operating contact channel at service level with quality-monitored, compliant interactions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A channel that always answers — and never decides."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Process Inquiries and Requests | uses-input: Manage Operations"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 40 (2026-09-21). Review channel does-not-own list, accessibility/vulnerable-customer sentence, and refined recording/monitoring statement adopted verbatim."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-9-2-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11749" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11749"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Service Delivery Data"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-9-2 ;
    skos:definition "Maintain the operational data service delivery runs on: entitlement and coverage data, installed-base and service-history records, appointments, service tasks, and service-event data — captured and verified at delivery time, linked to the governed case and customer records."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.9.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Data"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Five data distinctions apply: master data belongs to the Master Data processes (governed Party, Account, Product, Price, Tax, Location); case data to Customer Requests and Inquiries (the issue/request record and communication loop); service delivery data to this capability (entitlements, service tasks, service events, appointments, installed base, completion evidence, service-history facts); operational/physical asset data to the relevant Operations/Engineering/Asset/Terminal processes; and billing evidence is consumed by Billing where the service is billable. The case record is stewarded by the request capability (b35) and referenced, not duplicated (the incident-linkage pattern). This process keeps delivery-operational records accurate, linked, and consumable. Personal data handled here is governed under the enterprise personal-data governance framework, with collection, use, retention, and disclosure limited to what the framework and applicable law permit. Retention per the records-management policy.
In scope: Entitlement/coverage, installed-base, service-history, appointment, task, and event data stewardship | delivery-time capture and verification | linkage to governed case and customer records | quality and retention control
Out of scope: Master data (CM-1-3-7-1; governed submission) | the case record (CM-1-3-7-5-2) | operational/physical asset data (Operations/Engineering/Asset/Terminal processes) | billing execution (CM-1-3-8-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Delivery-time captures | governed customer/case references | entitlement sources | records policy"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Accurate, linked service delivery records consumable by fulfillment, the case, and billing evidence"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Delivery always runs on accurate, linked, governed operational data."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Fulfill Service Event | uses-input: Maintain Customer Master Data"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 40 (2026-09-21). Review five-way data distinction adopted verbatim. Preferred label 'Manage Service Delivery Data' recorded as alt label and queued — disambiguates the generic locked name."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-9-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20085" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20085"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Customer Interaction in Service Delivery"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-9-2 ;
    skos:definition "Manage the customer interaction during service delivery: identify and verify the customer per the Security-defined controls, set expectations from confirmed information, keep the customer informed through service notices during the event, and confirm completion."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.9.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Customer"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Interaction, not relationship or case: account relationships belong to the commercial processes, the case loop to the request capability (b35). Service delivery interactions may communicate confirmed appointment, entitlement, scope, progress, service completion, and next-step information. They do not promise price, compensation, credit, product quality, tax, legal, regulatory, safety, or contract outcomes unless confirmed by the owning process. Verification per Security-defined identity controls; communications are service notices under the consent rules.
In scope: Customer identification and verification per Security controls | expectation-setting from confirmed appointment, entitlement, scope, progress, completion, and next-step information | service notices during the event | completion confirmation
Out of scope: Promises of price, compensation, credit, product quality, tax, legal, regulatory, safety, or contract outcomes (Owning processes confirm) | the case loop (CM-1-3-7-5) | account relationship management (Commercial processes) | consent creation (Consent framework)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Routed events and confirmed owner information | Security identity controls | service-notice channels"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Verified, informed customers through delivery with confirmed completion"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The customer always knows what's confirmed — and never hears a promise nobody owns."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Process Inquiries and Requests | enables: Fulfill Service Event"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 40 (2026-09-21). Review confirmed-information rule adopted verbatim (the governed-answer rule, delivery side). Preferred label 'Manage Customer Interaction in Service Delivery' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-9-2-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10218" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10218"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Fulfill Service Events"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-9-2 ;
    skos:definition "Fulfill individual service events end to end: receive the routed event with its case linkage, verify entitlement from governed delivery data, execute the service work per the applicable agreement and procedures, capture completion evidence, and return the outcome to the case and — where billable — to billing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.9.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fulfill Service Event"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Execution within everyone's authority: parts, logistics, and third-party service partners engage through their owning processes and executed agreements (the no-implied-obligations rule); safety-relevant findings escalate immediately per the batch 35 safety rule. Service events close with an explicit outcome status: completed, partially completed, unable to perform, rescheduled, cancelled, requires escalation, or requires follow-on event — each classified billable or non-billable. A billable service event is invoiced only from approved service-completion evidence, entitlement/contract terms, applicable pricing/tax data, and any required customer acceptance or service confirmation. Service Delivery does not create prices, credits, tax outcomes, or invoices. Failed or partial events re-route through the case, never silently closed. One customer request may produce zero, one, or many service events. Each service event is linked to the originating case where applicable, but the service event is not itself a new customer-request case. Completion evidence returns to the case record and, where billable, to Billing.
In scope: Event receipt with case linkage | entitlement verification from governed data | service execution per agreements and approved procedures | explicit outcome-status closure (completed, partially completed, unable to perform, rescheduled, cancelled, requires escalation, requires follow-on event; billable/non-billable) | completion evidence capture | outcome return to the case and billing
Out of scope: Case ownership (CM-1-3-7-5) | pricing, credits, tax, invoicing (Their owners; CM-1-3-8-1) | partner terms (Contract management; Legal) | safety/quality determinations (EHS; Quality; their protocols)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Routed events with case linkage | governed delivery data and entitlements | approved procedures | partner agreements"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Fulfilled events with explicit outcome statuses and billing-grade completion evidence"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every routed event ends in an explicit, evidenced outcome — back on the case, and billed only from evidence."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Manage Customer | enables: Create & Distribute Bill | enables: Process Inquiries and Requests"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 40 (2026-09-21). Review outcome-status taxonomy and billable-evidence rule adopted verbatim. Preferred label 'Fulfill Service Events' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-9-3-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20920" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20920"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-9-3 ;
    skos:definition "Measure the performance of the service infrastructure from the business perspective: facilities fitness, technology and platform performance, and contact-channel performance — as evidence for the accountable owners."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.9.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Measure Service Infrastructure"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Business-side measurement: IT's own platform monitoring (b31) is IT's; this measures service-business outcomes of the infrastructure and routes findings to Manage Technology, Manage Facilities, Manage Contact Center, and their corporate counterparts. Service availability measurement distinguishes platform/system availability, contact channel availability, facility readiness, service capacity, integration/data availability, and knowledge availability. Evidence-layer; KPI Store.
In scope: Business-side measurement of facilities fitness, platform performance, and channel performance | availability measurement distinguishing platform/system, contact channel, facility readiness, service capacity, integration/data, and knowledge availability | findings routing to accountable owners
Out of scope: Technical platform monitoring (IT) | infrastructure decisions (Their owners) | KPI publication (KPI Store governance)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Channel, platform, and facility performance data | operating-model targets"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Evidence-layer infrastructure performance findings routed to owners"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The machinery's real performance, measured where the business feels it."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Manage Technology | enables: Manage Contact Center"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 40 (2026-09-21). Review availability-distinction sentence adopted verbatim; business outcomes measured here, never implying IT ownership of them."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-9-3-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10218" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10218"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Measure Service Workforce Performance"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-9-3 ;
    skos:definition "Measure workforce operational performance in the service operation — handle times, quality scores, utilization, schedule adherence, readiness — as evidence for capacity planning, training, and service improvement."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.9.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Measure Service Employees"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """The HR boundary, measurement side: individual performance evaluation, ratings, compensation decisions, and discipline are HR's and line management's under HR frameworks; this process's outputs inform them through those frameworks and never substitute for them. Service workforce metrics may be reported at team, queue, role, or aggregate level for capacity and improvement. Use of individual-level data for performance appraisal, compensation, discipline, surveillance, or employment action requires HR-approved process, lawful basis, privacy controls, transparency, and applicable labor/works-council controls. Employee personal data is governed under the enterprise personal-data governance framework exactly as customer data is. Evidence-layer; KPI Store.
In scope: Team, queue, role, and aggregate-level workforce operational metrics for capacity and improvement | quality and adherence measurement per approved designs | evidence to workforce planning and training
Out of scope: Individual performance appraisal, compensation, discipline, surveillance, employment actions (HR-approved processes with lawful basis, privacy, transparency, and labor/works-council controls) | measurement-design authority over HR data (HR; Privacy) | KPI publication (KPI Store governance)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Operational workforce data | approved measurement designs | HR/privacy requirements"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Aggregate workforce performance evidence for capacity, training, and improvement"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Operational workforce evidence for improvement — never a backdoor appraisal system."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Manage Service Employees | uses-input: Manage Operations"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 40 (2026-09-21). Review individual-level data rule adopted verbatim — sensitive contact-center productivity data handled deliberately. Preferred label 'Measure Service Workforce Performance' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-9-3-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11077" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11077"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-9-3 ;
    skos:definition "Measure overall service effectiveness: resolution outcomes, service-level attainment, customer satisfaction and effort through governed measurement, and cost-effectiveness per Finance-approved methods — informing the operating model and the accountable owners."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.9.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Measure Service Effectiveness"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Customer satisfaction/effort, resolution quality, repeat-contact rate, first-contact resolution, fulfillment timeliness, service-level attainment, and cost-effectiveness must be defined under approved measurement frameworks. They may be candidate KPIs but become enterprise KPIs only through KPI Store governance. Formal customer satisfaction, effort, quality, or experience research follows approved research design, sampling, privacy, consent, and analysis controls; case follow-up may supply one input but cannot substitute for a valid measurement program. Cost-effectiveness applies Finance-approved costing methods (the batch 32 profitability pattern — applied, never redefined). Findings inform Define Operating Model and the owning processes; nothing is decided here. Evidence-layer; KPI Store.
In scope: Effectiveness measurement under approved frameworks: satisfaction/effort, resolution quality, repeat-contact, first-contact resolution, timeliness, service-level attainment, cost-effectiveness | governed research design, sampling, privacy, consent, and analysis controls | insight delivery to the operating model and owners
Out of scope: Service, staffing, and model decisions (Their owners) | costing method definition (Finance) | replacing governed research with case feedback (CM-1-3-7-5-4 supplies one input) | enterprise KPI publication (KPI Store governance)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Case and event outcomes | governed research responses | Finance-approved cost methods | service-level data"@en ;
    intake:level "L5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed effectiveness findings informing the operating model and owners"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Whether service actually works, for customers and the business — measured properly."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Perform Customer Follow up | enables: Define Operating Model"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 40 (2026-09-21). Review metric-definition and formal-research control sentences adopted verbatim — the governed side of the batch 35 distinction."@en .

proc:CM-1-4 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1 ;
    skos:definition "Information Technology Includes Processes Related To Providing IT Support Across Commercial Operations. It Covers Strategy, Delivery & Governance Processes. Commercial Information Technology Includes Hardware, Software & Communication Infrastructure."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "IT Services"@en ;
    intake:level "L2"@en .

proc:L1-ehs-gov-reporting a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10522" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10522"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:L0-enabling-functions ;
    skos:definition "Protect people, assets, and the environment through process safety; occupational health and safety; environmental management and compliance; emergency preparedness and response; incident, release, and near-miss management; corrective-action learning; and required regulatory reporting to government authorities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:prefLabel "EHS & Gov Reporting"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "This domain governs EHS management, environmental and safety compliance, required regulator-facing reporting, and the enterprise coordination of incidents, releases, investigations, corrective actions, and emergency response. It does not replace operational ownership: operating sites, asset owners, maintenance, logistics, product stewardship, and business functions remain accountable for safely executing their work, preventing and containing events, and completing assigned corrective actions. Legal & Corporate Communications advises on legal privilege, enforcement exposure, disclosure, litigation hold, regulator strategy, and approved internal/external communications when those dimensions arise."@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REJECTED"@en ;
    intake:level "L1"@en .

proc:L1-finance a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10922" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10922"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:L0-enabling-functions ;
    skos:definition "Planning, recording, controlling, reporting, and managing the financial resources, performance, obligations, and risks of the downstream business, including financial planning and analysis, accounting, reporting, payroll, treasury, tax, and internal financial controls."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:prefLabel "Finance"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Includes budgeting, forecasting, financial and management accounting, period close, statutory and management reporting, accounts payable, expense reimbursement, payroll, treasury, liquidity and cash management, tax, financial compliance, internal controls, audit support, and financial risk management. Supports commercial, supply-chain, refining, midstream, distribution, and enabling-function processes through financial governance, measurement, and transaction processing. Excludes operational execution in those business domains and fixed-asset project accounting where it is intentionally governed and executed as an ERP-native capability outside this taxonomy's modeled scope."@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:level "L1"@en .

proc:L1-human-resources a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20958" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20958"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:L0-enabling-functions ;
    skos:definition "The function that plans, attracts, develops, rewards, engages, retains, and transitions the workforce to enable the downstream business, including workforce planning, talent acquisition, onboarding, learning and development, performance management, compensation and benefits, employee and labor relations, HR policy and compliance, workforce information, and HR service delivery."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:prefLabel "Human Resources"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Includes workforce strategy and planning; organizational and position management; recruiting, sourcing, selection, hiring, and onboarding; learning, development, performance, talent, succession, and career management; compensation, benefits, recognition, and payroll-input administration; employee and labor relations; workforce policy, employment compliance, ethics-related workforce processes, and employee communications; workforce data, HR information systems, people analytics, and HR service delivery; and employee transition, redeployment, separation, and retirement. Human Resources owns workforce policies, people processes, employee experience, and workforce information. It provides authorized employee, time, attendance, compensation, and deduction inputs to payroll; Finance or a designated payroll service owns pay calculation, payment execution, withholding/remittance, payroll accounting, and financial control."@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REJECTED"@en ;
    intake:level "L1"@en .

proc:L1-legal-corp-comm a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "18948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 18948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:L0-enabling-functions ;
    skos:definition "Provide legal counsel and legal-risk management, including contract and transaction support, litigation and dispute resolution, regulatory and compliance advice, corporate governance and entity support, and management of external legal providers; develop and deliver internal corporate communications and approved enterprise messaging."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:prefLabel "Legal & Corp Comm"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "In scope: general counsel and legal advice to management and functions; legal review and support for contracts, transactions, claims, disputes, investigations, and litigation; regulatory interpretation and legal/compliance advice; corporate governance, entity management, board/committee administration where retained in the function, and corporate records; intellectual-property and outside-counsel management where not separate domains; employee, leadership, change, policy, crisis, and enterprise-wide internal communications; approval, stewardship, and consistency of official corporate messages. Out of scope (Step 8 decision): government relations, lobbying, and industry-association engagement; investor relations, shareholder engagement, and external board relations; public relations, media relations, community relations, philanthropy, and external reputation campaigns; distributor incentives, trade discounts, channel allowances, and other commercial-program design."@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REJECTED"@en ;
    intake:level "L1"@en .

proc:L1-midstream a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:L0-downstream-operations ;
    skos:definition "The transportation, terminaling, storage, and bulk handling of crude oil, intermediates, and refined products between supply points, refineries, terminals, and downstream distribution locations."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:prefLabel "Midstream"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Includes receipt, dispatch, transfer, pipeline, marine, rail, and bulk-truck logistics; terminal and tank-farm operations; inventory management; blending and additive operations where performed outside refinery process units; product segregation; custody transfer; nominations; scheduling; and bulk delivery to downstream depots, customers, or other facilities."@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:level "L1"@en .

proc:L1-process-excellence-it a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20094" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20094"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:L0-enabling-functions ;
    skos:definition "The enabling function that drives operational excellence through process governance, standardization, performance management, continuous improvement, and transformation; and enables the enterprise through technology strategy, architecture, platforms, infrastructure, cybersecurity, data and integration, and digital capabilities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:prefLabel "Process Excellence & IT"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Includes enterprise process architecture and governance; process ownership, documentation, standardization, measurement, benchmarking, and maturity assessment; continuous improvement, lean/six-sigma methods, automation, and business transformation; enterprise and solution architecture; portfolio, product, project, and demand management; application, data, integration, cloud, infrastructure, network, end-user, and IT service management; cybersecurity, technology risk, resilience, and disaster recovery; and delivery of digital, analytics, AI, and automation capabilities. Enables and governs cross-functional ways of working and enterprise technology. It does not own the business outcomes or physical execution of refining, midstream, distribution, commercial, finance, HR, legal, or EHS processes, except where a specifically delegated enterprise service is modeled as such."@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REJECTED"@en ;
    intake:level "L1"@en .

proc:L1-refining a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:L0-downstream-operations ;
    skos:definition "The operation of petroleum refineries to convert crude oil and other hydrocarbon feedstocks into specification-compliant fuels, blendstocks, lubricants, asphalt, petrochemical feedstocks, and other refined products through separation, conversion, treatment, blending, and associated refinery operations."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:prefLabel "Refining"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Encompasses refinery production operations and the planning, execution, and recovery activities required to maintain refinery assets, reliability, integrity, safety, and operating availability, including major maintenance and turnarounds."@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:level "L1"@en .

proc:L1-shared-services a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19951" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19951"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:L0-enabling-functions ;
    skos:definition "An enterprise service-delivery model that consolidates common, repeatable business-support services for multiple business units, legal entities, or regions through a shared organization, standardized processes, common platforms, and defined service levels."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shared Services"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Delivers selected operational and transactional services on behalf of enterprise functions and business domains, including finance operations, HR operations, payroll administration, procurement operations, IT service delivery, master-data administration, customer and supplier support, and other standardized administrative services. Services may be delivered centrally, regionally, or virtually and are governed through service catalogs, service levels, performance measures, and, where applicable, chargeback or cost-allocation arrangements."@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:level "L1"@en .

proc:L1-supply-chain-mgmt a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10215" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10215"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:L0-enabling-functions ;
    skos:definition "The integrated planning, coordination, and optimization of feedstock, intermediate, product, inventory, capacity, logistics, and information flows across the downstream petroleum value chain — from supply and sourcing through refining, terminaling, distribution, and customer fulfilment."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:prefLabel "Supply Chain Mgmt."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Includes demand and supply balancing; sourcing and replenishment planning; production and refinery supply planning; inventory and working-capital optimization; network, terminal, and distribution planning; transportation and delivery planning; materials management; supplier and carrier coordination; performance management; and supply-chain risk and disruption management. It orchestrates, but does not replace, the physical execution performed by refining, midstream, distribution, procurement, and commercial operations."@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:level "L1"@en .

