    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Product distribution management involves managing product and transportation agreements, terminal operations."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Products Distribution Management"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-3-2-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10103" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10103"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-2 ;
    skos:definition "Develop and maintain the approved strategic channel-of-trade direction for downstream sales and distribution — which routes to market the company participates in, their strategic roles, and the target channel mix — through a recurring cycle of channel diagnostics and hypothesis-driven strategy development under the applicable enterprise approvals, so channel design and channel management execute a deliberate, evidenced, approved direction."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.2.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sales & Distribution Channel Strategy"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the channel-strategy lifecycle: conducting channel-of-trade diagnostics (CM-1-3-2-4-1) and establishing channel operating objectives and the channel-strategy hypothesis (CM-1-3-2-4-2), with the approved strategy developed and maintained at this L4. Channels of trade span company-operated retail, branded dealer, branded marketer/jobber and distributor, unbranded rack and wholesale, and direct commercial channels. These are illustrative external channel-of-trade labels. The enterprise's approved commercial/channel taxonomy governs internal classification. EIA's DTW/rack/retail categories are external statistical context only, per the SRC-EIA-PMM-001 limitations. Marketing Strategy develops and maintains approved brand, value-proposition, channel, and customer-experience direction using governed evidence from Marketing Analysis. It does not perform the analyses; design detailed offers, prices, channels, networks, communications, or operating models; plan campaigns and sales; or execute channel, sales, brand, loyalty, or service programs. Material strategic changes follow the applicable enterprise delegation, investment, legal, and governance approvals. Where an arrangement meets the applicable statutory and contractual criteria for a branded motor-fuel franchise, changes to trademark, supply, lease, renewal, termination, or non-renewal arrangements may be constrained by the PMPA and applicable state law. Legal classification, interpretation, notice requirements, termination/non-renewal analysis, and franchise documentation remain the responsibility of Legal & Corporate Communications. Strategy may set the strategic role of price in the proposition or channel — such as value, premium, parity, transparency, index linkage, or loyalty emphasis — but does not establish pricing strategy, price architecture, price rules, price levels, discounts, formula terms, or transaction prices. Those activities belong to Pricing Strategy Management and Sales Pricing. Strategy only: detailed channel design at CM-1-3-3-3, retail network design at CM-1-3-3-4, partner management at CM-1-3-6-1, sales strategy and planning at CM-1-3-5-1. Excludes channel design (CM-1-3-3-3); retail network design (CM-1-3-3-4); channel/partner management (CM-1-3-6-1); sales planning (CM-1-3-5-1); secondary distribution operations (CM-1-1-3-8, SCM-vs-Commercial locked rule).
In scope: Run the recurring channel-strategy cycle | develop and maintain approved channel-of-trade participation, roles, and target mix | recommend and maintain approved direction for channel investment, expansion, de-emphasis, or exit, subject to delegated investment, legal, franchise, and corporate approvals | issue channel direction to design, planning, and management
Out of scope: Channel design (CM-1-3-3-3) | retail network design (CM-1-3-3-4) | channel/partner management (CM-1-3-6-1) | sales planning (CM-1-3-5-1) | secondary distribution operations (CM-1-1-3-8, locked rule) | pricing strategy, rules, and levels (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Channel diagnostics (CM-1-3-2-4-1) | customer/marketer findings (CM-1-3-1-4) | cost-to-serve assessments (CM-1-2-6-1-3) | value propositions (CM-1-3-2-2/-3)"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The approved channel-of-trade strategy and target mix"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every route to market chosen on purpose, with evidence and the right approvals."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "strategic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-PMM-001 | SRC-PMPA-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Insight and Metrics | enables: Sales & Distribution Channel Design | enables: Channel & Partner Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 22 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one diagnostics-to-strategy lifecycle. Lifecycle order: Conduct Channel of trade diagnostics → Establish operating Objectives and develop Channel Strategy Hypothesis → approved strategy developed and maintained at this L4. Channel-of-trade vocabulary is illustrative (review caveat verbatim in scope note); enterprise taxonomy governs. Investment-authority wording per review: recommend and maintain approved direction, subject to delegated approvals — material for retail-network acquisitions, divestitures, dealer conversions, and branded/unbranded transitions. PMPA applicability fact-dependent (verbatim in scope note). Batch 22 durable rule and price-role boundary recorded verbatim in scope note. APQC 'Develop sales strategy' is a WEAK adjacent match (sales planning is CM-1-3-5-1)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10123" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10123"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6 ;
    skos:definition "Apply the approved pricing architecture in the market — determining posted and transaction sales prices within the approved parameters, corridors, rules, and eligibility, and coordinating their deployment through the accountable master-data and system processes — on the daily and intraday cycles downstream fuel pricing runs on, never modifying the architecture itself."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sales Pricing Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers sales pricing execution: determining sales prices (CM-1-3-6-3-1) and implementing them (CM-1-3-6-3-2), recurring daily and intraday, feeding realization and performance evidence to Pricing Strategy Management's feedback loop. The receiving side of the batch 23 pricing governance: applies the approved price architecture, effective-dated price rules, differentials, index linkages, corridors, floors, ceilings, customer/channel eligibility, and delegated exception criteria developed at CM-1-3-3-2 under its commercial, Finance, Legal, Compliance, and delegation-of-authority approvals; exceptions follow the approved criteria and authorities; architecture feedback flows to CM-1-3-3-2, never changed here. The legal guardrails apply in execution: the batch 23 fact-dependent Robinson-Patman statement and the standardized competition-information rule verbatim by reference — pricing situations with discrimination, below-cost, or unfair-sales risk escalate to Legal/Compliance, who determine applicability and sufficiency. Quote and contract pricing configuration is CM-1-3-7-2's, applying the same architecture transactionally; rebates at CM-1-3-6-4; price master data with the accountable Price Master Data process (CM-1-3-7-1); physical price display, pump-sign configuration, and legal metrology with site operations and EHS/regulatory processes (b24). Excludes pricing architecture, parameters, and rules (CM-1-3-3-2); quote/contract pricing configuration (CM-1-3-7-2); rebates (CM-1-3-6-4); price master-data ownership (CM-1-3-7-1); price display execution (site operations, b24); legal determinations (Legal, Compliance); trading/supply pricing (CM-1-2).
In scope: Determine posted and transaction prices within the approved architecture, eligibility, and delegated authority | coordinate deployment through the accountable master-data and system processes | route exceptions per the approved criteria | escalate legal-risk situations and feed realization evidence back
Out of scope: Pricing architecture/parameters/rules (CM-1-3-3-2) | quote/contract pricing (CM-1-3-7-2) | rebates (CM-1-3-6-4) | price master-data ownership (CM-1-3-7-1) | price display execution (site operations, b24) | legal determinations (Legal, Compliance) | trading/supply pricing (CM-1-2)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved architecture, rules, and eligibility (CM-1-3-3-2) | permitted market and cost inputs | exception criteria and authorities"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Determined, deployed sales prices within the approved architecture"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every posted and transaction price inside the approved system, every day."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FTC-RP-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Pricing Strategy Management | enables: Sales Execution | enables: Analyze Pricing Performance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 28 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — the daily pricing-execution cycle; horizon daily (execution runs daily and intraday; daily is the controlled taxonomy value, per review). Cycle: Determine Sales Price → coordinate price deployment → verify deployment/audit trail → feed realization evidence to Pricing Strategy Management. Applies, never modifies, the b23 architecture; RP and competition-information rules inherited verbatim by reference. APQC returned NONE at this L4."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-7 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20682" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20682"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Brand Standards Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6 ;
    skos:definition "The process of governing brand standards across the branded network: defining and maintaining the standards that govern brand presentation and customer experience at branded sites and channels, verifying conformance through inspection programs, and managing the brand imaging of sites and assets to those standards."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Brand Standard Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Standards-versus-rights boundary: brand assets — trademarks, trade dress, visual identity — are owned, registered, and legally protected under Manage Licensing & IP and the enterprise brand governance framework; this process defines, applies, monitors, and coordinates conformance to usage and presentation standards, makes no legal determinations, and grants no rights. Standards may cover visual identity, trade dress, site appearance, forecourt/store experience, signage principles, digital brand use, uniforms, collateral, partner use, and approved customer-experience manifestations. They do not establish safety, engineering, metrology, pricing, product-quality, regulatory-labeling, or legal requirements unless those requirements are incorporated by reference from their accountable owners. For licensed brands, the provider's standards are external constraints received through Manage Relationship with License Brand Providers and applied per the executed license agreements. Where standards conflict or appear ambiguous, the process escalates interpretation to the licensor and Legal; it does not certify provider-standard compliance or modify provider standards. Conformance follow-through against franchisees and dealers operates through the relationship processes and their agreements. Fact-dependent statement: where a remedy would affect a franchise relationship governed by the Petroleum Marketing Practices Act, the determination is reserved to Legal. Brand imaging plans may identify capital, maintenance, signage, reimage, equipment, digital-display, and site-work requirements. Investment authorities approve funding; Engineering/site operations/EHS execute physical work; Procurement contracts suppliers; Brand Standards verifies conformance. Downstream consumers of these standards include Ensure Card Design Compliance and marketing execution.
In scope: Define, maintain, and publish brand standards with change control | brand inspection programs | brand imaging governance | conformance monitoring and corrective-action coordination | conformance reporting to relationship owners
Out of scope: IP ownership, registration, legal protection, and rights enforcement (CM-1-3-4-4; Legal) | licensed-brand provider standards setting (providers; CM-1-3-6-1-2) | enforcement remedies under agreements (relationship management; Legal) | safety, engineering, metrology, pricing, product-quality, and regulatory-labeling requirements (their accountable owners) | site capital investment approval (investment authorities, b24) | advertising claim approval (b24 controls)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Brand governance framework and IP guidance (CM-1-3-4-4) | license agreements and provider standards | network and site data | inspection findings"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed brand standards with verified network conformance"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Consistent, protected brand presentation across the branded network."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-PMPA-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Manage Licensing & IP | uses-input: Manage Relationship with License Brand Providers | enables: Ensure Card Design Compliance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 30 (2026-09-20). Review conformance wording adopted verbatim — 'defines, applies, monitors, and coordinates conformance' replaces 'enforces'; technical-standards exclusion and imaging/investment operating-model statements adopted verbatim; b26 standards-vs-rights and b28 licensed-brand rules inherited. Normalized label 'Brand Standards Management' recorded as alt label and queued for the naming pass."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16820" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16820"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Network Product Scheduling"@en,
        "Short-Term Product Supply Scheduling"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Develop and maintain the short-term product supply schedule for the primary distribution network — deciding which products move between refineries, terminals, and supply points, in what quantities and sequence over the coming days and weeks — so the approved regional plan is translated into an executable movement program."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Product Scheduling"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers short-term network scheduling within the approved regional plan: consolidating demand, stock, and supply projections into a rolling network schedule, sequencing bulk product movements, coordinating the trades needed to balance the schedule, and adjusting to disruptions. Excludes plan-period supply and disposition decisions, owned by Regional Optimization (CM-1-1-2); excludes mode-level nomination, load, and movement administration, owned by Physical Distribution Scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6); excludes secondary deliveries, owned by Secondary Distribution Management (CM-1-1-3-8); excludes trade negotiation and execution, owned by Supply And Trading.
In scope: Consolidate short-term demand, stock, and supply projections | develop and maintain the rolling network product schedule | sequence and adjust bulk movements within plan and policy | initiate schedule-balancing trade requests
Out of scope: Plan-period supply and disposition optimization (Regional Optimization, CM-1-1-2) | nomination, load, and movement administration (CM-1-1-3-6) | secondary delivery scheduling (CM-1-1-3-8) | trade negotiation and execution (Supply And Trading)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "approved regional plan | stock positions and projections | short-term demand and lifting forecasts | transport and terminal availability"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "rolling short-term network product schedule"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Decide the short-term product movement schedule the primary network runs on."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ORP-DPSC-001 | SRC-LNL-T2S-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Regional Optimization | precedes: Physical Distribution Scheduling | uses-input: Monitor and Control Inventory Positions (CM-1-1-3-7-12)"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 05 (2026-09-19). Sits between the approved monthly regional plan (CM-1-1-2) and movement administration (CM-1-1-3-6): this L4 decides the short-term schedule; its sibling administers the movements it creates. APQC 16820 'Design for product servicing' is an unrelated lexical match; no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-4-4 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-4 ;
    skos:definition "Evaluate aligned scenarios for prompt month horizon"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.4.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Prompt Month Evaluations"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-4-6 a skos:Concept ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-4 ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.4.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Commercial Development"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:status "retired"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Retired per PTC-001 (step3c-parked-tree-changes.md), decided by Hamid 2026-09-18. The node carries no local meaning: its Step 3b provenance is a WEAK match against the APQC element “Manage employee development”, and under Option A all three of its children leave. Nothing remains to define, so it is retired rather than defined or left pending. The node itself is still present in downstream_process_map.json — removing it is a Step 3d tree change, not a workbook change. Retired here means “will not be defined”, not “gone from the tree”."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-5-9 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-5 ;
    skos:definition "Review notes and actions from key meetings and finalize backcasting report"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.5.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Distribute final report with agreed upon action plans"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "17042" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 17042"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Reconcile actual distribution inventory availability, movements, deliveries, transportation costs, and customer fulfillment outcomes against the approved distribution plan and its contemporaneous assumptions and constraints; quantify and explain variances; and use validated lessons to improve subsequent distribution planning and optimization cycles."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Distribution Backcasting"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Distribution Backcasting preserves and compares the approved distribution baseline, actual inventory availability and deployment, shipments, deliveries, transportation performance and cost, service outcomes, material constraints, decisions, and variance explanations for a completed period. It identifies commercially meaningful drivers — such as demand, supply availability, allocation, inventory, route or mode, freight, carrier capacity, terminal/rack availability, delivery reliability, and customer-service effects — and feeds validated findings into subsequent distribution planning. It does not dispatch equipment, operate terminals or transportation assets, conduct product movements, perform delivery, or replace Finance's freight-payment and financial-close processes."@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-7 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Refinery scheduling covering daily execution planning, production scheduling, and energy & utility management."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Refinery Scheduling"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-7-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19243" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19243"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Daily Refinery Scheduling"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-7 ;
    skos:definition "Translate the current production schedule into each day's refinery execution basis — updating the day's scheduling instructions and commitments, confirming the schedule-level resources the day depends on, and closing the daily production accounting balance — so the refinery works each day from a current, feasible, accounted-for scheduling basis."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.7.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Daily Execution Planning"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the daily schedule-to-execution cycle: issuing and updating the day's schedule-level instructions and commitments, assigning the schedule-level resources the day's plan assumes, and closing the daily production balance with actualized yields. Under the approved planning-scheduling-operations hierarchy, Refining owns unit-operation scheduling, tank and transfer line-ups, blend and process execution, and safe operational departures from the schedule; this process owns the daily schedule basis and its accounting. Excludes building the dated schedule itself, owned by Production Scheduling (CM-1-1-7-2); excludes the monthly plan (Refinery Planning, CM-1-1-4).
In scope: Issue and update daily schedule-level instructions and commitments | assign schedule-level resources for the day | close the daily production accounting balance | confirm daily feasibility of the issued basis
Out of scope: Unit-operation scheduling, line-ups, and execution (Refining) | dated schedule construction (CM-1-1-7-2) | monthly plan targets (CM-1-1-4)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "current production schedule | execution feedback and measured actuals | resource availability"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "issued daily scheduling basis with closed production balance"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Give the refinery a current, feasible, accounted-for daily scheduling basis."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-BJCE-REFPS-001 | SRC-SNF-REFPS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Production Scheduling | enables: Regional Backcasting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 06 (2026-09-19). Q1 decision (Hamid, 2026-09-19): the refinery planning-scheduling-operations hierarchy is approved. CM-1-1-7 owns the refinery production-scheduling layer: translating approved refinery plans into dated, feasible campaign, production, movement, and resource schedules; managing schedule exceptions and cross-functional interfaces; and maintaining schedule-level performance visibility. Refining owns detailed unit-operation scheduling and all operational execution, including process-control decisions, line-ups, transfers, blend execution, utility operation, and immediate safety/reliability departures from the schedule. This L4 is the daily rhythm of that scheduling layer. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-7-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20682" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20682"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Energy & Utilities Optimization"@en,
        "Refinery Energy Economics"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-7 ;
    skos:definition "Optimize energy, utility, and emissions-allowance economics within refinery planning and scheduling by setting sourcing strategies, economic targets, and allowance-position recommendations that inform the production schedule."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.7.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Energy & Utility Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers planning- and scheduling-level energy economics: evaluating internal generation versus purchased-energy options; incorporating energy, utility, and carbon costs into refinery planning and production scheduling; establishing economic operating targets and constraints for schedule development; maintaining an emissions-allowance and credit position; and preparing recommendations for allowance banking, purchase, or sale. Refining operates and controls utility systems, including boilers, cogeneration, steam, fuel-gas, power-distribution, and energy-recovery equipment. EHS & Government Reporting owns regulatory emissions monitoring, calculation, submission, and reporting under its locked rule. The relevant commercial or trading function owns energy contracting and allowance-trade negotiation and execution.
In scope: Evaluate internal generation versus purchased-energy options | incorporate energy, utility, and carbon costs into planning and scheduling | establish economic operating targets and constraints for schedule development | maintain the emissions-allowance and credit position and prepare banking, purchase, and sale recommendations
Out of scope: Utility system operation and control (Refining) | regulatory emissions monitoring and reporting (EHS & Government Reporting) | energy contracting and allowance-trade execution (commercial and trading owner) | financial accounting treatment of allowances and credits (Finance)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "energy prices and contract terms | utility system capabilities and constraints | production schedule and emissions position"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "energy sourcing strategy, economic targets, and allowance-position recommendations"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Minimize energy cost and carbon exposure from the planning side, within reliable operations."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM31-001 | SRC-EPA-EMTR-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Production Scheduling | enables: Daily Execution Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 06 (2026-09-19). Q2 decision (Hamid, Option A, narrowed): defined explicitly as the economic and scheduling layer of energy and utilities — the bare label could suggest ownership of utility systems, which belong to Refining. EHS reports (locked rule); the trading function executes; Finance records the accounting treatment of allowances and credits. Whether energy contracting is centralized or delegated to site commercial teams is a follow-up noted in the batch 06 PR. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Confirmations and Risk management consists of processes performed in the middle office to minimize risk of losses in the trading book due to change in market conditions i.e. commodity prices, interest rates, credit spreads, foreign-exchange rates. Middle office is responsible for managing confirmations, scenario modelling and price forecasting."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Confirmations & Risk Management"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-2-5-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10222" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10222"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Feedstock Demand Planning"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-5 ;
    skos:definition "Translate the refinery production plan into feedstock supply action — developing the feedstock demand signal, recommending the economically attractive feedstock slate and run rate to Refinery Planning, and coordinating the resulting requirements with the trading processes — so feedstock supply is planned against a validated demand and an economically informed recommendation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.5.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Crude/Feed Demand Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the feedstock demand cycle: developing projected feedstock requirements by grade, quality, quantity, and timing from the production plan (CM-1-2-5-2-2); recommending the economically attractive feedstock slate and run rate to Refinery Planning under PTC-002 (CM-1-2-5-2-3); and coordinating the resulting purchase, sale, and exchange requirements with the trading processes (CM-1-2-5-2-1). Under PTC-002 (Option 1, closed): Refinery Planning decides the binding slate and run rate; this cluster develops demand, recommends, and coordinates — it decides none of the plan. Trading decides the deal (batch 05 decide-versus-advise pattern). Excludes the binding production plan, slate, and run rate (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002); trade negotiation and execution (CM-1-2-1-3); crude evaluation (CM-1-1-4-7-2); procurement slate development (CM-1-2-5-1-3/-4).
In scope: Develop the feedstock demand signal from the production plan | recommend the economically attractive slate and run rate to Refinery Planning | coordinate resulting requirements with trading | keep demand, recommendation, and coordination consistent
Out of scope: Binding slate, run rate, and production plan (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002) | trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3) | crude evaluation (CM-1-1-4-7-2) | procurement slates (CM-1-2-5-1-3/-4)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Refinery production plan (CM-1-1-4) | procurement slates (CM-1-2-5-1-3/-4) | market conditions"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Feedstock demand, slate/run-rate recommendation, and coordinated trading requirements"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep feedstock supply planned against a validated demand and an economically informed, advisory recommendation."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CMU-REFPLAN-001 | SRC-ORP-DPSC-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Refinery Planning | enables: Trading Management | uses-input: Crude/Feed Quality Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one demand-to-coordination planning cycle (develop, recommend, coordinate), like the batch 03 Produce Demand Forecast precedent. PTC-002 honored at cluster level: this cluster advises and coordinates; Refinery Planning decides. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-5-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10269" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10269"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Feedstock Logistics Planning"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-5 ;
    skos:definition "Plan and manage the transportation of purchased crude and feedstocks to the refinery — coordinating movements across pipeline, marine, rail, and truck modes against the supply plan, scheduling deliveries into agreed refinery receipt windows, and planning arrivals with the refinery and terminal operations owners — so purchased feedstock arrives when, where, and how the refinery can receive it."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.5.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Crude/Feed Transportation Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers feedstock transportation planning: coordinating and monitoring movements for purchased volumes under the applicable carrier and capacity arrangements (CM-1-2-5-3-1); scheduling deliveries into agreed refinery receipt windows (CM-1-2-5-3-2); and planning arrival timing and sequence with the refinery and terminal operations owners (CM-1-2-5-3-3). Operates against carrier contracts administered under Manage Carrier Contracts (CM-1-2-1-2-9) and transportation deals captured under CM-1-2-1-3-9; movement actuals are recorded by Actualizations (CM-1-2-3-1); transportation charges settle under the mode settlement processes (CM-1-2-4-1); delay-compensation claims follow Demurrage Management (CM-1-2-4-4); physical movement execution belongs to carriers and operators. Excludes carrier contracting (CM-1-2-1-2-9); transportation deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-9); actualization (CM-1-2-3-1); freight settlement and demurrage (CM-1-2-4-1, CM-1-2-4-4); physical movement execution (carriers, operators); product distribution (CM-1-1-3, locked SCM boundary).
In scope: Coordinate feedstock movements across modes against the supply plan | schedule deliveries into agreed refinery receipt windows | plan arrivals with refinery and terminal operations owners | adjust movements as plans and conditions change
Out of scope: Carrier contracting (CM-1-2-1-2-9) | transportation deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-9) | actualization (CM-1-2-3-1) | freight settlement and demurrage (CM-1-2-4-1, CM-1-2-4-4) | physical execution (carriers, operators) | product distribution (CM-1-1-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Agreed purchases and supply plan (CM-1-2-5-2-1) | carrier terms and capacity (CM-1-2-1-2-9) | refinery receipt constraints and tankage positions"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Planned, scheduled feedstock movements and arrival plans"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Get purchased feedstock to the refinery gate at the right time, mode, and cost."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ORP-DPSC-001 | SRC-CMU-REFPLAN-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Coordinate Feedstock Trading | uses-input: Manage Carrier Contracts | precedes: Actualizations"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one per-movement transport-planning lifecycle (coordinate, schedule, plan arrival), the batch 13 variant test. Inbound-feedstock counterpart of the product-side distribution planning in CM-1-1-3 under the locked SCM boundary. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-6 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Refined product supply chain management consist of processes that are required to maintain an optimal supply network. This covers the generation of the demand forecast, associated customer allocations, the demand & supply balancing process & the execution of terminal replenishment."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Refined Product Supply Chain Management"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-2-6-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12899" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12899"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Supply Network Participation Analysis"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-6 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze the refined-product supply network's market participation — segmenting customers and markets on supply economics, evaluating market attractiveness for supply-network participation, and assessing cost-to-serve performance — and recommend where and whom the network should serve profitably, informing the network and participation decisions of the accountable commercial and planning owners."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.6.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Network Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the market-participation analysis cycle: supply-economics customer and market segmentation (CM-1-2-6-1-1), market attractiveness evaluation (CM-1-2-6-1-2), and cost-to-serve assessment (CM-1-2-6-1-3), combined into recommendations on network participation, service levels, and supply-envelope choices. Analyze-and-recommend (batch 11 pattern): supply, production, inventory, and allocation decisions are owned by Regional Optimization (CM-1-1-2, batch 03 rule); commercial and brand segmentation, pricing, and customer strategy by the Marketing processes (CM-1-3) where defined; network-investment decisions by their accountable owners. Excludes supply and allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2); commercial segmentation, pricing, and customer strategy (CM-1-3); network investment decisions (accountable owners); demand forecasting (CM-1-2-6-2-3, CM-1-1-1-1).
In scope: Segment customers and markets on supply economics | evaluate market attractiveness for supply-network participation | assess cost-to-serve performance | recommend network participation and service choices to accountable owners
Out of scope: Supply and allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2) | commercial segmentation, pricing, and customer strategy (CM-1-3) | network investment decisions (accountable owners) | demand forecasting (CM-1-2-6-2-3, CM-1-1-1-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Sales, volume, and margin history | supply and logistics cost data | market and competitor intelligence"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Network participation and service recommendations with segmentation and cost-to-serve evidence"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Know which markets and customers the supply network serves profitably — and recommend accordingly."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-LNL-T2S-001 | SRC-ORP-DPSC-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Regional Optimization | informed-by: Assess Cost-to-Serve Performance | enables: Refined Product Demand Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one periodic analysis cycle (segment, evaluate, assess, recommend). Q5 decision: supply-economics analysis, distinct from Marketing's commercial segmentation; analyze-and-recommend with decisions at the accountable owners. Normalized label 'Supply Network Participation Analysis' queued — the current label sounds like network design/operations ownership (Hamid, batch 19). APQC candidates are WEAK generic matches."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-6-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19697" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19697"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Operational Product Demand Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-6 ;
    skos:definition "Manage refined-product demand on the supply side — maintaining the operational demand picture the supply chain runs on, forecasting location-level product demand for supply operations, and managing product-exchange volumes as a balancing lever — so supply operations, replenishment, and trading coordination work from one current, operational demand view."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.6.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Refined Product Demand Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the operational demand cycle: managing the current demand picture against supply capability (CM-1-2-6-2-1), operational location-level demand forecasting for the supply-chain context (CM-1-2-6-2-3, under the batch 03 constraint), and operational management of product-exchange volumes as a supply and demand balancing lever (CM-1-2-6-2-2). The enterprise published demand forecast is owned by Produce Demand Forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) and consumed here as an input; trading's forward-view intake is Receive Demand Forecast (CM-1-2-1-1-2); the refinery-planning demand basis is Capture Product Demand Requirements (CM-1-1-4-7-1). Excludes the enterprise published forecast (CM-1-1-1-1, batch 03); trading forecast intake (CM-1-2-1-1-2); the refinery demand basis (CM-1-1-4-7-1); order capture and commitment (CM-1-3-7); supply and allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2).
In scope: Maintain the operational demand picture against supply capability | forecast location-level demand for supply operations | manage exchange volumes as a balancing lever | supply one operational demand view to supply operations and trading coordination
Out of scope: Enterprise published forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) | trading forecast intake (CM-1-2-1-1-2) | refinery demand basis (CM-1-1-4-7-1) | order capture (CM-1-3-7) | supply and allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Published enterprise forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) | orders and lifting patterns | exchange positions and entitlements | supply capability"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Operational demand picture, location-level forecasts, and exchange-utilization plans"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One current operational demand view behind supply operations, replenishment, and trading coordination."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-STEO-001 | SRC-ORP-DPSC-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Produce Demand Forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) | enables: Supply Operations | enables: Coordinate Product Trading"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one operational demand cycle. Batch 03 three-way forecast partition implemented: enterprise published forecast (CM-1-1-1-1), trading intake (CM-1-2-1-1-2), operational SCM-context forecasting here. APQC candidate is an unrelated documentation match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-6-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "S&T Product Supply Fulfillment"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-6 ;
    skos:definition "Fulfill approved refined-product replenishment and supply requirements from Supply & Trading sources by coordinating product availability at purchased-supply, exchange, transfer, and third-party source points; confirming feasible supply and transport arrangements; and handing approved movements to the processes that schedule, actualize, and settle them."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.6.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Supply Operations"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the S&T-sourced supply-fulfillment cycle under the Q2 (Option A) reading: coordinating commercial supply status and availability at source points where Supply And Trading supplies the network — purchased volumes, exchange locations, transfers, third-party terminals (CM-1-2-6-3-1); fulfilling network replenishment requirements from traded sources, consuming the replenishment decisions owned by Manage Inventory Replenishment (CM-1-1-3-7-9, batch 04) (CM-1-2-6-3-2); and coordinating transportation arrangements for S&T-sourced primary movements (CM-1-2-6-3-3). Supply Operations may identify feasible commercial sources and communicate fulfillment constraints, but it does not decide network replenishment quantity or timing, allocate constrained product, execute trades, administer primary movements, or direct physical operations. The complementarity rule: Physical Distribution Scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6, batch 05) owns primary-movement nominations and shipper-side administration; Secondary Distribution Management (CM-1-1-3-8) owns terminal-to-customer delivery; this cluster supplies the network from commercial sources and provides its movements to the owning scheduling, actualization, and settlement processes. Excludes replenishment decisions (CM-1-1-3-7-9, batch 04); allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2); primary movement scheduling and administration (CM-1-1-3-6, batch 05); secondary distribution (CM-1-1-3-8); trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3); exchange agreement administration (CM-1-2-7-1-1); physical terminal and transport operations (operators, carriers, Midstream).
In scope: Coordinate commercial supply status at S&T-supplied source points | fulfill approved replenishment requirements from traded, exchanged, and transferred product | confirm feasible supply and transport arrangements | hand approved movements to the owning scheduling, actualization, and settlement processes
Out of scope: Replenishment decisions (CM-1-1-3-7-9) | allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2) | primary movement scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6) | secondary distribution (CM-1-1-3-8) | trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3) | exchange administration (CM-1-2-7-1-1) | physical operations (operators, carriers, Midstream)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Replenishment requirements (CM-1-1-3-7-9) | operational demand and exchange plans (CM-1-2-6-2) | agreed purchases and transfers (CM-1-2-1-3)"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Fulfilled supply requirements handed to the owning scheduling and actualization processes"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The network reliably supplied from commercial sources, with every decision right left where earlier batches put it."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ORP-DPSC-001 | SRC-LNL-T2S-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Refined Product Demand Management | uses-input: Manage Inventory Replenishment | precedes: Physical Distribution Scheduling"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Q2 decision (Hamid, Option A with fulfillment language): commercial-source fulfillment and supply coordination — never a parallel scheduling or dispatch organization; 'operate' wording removed because it can imply terminal-operations or dispatch ownership. The product-side mirror of the batch 18 feedstock logistics cluster, complementing the batch 04 replenishment decision and batch 05 distribution scheduling ownerships. Concept-type: process — one fulfillment cycle. APQC candidate is an unrelated internal-operations match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-7-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12895" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12895"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Product Commercial Agreements"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-7 ;
    skos:definition "Negotiate, maintain commercial requirements for, and coordinate lifecycle changes to product-side agreements that enable product exchanges, customer delivery commitments, and storage/throughput access, so dependent supply, distribution, and settlement processes operate against current commercially agreed terms."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.7.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Contractual Agreements Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the commercial-agreement lifecycle for three product-side agreement families: product exchange agreements including their terms, entitlement rules, and contractual imbalance treatment (CM-1-2-7-1-1); customer-facing product transport and delivery agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-2, under the batch 09 boundary rule); and negotiation of storage and throughput agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-3, under the batch 09 storage split). The handoff model: commercial negotiation and commercial-requirement ownership sit in this cluster; legal drafting, legal review, and execution authority with Legal & Corporate Communications under its locked rule; the controlled contract record, document lifecycle, and renewal/termination/novation administration with Contract Management (CM-1-2-1-2) where the agreement is within its trading-contract scope; deal capture with Trade Capture (CM-1-2-1-3); operational use with Supply Operations, Distribution, and the relevant operating domains; settlement with Settlements (CM-1-2-4-1). Excludes trading contract administration and the controlled contract record (CM-1-2-1-2); legal services and execution authority (Legal & Corporate Communications, locked rule); deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3); settlement (CM-1-2-4-1); operational exchange utilization (CM-1-2-6-2-2, batch 19).
In scope: Negotiate product exchange agreements with entitlement and imbalance terms | negotiate and maintain commercial requirements for customer-facing delivery agreements | negotiate storage and throughput agreements | coordinate lifecycle changes and keep dependent processes supplied with current terms
Out of scope: Contract record and lifecycle administration (CM-1-2-1-2) | legal services and execution authority (Legal & Corporate Communications, locked rule) | deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3) | settlement (CM-1-2-4-1) | operational exchange utilization (CM-1-2-6-2-2)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Commercial requirements from demand, supply, and network processes | counterparty proposals | legal review (Legal & Corporate Communications)"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Negotiated product-side commercial agreements with maintained commercial requirements"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every exchange, delivery, and storage arrangement running on current, correct, commercially negotiated terms."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Manage Refined Product Exchanges | enables: Supply Operations | informed-by: Contract Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 20 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one agreement-lifecycle discipline across three agreement families (batch 09 pattern). Handoff model (Hamid, batch 20): negotiation and commercial requirements here; record administration with Contract Management where in trading scope — the two never both 'administer' the same lifecycle. Receiving end of the batch 09 exclusions and the batch 08/14/19 exchange bindings. APQC 'Establish lifting agreements' scored NONE; comparative only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-7-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Company-Managed Terminal & Fleet Operations"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-7 ;
    skos:definition "The capability to operate and maintain company-managed marketing and distribution terminals, loading facilities, and associated distribution fleet assets where the business unit is the designated operating entity, including site safety execution, fleet capability management, and site-asset integrity and availability."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.7.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Terminal Operations Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the management of company-managed marketing and distribution terminal and fleet operations under the Q2 reading: site safety management (CM-1-2-7-2-1), fleet management (CM-1-2-7-2-2), and site asset management (CM-1-2-7-2-3), applied where the Marketing/Distribution business is the designated operating entity. Asset ownership alone does not determine scope: this capability applies only where the Marketing/Distribution business is operationally accountable for the site or fleet — Refining-operated, Midstream-operated, third-party-operated, and centrally operated assets remain with their designated operating domains or providers, and where the company owns but does not operate a facility, its interest is held through agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-3), source-point coordination (CM-1-2-6-3-1), storage administration (CM-1-1-3-7-11), and settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-14). The capability is located in the Supply & Trading taxonomy because it supports marketing/distribution supply operations; its operational delivery may sit in a dedicated terminal operations, distribution operations, fleet, Midstream, engineering, or shared-services organization, depending on asset ownership and operating accountability. Scheduling remains with its owners (CM-1-1-3-6, CM-1-1-3-8, batch 05); EHS policy, regulatory reporting, and filings with EHS & Government Reporting under its locked rule. Excludes Refining-, Midstream-, centrally-, and third-party-operated assets (their designated operators, locked rules); movement and delivery scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6, CM-1-1-3-8); EHS policy and regulatory reporting (EHS & Government Reporting, locked rule); storage/terminal commercial agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-3).
In scope: Operate site safety programs within the enterprise EHS framework at company-managed sites | manage own and contracted fleet capability and compliance | manage site-asset integrity coordination and availability | keep company-managed operations safe, compliant, and available
Out of scope: Refining-, Midstream-, centrally-, and third-party-operated assets (designated operators, locked rules) | movement and delivery scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6, CM-1-1-3-8) | EHS policy and regulatory reporting (EHS & Government Reporting, locked rule) | storage/terminal agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Enterprise EHS framework and policies | fleet and asset condition data | supply and distribution requirements"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Safely and compliantly operated company-managed terminals and fleet"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Company-managed terminals and fleet that are safe, compliant, and reliably available."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-OSHA-PSM-001 | SRC-EI-HM32-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Secondary Distribution Management | enables: Supply Operations | governed-by: Develop Compliance Policies & Procedures"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 20 (2026-09-20). Q2 decision (Hamid, narrowed): this capability applies where the company is the designated operator — never categorically 'the terminal operator', since the same legal entity may own a facility operated by a third party, Midstream, Refining, a joint venture, or a terminal services company. Every prior operator exclusion is preserved: for facilities operated elsewhere, the operator remains external to this cluster. Concept-type: capability per the batch 11 test. Which terminals, tanks, racks, and fleet assets are actually company-managed by Marketing/Distribution is an asset-inventory follow-up carried in the batch 20 PR. APQC 'Analyze internal operations' scored NONE; comparative only."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-2-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19959" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19959"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-2 ;
    skos:definition "Develop and maintain the approved customer-experience strategy for the company's downstream customer and consumer interactions — the experience aspiration, guiding principles, and strategic pillars that every channel, offer, and service interaction is designed against — through a recurring strategy cycle grounded in governed consumer and customer evidence."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.2.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Customer Experience Strategy"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the experience-strategy lifecycle: identifying the customer-experience aspiration (CM-1-3-2-5-1), defining experience guiding principles (CM-1-3-2-5-2), and establishing the strategic pillars (CM-1-3-2-5-3). Develop and maintain the approved customer-experience strategy by consolidating the aspiration, principles, and pillars into a governed strategic direction issued to design, service, channel, and execution processes. 'Customer' includes consumers, commercial customers, channel partners, and marketers where they interact with the company's products, services, channels, support, digital touchpoints, and operational processes. The experience promise must be consistent with the value propositions (CM-1-3-2-2/-3) and brand strategy (CM-1-3-2-1). Marketing Strategy develops and maintains approved brand, value-proposition, channel, and customer-experience direction using governed evidence from Marketing Analysis. It does not perform the analyses; design detailed offers, prices, channels, networks, communications, or operating models; plan campaigns and sales; or execute channel, sales, brand, loyalty, or service programs. Material strategic changes follow the applicable enterprise delegation, investment, legal, and governance approvals. Strategy may set the strategic role of price in the proposition or channel — such as value, premium, parity, transparency, index linkage, or loyalty emphasis — but does not establish pricing strategy, price architecture, price rules, price levels, discounts, formula terms, or transaction prices. Those activities belong to Pricing Strategy Management and Sales Pricing. Strategy only: experience delivery sits with the channel, service, and execution processes (CM-1-3-6, CM-1-3-9); experience measurement with Service & Support Measurement (CM-1-3-9-3) and Marketing Performance Analysis (CM-1-3-5-5); experience evidence from Consumer and Customer/Marketer Analysis under their privacy controls. Excludes experience delivery (CM-1-3-6, CM-1-3-9); experience measurement (CM-1-3-9-3, CM-1-3-5-5); the analyses (CM-1-3-1-3/-4); loyalty operation (CM-1-3-6-6).
In scope: Run the recurring experience-strategy cycle | set the experience aspiration, principles, and pillars | consolidate them into the approved, governed experience strategy and maintain it | keep the experience promise consistent with brand and value propositions | issue experience direction to design, service, and execution
Out of scope: Experience delivery (CM-1-3-6, CM-1-3-9) | experience measurement (CM-1-3-9-3, CM-1-3-5-5) | the analyses (CM-1-3-1-3/-4) | loyalty operation (CM-1-3-6-6) | pricing strategy, rules, and levels (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Consumer and customer/marketer findings (CM-1-3-1-3/-4) | value propositions (CM-1-3-2-2/-3) | brand direction (CM-1-3-2-1)"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The approved, maintained customer-experience strategy (aspiration, principles, pillars)"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every customer interaction designed against one deliberate, approved experience promise."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "strategic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Consumer Analysis | informed-by: Customer And Marketer Analysis | enables: Service & Support Enablement"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 22 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one aspiration-to-strategy lifecycle. Lifecycle order per review: Identify Customer Experience Aspiration → Define Customer Experience Guiding Principles → Establish Customer Experience Strategic Pillars → develop and maintain the approved customer-experience strategy at this L4 (explicit strategy-maintenance output added per review, so the L4 is a strategy node, not a container). Cross-audience per review: 'Customer' definition verbatim in scope note — consumers, commercial customers, channel partners, and marketers at their interaction points. Experience evidence under batch 21 privacy controls. Batch 22 durable rule and price-role boundary recorded verbatim in scope note. APQC 'Develop customer experience strategy' scored 0.857 STRONG — the branch's strongest genuine APQC anchor; no validator agreement recorded, treated as comparative anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16824" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16824"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4 ;
    skos:definition "Set the innovation vision and implementation plans for downstream products and services — where the company will innovate, which market needs and signals justify it, and how R&D effort will be mobilized — through a recurring visioning cycle grounded in the governed evidence base, so concept and development work runs against deliberate innovation direction."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Visioning And Implementation Planning"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the innovation-visioning lifecycle: continuous market sensing (CM-1-3-4-1-2), periodic identification of market needs on the innovation lens (CM-1-3-4-1-1), and development of R&D implementation plans (CM-1-3-4-1-3), with sensing and needs identification continuously informing each other. The innovation vision translates approved corporate, brand, proposition, portfolio, and market direction into innovation themes and implementation priorities. It does not establish corporate strategy, approve investment, or select the final R&D portfolio. Innovation processes consume governed findings from Marketing Analysis and may commission purpose-run research through approved research controls. They do not recreate Market, Consumer, Customer/Marketer, Competitor, or Company Analysis; maintain a competing evidence base; publish the enterprise demand forecast; or alter the governed forecast without the appropriate owner's process. Excludes the subject analyses (CM-1-3-1); strategy ownership (CM-1-3-2, corporate strategy); concept and feasibility work (CM-1-3-4-2); development (CM-1-3-4-3); R&D portfolio selection and management (CM-1-3-4-5); R&D investment approval (delegated authorities).
In scope: Run the recurring innovation-visioning cycle | maintain continuous market and technology sensing | identify market needs on the innovation lens from governed evidence | develop approval-ready R&D implementation plans
Out of scope: The analyses (CM-1-3-1) | corporate strategy and strategy ownership (CM-1-3-2, corporate) | concept/feasibility (CM-1-3-4-2) | development (CM-1-3-4-3) | R&D portfolio selection (CM-1-3-4-5) | R&D investment approval (delegated authorities)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Governed insight (CM-1-3-1-6) | market and consumer findings (CM-1-3-1-1/-3) | technology and external signals | approved strategy direction (CM-1-3-2)"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The innovation vision and approval-ready R&D implementation plans"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Innovation pointed at evidenced needs, not at whatever seems interesting."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "strategic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Insight and Metrics | enables: Concept & Feasibility Study | enables: Product & Service Development"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 25 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one visioning-and-planning lifecycle; horizon strategic. Lifecycle: Market Sensing (continuous) and Identify Market Needs continuously inform each other → Develop R&D Implementation Plans → approved direction feeds concept/feasibility work. Vision-versus-strategy boundary and Q2 innovation-consumes-analysis rule (canonical wording) verbatim in scope note. APQC 'Develop plan for new product/service development and introduction/launch' is a WEAK broad match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16824" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16824"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4 ;
    skos:definition "Develop and evaluate product and service concepts — ideation from evidenced needs, research on candidate concepts, and feasibility assessment across market, technical, commercial, and regulatory dimensions — so only concepts that survive evidence and feasibility reach development."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Concept & Feasibility Study"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the concept lifecycle: ideation from prioritized needs and signals (CM-1-3-4-2-1) and research on candidate concepts (CM-1-3-4-2-2), producing feasibility evidence and a concept recommendation for gate and delegated approval of development entry, with Manage Research (CM-1-3-4-2-3) as the governance overlay that commissions, prioritizes, tracks, and assures research across the cycle. Feasibility conclusions are conditional evidence for stage-gate decision-making; they are not regulatory classification, product approval, investment approval, launch authorization, or legal clearance. Feasibility spans market, technical, commercial, operational, and regulatory dimensions, including early screening for the fuel-product regulatory gate: Where a proposed product is subject to US EPA fuel or fuel-additive registration requirements under 40 CFR Part 79, the applicable manufacturer, importer, or other regulated party must satisfy the relevant registration and testing requirements before the product is introduced into US commerce. Regulatory, Legal, Quality, Product Stewardship, and designated business owners determine applicability, regulated-party responsibility, submission requirements, and readiness. Innovation processes consume governed findings from Marketing Analysis and may commission purpose-run research through approved research controls. They do not recreate Market, Consumer, Customer/Marketer, Competitor, or Company Analysis; maintain a competing evidence base; publish the enterprise demand forecast; or alter the governed forecast without the appropriate owner's process. Consumer concept research operates under the batch 21 privacy controls and aggregate/de-identified default. Excludes development (CM-1-3-4-3); the subject analyses (CM-1-3-1); regulatory classification and registration (Regulatory, Legal, Product Stewardship); stage-gate and investment approval (CM-1-3-4-3-1, delegated authorities).
In scope: Run the concept-and-feasibility lifecycle per candidate | ideate from evidenced needs | research and assess feasibility across market, technical, commercial, and regulatory dimensions with early gate screening | deliver feasibility evidence and concept recommendations to the gates
Out of scope: Development (CM-1-3-4-3) | the analyses (CM-1-3-1) | regulatory classification/registration (Regulatory, Legal, Product Stewardship) | stage-gate/investment approval (CM-1-3-4-3-1, delegated authorities)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Prioritized needs (CM-1-3-4-1-1) | governed findings and purpose-run research | feasibility and regulatory screening inputs"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Feasibility evidence and concept recommendations for the gates"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Concepts killed cheaply on evidence before development pays for them."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EPA-FF-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Visioning And Implementation Planning | enables: Product & Service Development"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 25 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one per-candidate concept lifecycle; horizon event-driven. Lifecycle per review: Ideation → Conduct Research → feasibility evidence and recommendation → gate/delegated approval, with Manage Research as a governance overlay, not a third sequential step. Feasibility-conditionality, Q2 rule, and refined Q3 gate (regulated-party, fact-dependent) verbatim in scope note. APQC 'Develop plan for new product/service development and introduction/launch' is a WEAK broad match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19990" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19990"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4 ;
    skos:definition "Develop new and improved downstream products and services from approved concepts — governed development programs, per-product development execution, and controlled product and service change — within the applicable regulatory and quality gates, so what reaches offers and launch is developed, compliant, and controlled."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Product & Service Development"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the development cluster: coordinating the development program and its stage gates (CM-1-3-4-3-1), conducting per-product and per-service development (CM-1-3-4-3-2), and managing product and service change (CM-1-3-4-3-3), as a stage-gate cycle (gates → development → evidence and readiness → gate outcome to iterate, hold, stop, or advance → controlled change) rather than a pure line. Development completion is not authorization to sell, manufacture, distribute, market, or launch. Commercial availability requires completion of all applicable regulatory, quality, product-master, supply, tax, pricing, legal, channel, offer, launch, and delegated approval controls. Product development may include fuels, additives, lubricants, blend components, packaged products, and related physical offerings. Service development may include commercial programs, convenience/retail services, digital services, loyalty propositions, and channel/customer services. Regulatory and quality gates vary by the nature of the offering. Where a proposed product is subject to US EPA fuel or fuel-additive registration requirements under 40 CFR Part 79, the applicable manufacturer, importer, or other regulated party must satisfy the relevant registration and testing requirements before the product is introduced into US commerce. Regulatory, Legal, Quality, Product Stewardship, and designated business owners determine applicability, regulated-party responsibility, submission requirements, and readiness. Applicable regulatory, quality, product-stewardship, tax, legal, environmental, and operational gate requirements are determined by the accountable functions for the specific product/service, market, and route to market. The capability is located in the Marketing product/service innovation branch because it governs market-facing offerings. Development execution may be performed by cross-functional product, R&D, quality, engineering, operations, digital, or partner teams according to the operating model. Innovation processes consume governed findings from Marketing Analysis and may commission purpose-run research through approved research controls. They do not recreate Market, Consumer, Customer/Marketer, Competitor, or Company Analysis; maintain a competing evidence base; publish the enterprise demand forecast; or alter the governed forecast without the appropriate owner's process. Downstream separation: offers assemble developed products (CM-1-3-3-1); launch runs through Planning and Execution (CM-1-3-5/6); portfolio and lifecycle decisions at CM-1-3-4-5 and licensing/IP at CM-1-3-4-4 (batch 26); product master data at CM-1-3-7-1; fuel quality operations with the quality management processes (batch 18 architecture). Excludes offer definition (CM-1-3-3-1); launch planning/execution (CM-1-3-5/6); portfolio/lifecycle decisions (CM-1-3-4-5); licensing/IP (CM-1-3-4-4); regulatory registration execution (Regulatory, Legal, Product Stewardship); quality operations (quality processes, batch 18); product master data (CM-1-3-7-1).
In scope: Coordinate the development program and stage gates | conduct per-product and per-service development within the applicable gates | manage controlled product and service change | hand developed products to offers, launch, and master data through their controls
Out of scope: Offer definition (CM-1-3-3-1) | launch (CM-1-3-5/6) | portfolio/lifecycle (CM-1-3-4-5) | licensing/IP (CM-1-3-4-4) | regulatory registration execution (Regulatory, Legal, Product Stewardship) | quality operations (batch 18 processes) | product master data (CM-1-3-7-1) | authorization to sell or launch (downstream controls)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved concepts with feasibility evidence (CM-1-3-4-2) | R&D implementation plans (CM-1-3-4-1-3) | regulatory and quality gate requirements from the accountable functions"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Developed, gate-reviewed products and services with controlled specifications"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Products and services that arrive developed, compliant, and controlled — not improvised."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EPA-FF-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Concept & Feasibility Study | enables: Customer Offer Definition | enables: Lifecycle Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 25 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — governed stage-gate development cycle (b03/08 per-variant pattern at the execution child); horizon tactical. Review statements verbatim in scope note: development completion is not launch authorization; product-versus-service distinction (gates vary by offering); refined Q3 gate (regulated-party, fact-dependent); adjacent gates determined by accountable functions; cross-functional execution qualifier (organizational neutrality, b10/15/16/20 line); Q2 rule. APQC 'Define product/service development requirements' 0.75 STRONG — genuine comparative anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16500" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16500"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze marketing performance — spend, campaign returns, and customer programs — executing approved measurement methods against the governed measurement frameworks and stewarded metrics, producing the evidence-layer findings that planning and strategy correct from."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Marketing Performance Analysis"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers marketing performance analysis: marketing spend analysis (CM-1-3-5-5-1), campaign ROI analysis (CM-1-3-5-5-2), and customer program analysis (CM-1-3-5-5-3) — three parallel analyses whose findings converge to inform sales planning, communications planning, promotions planning, and the strategy and design refresh cycles. Marketing Performance Analysis executes approved measurement methods and analyzes observed performance against the relevant domain measurement frameworks. It produces evidence-layer findings, attribution assessments, variance explanations, and improvement recommendations. It does not set target changes, approve budget reallocations, publish enterprise KPIs, produce official financial reporting, or make legal determinations. Measures against the domain measurement frameworks (offer, pricing, channel, network, communications, operating model — batches 23/24) and the metric definitions stewarded at CM-1-3-1-6; findings are evidence-layer measures that may inform candidate KPIs but do not become enterprise KPIs unless separately classified, governed, approved, and published through the KPI Store. Uses governed spend and financial data from Finance (consumed, never produced); findings with potential legal, competition, or pricing-law implications escalate to Legal/Compliance (b23 pattern); personal data per the batch 21 controls. Excludes measurement-framework definition (b23/24 rows); metrics stewardship (CM-1-3-1-6); enterprise KPI governance (KPI Store workstream); financial reporting (Finance); the subject analyses (CM-1-3-1); loyalty operation (CM-1-3-6-6); target changes and budget reallocations (their planning owners with approvals).
In scope: Execute approved measurement methods against the governed frameworks and stewarded metrics | analyze spend, campaign returns, and customer programs | produce evidence-layer findings, attribution assessments, variance explanations, and improvement recommendations | feed planning and strategy corrections
Out of scope: Framework definition (b23/24 rows) | metrics stewardship (CM-1-3-1-6) | enterprise KPI governance (KPI Store) | financial reporting (Finance) | the analyses (CM-1-3-1) | loyalty operation (CM-1-3-6-6) | target changes/budget reallocations (planning owners)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Measurement frameworks (b23/24) | stewarded metrics (CM-1-3-1-6) | governed spend and outcome data (Finance, execution processes)"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Evidence-layer marketing performance findings, attribution assessments, and improvement recommendations"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Marketing measured against its own frameworks, honestly."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Insight and Metrics | enables: Marketing Communications Planning | enables: Sales Strategy & Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — the measurement-execution cluster batches 21–24 pointed at; periodic; three parallel children converging into planning, promotions, and strategy/design refresh cycles. Review role statement verbatim in scope note — executes measurement, decides nothing that belongs to planning, Finance, KPI Store, or Legal. APQC 'Analyze performance' is a WEAK match at this L4."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20682" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20682"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6 ;
    skos:definition "The process of administering customer and channel-partner rebate arrangements through their operational lifecycle: negotiating individual rebate terms within the approved discount and rebate architecture, implementing agreed arrangements in the systems and procedures that administer them, and settling earned rebates accurately against verified performance."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rebate Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """This process administers rebates; it does not design them. Rebate and discount architecture — which rebate structures exist, their forms, and the guardrails they operate within — is developed and approved under Pricing Strategy Management (CM-1-3-3-2) and the applicable delegations of authority; Rebate Management negotiates, implements, and settles individual arrangements inside that approved architecture. No individual arrangement may create a rebate structure, treatment, eligibility rule, payment term, customer classification, or price effect outside the approved architecture and delegation. Settlement follows the payment-authority rule: this process prepares the appropriate authorized credit, offset, accrual-release, or payment request through the accountable accounting/treasury processes; it never moves funds. Fact-dependent statement: where a rebate, discount, promotional allowance, or service/payment arrangement differs among purchasers that may compete, US price-discrimination or promotional-allowance requirements may be implicated depending on commodity comparability, purchaser status, competitive effect, available defenses, and other facts. Legal/Compliance determines applicability and sufficiency.
In scope: Negotiate individual rebate arrangements within the approved architecture, eligibility, and delegations of authority | implement agreed arrangements in rebate-administration systems | coordinate rebate accruals with Finance | validate earned-rebate calculations against verified sales performance | prepare authorized settlement requests | track arrangement performance and expiry
Out of scope: Rebate/discount architecture design and approval (CM-1-3-3-2) | sales price setting (CM-1-3-6-3) | payment execution and funds movement (Finance/Treasury) | accrual accounting policy and write-off decisions (Finance) | legal review of rebate terms and competition-law determinations (Legal/Compliance)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved discount and rebate architecture and guardrails (CM-1-3-3-2) | customer and partner agreements | verified sales and volume data | delegations of authority | Legal/Compliance guidance"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Accurately settled rebate arrangements with a complete administration record"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Rebate arrangements administered accurately and transparently from negotiation through settlement within approved pricing governance."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-FTC-RP-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Pricing Strategy Management | informed-by: Analyze Pricing Performance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Administer-not-design boundary under the b23 rebate guardrail (design at CM-1-3-3-2); review no-new-structure rule and refined Robinson-Patman statement adopted verbatim; settlement request wording per review replaces generic 'settlement instructions'; b14 payment-authority rule applies."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20622" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20622"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6 ;
    skos:definition "The process of executing approved marketing plans in market: coordinating and monitoring advertising, promotion and event, and direct-marketing activity according to the released plans, calendars, budgets, and creative deliverables produced by marketing planning, and returning execution and spend actuals for measurement."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Marketing Plan Execution"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """This process is the receiving end of the marketing release handoff: Marketing Communications Planning and related planning processes prepare and release approved deliverables under the release-authority statement; Marketing Plan Execution executes what has been released and does not itself approve strategy, creative, claims, budgets, prices, or releases. Coordinate and monitor in-market execution by internal teams, agencies, vendors, partners, channel operators, and sites under approved commitments; capture execution, delivery, and spend actuals. Execution results and spend actuals flow to Marketing Performance Analysis for measurement — this process executes and records; it does not evaluate effectiveness. Consumer contact in execution honors the contactability rule: permissioned contactability data is consumed under the enterprise consent framework; execution never creates or presumes consent.
In scope: Coordinate and monitor in-market execution by internal teams, agencies, vendors, partners, channel operators, and sites under approved commitments | verify release and approval status before activation | in-market quality control against released specifications | capture execution, delivery, and spend actuals
Out of scope: Developing or approving plans, creative, claims, budgets, or releases (planning processes) | effectiveness measurement (Marketing Performance Analysis) | fuel price setting or price-display execution (b24 POS exclusion) | media strategy | agency and vendor contracting terms (Procurement/Legal)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Released plans, calendars, budgets, and creative deliverables (CM-1-3-5-3) | approved media and vendor commitments | permissioned contactability data | brand standards"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Executed marketing activity with complete execution, delivery, and spend actuals"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Released marketing plans delivered in market faithfully, on schedule, and within approved commitments."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-FTC-ADM-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Marketing Communications Planning | enables: Marketing Performance Analysis"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Receiving end of the b27 prepare-and-release handoff; review coordination wording adopted verbatim — coordinates and monitors execution by agencies, vendors, partners, channel operators, and sites rather than owning all placement; b24 contactability rule applies."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-9 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Customer service & support consist of processes such as all support & service offerings to consumers & business partners both from a conceptual & operational view. It ranges from defining the methods of interaction (e.g., on site, call center, web), along defining, setting up & operating facilities & technology down to managing service staff."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Customer Service & Support"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-3-9-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20059" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20059"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-9 ;
    skos:definition "The capability that executes commercial service work: maintaining the data service delivery runs on, managing the customer interaction during delivery, and fulfilling service events routed from the request capability — concurrent delivery controls operating through to evidenced completion."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.9.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Service & Support Delivery"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Customer Requests and Inquiries owns the request experience: intake, case record, prioritization, routing, status communication, follow-up, and closure coordination. Service & Support Enablement provides the service machinery: operating model, service operations, workforce, facilities, technology, and contact-center channel. Service & Support Delivery fulfills service events: delivery data, customer interaction during service, entitlement verification, service execution, completion evidence, and return of outcomes to the request case. Service & Support Measurement measures service resources and outcomes: infrastructure, workforce operational data, and service effectiveness. One customer request may produce zero, one, or many service events. Each service event is linked to the originating case where applicable, but the service event is not itself a new customer-request case. Completion evidence returns to the case record and, where billable, to Billing. Service and Support spans Commercial, Operations, Technology, Facilities, HR, Security, Quality, EHS, and third-party providers. Taxonomy placement in Commercial & Marketing reflects the commercial service context; it does not establish operational ownership of every service resource or service event. A billable service event is invoiced only from approved service-completion evidence, entitlement/contract terms, applicable pricing/tax data, and any required customer acceptance or service confirmation. Service Delivery does not create prices, credits, tax outcomes, or invoices. Delivery operates within entitlements and agreements; it creates no terms, prices, credits, or master data (governed submission only), and honors the governed-answer rule in all customer communication.
In scope: Service delivery data stewardship | customer interaction during delivery | service event fulfillment with entitlement verification | completion evidence capture and return to the case and, where billable, to Billing
Out of scope: The request/case experience (CM-1-3-7-5) | prices, credits, tax outcomes, and invoices (Their owning processes; CM-1-3-8-1) | master data (CM-1-3-7-1; governed submission) | the service machinery (CM-1-3-9-1) | outcome measurement (CM-1-3-9-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Routed service events with case linkage (CM-1-3-7-5) | entitlements and agreements | delivery data | the service machinery (CM-1-3-9-1)"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Fulfilled, evidenced service events with outcomes returned to the case and billing"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Service work done, evidenced, and returned to the case — and to billing only through evidence."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage Customer Requests and Inquiries | enables: Create & Distribute Bill | uses-input: Service & Support Enablement"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 40 (2026-09-21). Reclassified to capability per review — delivery data, customer interaction, and event fulfillment are concurrent controls, not a strict sequence; case-to-service-event linkage and billable-service evidence rules adopted verbatim."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-9-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20875" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20875"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-9 ;
    skos:definition "The process of measuring the service operation on a recurring cycle: infrastructure and channel performance, workforce operational performance, and overall service effectiveness — consolidated into an integrated service performance view."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.9.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Service & Support Measurement"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """The process consolidates the three measurement domains into an integrated service performance view for operating-model, workforce, technology, facilities, and service-improvement owners. It does not set staffing, change technology, approve capital, alter service levels, or publish enterprise KPIs. The analysis rules apply in full (b21/b23/b35): measurement analyzes and informs — the owners decide; all metrics are evidence-layer with candidate KPIs routed only via the KPI Store; and this is the governed measurement side of the batch 35 formal-research distinction. Employee measurement per the boundary on CM-1-3-9-3-2.
In scope: The recurring service measurement cycle | consolidation of infrastructure, workforce, and effectiveness evidence into an integrated performance view | insight delivery to operating-model, workforce, technology, facilities, and improvement owners
Out of scope: Staffing, technology, capital, and service-level decisions (Their owners) | enterprise KPI publication (KPI Store governance) | case resolution and delivery (CM-1-3-7-5; CM-1-3-9-2)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Delivery, operations, channel, and workforce data | follow-up signals (b35) | Finance-approved cost methods"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The integrated service performance view informing accountable owners"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One integrated, evidence-layer view of how the service operation actually performs."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Service & Support Delivery | uses-input: Perform Customer Follow up | enables: Define Operating Model"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 40 (2026-09-21). Periodic process per review, with the integration-purpose scope sentence adopted verbatim — the L4 consolidates, it does not merely group."@en .

proc:CM-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10102" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10102"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:L0-downstream-operations ;
    skos:definition "The downstream business function that creates, manages, and optimizes the commercial value of petroleum products and services through market strategy, customer and channel management, supply and trading, commercial planning and scheduling, pricing, sales, and order-to-cash activities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Spans the commercial hydrocarbon lifecycle from supply acquisition and commercial optimization through product positioning, customer order capture, allocation, fulfilment coordination, invoicing, payment, and commercial performance management. It coordinates with refining, midstream, distribution, finance, and enterprise IT, but does not itself encompass their underlying physical operations or enterprise-wide technology services."@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:level "L1"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-2-3 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Discuss market demand and pricing outlook and align on list of standard scenarios for evaluation"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Conduct Market Outlook and Avails Meeting"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-2-8 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Review plan outlook and strategy with management team"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.2.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Management Review"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-1 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Network premise is an input into regional optimization plan"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Network Premise for Prompt and Out Month"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20022" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20022"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Movement Scheduling & Administration"@en,
        "Primary Distribution Scheduling"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Schedule and administer the primary distribution movements that execute the product schedule — nominating volumes to pipelines, vessels, rail, and terminals, administering load and discharge events, and tracking movements to closure — so scheduled product arrives where the plan requires, fully documented."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Physical Distribution Scheduling"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers mode-level movement scheduling and administration from planned movement to closed load: nominations and confirmations with carriers and terminal operators, load authorization and documentation, in-transit tracking, and discharge confirmation with per-load reconciliation. Midstream, carriers, and terminal operators perform the physical loading, transport, and discharge; this process owns the schedule, the shipper-side administration, and the movement record, per the locked Midstream rule and the owner-versus-executor pattern. Excludes deciding what moves where and when, owned by Product Scheduling (CM-1-1-3-5); excludes secondary deliveries (CM-1-1-3-8).
In scope: Nominate and confirm movements with carriers and terminals | authorize and administer load and discharge events | track movements to closure | maintain the movement record
Out of scope: Short-term schedule decisions (Product Scheduling, CM-1-1-3-5) | physical loading, transport, and discharge operation (Midstream, carriers, terminal operators) | secondary delivery scheduling (CM-1-1-3-8)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "short-term network schedule | carrier and terminal capacities and cycles | movement documentation"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "nominated, administered, and closed primary distribution movements"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Turn the product schedule into nominated, tracked, closed movements."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-DOE-RPPL-001 | SRC-EI-HM32-001 | SRC-LNL-T2S-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Product Scheduling | enables: Distribution Backcasting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 05 (2026-09-19). 'Physical' in the label refers to the movements being scheduled, not to this process performing them — the locked Midstream rule holds. Nomination-cycle and batching practice per the DOE refined-product pipeline backgrounder. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-8 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "17042" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 17042"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Secondary Distribution"@en,
        "Terminal-to-Site Delivery Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Manage the delivery of products from terminals and depots to retail sites, commercial customers, and other end-delivery points — generating delivery orders for managed sites, scheduling vehicles and routes, and monitoring delivery performance — so site and customer demand is met reliably at efficient delivery cost."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Secondary Distribution Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the secondary distribution leg: order generation for managed sites, daily vehicle and route scheduling within fleet, site, and product constraints, maintenance of the scheduling basis, and delivery-performance monitoring. Excludes primary bulk movements, owned by Physical Distribution Scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6); excludes customer-initiated order capture and fulfillment, owned by Order Management (CM-1-3-7); excludes physical haulage execution, performed by the designated distribution operator, carrier, or logistics provider — outside the scope of this scheduling process; excludes plan-variance analysis, owned by Distribution Backcasting (CM-1-1-6) under its locked rule.
In scope: Generate delivery orders for managed sites | schedule vehicles, routes, and delivery windows | maintain the scheduling basis | monitor delivery performance
Out of scope: Primary distribution movements (CM-1-1-3-6) | customer-initiated order capture (Order Management, CM-1-3-7) | physical haulage execution (designated distribution operator, carrier, or logistics provider) | plan-variance backcasting (Distribution Backcasting, CM-1-1-6)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "site demand and stock signals | fleet and route constraints | terminal availability"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "scheduled, monitored secondary deliveries"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Run the terminal-to-site delivery operation to demand, within cost and constraints."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-LNL-T2S-001 | SRC-EI-HM32-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Develop Finished Product Inventory Policy | enables: Distribution Backcasting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 05 (2026-09-19). 'Secondary distribution' per downstream practice: terminal and depot to station and customer deliveries, short-notice demand at many points — Mota et al. 2017 (the Galp case) is the direct evidence. Executor wording per Hamid's batch 05 edit: physical haulage execution is performed by the designated distribution operator, carrier, or logistics provider — the taxonomy stays operating-model neutral and does not hard-code Midstream as the secondary-delivery executor. The locked Distribution Backcasting rule is honored: CM-1-1-6 explains variance against the approved plan; this L4's monitoring child watches execution against the issued schedule. Accountable ownership of delivery-performance improvement when fleet execution is outsourced is a follow-up noted in the batch 05 PR."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-4-1 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-4 ;
    skos:definition "Refinery Premise serves as input into Regional Optimization Plan"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.4.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Refinery Premise for Prompt and Out Month"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Trading management consists of processes performed in the front office such as forecasting, pre-trade analysis, managing counterparty credit and contracts, executing and capturing trades."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Trading Management"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Settlements and Accounting process covers deal attributes and deal terms at time of trade execution with counterparty through netting, invoicing and settlement post trade expiry and various accounting postings throughout deal lifecycle."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settlements & Accounting"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-2-5 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Crude/feed supply chain management consists of processes required to plan, organize, coordinate & control crude/feed supply & processing at the refinery. This includes crude/feed transportation, slate, and supply & demand management."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Crude/Feed Supply Chain Management"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-2-5-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "17498" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 17498"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Feedstock Quality & Slate Development"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-5 ;
    skos:definition "The capability to keep feedstock selection running on trustworthy quality and economic information — maintaining validated crude and feedstock quality data, reviewing the integrity of the evaluation models that consume it, and developing the quality-screened term and spot procurement slates that feed the feedstock recommendation and trading execution."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.5.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Crude/Feed Quality Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the feedstock quality-information capability: crude and feedstock assay and quality-data stewardship (CM-1-2-5-1-1); integrity review of the LP and evaluation model representations that consume that data (CM-1-2-5-1-2); and development of the term (CM-1-2-5-1-3) and spot (CM-1-2-5-1-4) procurement slates, quality-screened against refinery capability. Under PTC-002 (Option 1, closed), the binding production slate and run rate are set by Refinery Planning (CM-1-1-4); this cluster's slates are procurement recommendations. Crude and feedstock evaluation — suitability and economic value of individual crudes — is owned by Evaluate Crude & Feedstock (CM-1-1-4-7-2, batch 02). Excludes the binding production slate (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002); crude evaluation conclusions (CM-1-1-4-7-2); trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3); enterprise master-data governance (batch 05 exclusion); laboratory testing operations (Refining/laboratory functions).
In scope: Steward validated crude and feedstock quality data | review evaluation-model input integrity | develop quality-screened term and spot procurement slates | feed the feedstock recommendation and trading execution
Out of scope: Binding production slate and run rate (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002) | crude evaluation (CM-1-1-4-7-2) | trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3) | enterprise master-data governance (batch 05 exclusion) | laboratory testing (Refining/laboratory)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Crude assays and quality data | refinery capability and constraint data (CM-1-1-4) | market availability and pricing"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Validated quality data, model-integrity findings, and term/spot procurement slates"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Make every feedstock decision run on validated quality data, sound models, and quality-screened procurement options."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-DOE-SPRASSAY-001 | SRC-CMU-REFPLAN-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Plan Optimal Feedstock Slate And Run Rate | informed-by: Evaluate Crude & Feedstock | enables: Coordinate Feedstock Trading"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: capability per the batch 11/12/15 test — data stewardship, model review, and slate development are heterogeneous concurrent functions. PTC-002 honored: production slate decided by CM-1-1-4; procurement slates recommended here. The L4 label understates the slate-development content; naming-pass note recorded. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10106" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10106"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-1 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze the downstream product markets the company serves or could serve — market size, demand and supply balances, channel structure, margin pools, and regulatory and structural change — through a recurring, governed analysis cycle, so marketing strategy and planning decisions rest on a current, evidenced view of the market."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Market Analysis"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the recurring market-analysis lifecycle for downstream fuels and products markets: sourcing market data (CM-1-3-1-1-1), defining each cycle's objectives and scope (CM-1-3-1-1-2), analyzing market size, demand and supply, channel structure, pricing environments, and margin pools (CM-1-3-1-1-3), and documenting governed findings (CM-1-3-1-1-4). Analyze-and-inform: strategy decisions belong to the strategy and planning branches (CM-1-3-2/3/5); the enterprise demand forecast to Produce Demand Forecast (CM-1-1-1-1, batch 03); supply-network economics to CM-1-2-6-1 (batch 19 four-lens rule); enterprise KPI definition to the KPI workstream. Excludes strategy decisions (CM-1-3-2/3); demand forecast production (CM-1-1-1-1); supply-economics analysis (CM-1-2-6-1); KPI definition (KPI workstream); competitor-specific analysis (CM-1-3-1-5).
In scope: Run the recurring market-analysis cycle | analyze market size, demand-supply, channels, and margin pools | maintain a current evidenced market view | feed strategy, planning, and Insight and Metrics
Out of scope: Strategy decisions (CM-1-3-2/3) | demand forecast production (CM-1-1-1-1) | supply-economics analysis (CM-1-2-6-1) | KPI definition (KPI workstream) | competitor analysis (CM-1-3-1-5)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Government and industry market data (EIA PMM/STEO) | licensed vendor data | internal sales and channel data"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed market-analysis findings"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A current, evidenced market view under every marketing decision."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-PMM-001 | SRC-EIA-STEO-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Insight and Metrics | enables: Brand Strategy | informed-by: Produce Demand Forecast (CM-1-1-1-1)"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 21 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one four-step analysis lifecycle (the batch 08 variant pattern; this cluster is the template's first instance), confirmed in review. Lifecycle order normalized per review: Define Objectives & Scope precedes Source & Collect Information precedes Analyze Data precedes Document Findings. Market data for US fuels marketing is anchored by EIA's Petroleum Marketing Monthly and STEO within their US-only, survey-category limitations; vendor data under its licensing terms (batch 12 follow-up). Batch 21 durable rule (review): \"Marketing Analysis produces governed evidence, insight, hypotheses, and recommendations. It does not itself set strategy, approve investments, establish prices, allocate product, publish the enterprise forecast, define enterprise KPIs, make commercial offers, or execute campaigns.\" APQC candidate is a WEAK generic intelligence match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "13169" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 13169"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-1 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze the company's own position and performance in its downstream markets — market share, portfolio and asset position, capability strengths and gaps, and performance against the market, using approved internal data, external benchmarks, and stated comparability assumptions — through a recurring, governed analysis cycle, so strategy is built on an honest view of where the company stands."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Company Analysis"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the recurring company-analysis lifecycle: sourcing internal performance, share, portfolio, and capability data alongside market benchmarks (CM-1-3-1-2-1); framing each cycle's questions (CM-1-3-1-2-2); analyzing share, position, capability, and relative performance (CM-1-3-1-2-3); and documenting governed findings (CM-1-3-1-2-4). Uses governed financial and operational data from its accountable owners (Accounting, batch 15; operations processes); it analyzes position, it does not produce financial results or decide strategy. Share and comparative-performance conclusions state their category definitions, data coverage, channel coverage, geographic scope, and comparability assumptions. Excludes strategy decisions (CM-1-3-2/3); financial reporting production (CM-1-2-4-2, Finance); cost-to-serve analysis (CM-1-2-6-1-3, batch 19); competitor analysis (CM-1-3-1-5); KPI definition (KPI workstream).
In scope: Run the recurring company-analysis cycle | analyze share, portfolio, capability, and relative performance | maintain an honest evidenced self-view | feed strategy and Insight and Metrics
Out of scope: Strategy decisions (CM-1-3-2/3) | financial reporting (CM-1-2-4-2, Finance) | cost-to-serve (CM-1-2-6-1-3) | competitor analysis (CM-1-3-1-5) | KPI definition (KPI workstream)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Governed financial and operational data (CM-1-2-4-2, operations) | share and benchmark data | capability assessments"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed company-position findings"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Strategy built on where the company actually stands, not where it hopes it stands."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-PMM-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Insight and Metrics | enables: Brand Strategy | uses-input: Accounting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 21 (2026-09-20). Q2 partition: the company's own position and performance — the self-analysis lens; competitor conduct sits at CM-1-3-1-5 and market structure at CM-1-3-1-1. Data-quality qualifier added in review: approved internal data, external benchmarks, and stated comparability assumptions. Shared lifecycle, second instance; normalized order applies. Batch 21 durable rule (review): \"Marketing Analysis produces governed evidence, insight, hypotheses, and recommendations. It does not itself set strategy, approve investments, establish prices, allocate product, publish the enterprise forecast, define enterprise KPIs, make commercial offers, or execute campaigns.\" APQC candidate is a WEAK pricing match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "13169" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 13169"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-1 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze end-consumer behavior in the company's downstream markets — needs, preferences, buying patterns, journey and experience drivers, and segment dynamics — through a recurring, governed analysis cycle operating within the applicable privacy frameworks, so consumer-facing strategy rests on evidenced consumer understanding."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Consumer Analysis"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the recurring consumer-analysis lifecycle: sourcing consumer research, transaction, loyalty, and market data within privacy and consent constraints (CM-1-3-1-3-1); framing cycles (CM-1-3-1-3-2); analyzing needs, behavior, journeys, and segment dynamics (CM-1-3-1-3-3); documenting governed findings (CM-1-3-1-3-4). 'Consumer' means the end consumer (retail/B2C); direct customers and branded marketers are analyzed at CM-1-3-1-4. Personal, customer, loyalty, transaction, and behavioral data may be sourced and analyzed only for approved purposes, under the applicable lawful basis, notice/consent status where required, retention rules, access controls, data-minimization requirements, and privacy/security classifications established by the accountable Data Governance, Privacy, Legal, and Security owners. Analysis operates within these controls; it does not own privacy policy, consent and notice management, data-subject rights, data security, retention/deletion execution, or marketing-execution use of personal data. Excludes customer and marketer analysis (CM-1-3-1-4); consumer strategy decisions (CM-1-3-2-3, CM-1-3-2-5); privacy framework ownership (accountable governance owners); loyalty program operation (CM-1-3-6-6).
In scope: Run the recurring consumer-analysis cycle within privacy frameworks | analyze needs, behavior, journeys, and segments | maintain evidenced consumer understanding | feed consumer strategy and Insight and Metrics
Out of scope: Customer/marketer analysis (CM-1-3-1-4) | strategy decisions (CM-1-3-2-3/-5) | privacy framework ownership and consent, rights, security, and retention operations (governance owners) | loyalty operation (CM-1-3-6-6)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Consumer research and panels | transaction and loyalty data within consent | market and demographic data"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed consumer-analysis findings"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Consumer-facing decisions built on evidenced, lawfully obtained consumer understanding."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-PMM-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Consumer Value Proposition | enables: Insight and Metrics | informed-by: Market Analysis"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 21 (2026-09-20). Q2 partition: end consumers here; direct customers and branded marketers at CM-1-3-1-4 — the B2C/B2B lens split. Q3 review enhancement: the shared personal-data governance statement (verbatim in scope note) replaces the general privacy wording — analysis within approved controls is distinguished from ownership of privacy policy, consent, rights, security, and retention. Shared lifecycle, third instance; normalized order applies. Batch 21 durable rule (review): \"Marketing Analysis produces governed evidence, insight, hypotheses, and recommendations. It does not itself set strategy, approve investments, establish prices, allocate product, publish the enterprise forecast, define enterprise KPIs, make commercial offers, or execute campaigns.\" APQC candidate is a WEAK pricing match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-1-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10106" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10106"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-1 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze the company's direct customers and branded marketers — commercial and industrial customers, resellers, and the branded wholesalers, jobbers, and distributors who market under or alongside the brand — their needs, economics, performance, and relationship health, through a recurring, governed analysis cycle, so B2B and channel strategy rests on evidenced counterparty understanding."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.1.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Customer And Marketer Analysis"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the recurring customer-and-marketer analysis lifecycle: sourcing customer and marketer performance, volume, and relationship data (CM-1-3-1-4-1); framing cycles (CM-1-3-1-4-2); analyzing needs, channel economics, performance, and relationship health (CM-1-3-1-4-3); documenting governed findings (CM-1-3-1-4-4). 'Marketer' is read in the downstream fuels sense: branded wholesalers, jobbers, and distributors reselling under or alongside the brand. This is the marketing analysis lens of the batch 19 four-lens rule: the same entities appear in supply-economics segmentation (CM-1-2-6-1-1), credit assessment, and the party hierarchy through their own lenses. Personal, customer, loyalty, transaction, and behavioral data may be sourced and analyzed only for approved purposes, under the applicable lawful basis, notice/consent status where required, retention rules, access controls, data-minimization requirements, and privacy/security classifications established by the accountable Data Governance, Privacy, Legal, and Security owners. Assess relationship health and retention risk as an analytical signal; do not own account interventions, retention offers, sales actions, or account-management execution. Excludes end-consumer analysis (CM-1-3-1-3); credit assessment (batch 11 boundary); supply-economics segmentation (CM-1-2-6-1-1); channel strategy decisions (CM-1-3-2-4, CM-1-3-3-3); account management execution (CM-1-3-6).
In scope: Run the recurring customer-and-marketer analysis cycle | analyze needs, channel economics, performance, and relationship health as analytical signals | maintain evidenced counterparty understanding | feed channel strategy and Insight and Metrics
Out of scope: Consumer analysis (CM-1-3-1-3) | credit assessment (batch 11 boundary) | supply-economics segmentation (CM-1-2-6-1-1) | channel strategy decisions (CM-1-3-2-4, CM-1-3-3-3) | account management execution (CM-1-3-6)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Customer and marketer volume, margin, and relationship data | channel economics | market context (CM-1-3-1-1)"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed customer-and-marketer findings"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "B2B and channel decisions built on evidenced understanding of the customers and marketers who move the volume."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-PMM-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Customer And Marketer Value Proposition | enables: Insight and Metrics | informed-by: Market Analysis"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 21 (2026-09-20). Q2 decision: 'marketer' in the downstream fuels sense — branded wholesalers, jobbers, distributors — alongside direct commercial customers; the B2B lens paired with the consumer (B2C) lens at CM-1-3-1-3. Glossary candidate (review): \"Branded Marketer: a wholesaler, jobber, distributor, dealer, or other channel intermediary that markets or resells petroleum products under, alongside, or in association with a branded product, supply, or commercial arrangement\" — defined so it is not confused with the corporate marketing function. Four-lens rule (batch 19) applied: analysis lens here, credit and supply-economics and hierarchy lenses elsewhere. Shared personal-data governance statement and account-management boundary added per review. Shared lifecycle, fourth instance. Batch 21 durable rule (review): \"Marketing Analysis produces governed evidence, insight, hypotheses, and recommendations. It does not itself set strategy, approve investments, establish prices, allocate product, publish the enterprise forecast, define enterprise KPIs, make commercial offers, or execute campaigns.\" APQC candidate is a WEAK generic intelligence match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-1-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "13169" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 13169"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-1 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze competitors in the company's downstream markets — their positions, conduct, capabilities, network moves, and likely responses — through a recurring, governed analysis cycle using legitimately obtained information, so strategy anticipates the competition it will actually face."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.1.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Competitor Analysis"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the recurring competitor-analysis lifecycle: sourcing legitimately obtainable competitor information (CM-1-3-1-5-1); framing cycles (CM-1-3-1-5-2); analyzing competitor positions, conduct, economics, and likely responses (CM-1-3-1-5-3); documenting governed findings (CM-1-3-1-5-4). Competitive intelligence is gathered only from legitimate sources and methods, within competition-law boundaries owned by Legal & Corporate Communications and the compliance framework (batch 16). The process must not solicit, receive, retain, or use competitively sensitive information obtained through improper channels, including non-public competitor pricing, future commercial plans, capacity intentions, customer terms, or strategic information where acquisition or use would breach law, confidentiality obligations, or competition-policy controls. It may not determine competition-law legality independently; concerns are referred to Legal & Corporate Communications and the compliance framework. Excludes market-structure analysis (CM-1-3-1-1); competition-law compliance ownership (Legal, compliance per batch 16); strategy decisions (CM-1-3-2/3); pricing decisions (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3).
In scope: Run the recurring competitor-analysis cycle on legitimate sources | analyze competitor positions, conduct, and likely responses | maintain evidenced competitive understanding | feed strategy and Insight and Metrics
Out of scope: Market-structure analysis (CM-1-3-1-1) | competition-law compliance ownership (Legal, CM-1-2-4-3 framework) | strategy decisions (CM-1-3-2/3) | pricing decisions (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3) | improper-channel information (prohibited; referred to Legal/Compliance)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Public and licensed competitor information | market observations | network and pricing observations from legitimate sources"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed competitor-analysis findings"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Never surprised by a competitor move the evidence already signaled."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-PMM-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Brand Strategy | enables: Insight and Metrics | informed-by: Market Analysis"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 21 (2026-09-20). Q2 partition: competitor conduct and position here; market structure at CM-1-3-1-1. Review rule added verbatim: no solicitation, receipt, retention, or use of competitively sensitive information from improper channels; competition-law legality is never determined independently — concerns go to Legal/Compliance. Follow-up (review): if this branch is operationalized, register a legal/competition-policy source or the approved enterprise competition-law policy — the current CFTC/EIA source set carries no antitrust authority. Shared lifecycle, fifth instance. Batch 21 durable rule (review): \"Marketing Analysis produces governed evidence, insight, hypotheses, and recommendations. It does not itself set strategy, approve investments, establish prices, allocate product, publish the enterprise forecast, define enterprise KPIs, make commercial offers, or execute campaigns.\" APQC candidate is a WEAK pricing match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-10 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Terminal Loading consist of processes such as monitoring customer sales activity and implementing control strategies based upon supply, customer credit and/or contract limitations. Allocation decisions are based on evaluating deals and transaction data to determine which product at the terminal should be made available to the customers. It is responsible for sending locks and unlocks to the terminal when it determines that availability should change."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.10" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Terminal Loading"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-3-2-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11095" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11095"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-2 ;
    skos:definition "Develop and maintain the approved brand strategy for the company's downstream fuels and products marketing — brand positioning, brand architecture, and where and how the brand competes — through a recurring, hypothesis-driven strategy cycle grounded in the governed marketing evidence base and the applicable enterprise delegation and approval process, so brand investments and branded-channel commitments follow deliberate, evidenced, approved direction."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Brand Strategy"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the brand-strategy lifecycle: establishing brand objectives and testable hypotheses (CM-1-3-2-1-2), formulating and evaluating strategic operating alternatives for how the brand goes to market (CM-1-3-2-1-1), developing and testing brand hypotheses against evidence (CM-1-3-2-1-3), and developing and updating the approved brand strategy (CM-1-3-2-1-4). Marketing Strategy develops and maintains approved brand, value-proposition, channel, and customer-experience direction using governed evidence from Marketing Analysis. It does not perform the analyses; design detailed offers, prices, channels, networks, communications, or operating models; plan campaigns and sales; or execute channel, sales, brand, loyalty, or service programs. Material strategic changes follow the applicable enterprise delegation, investment, legal, and governance approvals. Where an arrangement meets the applicable statutory and contractual criteria for a branded motor-fuel franchise, changes to trademark, supply, lease, renewal, termination, or non-renewal arrangements may be constrained by the PMPA and applicable state law. Legal classification, interpretation, notice requirements, termination/non-renewal analysis, and franchise documentation remain the responsibility of Legal & Corporate Communications. Strategy may set the strategic role of price in the proposition or channel — such as value, premium, parity, transparency, index linkage, or loyalty emphasis — but does not establish pricing strategy, price architecture, price rules, price levels, discounts, formula terms, or transaction prices. Those activities belong to Pricing Strategy Management and Sales Pricing. Excludes brand standard enforcement and identity execution (CM-1-3-6-7); marketing communications strategy (CM-1-3-3-5); trademark and licensing legal work (Legal, locked rule; CM-1-3-4-4); campaign planning and execution (CM-1-3-5, CM-1-3-6); the analyses consumed (CM-1-3-1).
In scope: Run the recurring brand-strategy cycle | develop and maintain approved brand positioning and architecture | develop and maintain approved direction on where and how the brand competes, including branded versus unbranded participation | recommend brand investment priorities under the applicable approvals
Out of scope: Brand standard enforcement (CM-1-3-6-7) | marketing communications strategy (CM-1-3-3-5) | trademark/licensing legal work (Legal locked rule; CM-1-3-4-4) | campaign planning/execution (CM-1-3-5/6) | the analyses (CM-1-3-1) | pricing strategy, rules, and levels (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Governed insight (CM-1-3-1-6) | market, company, consumer, and competitor findings | brand performance evidence"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The approved brand strategy with objectives and tested hypotheses"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A deliberate, evidenced, approved answer to where and how the brand competes."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "strategic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-PMPA-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Insight and Metrics | enables: Marketing Communications Strategy | enables: Sales & Distribution Channel Strategy"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 22 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one hypothesis-driven strategy lifecycle (batch 08/21 variant pattern at the strategy layer). Lifecycle order: Establish Brand Objectives and hypothesis → Formulate and Evaluate Strategic Operating Alternative → Develop & Test Brand Hypothesis → Develop / update Brand. Review governance rule: strategy develops, recommends, and maintains approved direction under the applicable delegation of authority — it never unilaterally alters franchise, trademark, or investment commitments. PMPA applicability is fact-dependent (statement verbatim in scope note); Legal owns all franchise legal analysis. Batch 22 durable rule and price-role boundary recorded verbatim in scope note. APQC 'Develop KM strategy' is a false lexical match ('strategy' senses differ)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11168" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11168"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-2 ;
    skos:definition "Develop and maintain the approved value proposition offered to the company's direct customers and branded marketers — the strategic promise concerning supply, brand, program, service, and support value made to commercial customers, resellers, jobbers, distributors, and dealers — through a recurring, hypothesis-driven strategy cycle, so B2B and channel relationships are built on a deliberate, evidenced, approved promise."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Customer And Marketer Value Proposition"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the B2B/channel value-proposition lifecycle: establishing CVP principles and objectives (CM-1-3-2-2-3), formulating and evaluating strategic alternatives for how the proposition is delivered (CM-1-3-2-2-2), testing the proposition with customer and marketer evidence (CM-1-3-2-2-1), and developing and updating the approved proposition strategy (CM-1-3-2-2-4). 'Marketer' carries the batch 21 downstream-fuels reading (Branded Marketer glossary candidate). The proposition is the strategic promise; the Offer Definition, Pricing, Channel Design, and Channel & Partner Management branches turn that promise into actual products, terms, service models, and partner activity. Marketing Strategy develops and maintains approved brand, value-proposition, channel, and customer-experience direction using governed evidence from Marketing Analysis. It does not perform the analyses; design detailed offers, prices, channels, networks, communications, or operating models; plan campaigns and sales; or execute channel, sales, brand, loyalty, or service programs. Material strategic changes follow the applicable enterprise delegation, investment, legal, and governance approvals. Where an arrangement meets the applicable statutory and contractual criteria for a branded motor-fuel franchise, changes to trademark, supply, lease, renewal, termination, or non-renewal arrangements may be constrained by the PMPA and applicable state law. Legal classification, interpretation, notice requirements, termination/non-renewal analysis, and franchise documentation remain the responsibility of Legal & Corporate Communications. Strategy may set the strategic role of price in the proposition or channel — such as value, premium, parity, transparency, index linkage, or loyalty emphasis — but does not establish pricing strategy, price architecture, price rules, price levels, discounts, formula terms, or transaction prices. Those activities belong to Pricing Strategy Management and Sales Pricing. Excludes the consumer proposition (CM-1-3-2-3); offer definition (CM-1-3-3-1); pricing (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3); account and partner management (CM-1-3-6-1/-2); customer and marketer analysis (CM-1-3-1-4).
In scope: Run the recurring B2B/channel value-proposition cycle | develop and maintain the approved strategic promise to direct customers and branded marketers | test the proposition against counterparty evidence | keep the approved proposition current for offer design and channel management
Out of scope: Consumer proposition (CM-1-3-2-3) | offer definition (CM-1-3-3-1) | pricing (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3) | account/partner management (CM-1-3-6-1/-2) | the analysis (CM-1-3-1-4)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Customer and marketer findings (CM-1-3-1-4) | governed insight (CM-1-3-1-6) | channel economics"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The approved customer-and-marketer value proposition"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A deliberate, evidenced, approved promise to the customers and marketers who move the volume."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "strategic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-PMPA-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Customer And Marketer Analysis | enables: Customer Offer Definition | enables: Sales & Distribution Channel Strategy"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 22 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one hypothesis-driven VP lifecycle (template shared with CM-1-3-2-3; batch 08/21 variant pattern). Lifecycle order: Establish CVP principles and objectives → Formulate and Evaluate Strategic Operating Alternatives (CVP) → Test Customer value proposition → Develop/Update Strategy (CVP). 'Marketer' per the batch 21 Branded Marketer glossary candidate. Review wording: the strategic promise concerning supply, brand, program, service, and support value — operational promises live with the design and management branches. PMPA applicability fact-dependent (verbatim in scope note). Batch 22 durable rule and price-role boundary recorded verbatim in scope note. APQC 'Define offering and customer value proposition' scored STRONG at 3b with no validator agreement — treated as genuine comparative anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-2-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11168" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11168"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-2 ;
    skos:definition "Develop and maintain the approved value proposition offered to end consumers in the company's downstream markets — the strategic promise concerning fuel, convenience, loyalty, and experience value — through a recurring, hypothesis-driven strategy cycle grounded in governed consumer evidence, so consumer-facing offers and experiences deliver a deliberate, approved promise."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Consumer Value Proposition"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the consumer (B2C) value-proposition lifecycle: establishing principles and objectives (CM-1-3-2-3-3), formulating and evaluating delivery alternatives (CM-1-3-2-3-2), testing with consumer evidence (CM-1-3-2-3-1), and developing and updating the approved proposition strategy (CM-1-3-2-3-4). Consumer evidence comes from Consumer Analysis (CM-1-3-1-3) under its privacy and publication controls (batch 21 shared statement; aggregate/de-identified default). The proposition is the strategic promise; concrete offers, network, and experience designs live at CM-1-3-3-1/-4 and CM-1-3-2-5; loyalty operation at CM-1-3-6-6. Marketing Strategy develops and maintains approved brand, value-proposition, channel, and customer-experience direction using governed evidence from Marketing Analysis. It does not perform the analyses; design detailed offers, prices, channels, networks, communications, or operating models; plan campaigns and sales; or execute channel, sales, brand, loyalty, or service programs. Material strategic changes follow the applicable enterprise delegation, investment, legal, and governance approvals. Strategy may set the strategic role of price in the proposition or channel — such as value, premium, parity, transparency, index linkage, or loyalty emphasis — but does not establish pricing strategy, price architecture, price rules, price levels, discounts, formula terms, or transaction prices. Those activities belong to Pricing Strategy Management and Sales Pricing. Excludes the B2B/marketer proposition (CM-1-3-2-2); offer definition (CM-1-3-3-1); retail network design (CM-1-3-3-4); loyalty operation (CM-1-3-6-6); consumer analysis (CM-1-3-1-3).
In scope: Run the recurring consumer value-proposition cycle | develop and maintain the approved fuel, convenience, loyalty, and experience promise | test the proposition against governed consumer evidence | keep the approved proposition current for offer and experience design
Out of scope: B2B/marketer proposition (CM-1-3-2-2) | offer definition (CM-1-3-3-1) | retail network design (CM-1-3-3-4) | loyalty operation (CM-1-3-6-6) | the analysis (CM-1-3-1-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Consumer findings (CM-1-3-1-3) | governed insight (CM-1-3-1-6) | market findings (CM-1-3-1-1)"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The approved consumer value proposition"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A deliberate, evidenced, approved promise to the consumer at the pump and in the store."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "strategic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-PMM-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Consumer Analysis | enables: Customer Offer Definition | enables: Customer Experience Strategy"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 22 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — the consumer instance of the VP lifecycle template (shared with CM-1-3-2-2). Lifecycle order: Establish CVP principles and objectives → Formulate and Evaluate Strategic Operating Alternatives (CVP) → Test Customer value proposition → Develop/Update Strategy (CVP). Review naming decision: the child names reuse the template's 'Customer'/'CVP' wording under the Consumer cluster — consumer-directional normalized labels (Test Consumer Value Proposition; Formulate and Evaluate Consumer Value Proposition Alternatives; Establish Consumer Value Proposition Principles and Objectives; Develop and Update Consumer Value Proposition Strategy) queued for the naming pass to prevent B2C/B2B ontology confusion. Consumer evidence under the batch 21 privacy controls. Batch 22 durable rule and price-role boundary recorded verbatim in scope note. APQC 'Define offering and customer value proposition' is the comparative anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10464" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10464"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3 ;
    skos:definition "Define the concrete customer offers that deliver the approved value propositions — which customer groups are targeted, what each offer contains, how it is validated, and how its performance will be measured — through a recurring offer-design cycle, so execution sells defined, validated offers rather than improvised ones."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Customer Offer Definition"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the offer-definition lifecycle: defining target customer groups (CM-1-3-3-1-1), defining the customer offer (CM-1-3-3-1-2), validating it with evidence (CM-1-3-3-1-3), and defining its measurement framework (CM-1-3-3-1-4). Designs within approved strategic direction: the value propositions (CM-1-3-2-2/-3) and brand strategy (CM-1-3-2-1) set the promise; this cluster turns the promise into defined offers. Defined offers become executable only after required commercial, pricing, legal, operational, product/service, and approval controls are satisfied. Offer price terms are designed with, and governed by, Pricing Strategy Management (CM-1-3-3-2) and executed by the pricing processes; product and service development beyond offer assembly sits at CM-1-3-4-3; campaign and sales execution at CM-1-3-5/6. Excludes value-proposition strategy (CM-1-3-2-2/-3); pricing strategy, rules, and levels (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3); product/service development (CM-1-3-4-3); campaign planning/execution (CM-1-3-5/6); enterprise KPI definition (KPI workstream).
In scope: Run the recurring offer-definition cycle | define target customer groups on the marketing lens | define and validate concrete offers from the approved propositions | define offer measurement frameworks with KPI Store routing
Out of scope: VP strategy (CM-1-3-2-2/-3) | pricing (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3) | product development (CM-1-3-4-3) | campaign/sales execution (CM-1-3-5/6) | enterprise KPI definition (KPI workstream) | offer launch approval (applicable approval controls)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved value propositions (CM-1-3-2-2/-3) | governed insight (CM-1-3-1-6) | offer performance evidence"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Defined, validated customer offers with measurement frameworks"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The approved promise turned into offers a salesperson can actually sell."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Customer And Marketer Value Proposition | informed-by: Consumer Value Proposition | enables: Sales Execution"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one offer-design lifecycle; horizon tactical (design layer). Lifecycle order: Define Target Customer Groups → Define Customer Offer → Validate Customer Offer → Define Offer Measurement Framework. Middle layer of the batch 22 three-layer rule: designs within approved strategic direction, never re-decides it. Launch control per review: defined does not mean sellable — the applicable approval controls gate executability. APQC 'Negotiate offer' is a FALSE match (HR job-offer negotiation)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10344" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10344"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3 ;
    skos:definition "Develop and maintain the approved retail-network design—operating-model alternatives, target footprint, outlet formats, market coverage, and location criteria—within approved channel strategy and investment constraints, so site investment and network-development processes act against an evidence-based network blueprint."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Network Design (Retail)"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the retail network-design lifecycle: formulating and evaluating network strategic operating alternatives (CM-1-3-3-4-2), defining target network size and formats (CM-1-3-3-4-3), defining target locations (CM-1-3-3-4-1), and defining the network measurement framework (CM-1-3-3-4-4), with size and location iterating within a cycle and evidence feeding back into the next cycle. Network Design recommends and maintains approved footprint direction, target size, outlet formats, trade-area criteria, and prioritized location targets. It does not authorize capital expenditure, acquire or divest sites, negotiate leases, execute dealer conversions, approve project business cases, or operate retail locations. Designs within the approved channel strategy (CM-1-3-2-4) and channel design (CM-1-3-3-3); changes affecting existing branded dealer arrangements inherit the batch 22 fact-dependent PMPA statement, with franchise legal analysis reserved to Legal & Corporate Communications. Excludes site acquisition, divestment, lease negotiation, and project approval (Investment, Real Estate, Legal, Finance, delegated authorities); dealer conversion execution (channel management, Legal); site engineering, construction, maintenance, and EHS standards (their owners, batch 20 architecture); retail operations (designated operating functions); channel strategy and mix (CM-1-3-2-4); terminal and distribution networks (CM-1-1, locked rules).
In scope: Run the recurring network-design cycle | design the network operating model, target size, outlet formats, and location criteria within approved strategy | recommend and maintain approved footprint direction | define network measurement with KPI Store routing
Out of scope: Capital expenditure authorization, site acquisition/divestment, and leases (Investment, Real Estate, Finance, delegated authorities) | dealer conversion execution (channel management, Legal) | site engineering/EHS standards (their owners) | retail operations (operating functions) | channel strategy and mix (CM-1-3-2-4) | terminal/distribution networks (CM-1-1, locked rules)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved channel strategy (CM-1-3-2-4) | channel design outputs (CM-1-3-3-3) | market and consumer findings (CM-1-3-1-1/-3) | network performance evidence"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The approved retail-network design: operating model, target size, formats, and location criteria"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A retail footprint that was designed on evidence before it was funded."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-PMPA-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Sales & Distribution Channel Design | informed-by: Consumer Value Proposition | enables: Channel & Partner Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 24 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one network-design lifecycle; tactical. Review definition adopted verbatim: a blueprint/target-setting process, never investment execution (no-capex scope statement verbatim in scope note). Lifecycle per review: Evaluate network operating alternatives → Define provisional target size and formats ↔ Define and prioritize location criteria/targets → Define network measurement framework → feed evidence back into the next design cycle; size and location iterate — the recorded precedes relation is a logical dependency, not a one-way sequence. PMPA fact-dependent per the batch 22 statement. APQC 'Design logistics network' STRONG, no validator — adjacent match (logistics network, not retail network); comparative only."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16824" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16824"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4 ;
    skos:definition "Manage the lifecycle and portfolio of downstream products, services, and R&D initiatives by identifying technology needs, valuing and assessing portfolio elements, and developing evidence-based stage, stop, accelerate, extend, reposition, and retirement recommendations for the applicable delegated authorities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Lifecycle Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the portfolio-and-lifecycle cycle: identifying technology needs and opportunities (CM-1-3-4-5-1), valuing projects and portfolio elements (CM-1-3-4-5-2), assessing portfolio composition and lifecycle status (CM-1-3-4-5-3), and developing portfolio recommendations and tracking delegated decisions (CM-1-3-4-5-4), with approved changes fed to change control, development, and operations. The portfolio split per review: the product and service portfolio (in-market offerings, lifecycle stage, performance, retire/reposition/extend decisions), the R&D portfolio (concept, research, and development programs; stage/stop/accelerate priorities), and technology needs (capability gaps feeding R&D planning) are managed here; the IP portfolio is separate under Licensing & IP (CM-1-3-4-4-6). Extension includes reinvestment, renovation, reformulation, repackaging, and relaunch candidates. Lifecycle recommendations affecting in-market products or services must be translated into effective-dated change, run-out, customer/partner communication, contract, pricing, tax, master-data, inventory, supply, quality, regulatory, and operating transition plans by the accountable processes. Commercial valuation evidence follows the b21 discipline; financial valuation policy is Finance's (lock). Excludes investment and portfolio approval (delegated authorities); change implementation (CM-1-3-4-3-3, accountable processes); development execution (CM-1-3-4-3); financial valuation policy (Finance); intellectual-asset portfolios (CM-1-3-4-4-6); enterprise KPI definition (KPI workstream).
In scope: Run the portfolio-and-lifecycle cycle | identify technology needs and opportunities | value and assess the product, service, and R&D portfolios | develop stage/stop/accelerate/extend/reposition/retire recommendations and track delegated decisions
Out of scope: Investment/portfolio approval (delegated authorities) | change implementation (CM-1-3-4-3-3, accountable processes) | development execution (CM-1-3-4-3) | financial valuation policy (Finance) | IP portfolios (CM-1-3-4-4-6) | enterprise KPI definition (KPI workstream)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Development and market evidence (CM-1-3-4-3, CM-1-3-1) | sensing signals (CM-1-3-4-1-2) | portfolio performance data"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Evidence-based portfolio and lifecycle recommendations for the delegated authorities"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A portfolio that is composed on purpose, pruned on evidence."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Product & Service Development | enables: Manage Product/Service Change | informed-by: Insight and Metrics"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 26 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one identify-value-assess-recommend portfolio cycle; horizon periodic. Review definition adopted verbatim (adds 'extend' — reinvestment, renovation, reformulation, repackaging, relaunch); portfolio split recorded (product/service vs R&D vs technology needs vs IP-separate); transition statement verbatim — recommendations become effective-dated transition plans through the accountable processes (the batch 25 change-control link made concrete). Lifecycle order: Identify Technology Needs/Opportunities → Value Projects/Portfolios → Assess Portfolios → Manage R&D Portfolios (develop recommendations and track decisions). APQC candidate is a WEAK broad match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16824" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16824"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4 ;
    skos:definition "Coordinate the collaboration channels through which product and service innovation works with manufacturing, customers and technical service, research partners, and marketing — consistent intake, IP, and confidentiality controls across all channels — so collaboration accelerates innovation without leaking assets or blurring operational boundaries."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "R&D Collaboration"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the collaboration channel set: manufacturing collaboration (CM-1-3-4-6-1), customer and technical-service collaboration (CM-1-3-4-6-2), research-partner collaboration (CM-1-3-4-6-3), and marketing collaboration (CM-1-3-4-6-4) — a parallel variant set integrated at this L4 (consistency of intake, IP, and confidentiality controls; prioritization; conflict resolution — the batch 24 integration pattern). No collaboration channel creates an implied confidentiality, ownership, compensation, exclusivity, procurement, licensing, co-development, publication, data-sharing, or evaluation obligation outside approved intake, Legal/IP, Procurement, Privacy, Security, and delegated-authority controls. All channels operate under the intellectual-asset framework (CM-1-3-4-4-1) with agreements drafted and executed by Legal and sourced through Procurement (batch 25 boundary), and the batch 21 privacy and competitor-information controls where customer or market information is involved. Excludes collaboration agreement drafting/execution (Legal, Procurement); the development work itself (CM-1-3-4-3); operational direction of any counterpart function (their owners, locked rules); IP framework ownership (CM-1-3-4-4-1, Legal).
In scope: Coordinate the four collaboration channels with consistent intake, IP, and confidentiality controls | integrate collaboration input into the innovation processes | prioritize and resolve conflicts across channels
Out of scope: Agreement drafting/execution (Legal, Procurement) | development execution (CM-1-3-4-3) | operational direction of counterpart functions (their owners, locked rules) | IP framework ownership (CM-1-3-4-4-1, Legal)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Innovation priorities (CM-1-3-4-1) | IP and confidentiality framework (CM-1-3-4-4-1) | channel inputs"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed, integrated collaboration across the four channels"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Collaboration that speeds innovation up without giving its assets away."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Visioning And Implementation Planning | enables: Concept & Feasibility Study | enables: Product & Service Development"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 26 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — parallel collaboration variant set integrated at the L4 (batch 24 pattern); children continuous (standing channels). Review collaboration rule verbatim in scope note — no channel creates implied obligations outside the approved control set; strengthens the batch 25 external-idea boundary. APQC candidate is a WEAK broad match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10103" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10103"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Sales Planning"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5 ;
    skos:definition "Plan the sales effort that delivers the approved strategies — the agreed sales-input process into the governed demand forecast, allocated sales targets, account plans, and the margin-gap feedback loop — so sales execution runs against deliberate, evidenced plans rather than improvised quotas."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sales Strategy & Planning"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the sales-planning cycle: the enduring sales-side forecast-input operating agreement (CM-1-3-5-1-2), determining and allocating sales targets (CM-1-3-5-1-1), performing account planning (CM-1-3-5-1-3), and analyzing gaps between forecast, plan, targets, and actual margin performance (CM-1-3-5-1-4) as the feedback loop into the next cycle and forecast-input improvements. The enterprise demand forecast is produced, published, changed, versioned, and governed only by Produce Demand Forecast (CM-1-1-1-1). The sales organization supplies agreed sales intelligence, account plans, pipeline evidence, promotion calendars, channel signals, and other approved inputs to that process. Sales targets are management-accountability commitments allocated from approved plans, not forecasts; they must never be relabeled, substituted for, or used to overwrite the governed demand forecast. Plans within approved direction: sales strategy direction is owned by the strategy branch (CM-1-3-2-4, CM-1-3-2-2, batch 22) — this cluster is sales planning, not strategy; sales force sizing assumptions come from the channel structure designs (CM-1-3-3-3-4, batch 23 headcount boundary — HR and management own headcount). Excludes sales strategy direction (CM-1-3-2, batch 22); the enterprise demand forecast (CM-1-1-1-1, locked); sales execution (CM-1-3-6-2); pricing (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3); channel/partner management (CM-1-3-6-1); headcount and appointments (HR, management); quote/contract processes (CM-1-3-7-2).
In scope: Run the sales-planning cycle | maintain the agreed sales input process into the governed forecast | allocate targets from approved plans | plan accounts | analyze forecast/plan/target/actual margin gaps as feedback
Out of scope: Sales strategy direction (CM-1-3-2, batch 22) | enterprise demand forecast (CM-1-1-1-1, locked) | sales execution (CM-1-3-6-2) | pricing (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3) | partner management (CM-1-3-6-1) | headcount (HR, management) | quotes/contracts (CM-1-3-7-2)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved strategies (CM-1-3-2) | governed demand forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) | channel and offer designs (CM-1-3-3) | margin actuals (governed sources)"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Agreed sales plans: input process, targets, and account plans"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A sales effort planned on evidence, measured against its own plan."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Sales & Distribution Channel Strategy | uses-input: Produce Demand Forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) | enables: Sales Execution"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one sales-planning cycle with a feedback loop; tactical. Review label decision: strategy direction is owned upstream (batch 22), so the preferred label 'Sales Planning' is queued prominently to prevent scope creep from the locked 'Sales Strategy & Planning' name. Canonical forecast-versus-targets rule verbatim in scope note. Glossary distinctions (review): demand forecast = governed prediction produced through Produce Demand Forecast; sales target = management commitment or plan expectation allocated to a segment, channel, account group, or period; quota = individual/team performance commitment in performance-management or incentive context; sales plan = planned actions, account priorities, resources, and commercial activity to pursue targets; actual = governed realized volume, margin, revenue, cost, or activity evidence from accountable systems/processes. Lifecycle: forecast-input agreement (enduring) enables targets and account planning → margin-gap analysis → next cycle. APQC 'Develop sales strategy' STRONG, no validator — genuine anchor, direction owned at CM-1-3-2."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19243" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19243"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5 ;
    skos:definition "Plan the promotions program — the promotional plan and the event, point-of-purchase, and site-level marketing calendars — within the pricing, claims, and POS boundaries and the conditional readiness framework, so promotion execution runs from one governed promotional calendar set."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Promotions Planning"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers promotions planning: defining the promotional plan (CM-1-3-5-4-1) and maintaining the promotional event calendar (CM-1-3-5-4-2), the point-of-purchase promotional calendar (CM-1-3-5-4-3), and the site-level marketing calendar (CM-1-3-5-4-4), with the calendars synchronizing iteratively against supply, inventory, site, and execution readiness. Conditional readiness framework (review): before a promotion enters a calendar or execution handoff, the plan confirms the applicable readiness — offer and eligibility defined; pricing rule and price-execution path approved; rebate/coupon/loyalty mechanics controlled; claims and disclosures approved; supply and inventory readiness validated; channel, site, and terminal readiness confirmed; POS, digital, and communications deliverables approved; tax, legal, privacy, and regulatory impacts assessed where applicable; measurement and rollback conditions defined. Guardrails: promotional pricing follows the pricing processes — architecture and guardrails at CM-1-3-3-2, execution at CM-1-3-6-3; rebates at CM-1-3-6-4; POP and site calendars inherit the batch 24 POS exclusions (no fuel-price setting, pump-sign configuration, legal metrology, product labeling, or price-display execution); promotional claims follow the b24 substantiation controls; promotion execution is CM-1-3-6-5's. Calendars synchronize with the communications plan (CM-1-3-5-3-6) and feed the forecast-input process (CM-1-3-5-1-2). Excludes promotional pricing (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3); rebates (CM-1-3-6-4); promotion execution (CM-1-3-6-5); POS regulatory display execution (site operations, b24); claim approval (Legal, Compliance).
In scope: Define the promotional plan | maintain the event, POP, and site-level calendars synchronized against readiness | confirm the conditional readiness framework before calendar entry and execution handoff | operate within pricing, claims, and POS boundaries
Out of scope: Promotional pricing (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3) | rebates (CM-1-3-6-4) | execution (CM-1-3-6-5) | POS display execution (site operations, b24) | claim approval (Legal, Compliance)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Offers and campaigns (CM-1-3-3-1, CM-1-3-5-3) | pricing guardrails (CM-1-3-3-2) | supply, site, and execution readiness inputs"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The promotional plan and governed, readiness-confirmed calendar set"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Promotions that land on calendar, on price policy, and on claim."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Customer Offer Definition | enables: Marketing Plan Execution | enables: Develop & Agree Demand Forecasting Process"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — plan plus three parallel calendars with iterative readiness synchronization (review); tactical. Conditional readiness framework recorded once at this L4 per review — not repeated in every child definition. APQC 'Conduct resource planning' is a WEAK generic match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10141" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10141"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6 ;
    skos:definition "Manage the company's channel partner relationships — franchisees, licensed-brand providers, dealers, jobbers, and marketers — executing the designed engagement models, coordinating partner qualification and activation through the accountable control owners, reporting partner performance, and developing partner capability."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Channel & Partner Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers channel partner relationship execution: managing franchisee relationships under their franchise agreements (CM-1-3-6-1-1), managing licensed-brand provider relationships (CM-1-3-6-1-2), reporting franchise performance (CM-1-3-6-1-3), and training and certifying franchise site managers (CM-1-3-6-1-4). Executes the engagement models designed at CM-1-3-3-3-4 and the channel offerings created at CM-1-3-3-3-1. Onboarding model (review): this process owns the commercial side — coordinate commercial partner qualification, selection recommendation, onboarding readiness, and relationship activation through the applicable delegated approvals, Credit, Compliance, Legal, Contract Management, Master Data, training, and operating-readiness processes — while each control retains its owner: credit review and limits with the credit process (b11); compliance/KYC/sanctions with Compliance, Legal, and the counterparty process; contract drafting, review, and execution with Legal; contract record administration with Contract Management where applicable; master-data setup with Manage Master Data (CM-1-3-7-1); brand/operator training via CM-1-3-6-1-4; site and operating readiness with the operating functions; final onboarding approval with the delegated authority. Where an arrangement meets the applicable statutory and contractual criteria for a motor-fuel franchise, changes involving trademark, supply, lease, renewal, termination, or non-renewal may be subject to PMPA and state-law constraints. Legal & Corporate Communications determines applicability, notice requirements, legal analysis, documentation, and formal legal action. Brand standards are owned at CM-1-3-6-7 (applied and reinforced here); rebates at CM-1-3-6-4. Excludes channel strategy and design (CM-1-3-2-4, CM-1-3-3-3); franchise and licensing legal work (Legal, locked rule); credit decisions (b11 boundary); compliance/KYC ownership (Compliance, Legal); master-data setup (CM-1-3-7-1); brand standards ownership (CM-1-3-6-7); rebate administration (CM-1-3-6-4); pricing (CM-1-3-6-3); site operations (operating functions).
In scope: Execute the designed partner engagement models | manage franchisee and licensed-brand relationships within their legal frameworks | coordinate commercial partner qualification, selection recommendation, onboarding readiness, and relationship activation through the accountable control owners | report partner performance and develop partner capability
Out of scope: Channel strategy/design (CM-1-3-2-4, CM-1-3-3-3) | franchise/licensing legal work (Legal, locked rule) | credit decisions (b11) | compliance/KYC ownership (Compliance, Legal) | master-data setup (CM-1-3-7-1) | brand standards ownership (CM-1-3-6-7) | rebates (CM-1-3-6-4) | pricing (CM-1-3-6-3) | site operations (operating functions)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Engagement models and channel offerings (CM-1-3-3-3) | account plans (CM-1-3-5-1-3) | partner performance data | Legal-executed agreements"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Managed partner relationships with coordinated activation, performance reporting, and capability development"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Partner relationships run the way the channel design intended."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-PMPA-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Sales & Distribution Channel Design | uses-input: Perform Account Planning | enables: Sales Execution"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 28 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — the partner-relationship execution cluster; tactical, with parallel relationship, reporting, and training children (no strict lifecycle, per review). Review onboarding correction: batch 23 excluded individual partner selection from Channel Design without assigning all onboarding here — this process coordinates the commercial side while Credit, Compliance, Legal, Contract Management, Master Data, training, and operating-readiness controls stay with their owners, and the delegated authority grants final onboarding approval. PMPA fact-dependence per the review wording verbatim. APQC 'Develop partner and alliance management strategies' is a WEAK strategy-side match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Operational Accounting includes processes that denotes the ledger like capability leveraged by the users for the ease of auditing and record keeping."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Operational Accounting"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Receivables Resolution"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8 ;
    skos:definition "The capability that resolves what customers owe but have not paid: adjudicating disputed billed amounts against the evidence chain, collecting undisputed overdue receivables under the approved collection strategy, executing Finance-approved expected-credit-loss estimation and write-off processes, and turning dispute and collection patterns into root-cause action plans with the owning processes — concurrent controls with distinct authorities and cadences."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Collection and Disputes"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Receivables Resolution Separation Rule: Dispute Management adjudicates the validity of billed amounts using the governed evidence chain and authorized decision rights. Collection Management recovers undisputed, due receivables under approved collection strategy and does not adjudicate validity, alter bills, grant credits, or write off debt. Finance determines expected-credit-loss allowances and authorizes/accounting-records write-offs under approved methodology and delegation. A billing correction, credit memo, refund, settlement adjustment, concession, or write-off must be initiated, approved, documented, and executed by its accountable process. No function may silently net, absorb, forgive, or reclassify a receivable outside approved rules and audit trails. Only the disputed component is held from collection where policy requires; undisputed amounts remain subject to collection. Credit exposure, availability, holds, and service treatment follow the applicable Credit policy and customer-specific decision. Cash recovered arrives only through the cash-application controls; payment plans and concessions only within the applicable Credit/Finance/Legal authority; agency and legal action only through Legal and executed agreements.
In scope: Dispute intake, investigation, and adjudication | collection strategy execution on undisputed receivables | Finance-approved allowance estimation and write-off execution | root-cause analysis and owner-agreed action plans | complete case and decision records
Out of scope: Credit-memo and correction issuance (CM-1-3-8-1 controlled documents, on instruction) | cash application (CM-1-3-8-2) | credit limits and credit-driven closure (CM-1-3-7-4) | legal action conduct and debt-forgiveness advice (Legal) | allowance methodology and accounting elections (Finance governance) | service-request handling (CM-1-3-7-5)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Routed disputes, short-pays, and deductions (b35/b36/b37) | overdue receivables and aging | the governed evidence chain (billing, fulfillment, contracts, tax) | Finance methodology and delegations"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Adjudicated disputes, recovered receivables, Finance-determined allowances and write-offs, and owner-agreed corrective actions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every unpaid dollar is either validly disputed, actively collected, or deliberately provided for — never ignored."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage Cash Application, A/R & Revenue Accounting | uses-input: Monitor Credit Against Limit | enables: Period End Processing"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Cross-functional — Finance / O2C Operations / Credit / Legal"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 38 (2026-09-21). Reclassified to capability per review — dispute adjudication, continuous collection, periodic CECL estimation, and periodic prevention run concurrently with separate authorities (the standing capability test); canonical Receivables Resolution Separation Rule adopted verbatim as a durable financial-control decision; disputed-portion-only hold rule adopted verbatim. Preferred label 'Manage Receivables Resolution' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:L0-downstream-operations a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:definition "The operating businesses and value-chain domains in scope for the enterprise's downstream oil and gas model: Commercial & Marketing, Refining, and the Midstream transportation, terminaling, storage, and bulk-logistics activities that support downstream supply, distribution, and delivery of hydrocarbon feedstocks and refined products."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:prefLabel "Downstream Operations"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Scheme root, not a process or capability: groups the operating business domains in scope for the downstream enterprise model: Commercial & Marketing, Refining, and Midstream activities where they support downstream supply, terminaling, storage, transportation, distribution, and delivery. It carries no activities, processes, authority, ownership, controls, assets, or decision rights of its own. Functions that enable, govern, or provide enterprise services to these operating domains are grouped under the sibling root Enabling Functions. This root does not assert that all industry Midstream activity is downstream; it defines the Midstream scope included in this enterprise taxonomy."@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf mod:core ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "not-process"@en ;
    intake:level "L0"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Anchor the operating side of the enterprise downstream taxonomy — a navigation root grouping Commercial & Marketing, Refining, and in-scope Midstream activities."@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-W3C-SKOS-001"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 42 (2026-09-21). Scheme root approved per the structural-anchor review decision (definition + scope note; no horizon, domain, or relationships; no decision authority). concept_type_check 'not-process' approved under the SCHEME_ROOT_APPROVED gate amendment (option A, Hamid); primary_purpose and reference_sources populated because the post-#31 gate requires them on newly approved rows. Midstream scope wording per review: in-scope Midstream activities support the downstream model; no categorical industry claim."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10240" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10240"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Product Demand Forecasting"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Develop, agree, and publish the recurring product demand forecast that downstream commercial planning runs on — expected demand by product, market, location, and period — by combining a statistical baseline with customer input and market intelligence, and monitor forecast performance so the published forecast remains a reliable planning basis."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Produce Demand Forecast"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the recurring demand-forecast production cycle: preparing forecasting master data and history, developing the statistical baseline, adjusting it with customer input and market intelligence, publishing the approved forecast with its component-level breakdown, and monitoring forecast performance. Its output is the single approved demand forecast consumed by Regional Optimization, Refinery Optimization, and Supply And Trading. Excludes deciding how demand is met — supply, production, inventory, and allocation choices are owned by Regional Optimization (CM-1-1-2); excludes capturing or committing customer orders, owned by Order Management (CM-1-3-7); excludes Supply And Trading's operational refined-product demand forecasting (Forecast Refined Product Demand, CM-1-2-6-2-3), bounded per the batch 03 decision; excludes designing and agreeing the cross-functional forecasting process with sales, owned by Develop & Agree Demand Forecasting Process (CM-1-3-5-1-2).
In scope: Prepare and validate forecasting inputs | develop the statistical baseline forecast | incorporate customer input and market intelligence | publish the approved forecast and component-level forecast plan | monitor forecast accuracy, bias, and adjustment effect
Out of scope: Supply, production, inventory, and allocation decisions (Regional Optimization, CM-1-1-2) | customer order capture and commitment (Order Management, CM-1-3-7) | trading-side receipt and use of the published forecast (Receive Demand Forecast, CM-1-2-1-1-2) | Supply And Trading operational refined-product demand forecasting (Forecast Refined Product Demand, CM-1-2-6-2-3) | sales forecasting process design (Develop & Agree Demand Forecasting Process, CM-1-3-5-1-2)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "cleansed demand history | customer plans and commitments | market intelligence | product and market master data"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "approved and published demand forecast with component-level forecast plan"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Produce and maintain the single approved product demand forecast that downstream commercial planning runs on."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-IEA-OMR-001 | SRC-EIA-STEO-001 | SRC-ORP-DPSC-001 | SRC-ASCM-CSCP-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Regional Optimization | enables: Capture Product Demand Requirements | enables: Receive Demand Forecast"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 03 (2026-09-19). Q1 decision (Hamid, 2026-09-19, Option A): the L4 carries the same label as its L3 parent (CM-1-1-1) and is its only child; authored in place as the operational forecast-production cycle, with the L3 remaining the domain umbrella — no tree change, consistent with the restraint precedent on PTC-001; a later naming pass may disambiguate the duplicate prefLabels. Q2 decision (Hamid, 2026-09-19, Option A): binding constraint on the future definition of CM-1-2-6-2-3 — it must be scoped as Supply And Trading's operational demand forecasting within Refined Product Supply Chain Management and must not claim ownership of the approved commercial planning forecast; same decide-versus-advise pattern as PTC-002. Evidence basis: the planning-cycle forecast (monthly, product/market level; IEA OMR and EIA STEO practice) is distinct from operational network demand forecasting (short-notice, terminal/station level; Mota et al. 2017). APQC 10240 'Evaluate and revise demand forecasting approach' is REVIEW LINK only; approach-governance content sits closest to CM-1-3-5-1-2, not here. Step 3d naming pass (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Demand Forecasting' to 'Produce Demand Forecast' — critical semantic-collision resolution. Prior label not retained as altLabel: the L3 keeps it as prefLabel."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-2-7 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Perform Product Evaluations - COP / ACOP"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.2.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Product Evaluations - COP / ACOP"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-5-7 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-5 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze backcast run outcomes and communicate learnings"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.5.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Compile and communicate learnings by business decisions and functions"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20682" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20682"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Trade Confirmation Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2 ;
    skos:definition "Independently confirm and match executed trades with counterparties by generating, receiving, validating, documenting, modifying, reporting, and resolving discrepancies in trade confirmations, so the organization and its counterparty have evidenced agreement on the applicable trade terms."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Confirmations Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the per-trade confirmation lifecycle after capture: outgoing confirmation generation, incoming confirmation matching, documentation control, modification processing, continuation reporting where required, and dispute resolution. This process performs an independent control over the trade-capture record. The enterprise may operate that control through a middle-office, operations, shared-services, or other designated function; this taxonomy does not prescribe the organizational delivery model. Excludes deal capture and its corrections, owned by Trade Capture (CM-1-2-1-3); excludes master agreement establishment and administration, owned by Contract Management (CM-1-2-1-2); excludes settlement and settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1, CM-1-2-4-1-17); excludes risk measurement (Market Risk Management, CM-1-2-2-3).
In scope: Generate and match trade confirmations | maintain confirmation documentation and evidence | process agreed trade modifications through re-confirmation | report confirmation and continuation events where required | resolve confirmation disputes
Out of scope: Deal capture and capture corrections (CM-1-2-1-3) | master agreement administration (CM-1-2-1-2) | settlement and settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1) | risk measurement and limits (CM-1-2-2-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "captured deal records | counterparty confirmations and acknowledgements | governing agreement terms"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "matched, evidenced trade confirmations"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Establish timely, retained evidence that the organization and counterparty agree — or have a controlled, visible disagreement — on the applicable trade terms."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ISDA-MA-001 | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Trade Capture | precedes: Settlements | governed-by: Regulatory & Compliance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 10 (2026-09-19). Concept type: process (Hamid confirmed) — a per-trade lifecycle (generate, document, modify, report, dispute), per the batch 08 variant test. Reworded at Hamid's direction to describe an organizationally neutral independent control rather than prescribing a middle-office delivery model — the approved L3 definition's 'middle office' names the control function, not a mandated organization. Confirmation structure per ISDA documentation for applicable OTC frameworks; physical and other deal types are confirmed per their governing agreements, standard terms, exchange rules, or platform matching. APQC 20682 is an unrelated IT candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-2 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Strategy process establishes target markets to work in and, at a micro level, provides the strategy for where store locations should be established. For B2B this considers supply envelopes & establishes target geographic markets."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Strategy"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10123" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10123"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3 ;
    skos:definition "Establish and manage the pricing strategy for downstream marketing — the strategic pricing approach, the integrated pricing strategy and approved price parameters and guardrails across products, channels, and customer groups, and the piloting and performance feedback that keep it evidenced — within the applicable price-related legal frameworks and approvals, so pricing execution operates against governed, deliberate pricing direction."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pricing Strategy Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the pricing-strategy lifecycle: defining the strategic pricing approach (CM-1-3-3-2-1), developing the integrated pricing strategy (CM-1-3-3-2-2), defining the pricing measurement framework (CM-1-3-3-2-5), conducting pricing pilots (CM-1-3-3-2-4), and analyzing pricing performance (CM-1-3-3-2-3), with pilots and performance analysis operating as a feedback loop into the integrated strategy rather than a rigid sequence. The receiving side of the batch 22 price-role boundary. Develops and maintains approved price architecture, pricing rules, differential logic, index-linkage principles, discount/terms guardrails, and exception criteria under the applicable commercial, Finance, Legal, Compliance, and delegation-of-authority approvals. Approved parameters include price corridors, floors, and ceilings where governed, price-index and timing conventions where applicable, and discount/rebate guardrails — never rebate administration, specific posted prices, or contract rates. Execution stays out: transaction and sales pricing at CM-1-3-6-3; quote and contract pricing at CM-1-3-7-2. Where applicable, pricing strategy must be designed and applied with regard to price-discrimination risk in sales to competing purchasers, applicable defenses, contract structures, product comparability, competitive effects, state motor-fuel below-cost/unfair-sales laws, and other jurisdictional requirements. Legal and Compliance determine applicability and legal sufficiency. No pricing strategy, pilot, or performance analysis may use non-public competitor price, cost, capacity, customer, or future commercial information obtained through improper channels. Public, licensed, independently gathered, and legally permissible observations must be traceable to source and permitted use. Excludes transaction/sales pricing execution (CM-1-3-6-3); quote/contract pricing (CM-1-3-7-2); trading and supply pricing (CM-1-2, its processes); rebate design administration (CM-1-3-6-4); legal ownership of pricing law compliance (Legal, compliance framework); enterprise KPI definition (KPI workstream).
In scope: Run the pricing-strategy lifecycle | develop and maintain approved price architecture, rules, parameters, and guardrails under the applicable approvals | pilot and analyze pricing as a feedback loop | operate within price-related legal frameworks | issue governed pricing direction to execution
Out of scope: Transaction/sales pricing (CM-1-3-6-3) | quote/contract pricing (CM-1-3-7-2) | trading/supply pricing (CM-1-2) | rebate administration (CM-1-3-6-4) | pricing-law ownership (Legal, compliance) | enterprise KPI definition (KPI workstream) | specific posted prices and contract rates (execution)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved strategy direction (CM-1-3-2) | market and competitor findings (CM-1-3-1-1/-5, legitimate sources) | pricing performance evidence | cost-to-serve (CM-1-2-6-1-3)"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The governed pricing strategy: approved architecture, rules, parameters, and guardrails"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Deliberate, evidenced, lawful pricing direction under every posted and negotiated price."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "strategic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FTC-RP-001 | SRC-EIA-PMM-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Insight and Metrics | enables: Sales Pricing Management | enables: Customer Offer Definition"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one strategy-pilot-feedback lifecycle; horizon strategic. Lifecycle per review: Define Strategic Pricing Approach → Develop Integrated Pricing Strategy → Define Pricing Measurement Framework → Conduct Pricing Pilots and/or deploy through execution → Analyze Pricing Performance → feed back to Integrated Pricing Strategy; pilots and performance analysis are evidence sources into the strategy, not a rigid chain. Review governance rule verbatim in scope note: price architecture/rules/guardrails under commercial, Finance, Legal, Compliance, and delegation-of-authority approvals; owns approved price parameters and guardrails, never specific posted prices or contract rates. Robinson-Patman applicability fact- and jurisdiction-dependent (verbatim); competition-information rule standardized (verbatim). APQC 'Define pricing strategy' STRONG, no validator — genuine comparative anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16819" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16819"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3 ;
    skos:definition "Design the sales and distribution channel system that implements the approved channel strategy — segmenting channels, defining the channel portfolio, creating channel-specific offerings, and developing channel structure, size, and engagement models — so channel management executes a designed system rather than an accumulation of arrangements."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sales & Distribution Channel Design"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the channel-design lifecycle: segmenting channels (CM-1-3-3-3-3), defining the channel portfolio (CM-1-3-3-3-2), creating channel offerings and value propositions (CM-1-3-3-3-1), developing channel structure, size, and engagement models (CM-1-3-3-3-4), and defining the channel measurement framework (CM-1-3-3-3-5). Designs within the approved channel strategy (CM-1-3-2-4) and value propositions (CM-1-3-2-2/-3); design changes affecting existing branded dealer or distributor arrangements inherit the batch 22 fact-dependent PMPA statement, with all franchise legal analysis reserved to Legal & Corporate Communications. Excludes selection, onboarding, negotiation, and management of individual channel partners, dealers, jobbers, distributors, or marketers; those processes follow Channel & Partner Management and applicable Contract Management/Legal processes. Retail network design (site-level) sits at CM-1-3-3-4 (batch 24); secondary distribution operations at CM-1-1-3-8 (locked rule). Excludes channel strategy (CM-1-3-2-4); retail network design (CM-1-3-3-4); distribution operations (CM-1-1-3-8, locked rule); franchise legal work (Legal, locked rule); pricing (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3).
In scope: Run the channel-design lifecycle | segment channels and define the channel portfolio within approved strategy | create channel-specific offerings from approved propositions | develop structure, size, and engagement models | define channel measurement with KPI Store routing
Out of scope: Channel strategy (CM-1-3-2-4) | retail network design (CM-1-3-3-4) | individual partner selection, onboarding, negotiation, and management (CM-1-3-6-1, Contract Management/Legal) | distribution operations (CM-1-1-3-8, locked rule) | franchise legal work (Legal) | pricing (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved channel strategy (CM-1-3-2-4) | approved propositions (CM-1-3-2-2/-3) | channel diagnostics (CM-1-3-2-4-1) | cost-to-serve (CM-1-2-6-1-3)"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The designed channel system: portfolio, offerings, structure, and engagement models"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A channel system that was designed, not accumulated."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-PMPA-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Sales & Distribution Channel Strategy | enables: Channel & Partner Management | enables: Network Design (Retail)"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one channel-design lifecycle; horizon tactical. Lifecycle order: Segment Channels → Define Channel Portfolio → Create Channel Offering & Value Proposition → Develop Channel Structure, Size & Engagement Model → Define Channel Measurement Framework. Middle layer of the batch 22 three-layer rule; PMPA applies fact-dependently per the batch 22 statement (referenced, not restated); partner-selection exclusion per review verbatim in scope note. APQC returned NONE — no meaningful candidate; 'Design for manufacturing' is a false match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20966" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20966"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3 ;
    skos:definition "Design the blueprint for how Marketing operates approved strategies and designs, including business-process architecture, role/capability requirements, organizational interfaces, procedural frameworks, control points, and measurement requirements, without setting headcount, appointing individuals, owning enterprise policy, or implementing technology architecture."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Operating Model Design"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the operating-model design lifecycle: translating the approved channel-of-trade mix into operating-model requirements (CM-1-3-3-6-3), developing the business process blueprint (CM-1-3-3-6-1), developing the organizational blueprint (CM-1-3-3-6-2), defining the procedural framework and guidelines (CM-1-3-3-6-4), and defining the operating-model measurement framework (CM-1-3-3-6-5). The process and organizational blueprints are co-developed iteratively; the listed order is a logical dependency, not a rigid one-way sequence. The blueprint may identify technology, data, integration, and automation requirements for subsequent architecture and delivery work, but it does not own enterprise architecture, solution design, product selection, implementation, or technical operation. The receiving end of the batch 23 structure-model boundary: blueprint-level design only — HR headcount, appointments, and management authority follow HR and management processes; enterprise policy stays with its accountable functions (batch 15 procedures-not-policy discipline); organizational/delivery-model neutrality applies (b10/15/16/20). Excludes HR headcount and appointments (HR, management); enterprise policy ownership (accountable functions); channel mix decisions (CM-1-3-2-4); channel system design (CM-1-3-3-3); enterprise architecture, solution design, and implementation (technology owners); enterprise KPI definition (KPI workstream).
In scope: Run the operating-model design lifecycle | translate approved strategies and designs into process and organizational blueprints | define procedural frameworks within enterprise policies | identify technology, data, integration, and automation requirements for downstream architecture work | define operating-model measurement with KPI Store routing
Out of scope: HR headcount and appointments (HR, management) | enterprise policy ownership (accountable functions) | channel mix (CM-1-3-2-4) | channel system design (CM-1-3-3-3) | enterprise/solution architecture and implementation (technology owners) | enterprise KPI definition (KPI workstream)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved strategies (CM-1-3-2) | design outputs (CM-1-3-3-1/-2/-3/-4/-5) | operating performance evidence"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The marketing operating-model blueprint: process, organizational, procedural, technology-requirement, and measurement designs"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "An operating model designed for the strategy it serves."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Sales & Distribution Channel Design | enables: Sales Strategy & Planning | enables: Marketing Plan Execution"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 24 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one operating-model design lifecycle; tactical. Review definition adopted verbatim: an architecture/blueprint process, never an organizational-decision, technology-architecture, HR, or policy owner. Lifecycle: Determine Channel Of Trade Mix (translation) → Develop Business Process Blueprint → Develop Organizational Blueprint → Define Procedural Framework & Guidelines → Define Operating Model Measurement Framework, with the process and organizational blueprints co-developed iteratively per review. Technology-implication statement verbatim in scope note — connects to the future System Integration and Domain Modeling workstreams without expanding scope. APQC 'Design resource model for KM approaches' is a WEAK false-direction match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Marketing sales & operations planning consist of processes that define the go-to-market approach to a level of detail that enables activity planning, forecasting, & performance review against each of the elements (customer offer, sales channel & operations, distribution network)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Marketing & Sales Operations Planning"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Order Management is the end-to-end process that addresses all aspects of collecting cash - from initial order receipt & credit authorization to collections & revenue assurance activities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Order Management"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20085" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20085"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8 ;
    skos:definition "The process of billing customers for delivered products and services: creating and distributing invoices from confirmed evidence and governed commercial data, determining transaction taxes per the Tax function's determinations, accounting for customer self-billing under executed agreements, and issuing every correction — credit memos, exception invoices, reversals, and rebills — as controlled document events."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Customer Invoicing and Billing"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Billing Integrity Rule: a bill or self-bill is created only from validated, linked, and governed inputs: confirmed fulfillment evidence, applicable commercial terms, effective-dated price/discount conditions, approved tax data and determinations, and authorized customer/account information. Billing does not alter physical quantities, invent prices, override credit/terms/tax rules, or replace missing evidence. Every billing correction is a controlled document event — credit memo, debit/exception invoice, cancellation, reversal, or replacement document — with original-document linkage, reason code, authorization evidence, effective date, and audit trail. Original billing documents are not edited in place or deleted; corrections preserve the complete document chain. This L4 is the executing owner that the locked coordination boundaries point at: card-program billing (b29), rebate and returns credits (b29/b33), portal document presentation (b32), and service-desk billing corrections (b35) all execute here under these controls. Taxability and self-billing operate as controlled variants of the one billing-document lifecycle. Receivables, cash application, collections, and revenue accounting are the adjacent processes' (CM-1-3-8-2/-3/-4); payment execution follows the payment-authority rule.
In scope: Invoice creation from confirmed channel evidence and governed data | transaction tax determination application | invoice distribution per customer terms, channels, and Tax/Legal-directed mandates | self-billing accounting under executed agreements | controlled credit memos, exception invoices, reversals, and rebills | billing document records and audit trails
Out of scope: Price, terms, and rebate determination (their owners) | tax positions and interpretations (Tax function) | fulfillment evidence creation (physical processes) | cash application and AR (CM-1-3-8-2) | collections and disputes (CM-1-3-8-3) | revenue accounting and period close (CM-1-3-8-2-6; CM-1-3-8-4) | payment execution and refunds (Finance/Treasury)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Confirmed channel evidence (b33) | governed price/discount, tax, and customer master data | established terms | authorized correction instructions"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Accurate, distributed invoices and controlled correction documents with complete audit trails"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every delivery becomes an accurate, evidenced bill, correctable only by controlled document events."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage Fulfillment | uses-input: Maintain Price & Discount Master Data | enables: Manage Cash Application, A/R & Revenue Accounting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 36 (2026-09-21). Continuous process per review — one billing-document lifecycle with taxability and self-billing as controlled variants, not a capability; canonical Billing Integrity Rule adopted verbatim as a durable decision-log rule (the financial-document counterpart of the order-history and governed-submission rules)."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-2-2-3 a skos:Concept ;
