proc:CM-1-1-7-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10303" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10303"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Refinery Production Scheduling"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-7 ;
    skos:definition "Develop and maintain the refinery production schedule that implements the approved monthly plan — sequencing campaigns, schedule-level unit commitments, blends, and movements over the scheduling horizon, managing schedule exceptions, and coordinating the interfaces the schedule depends on — so the plan becomes a current schedule that provides the approved basis for refinery execution."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.7.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Production Scheduling"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the refinery scheduling layer between planning and operations: maintaining scheduling master data, reviewing the production wheel and run lengths, creating the dated sequencing plan, managing exceptions, measuring scheduling performance, and managing interfaces to planning, operations, and engineering and maintenance. Under the approved planning-scheduling-operations hierarchy, Refining owns detailed unit-operation scheduling and all operational execution; this L4 owns the production schedule that provides the approved basis for that execution. Excludes the monthly plan itself, owned by Refinery Planning (CM-1-1-4); excludes the daily scheduling basis, owned by Daily Execution Planning (CM-1-1-7-1).
In scope: Maintain the scheduling basis and master data | maintain the production wheel and run lengths | create and refresh the dated sequencing plan | manage schedule exceptions and interfaces | measure scheduling performance
Out of scope: Monthly production plan (CM-1-1-4) | unit-operation scheduling and execution (Refining) | daily instruction issuance (CM-1-1-7-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "approved monthly plan | production wheel and constraints | outage and turnaround windows | execution feedback"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "current production schedule providing the approved basis for execution"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Turn the approved monthly plan into a current schedule that provides the approved basis for refinery execution."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-BJCE-REFPS-001 | SRC-SNF-REFPS-001 | SRC-CMU-REFPLAN-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Refinery Planning | precedes: Daily Execution Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 06 (2026-09-19). Q1 decision (Hamid, 2026-09-19): the refinery planning-scheduling-operations hierarchy is approved. CM-1-1-7 owns the refinery production-scheduling layer: translating approved refinery plans into dated, feasible campaign, production, movement, and resource schedules; managing schedule exceptions and cross-functional interfaces; and maintaining schedule-level performance visibility. Refining owns detailed unit-operation scheduling and all operational execution, including process-control decisions, line-ups, transfers, blend execution, utility operation, and immediate safety/reliability departures from the schedule. Supported by the planning/scheduling/operations separation documented in SNF R29/08 and Moro (BJCE); the locked rule's 'detailed unit-operation scheduling' is the operations layer, not this one. APQC 10303 is a WEAK generic candidate; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19569" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19569"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Pre-Trade Forecasting & Optimization"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1 ;
    skos:definition "The front-office capability to assemble and maintain a forward commercial view of supply, demand, generation, renewables, market conditions, and revenue, and to identify how the asset portfolio can be used commercially within approved plans and constraints."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Forecasting & Optimization"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers receiving and consolidating production, demand, and power-generation forecasts from their owning processes; producing the renewables forward view; identifying and valuing commercial use of asset flexibility; and projecting trading revenue, margin, and exposure. This capability consumes forecasts rather than owning their production: the approved demand forecast is owned by Produce Demand Forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) per the batch 03 decision, and production plans and schedules by Refinery Planning (CM-1-1-4) and Refinery Scheduling (CM-1-1-7). Excludes trade execution and capture, owned by Trade Capture (CM-1-2-1-3); excludes setting the regional or refinery plans, owned under the locked Regional Optimization and Refinery Planning rules; excludes risk measurement and limits, owned by Market Risk Management (CM-1-2-2-3).
In scope: Receive and consolidate production, demand, and generation forecasts | develop the renewables forward view | identify and value commercial use of asset flexibility | project trading revenue, margin, and exposure
Out of scope: Forecast production ownership (CM-1-1-1-1, CM-1-1-4, CM-1-1-7) | trade execution and capture (CM-1-2-1-3) | regional and refinery plan setting (locked rules) | risk measurement and limits (CM-1-2-2-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "published demand forecast | production plans and schedules | generation forecasts | market prices and forward curves"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "current trading forward view and asset-optimization recommendations"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep trading running on a current forward commercial view and an identified, valued set of asset opportunities."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001 | SRC-EIA-STEO-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Produce Demand Forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) | informed-by: Refinery Planning | enables: Trading Strategy Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 07 (2026-09-19). Q1 decision (Hamid, Option A): capability — it comprises distinct forecast-intake, renewable-position forecasting, asset-optimization, and revenue-projection processes that collectively enable a current front-office forward view and commercially optimized asset position; it is not a single sequential process. Same test as the batch 04 Inventory L4. Front-office framing per the approved Trading Management (CM-1-2-1) definition; 'market conditions' made explicit at Hamid's direction so the forward-curve context is not read as physical volumes only. Q2 boundary recorded on CM-1-2-1-1-4. APQC 19569 'Perform real-time optimization' is process-control software; no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-1 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Market analysis & insight process determines market attractiveness & identifies evolving opportunities & threats as to the industry in general & company specifically."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Market Analysis & Insight"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Offer design & development process consist of the elements of the go-to-market approach determined in the marketing operations strategy. It links the long-term strategy with the operational planning by detailing all strategic aspects (customer offer, sales channel & operations, distribution network)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Offer Design & Development"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "New product & service development process details step-by-step procedure to do feasibility study for a new product and conceptualize it, defines vision based on market need and developed implementation plan, and manages R&D collaboration, licensing & IP, and lifecycle management."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "New Product & Service Development"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-3-4-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19696" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19696"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-4 ;
    skos:definition "The capability to manage the company's intellectual assets and licensing on the business side — maintaining the intellectual-asset framework, coordinating registration, valuing assets for decisions, protecting assets operationally, exploiting them commercially, and stewarding the asset portfolio — with all IP legal work executed by Legal & Corporate Communications."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.4.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Licensing & IP"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers six concurrent functions: the intellectual-asset framework (CM-1-3-4-4-1), registration coordination (CM-1-3-4-4-2), valuation for decisions (CM-1-3-4-4-3), operational protection (CM-1-3-4-4-4), commercial exploitation (CM-1-3-4-4-5), and portfolio stewardship (CM-1-3-4-4-6). Operating-model note: This capability is cross-functional. Legal & Corporate Communications owns legal IP policy, filings, prosecution, enforcement, agreement drafting, execution, and legal advice. Commercial, Marketing, Product, R&D, and Innovation functions own business opportunity identification, commercial valuation evidence, use cases, licensing-business input, brand alignment, and portfolio recommendations within delegated authority. The taxonomy location does not prescribe a single organizational owner. Business-side asset identification, business input, commercial valuation, operational protection, exploitation opportunity identification, and portfolio stewardship are in scope. Legal work—filings, prosecution, maintenance filings, enforcement, litigation, drafting, review, and execution of IP/confidentiality/license agreements—belongs to Legal. Business recommendations and delegated commercial choices do not constitute legal determinations regarding registrability, patentability, freedom to operate, ownership, inventorship, infringement, validity, enforceability, confidentiality obligations, or territorial protection strategy. Those matters are determined by Legal and its authorized IP counsel. Intellectual assets span patents, trademarks (central to the branded-marketer and brand-licensing model, batches 21/22), trade secrets and know-how, copyrights, and data assets where classified as intellectual assets. Licensing economics operate within the pricing and commercial approval frameworks (batch 23); brand licensing aligns with Brand Strategy (CM-1-3-2-1) and, where franchise arrangements are affected, the batch 22 fact-dependent PMPA statement. Excludes IP legal work of every kind (Legal, locked rule); accounting valuation and impairment (Finance lock); brand strategy (CM-1-3-2-1); development of the underlying products (CM-1-3-4-3); R&D portfolio management (CM-1-3-4-5).
In scope: Maintain the intellectual-asset framework | coordinate registration through Legal | value assets for business decisions | protect assets operationally | exploit assets commercially under delegated authority | steward the asset portfolio
Out of scope: IP legal work and legal determinations (Legal, locked rule) | accounting valuation/impairment (Finance) | brand strategy (CM-1-3-2-1) | product development (CM-1-3-4-3) | R&D portfolio (CM-1-3-4-5)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Development outputs (CM-1-3-4-3) | brand direction (CM-1-3-2-1) | legal advice and registrations status (Legal)"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed intellectual-asset decisions, coordination, and portfolio stewardship"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The company's ideas, marks, and know-how managed as assets, with Legal executing the law."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-USPTO-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Product & Service Development | informed-by: Brand Strategy | enables: R&D Collaboration"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Cross-functional (Legal & Corporate Communications / Commercial & Marketing)"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 26 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: capability — six concurrent, differently cadenced control functions failing the single-lifecycle test (b04/07/11/12/15/21 pattern); horizon continuous. Review ownership decision: cross-functional — the operating-model note verbatim in scope note and the responsible_domain set to a cross-functional value (b15 free-text precedent); the taxonomy location prescribes no single owner. IP legal boundary on the b14 prepare/coordinate-never-execute pattern, with the review's legal-determination exclusion verbatim. APQC 'Govern and manage product/service development program' is a WEAK false-direction match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "14208" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 14208"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Commercial Master Data Stewardship"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7 ;
    skos:definition "The capability that stewards the governed commercial master data on which sales, pricing, fulfillment, billing, and service transactions depend — customer, price and discount, product and service, tax, and location/site records, together with the workflow configurations and commercial policy content that control how they change — maintaining each domain as validated, effective-dated, auditable records through concurrent stewardship processes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Master Data (Customer, Price, Product, Tax, Location)"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Governed Submission and Stewardship Rule: accountable business processes decide the business content of a governed record — such as a price, rebate term, credit status, tax position, product definition, policy, workflow authority, or customer qualification outcome. Master-data stewardship validates submission completeness and authority, applies only approved changes through controlled workflows, effective-dates and versions the record, preserves lineage and audit evidence, monitors quality, and distributes the governed record to consumers. Submitting processes do not write directly to governed master data. Master-data processes do not create, alter, waive, or self-authorize the business decision encoded in the record. This capability is cross-functional. Commercial, Sales, Pricing, Product, Tax, Finance, Legal, Credit, Compliance, Operations, Data Governance, Security, and IT own different business decisions, controls, systems, and policies represented in commercial master data. The master-data stewardship capability governs controlled application, effective dating, quality, lineage, access, and distribution of approved records; taxonomy placement does not assign a single organizational owner. Location/site master stewardship is in scope at the capability level pending taxonomy normalization. It includes governed commercial location references needed for transactions — terminal, depot, rack, station, customer site, ship-to, delivery point, airport, port, route/service point, and related location-role mappings — applied from authorized submissions under location/governance controls. The capability operates within the enterprise data governance framework and Security-defined access controls. Customer personal data is governed under the enterprise personal-data governance framework, with collection, use, retention, and disclosure limited to what the framework and applicable law permit.
In scope: Customer, price/discount, product and service, tax, and location/site master data stewardship | governed change validation and application | data quality monitoring and remediation | effective-dating, versioning, lineage, and audit trails | workflow configuration and commercial policy content administration | access-controlled distribution to consuming processes
Out of scope: The business decisions records encode (pricing, credit, KYC, tax positions, product definitions, policy approvals — their accountable processes) | direct writes by submitting processes | data platform/tooling ownership (IT) | enterprise data governance framework setting (Data Governance) | consent creation (consent/preference framework owner)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Validated submissions from accountable processes | delegations of authority and approved workflows | enterprise data governance standards | Security access controls"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed commercial master data consumed reliably by sales, fulfillment, billing, and service processes"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every commercial transaction computed from governed, current, auditable records."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Capture And Manage Order Fulfillment | enables: Manage Customer Invoicing and Billing | uses-input: Implement sales price"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Cross-functional (Commercial & Marketing / Data Governance / Sales / Pricing / Product / Tax / Finance / Legal / Credit / Compliance / Operations / Security / IT)"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 31 (2026-09-20). Capability per the heterogeneous-concurrent-control test; review cross-functional operating-model statement adopted verbatim and responsible_domain set cross-functional (b26 precedent); canonical Governed Submission and Stewardship Rule adopted verbatim as a durable decision. OPEN TAXONOMY ISSUE (logged, no PTC): the locked taxonomy lacks a dedicated 'Maintain Location and Site Master Data' child although the L4 name lists Location — location/site stewardship carried at capability level pending taxonomy normalization. Preferred label 'Manage Commercial Master Data Stewardship' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20593" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20593"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Commercial Terms, Quoting, and Customer Commercial Services"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7 ;
    skos:definition "The capability that turns approved prices, policies, and credit decisions into governed customer commercial arrangements: establishing commercial terms and customer-facing frameworks, issuing quotes and renewals, planning and monitoring commercial revenue, defining offered financing and payment methods, analyzing customer profitability, and operating the customer self-service portal — concurrent commercial controls in O2C operations."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Pricing & Contracting (Manage Quote & Sale)"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """This capability operationalizes decisions made elsewhere and decides none of them: prices are determined under Sales Pricing Management and pricing governance; policies are approved by their governance owners and consumed from governed policy content; credit terms and exposure are Credit's; payment instruments and banking arrangements are Treasury's; legal terms are Legal's. This capability establishes O2C commercial terms and customer-facing commercial frameworks. Enterprise/trading contract lifecycle administration, legal document control, counterparty-contract records, novation, termination, and master-agreement administration remain with Contract Management and Legal where applicable. Its rows are the operational counterparts of the sales-motion rows in Sales Execution: Establish Commercial Terms & Contracts sets up the durable terms framework within which Manage Contract & Order transacts deal-by-deal, and Issue Quotes & Renewals formally issues and lifecycle-manages what Develop Quote composes. All records flow through the Governed Submission and Stewardship Rule — terms, conditions, and methods take effect only as governed master data and policy content.
In scope: Commercial terms and customer-facing framework establishment | quote issuance and renewal management | commercial revenue planning and monitoring | definition of offered financing and payment methods within approved instruments | customer profitability analysis | customer portal channel operation
Out of scope: Price and discount determination (CM-1-3-6-3; CM-1-3-3-2) | credit decisions (CM-1-3-7-4) | payment instruments and banking arrangements (Treasury) | enterprise/trading contract lifecycle, legal document control, and counterparty-contract records (Contract Management; Legal) | order capture and fulfillment (CM-1-3-7-3) | invoicing (CM-1-3-8-1) | targets and quotas (their owners)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Governed price/policy/customer master data | credit decisions and terms | Treasury-approved instruments | delegations of authority"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed commercial arrangements — terms, quotes, plans, methods, portal services — consumed by order-to-cash execution"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every customer relationship transacts on governed, current, approved commercial arrangements."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Maintain Price & Discount Master Data | uses-input: Maintain Commercial Policies | enables: Capture And Manage Order Fulfillment"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 32 (2026-09-20). Capability per the heterogeneous-concurrent-control test; review Contract Management (batch 09) distinction adopted verbatim; 'Pricing' in the locked name is a misnomer — pricing authority is deliberately elsewhere. Preferred label 'Manage Commercial Terms, Quoting, and Customer Commercial Services' recorded as alt label and queued."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10332" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10332"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7 ;
    skos:definition "The process of managing customer orders through their commercial lifecycle: capturing and validating orders against governed controls, allocating and releasing them per approved rules, coordinating fulfillment through the accountable physical operations, handling changes, cancellations, and returns, and tracking the order book through to completed, evidenced fulfillment ready for invoicing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Capture And Manage Order Fulfillment"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Commercial/physical split: this process owns the governed commercial order lifecycle and commercial fulfillment coordination; scheduling and dispatch, terminal loading, transport, delivery, and custody transfer belong to the accountable supply chain, terminal, and logistics processes — fulfillment coordinates with them and never directs equipment, drivers, or terminal operations. Custody-transfer quantities and documents (bills of lading, delivery tickets, meter records) are measurement evidence produced by the physical processes; the order processes receive, link, validate for commercial completeness, and retain fulfillment evidence supplied by physical processes as the fulfillment record and the invoicing basis, and never restate measured quantities. Order status, fulfillment status, inventory status, custody status, title-transfer status, billing status, and payment status are distinct states. This process coordinates the commercial order and fulfillment record; physical operators establish custody/measurement evidence, contract terms determine title/risk transfer, invoicing consumes billable fulfillment evidence, and payment processes establish financial settlement. Commercial exceptions include substitution, partial fulfillment, shortage, non-delivery, backorder, hold, cancellation, return, quality exception, and delivery discrepancy. The process applies approved commercial rules and routes physical/quality/safety decisions to their owners. All validation applies governed master data, credit, tax, and terms controls under the no-self-exception rule.
In scope: Order capture and validation | allocation and release per approved rules | commercial fulfillment coordination and exception management | change, cancellation, and return authorization handling | order tracking and the operational order-book forecast | fulfillment evidence receipt, linkage, validation, and retention for billing
Out of scope: Physical scheduling, dispatch, loading, transport, delivery, and custody transfer (supply chain/terminal/logistics processes) | measurement and quantity determination (measurement processes) | price, credit, tax, and terms decisions (their owners) | invoicing (CM-1-3-8-1) | allocation policy setting (commercial/supply leadership) | inventory ownership (supply chain)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Governed customer, product, price, tax master data | credit decisions | approved allocation rules | fulfillment evidence from physical operations"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Completed, evidenced fulfillments ready for invoicing, with a governed order record end to end"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every order flows from capture to evidenced fulfillment under governed controls, ready to bill."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage Master Data (Customer, Price, Product, Tax, Location) | governed-by: Manage Credit & Risk | enables: Manage Customer Invoicing and Billing"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 33 (2026-09-20). Review wording adopted verbatim: governed commercial order lifecycle and commercial fulfillment coordination; the order/fulfillment/inventory/custody/title/billing/payment status distinction; and the commercial-exception model. Evidence is received, linked, validated, and retained — never created — by O2C."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11211" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11211"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7 ;
    skos:definition "The capability that owns customer credit and counterparty risk in commercial operations: maintaining credit policies that encode the approved risk appetite, establishing customer credit limits and risk codes, monitoring exposure against limits continuously, analyzing portfolio credit risk, deciding credit-driven closure and reinstatement of commercial eligibility, and executing KYC due diligence — the decision home for every credit control the commercial processes apply."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Credit & Risk"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Credit authority rule: enterprise credit-risk appetite, reserve/impairment methodology, and bad-debt accounting are owned by Finance and designated governance bodies. Credit policies translate approved appetite into decision criteria. Customer credit limits, risk codes, terms eligibility, security requirements, holds, releases, and credit-driven closure/reinstatement are decided by the authorized Credit process under those policies and delegations. Commercial, order, card, terms, payment-method, and fulfillment processes apply those decisions and never waive, override, or substitute them. Exceptions follow the approved delegation and escalation path. Accountability layers: Finance owns enterprise risk appetite, reserves, impairment, and bad-debt accounting; Credit and its delegated governance own credit policy substance, limits, holds/releases, and security requirements; Commercial O2C and Master Data own customer-data and commercial application; Compliance and Legal own the KYC/sanctions/AML frameworks and legal determinations, with designated KYC/Compliance operations executing; O2C Collections, Finance, and Legal own financial collections and disputes as applicable. Taxonomy placement does not assign a single functional owner. This capability covers O2C commercial customer credit; trading counterparty credit risk for trading positions remains with the counterparty credit-risk processes (the batch 11 cluster). KYC and sanctions screening execute the Compliance/Legal-owned frameworks and determine nothing themselves. Decisions take effect through governed master data submission. Personal data handled here is governed under the enterprise personal-data governance framework, with collection, use, retention, and disclosure limited to what the framework and applicable law permit.
In scope: Credit policy maintenance within approved appetite | customer limit and risk-code decisions under delegated authority | continuous exposure monitoring, holds, and releases | portfolio credit-risk analysis | credit-driven closure and reinstatement of commercial eligibility | KYC due diligence and sanctions-screening execution
Out of scope: Enterprise risk appetite, reserves, impairment, and bad-debt accounting (Finance; designated governance) | trading counterparty credit risk (the counterparty credit-risk processes) | collections and dispute management (CM-1-3-8-3) | sanctions/AML framework ownership and match adjudication (Compliance; Legal) | the commercial transactions credit controls gate (their processes) | payment execution"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Finance-approved risk appetite | customer financial and payment data | KYC outcomes | exposure data from orders, fulfillment, cards, and AR"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed credit decisions consumed by every commercial process that extends or services credit"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Commercial growth extended only within known, approved, monitored credit risk."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Maintain Credit Policies | enables: Capture And Manage Order Fulfillment | enables: Manage Collection and Disputes"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Cross-functional — Commercial Operations / Credit / Finance / Compliance / Legal"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 34 (2026-09-20). Capability per the heterogeneous-concurrent-control test; review credit authority rule adopted verbatim, replacing the absolute 'nowhere else' wording with the delegated-governance model; accountability-layer framing and trading-counterparty distinction adopted verbatim; responsible_domain set cross-functional per review (b26/b31 precedent)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-7-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10388" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10388"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-7 ;
    skos:definition "The capability that operates commercial customer service: receiving and prioritizing customer requests, inquiries, and complaints from all channels, resolving directly what governed data and approved documentation can answer, routing the substance of everything else to its owning process, maintaining the case record end to end, following up to confirmed resolution, running the customer service-review cadence, and delivering customer training from approved content."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.7.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Customer Requests and Inquiries"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """This capability owns the commercial customer-request experience: intake, prioritization, case stewardship, routing, status communication, follow-up, and closure coordination. It resolves directly only those requests that can be answered from governed data or approved documentation. Substantive resolution remains with the accountable process owner. Resolution-routing rule: Customer Requests and Inquiries manages one case record and one customer communication loop. It may answer governed status, documentation, and information requests directly. For all matters requiring a business decision, record change, financial adjustment, physical action, legal conclusion, technical assessment, or control override, it routes the case to the owning process, tracks the outcome, communicates the confirmed result, and closes the loop. It does not adjudicate disputes, issue credits, alter orders, modify master data, make credit decisions, apply prices, perform physical operations, or override controls. One case record follows the request wherever it goes. Safety-escalation rule: Any request, complaint, or communication indicating a potential product-quality, safety, environmental, spill/release, contamination, security, or regulatory event is immediately captured and escalated to the accountable Quality, EHS, Security, and Operations processes under their incident protocols. The service capability does not assess severity, determine reportability, investigate the event, delay escalation pending normal prioritization, or close the incident; it tracks the customer-facing case and communications. The service record references the incident/case identifier managed by the accountable process. It does not become the incident system of record. Customer personal data is governed under the enterprise personal-data governance framework; service communications are service notices, with marketing communications under the separate consent/contactability framework.
In scope: Multi-channel request/inquiry/complaint intake | prioritization and immediate safety/security escalation | direct resolution from governed data and approved documentation | substance routing to owning processes with tracking | case record stewardship | follow-up to confirmed resolution | customer service reviews | customer training delivery from approved content
Out of scope: Dispute adjudication, credits, order changes, credit decisions, card servicing, pricing, physical operations, and master-data changes (their owning processes) | quality/safety/environmental/security assessment and incident ownership (Quality; EHS; Security; Operations) | customer-experience strategy and service delivery owned elsewhere | commercial negotiations and account strategy (Manage Key Accounts; sales processes) | service platform ownership (IT) | consent creation"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Channel submissions (portal, phone, email, EDI) | governed status and document data | owning-process resolutions | prioritization framework and incident protocols"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Resolved, confirmed, fully recorded customer requests with routed substance and closed loops"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every customer request lands, moves, resolves, and closes — visibly, once, through the right owner."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage Customer Portal | enables: Dispute Management | uses-input: Track & Forecast Order"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 35 (2026-09-20). Capability per the heterogeneous-concurrent-control test; review 'commercial customer-request experience' wording adopted verbatim (never the whole customer service experience — delivery and CX strategy live elsewhere); canonical resolution-routing rule and expanded safety/security escalation with incident-record linkage adopted verbatim."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10759" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10759"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8 ;
    skos:definition "The process of turning billed transactions into accounted revenue and settled receivables: Record and process receipt notifications, bank/lockbox/acquirer settlement information, remittance evidence, and accounting events relating to customer payments received through Treasury-managed arrangements. Capture remittance information, apply receipts against governed receivables, manage unapplied and exception items to resolution, reconcile receivables against bank and processor settlement information and the general ledger, and perform revenue accounting under Finance-approved policy."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Cash Application, A/R & Revenue Accounting"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Cash Application Integrity Rule: a customer receipt is applied only to governed receivables, credit documents, or authorized offsets using remittance evidence and approved application rules. Unidentified, unapplied, partial, disputed, duplicate, overpaid, short-paid, or otherwise exceptional amounts remain visible as owned, aged exception items until resolved through an authorized process. A payment does not alter the underlying bill, fulfillment evidence, price, tax, contract, credit decision, or accounting treatment. Application reversals, offsets, transfers, refunds, write-offs, and reclassifications occur only through controlled, authorized events with complete audit trails. Treasury boundary: bank accounts, lockboxes, merchant and acquirer arrangements, payment rails, and funds movement are Treasury's; this process records, matches, applies, reconciles, and accounts — it opens no accounts, initiates no transfers, and executes no refunds (a refund is a separate authorized payment request under the payment-authority rule). Revenue-recognition policy, method elections, and interpretations are Finance's; this process executes them. Write-offs and allowances remain with Finance via Bad Debt Allowance; collections and dispute actions with Manage Collection and Disputes.
In scope: Receipt notification and settlement-information recording across approved methods | remittance capture and normalization | receipt application per approved rules | unapplied/exception management to resolution | daily settlement-information and bank reconciliation and GL reconciliation support | revenue accounting execution under approved policy
Out of scope: Banking, lockbox, and acquirer arrangements, payment rails, and funds movement (Treasury) | billing and corrections (CM-1-3-8-1) | dispute adjudication and collections (CM-1-3-8-3) | write-offs and allowances (Finance; Bad Debt Allowance) | revenue-recognition policy (Finance) | period-end close ownership (CM-1-3-8-4-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Invoices and credit memos (CM-1-3-8-1) | receipt notifications and settlement information via Treasury arrangements | remittance evidence | Finance-approved application and revenue policies"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Applied receipts, reconciled receivables, and accounted revenue with complete audit trails"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every billed dollar is received, applied, reconciled, and accounted — or visibly in a managed exception."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage Customer Invoicing and Billing | enables: Manage Collection and Disputes | enables: Period End Processing"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Cross-functional — Finance / Treasury / O2C Operations / Commercial Operations"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 37 (2026-09-21). Continuous process per review; canonical Cash Application Integrity Rule adopted verbatim as a durable financial-control decision (receipts-side counterpart of the Billing Integrity Rule); responsible_domain set cross-functional per review — control authority is mostly Finance/Treasury/Accounting with an O2C operating home; receipt activity worded as recording/notification processing, never physically receiving funds."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-9-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20875" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20875"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-9 ;
    skos:definition "The capability that builds and runs the machinery of commercial service and support: defining the service operating model, managing day-to-day service operations, and managing the workforce, facilities, technology, and contact-center channel the service processes run on."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.9.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Service & Support Enablement"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Customer Requests and Inquiries owns the request experience: intake, case record, prioritization, routing, status communication, follow-up, and closure coordination. Service & Support Enablement provides the service machinery: operating model, service operations, workforce, facilities, technology, and contact-center channel. Service & Support Delivery fulfills service events: delivery data, customer interaction during service, entitlement verification, service execution, completion evidence, and return of outcomes to the request case. Service & Support Measurement measures service resources and outcomes: infrastructure, workforce operational data, and service effectiveness. Service and Support spans Commercial, Operations, Technology, Facilities, HR, Security, Quality, EHS, and third-party providers. Taxonomy placement in Commercial & Marketing reflects the commercial service context; it does not establish operational ownership of every service resource or service event. Corporate-function boundaries throughout: HR owns employment frameworks; IT owns platforms and technical operations (the batch 31 distinction); corporate Facilities/Real Estate owns leases, capital works, and building operations; EHS owns safety requirements; Security owns access and verification standards. This capability manages the business/occupant side of each and creates no authority the owners hold.
In scope: Service operating model definition and maintenance | service operations management | workforce planning, scheduling, and readiness within HR frameworks | occupant-side facilities management | business ownership of service technology | contact-center channel operation
Out of scope: The customer-request experience (CM-1-3-7-5) | employment policy, compensation, performance management, discipline (HR; line management under HR authority) | platform and technical operations (IT) | leases, capital works, building operations (Corporate facilities) | safety requirements (EHS) | service delivery and measurement (CM-1-3-9-2/-3)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Service demand signals (CM-1-3-7-5; channels) | HR, IT, Facilities, EHS, Security frameworks | approved budgets and plans"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "An operating service organization: model, operations, workforce, facilities, technology, and channel"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The service machinery exists, runs, and stays fit — so requests and events can be served."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Manage Customer Requests and Inquiries | enables: Service & Support Delivery"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 40 (2026-09-21). Capability per the concurrent-control test; canonical four-part request/enable/deliver/measure rule and delivery-model domain note adopted verbatim."@en .

proc:CM-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1 ;
    skos:definition "Planning And Optimization Includes Processes Such As Demand Forecasting, Local And Regional Optimization, Backcasting And Inventory Management Across The Value Chain."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Planning & Scheduling"@en ;
    intake:level "L2"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19243" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19243"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Develop the commercially optimal refinery production plan — selecting crude and feedstock intake, refinery operating modes, unit-throughput targets, product yield and quality targets, and production horizons — to fulfill commercial commitments and maximize margin within feasible refinery, supply, inventory, product-specification, and contractual constraints."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Refinery Planning"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Refinery Planning owns the commercial and economic production-plan targets supplied to the refinery, including feedstock and crude-slate choices, target throughput and operating modes, target product yields and specifications, planned production availability, and economically preferred production alternatives. It does not own detailed unit-operation scheduling, tank and transfer line-ups, blend execution, process control, operating procedures, maintenance, turnaround execution, or the operational decision to depart from plan for safety, reliability, or immediate process conditions; those belong to Refining and its operating functions."@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1 ;
    skos:definition "Supply And Trading Includes Processes Such As Managing Supply, Demand And Trading Of Crude Oil And Refined Products. It Involves Trading As Well As Planning & Managing Movements In The Most Efficient Way, Including Primary & Secondary Distribution."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Supply And Trading"@en ;
    intake:level "L2"@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16463" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16463"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Trading Compliance"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4 ;
    skos:definition "The capability to keep supply and trading activity compliant with external law, regulation, and market rules and with the organization's own compliance framework — maintaining the trading compliance program's policies and procedures, monitoring external regulatory compliance, internal policy adherence, and the operation of compliance-supporting controls, assuring tax compliance, coordinating regulatory reporting obligations with their accountable owners, and managing compliance exceptions through to closure."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Regulatory & Compliance"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the trading compliance capability: trading-specific compliance policies and procedures, external trading compliance monitoring including market-conduct surveillance, internal compliance-policy adherence monitoring, monitoring of internally implemented controls that support external obligations, tax compliance monitoring and assurance, RFS/RINs reporting coordination, and compliance exception management. The taxonomy places this capability in the Supply & Trading branch because it governs trading activity. It is normally delivered with functional independence from trading decision-makers through an enterprise Compliance, Legal/Compliance, Risk/Compliance, or equivalent second-line function. Taxonomy placement does not establish operational reporting lines, approval authority, or organizational ownership. Boundary architecture: adherence to the internal market-risk framework is monitored by Monitor Risk Policy Compliance (CM-1-2-2-3-10) under the batch 12/16 reciprocal split; operational execution of regulatory reporting sits with its lifecycle owners (Report Trades to SDR, CM-1-2-1-3-10; Report Confirmation To SDR, CM-1-2-2-2-4; Perform RINs/REC Actualization, CM-1-2-3-1-7); environmental, health, safety, and government reporting filings, attestations, and submissions are owned by EHS & Government Reporting under its locked rule; legal interpretation, enforcement response, and proceedings by Legal & Corporate Communications; transaction-tax accounting execution by Manage Taxes (CM-1-2-4-2-11); enterprise tax policy and returns by the enterprise Tax function.
In scope: Maintain trading compliance policies and procedures | monitor external regulatory compliance and internal policy adherence | monitor compliance-supporting controls and assure tax compliance | coordinate reporting obligations with accountable owners and manage exceptions to closure
Out of scope: Internal market-risk framework monitoring (CM-1-2-2-3-10) | operational regulatory reporting execution (CM-1-2-1-3-10, CM-1-2-2-2-4, CM-1-2-3-1-7) | EHS and government reporting filings and attestations (EHS & Government Reporting, locked rule) | legal interpretation and proceedings (Legal & Corporate Communications) | transaction-tax execution (CM-1-2-4-2-11) and enterprise tax policy/returns (Tax function)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Applicable law, regulation, and market rules | trading activity and control evidence from the lifecycle processes | enterprise compliance and legal frameworks"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "A monitored, evidenced trading compliance posture with exceptions managed to closure"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep trading activity provably compliant — with the law outside and the framework inside — and every compliance gap managed to closure."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-P180-001 | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "assures: Trading Management | assures: Settlements | informed-by: Develop Compliance Policies & Procedures"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 16 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: capability per the batch 11/12/15 test. Monitor partition (Hamid, batch 16): external rules (CM-1-2-4-3-2); internal compliance policies (CM-1-2-4-3-3); compliance-supporting control operation (CM-1-2-4-3-4); internal market-risk framework (CM-1-2-2-3-10) — the batch 12 dependency is resolved under Option A with reciprocal exclusions on both sides. Q5 decision: taxonomy placement retained under Commercial & Marketing with the mandatory independence and delivery-model statement in the scope note. APQC 16463 scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-3-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10102" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10102"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-3 ;
    skos:definition "Define the marketing communications strategy that carries the approved brand and value propositions to their audiences — integrating the media, direct, advertising, point-of-sale, digital, and internal communication strategies into one coherent direction — within advertising and marketing law, so communications planning and execution deliver one coherent, lawful voice."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.3.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Marketing Communications Strategy"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the communications-strategy set: media strategy (CM-1-3-3-5-1), direct marketing strategy (CM-1-3-3-5-2), advertising strategy (CM-1-3-3-5-3), point-of-sale communication strategy (CM-1-3-3-5-4), digital marketing strategy (CM-1-3-3-5-5), and internal marketing communications strategy (CM-1-3-3-5-6, previously approved), with this L4 performing the integration: consistency checks, prioritization, and conflict resolution across the parallel channel strategies into one integrated communications strategy implementing the approved brand strategy (CM-1-3-2-1). Marketing Communications Strategy may set strategic communication roles, audience principles, claims territory, channel priorities, and measurement expectations. It does not approve specific claims, substantiation, disclaimers, endorsements, disclosures, contact lists, consent status, channel executions, media buys, or campaign releases; those activities follow the applicable Legal, Compliance, Privacy, Technology, Procurement, Planning, and Execution controls. Communications strategy operates within truth-in-advertising and substantiation requirements, disclosure and endorsement standards, CAN-SPAM, the Telemarketing Sales Rule, and applicable state, FCC, and non-US regimes — fact- and jurisdiction-dependent, with interpretation owned by Legal & Corporate Communications and the compliance framework; personal data is used only within the batch 21 shared governance statement. Excludes brand strategy (CM-1-3-2-1); campaign and communications planning (CM-1-3-5-2/-3); marketing execution (CM-1-3-6-5); brand standard management (CM-1-3-6-7); consent, preference, and suppression operations (their owners); advertising legal review (Legal); enterprise KPI definition (KPI workstream).
In scope: Define and integrate the parallel channel-communication strategies with consistency checks, prioritization, and conflict resolution | implement the approved brand strategy in communications terms | operate within advertising and marketing law | issue the integrated communications strategy to planning and execution
Out of scope: Brand strategy (CM-1-3-2-1) | campaign/communications planning (CM-1-3-5-2/-3) | execution, media buys, and campaign releases (CM-1-3-6-5, execution controls) | brand standards (CM-1-3-6-7) | consent/suppression operations (their owners) | claim and disclosure approval (Legal, Compliance) | enterprise KPI definition (KPI workstream)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved brand strategy (CM-1-3-2-1) | value propositions (CM-1-3-2-2/-3) | audience findings (CM-1-3-1-3/-4) | communications performance evidence"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The integrated marketing communications strategy"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One coherent, lawful voice across every channel the brand speaks through."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FTC-ADM-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Brand Strategy | enables: Marketing Communications Planning | enables: Integrated Marketing Planning (Advertising)"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 24 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process; tactical. Cluster shape: parallel variant set (batch 08/21 pattern) with the integration performed at this L4 per review — no precedes chain among the children. Review control statement verbatim in scope note: sets direction, approves nothing that Legal, Compliance, Privacy, Technology, Procurement, Planning, and Execution control. CM-1-3-3-5-6 approved earlier via APQC adoption, untouched. Legal guardrails via SRC-FTC-ADM-001; Legal owns interpretation. APQC 'Develop marketing strategy' STRONG, no validator — comparative anchor."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10103" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10103"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6 ;
    skos:definition "Execute the sales effort against the plans — pipeline, opportunities, key accounts, quotes, contracts and orders, and non-retail customer serving — as the commercial pursuit whose qualified outcomes are handed to the controlled transaction processes, measured against its own plan."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sales Execution"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the sales-execution cluster: managing the sales pipeline (CM-1-3-6-2-1) and opportunities (CM-1-3-6-2-3), managing key accounts as a continuous overlay (CM-1-3-6-2-2), developing quotes (CM-1-3-6-2-4), managing the commercial contract and order relationship (CM-1-3-6-2-5), serving non-retail customers (CM-1-3-6-2-6), and measuring and reporting sales performance (CM-1-3-6-2-7). The commercial-pursuit/transaction partition governs: this cluster owns the pursuit; the O2C branch owns the transaction — quote/sale processing and contract pricing configuration at CM-1-3-7-2, order capture and fulfillment management at CM-1-3-7-3, credit at CM-1-3-7-4 (b11 boundary), invoicing and A/R at CM-1-3-8. Commercially qualified opportunities are handed to the appropriate quote, contract, credit, and order-to-cash processes for controlled transaction completion. Prices are applied from Sales Pricing Management (CM-1-3-6-3) within the approved architecture; contract legal drafting and execution are Legal's (locked rule); physical delivery is Secondary Distribution's (CM-1-1-3-8, locked rule); service delivery is Service & Support's (CM-1-3-9). Executes against targets and account plans (CM-1-3-5-1), never re-planning them. Excludes quote/sale and contract-pricing transaction processing (CM-1-3-7-2); order capture/fulfillment (CM-1-3-7-3); credit (CM-1-3-7-4, b11); invoicing/A/R (CM-1-3-8); price determination (CM-1-3-6-3); contract legal work (Legal, locked rule); physical delivery (CM-1-1-3-8, locked rule); service delivery (CM-1-3-9); sales planning (CM-1-3-5-1).
In scope: Run the commercial pursuit from pipeline to serving | manage key accounts continuously | develop quotes and steward contract-and-order relationships commercially | measure and report against the plan | hand qualified outcomes to the controlled transaction processes
Out of scope: O2C transaction processing (CM-1-3-7-2/-3, CM-1-3-8) | credit (CM-1-3-7-4, b11) | price determination (CM-1-3-6-3) | contract legal work (Legal) | physical delivery (CM-1-1-3-8, locked) | service delivery (CM-1-3-9) | sales planning (CM-1-3-5-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Targets and account plans (CM-1-3-5-1) | offers (CM-1-3-3-1) | applied prices (CM-1-3-6-3) | partner relationships (CM-1-3-6-1)"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Commercially qualified opportunities handed to the quote, contract, credit, and order-to-cash processes for controlled transaction completion"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The plan turned into orders, one pursued opportunity at a time."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Sales Strategy & Planning | uses-input: Sales Pricing Management | enables: Manage Pricing & Contracting (Manage Quote & Sale)"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 28 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — the standing commercial-pursuit cluster; continuous. Lifecycle: Pipeline → Opportunity → Quote → Commercial Contract and Order Relationship → Serve, with Key Accounts a continuous overlay across the full lifecycle and Measure & report the periodic feedback loop to planning, pipeline, account plans, offers, and pricing strategy — not a strict line (review). Controlled-handoff wording per review: won business exists only after quote acceptance, credit clearance, contract/legal readiness, and order acceptance in the accountable processes. APQC 'Develop sales strategy' is a WEAK strategy-side match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-8-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16463" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16463"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-8 ;
    skos:definition "The capability that closes and evidences O2C's books and obligations: running period-end processing on the Finance calendar, reconciling receivables to the general ledger with control evidence, analyzing and calculating accruals, reconciling and preparing indirect-tax reporting, preparing taxability-treatment recommendations for Tax approval, executing intercompany invoicing per approved transfer-pricing policies, and administering royalty, brand-fee, and contribution streams under executed agreements."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.8.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Accounting, Regulatory Reporting and Compliance"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Accounting Execution Rule: O2C accounting, reconciliation, accrual calculation, tax-reporting preparation, intercompany invoicing, and royalty/fee administration execute under Finance- and Tax-approved policies, methodologies, calendars, agreements, delegations, and controlled evidence requirements. Finance and Tax retain policy, accounting/tax interpretation, elections, filing ownership, sign-off authority, and financial-statement responsibility. Period-end close proceeds only with complete, reconciled, evidenced inputs from feeding processes or a documented Finance-approved close exception. Prepared reports, returns, intercompany documents, and administered obligation streams retain evidence lineage to governed transaction, master-data, agreement, billing, cash, and accounting records. No period closes over a missing, unresolved, or unreconciled material feed without a documented Finance-approved exception, compensating control, owner, resolution plan, and aging/true-up requirement. Enterprise financial statements, disclosure, statutory filing ownership, and audit relationships remain with Finance; tax positions and filings with Tax; enterprise regulatory compliance outside commercial-agreement and tax-reporting scope with its accountable functions (the batch 30 narrow-compliance precedent).
In scope: Period-end processing on the Finance calendar | AR-to-GL reconciliation with O2C control evidence | accrual analysis and calculation per approved methodologies | indirect-tax reconciliation and reporting preparation | taxability-treatment recommendations under Tax authority | intercompany invoicing per approved policies and agreements | royalty, brand-fee, and contribution stream administration
Out of scope: Accounting policy, elections, and interpretations (Finance) | tax positions, filings, and interpretations (Tax) | financial statements, disclosure, and audit relationships (Finance) | enterprise regulatory compliance beyond this scope (Accountable functions) | billing, cash application, and collections execution (CM-1-3-8-1/-2/-3) | funds movement (Finance/Treasury; payment-authority rule)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO SOURCE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Feeds and evidence from billing, cash application, allowances, and resolution processes | Finance/Tax policies, calendars, and methodologies | executed agreements | governed master data"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Closed periods, reconciled and evidenced positions, prepared reports and filings, and administered obligation streams"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "O2C's books close on time, reconciled, evidenced, and compliant — with every exception documented and owned."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage Customer Invoicing and Billing | uses-input: Manage Cash Application, A/R & Revenue Accounting | uses-input: Manage Collection and Disputes"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Cross-functional — Finance / Tax / O2C Operations / Legal / Commercial Administration"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 39 (2026-09-21). Capability per the concurrent-control test; canonical Accounting Execution Rule adopted verbatim as a durable rule, with the review's qualified close-exception wording replacing the absolute form; responsible_domain includes Commercial Administration per review (royalty/fee and agreement obligations operationally owned there)."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-2-1 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Make updates to models and other systems (Profisee) in response to new configurations required."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Model and System Maintenance"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Generate model input spreadsheet in Planning and Scheduling UI"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Generate Inputs for Regional Optimization Plan"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Covers distribution planning connections to Regional Optimization plus product scheduling, physical distribution scheduling, inventory management, and secondary distribution."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Distribution Planning & Scheduling and Inventory Management"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-4-7 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19569" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19569"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Refinery Economic Optimization"@en,
        "Refinery LP Optimization"@en,
        "Refinery Plan Optimization"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-4 ;
    skos:definition "Run the recurring economic-optimization cycle that produces the refinery production plan — assembling the case basis, building and running optimization cases, analyzing scenarios, and selecting the economically preferred production case for approval."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.4.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Refinery Optimization"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the optimization cycle inside Refinery Planning: gathering the demand, crude availability, price, constraint, inventory, and current-plan basis for a case; building and running the optimization model; comparing alternatives through scenario analysis; and recommending the economically preferred case. Accountability for the approved production plan and its commercial targets remains with Refinery Planning. Excludes detailed unit-operation scheduling, tank and transfer line-ups, blend execution, and process control, owned by Refining; excludes optimization-model and application administration, owned by Process Excellence & IT.
In scope: Assemble case basis from demand, crude availability, price, constraints, inventory, and current plan | build and run optimization cases | scenario and sensitivity analysis | compare production alternatives on margin | recommend the economically preferred production case
Out of scope: Approval and ownership of the production plan and its commercial targets (Refinery Planning) | detailed unit scheduling, tank line-ups, and blend execution (Refining, Refinery Scheduling) | process control and operational departures from plan (Refining) | optimization application administration and model platform support (Process Excellence & IT) | real-time and closed-loop unit optimization (Refining)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "product demand requirements | crude and feedstock availability and assays | crude, feedstock, and product prices | refinery capacity and operating constraints | inventory position | current approved operating plan"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:parkedChildren "Build Optimization Case | Validate Case Basis | Recommend Preferred Production Case"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "recommended economically preferred refinery production case with supporting scenario analysis"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Produce and recommend the economically preferred refinery production case for the planning period."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CMU-REFPLAN-001 | SRC-SNF-REFPS-001 | SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Refinery Planning | informed-by: Produce Demand Forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) | informed-by: Crude Allocation Decision | constrained-by: Inventory Management (CM-1-1-3-7) | precedes: Refinery Scheduling"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Parent-versus-child distinctness resolved per Hamid's decision (2026-09-18, Option 1): Refinery Planning (CM-1-1-4) owns the approved plan and its commercial targets; this node owns the recurring optimization run cycle that produces the candidate plan. Without that split the two primary purposes were near-identical. The eight existing L5 children — six “Capture …” nodes, Evaluate Crude & Feedstock, and Perform Scenario Analysis — are coherent under this reading as case-basis assembly and case analysis. “Capture Monthly Operating Plan” (CM-1-1-4-7-7) appears to name a document rather than a process and is flagged for review in the L5 batch, not resolved here. Avoided naming a specific optimization application; “LP” appears only as an alternate label because it is established industry shorthand."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-5-5 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-5 ;
    skos:definition "Build and run optimization case stack in XPIMS against the plan and actual prices"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.5.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Evaluate optimization cases"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20711" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20711"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Counterparty Credit Risk Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2 ;
    skos:definition "The capability to control counterparty credit risk in trading — assessing counterparty creditworthiness, setting and maintaining credit limits, administering credit support and margin, measuring exposure, analyzing portfolio credit risk, monitoring positions against limits, and reporting the credit risk picture — so credit losses are anticipated, bounded, and visible before they happen."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Credit Risk Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the independent credit-risk control over trading counterparties: reviews, limits, credit-support and collateral administration, exposure calculation, margining, advanced analytics, continuous monitoring, and reporting. This is an independent control function; the enterprise may operate it through middle-office, risk, or another designated function — the taxonomy does not prescribe the delivery model. Excludes trading decisions and pre-trade checks, which apply these limits (Manage Pre-Trade Analysis, CM-1-2-1-3-1); excludes commercial customer credit in order-to-cash, owned by Manage Credit & Risk (CM-1-3-7-4) — the boundary is the relationship and exposure type, not the organizational branch; excludes market risk, owned by Market Risk Management (CM-1-2-2-3); excludes inventory risk (Assess & Manage Inventory Risk, CM-1-2-5-4-1); excludes credit-loss accounting — provisions, impairments, write-offs, and disclosures — owned by Finance under its locked rule; excludes legal enforcement, security documentation, and insolvency action, owned by Legal & Corporate Communications.
In scope: Assess counterparty creditworthiness | set and maintain credit limits | administer credit support, collateral, and margining | calculate and monitor credit exposure | analyze and report portfolio credit risk
Out of scope: Trading decisions and pre-trade checks (CM-1-2-1-3-1) | commercial customer credit (CM-1-3-7-4) | market risk (CM-1-2-2-3) | inventory risk (CM-1-2-5-4-1) | credit-loss accounting: provisions, write-offs, disclosures (Finance) | legal enforcement and security documentation (Legal & Corporate Communications)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "counterparty financials and ratings | positions and mark-to-market | credit-support and agreement terms | risk appetite"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "bounded, monitored, reported counterparty credit risk"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Credit losses are anticipated, bounded, and visible before they happen."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-001 | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001 | SRC-ISDA-MA-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Regulatory & Compliance | enables: Trade Capture | enables: Contract Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 11 (2026-09-19). Concept type: capability (Hamid confirmed) — heterogeneous recurring control processes jointly enabling one outcome; a counterparty can be reviewed, monitored, collateralized, margined, reported, and re-limited concurrently, so there is no single per-counterparty lifecycle. Binding credit-boundary rule (Hamid, batch 11): Trading Counterparty Credit Risk governs exposure arising from trading, commodity and capacity positions, settlement cycles, collateral, margin, master agreements, and counterparty default risk. Commercial Customer Credit governs exposure arising from customer orders, deliveries, invoices, receivables, payment terms, credit holds, and collections (Manage Credit & Risk, CM-1-3-7-4). The same legal entity may be subject to both processes in different roles; shared legal identity does not merge the exposure, decision rights, limit structures, or control processes. Organizational delivery model not prescribed, per the batch 10 neutrality decision. APQC 20711 is an IT-risk candidate; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-3-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Deal Actualization"@en,
        "Movement Actualization"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-3 ;
    skos:definition "Update trading deal, movement, and entitlement records with contractually applicable actual quantities, qualities, dates, statuses, and charge determinants from execution, custody-transfer, registry, and other agreed evidence, replacing planned or estimated values so settlement, economic P&L, risk, and downstream reporting use controlled actuals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Actualizations"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the per-movement actualization lifecycle across transport and instrument modes: matching execution evidence to the captured deal and scheduled movement, recording actual quantities, qualities, dates, and statuses on the contractual measurement basis, recording verified charge determinants, and closing the movement in the trading record. Actualizations may record or validate charge determinants — such as tariff basis, freight basis, throughput quantity, storage days, accessorial events, loss allowances, quality adjustments, and contractual rate references — and may calculate provisional amounts where the controlled trading-system design assigns that function; it does not own invoice generation, final settlement calculation, payment, accounting accrual or posting, or financial dispute resolution. Actualization updates the trading system's deal, movement, and entitlement records; it consumes measurement results and does not perform physical measurement, which is executed at custody transfer by operating personnel and independent inspectors under the applicable measurement standards (API MPMS). Excludes trade capture (CM-1-2-1-3); confirmation of agreed terms (CM-1-2-2-2); settlement and invoicing (CM-1-2-4-1); per-movement closure in distribution scheduling and inventory records and network book-to-physical stock reconciliation, owned by Discharge & Reconcile Load (CM-1-1-3-6-4) and Perform Inventory Reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5) under the locked SCM boundary; demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4).
In scope: Match execution and measurement evidence to deals and scheduled movements | record actual quantities, qualities, dates, and statuses on the contractual measurement basis | record verified charge determinants and, where the trading system is configured to do so, provisional or contractually derived charge amounts for downstream settlement validation | close movements in the trading record for settlement
Out of scope: Physical measurement at custody transfer (operations and independent inspection per API MPMS) | trade capture (CM-1-2-1-3) | confirmation (CM-1-2-2-2) | invoice generation, final settlement calculation, payment, and accrual (CM-1-2-4-1, Accounting) | distribution movement closure and stock reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-6-4, CM-1-1-3-7-5) | demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Captured deal and scheduled movement records | meter tickets, bills of lading, and run tickets | inspection and outturn reports | carrier, terminal, and registry statements"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Actualized deal, movement, and entitlement records ready for settlement"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Ensure the trading record reflects what was actually delivered, received, and measured, so settlement, accounting, and risk run on controlled actuals rather than estimates."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-API-MPMS-001 | SRC-EI-HM32-001 | SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Trade Capture | informed-by: Confirmations Management | precedes: Settlements"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 13 (2026-09-19). 'Actualization' is energy-trading (ETRM/CTRM) practice terminology: replacing planned or estimated deal and movement values with measured actuals. Concept-type: process per the batch 08 variant test — one per-movement lifecycle discipline applied across mode variants, like Trade Capture. 'Entitlement' covers commingled stock, exchanges, storage, and instrument arrangements where the commercial fact is not a uniquely identifiable physical parcel. Q2 decision (Hamid, batch 13): charge-determinant actualization, not universal cost actualization — Actualizations records the verified operational and contractual facts needed to determine charges (actual quantity, quality, duration, route, capacity use, tariff/fee basis, dates, loss/gain treatment, and other rate inputs); Settlements applies rates, calculates settlement amounts, invoices, pays or receives, accrues, nets, and resolves financial disputes. Trading-record versus stock-record boundary: the same custody-transfer documents feed this process (trading deal, movement, and entitlement records) and the SCM inventory processes (physical stock and scheduling records); each updates its own record. Measurement authority: API MPMS (tank gauging Ch. 3; marine cargo Ch. 17; lease-tank custody transfer Ch. 18). 'P&L' prose convention per batch 12. APQC 19948 is an unrelated candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20682" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20682"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Demurrage & Claims Management"@en,
        "Laytime Claims"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4 ;
    skos:definition "Manage outbound and inbound claims for demurrage, despatch, detention, and other contractually defined delay or capacity-usage compensation by evaluating verified time, notice, and event evidence under the governing agreement; establishing entitlement or liability; creating, assessing, negotiating, documenting, invoicing, and tracking agreed claims; and escalating unresolved matters through the applicable commercial and legal processes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Demurrage Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the two-directional claim lifecycle: identifying potential claims from actualized time, notice, and event facts (Perform Vessel & Barge Actualization, CM-1-2-3-1-4, per the batch 13 handoff); creating, documenting, and pursuing outbound claims; receiving, assessing, and negotiating inbound claims; and preparing and submitting authorized receivable and payment requests through the designated finance processes per the batch 14 payment-authority rule. Under the batch 10 dispute partition, this cluster answers 'was delay or capacity usage compensable'. Includes marine demurrage and despatch under charter parties and contracts of affreightment, and detention or delay/capacity-usage claims only where the governing charter party, terminal agreement, or other commercial agreement treats them as a claim managed through this process; rail car-hire and detention charges settle with Settle Rail (CM-1-2-4-1-13) and truck detention-type accessorials with Settle Trucks (CM-1-2-4-1-4) where their transport agreements govern them, per the batch 14 carve-outs. Claims may be resolved by contractual determination, surveyor or terminal evidence, settlement agreement, credit note, set-off, arbitration, or legal process — not only bilateral negotiation; arbitration and litigation are conducted by Legal & Corporate Communications. Laytime, demurrage, and related terms are calculated under the governing agreement; industry definitions (BIMCO Laytime Definitions for Charter Parties 2013) provide terminology, not contract terms. Excludes freight and voyage-charge settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-5); the underlying time-fact actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-4); confirmation, actualization, and settlement disputes (CM-1-2-2-2-5, CM-1-2-3-1-8, CM-1-2-4-1-17); legal proceedings and arbitration conduct (Legal & Corporate Communications); AR/AP booking and cash application (CM-1-2-4-2-4, CM-1-2-4-2-9); chartering and voyage operations (marine logistics processes).
In scope: Determine potential claims from actualized time and event facts under governing terms | create, document, and pursue outbound claims | receive, assess, and negotiate inbound claims | prepare and submit authorized receivable and payment requests and track claims to evidenced closure
Out of scope: Freight settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-5) | time-fact actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-4) | rail and truck detention-type charges under transport agreements (CM-1-2-4-1-13, CM-1-2-4-1-4) | other dispute quadrants (CM-1-2-2-2-5, CM-1-2-3-1-8, CM-1-2-4-1-17) | legal proceedings and arbitration (Legal & Corporate Communications) | AR/AP booking and cash application (CM-1-2-4-2)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Actualized time, notice, and event facts (CM-1-2-3-1-4) | charter party, contract of affreightment, and terminal terms (CM-1-2-1-2-9) | counterparty claims and correspondence"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Resolved demurrage claims — pursued, defended, negotiated, invoiced, and evidenced"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Recover and defend delay compensation correctly — no entitlement missed, no invalid claim paid, every claim evidenced."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-BIMCO-LD-001 | SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Perform Vessel & Barge Actualization | uses-input: Manage Carrier Contracts | precedes: Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 17 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process per the batch 08/13/14 variant test — one claim lifecycle in outbound and inbound directional variants. Dispute-partition placement principle (Hamid, batches 10/17, approved cross-branch): what did the parties agree — Manage Confirm Disputes (CM-1-2-2-2-5); what was delivered, received, measured, or performed — Manage Actualization Disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8); what is owed, invoiced, received, paid, netted, or applied — Manage Settlement Disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17); is delay, laytime, capacity usage, detention, demurrage, despatch, or similar compensation payable under the governing agreement — here; what legal remedy, enforcement, arbitration, or litigation applies — Legal & Corporate Communications. A single incident can raise questions in more than one category; the processes exchange evidence and referrals rather than assuming a rigid sequence. Detention guardrail (Q2): demurrage relates to time beyond laytime, despatch to early completion, and detention may arise outside laytime mechanics or under distinct agreements — the contractual wording decides whether a delay charge is demurrage, detention, despatch, an accessorial, or a liquidated-damages claim, and only contract-governed claims route here. Terminology per BIMCO Laytime Definitions 2013 (laytime, demurrage, despatch, Notice of Readiness); the governing charter party controls over any standard definition. APQC 20682 is an unrelated IT candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Marketing & Sales Operations Management covers channel & partner management, sales execution, pricing, rebates, loyalty/cards, brand standards, compliance, and marketing plan execution."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Marketing & Sales Operations Management"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:L0-enabling-functions a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10922" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10922"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:definition "The enterprise functions in scope that enable, govern, control, support, and provide shared services to the downstream operating businesses: Supply Chain Management, Finance, Shared Services, Process Excellence & IT, Human Resources, Legal & Corporate Communications, and Environment, Health, Safety & Government Reporting."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:prefLabel "Enabling Functions"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Scheme root, not a process or capability: groups the enabling and governance functions in scope for the enterprise taxonomy. It carries no activities, processes, authority, ownership, controls, assets, or decision rights of its own. Authorities such as Finance, Treasury, Tax, Legal, Compliance, Security, IT, HR, EHS, and Data Governance are exercised by named processes, capabilities, governance bodies, and delegated authorities within or across these functions; grouping under this root does not itself confer authority. Operating domains are grouped under the sibling root Downstream Operations."@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf mod:core ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "not-process"@en ;
    intake:level "L0"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Anchor the enabling side of the enterprise downstream taxonomy — a navigation root grouping the functions that enable and govern the operating businesses."@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-W3C-SKOS-001"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 42 (2026-09-21). Scheme root approved per the structural-anchor review decision (definition + scope note; no horizon, domain, or relationships; no decision authority conferred by grouping). concept_type_check 'not-process' approved under the SCHEME_ROOT_APPROVED gate amendment (option A, Hamid); primary_purpose and reference_sources populated because the post-#31 gate requires them on newly approved rows. Expanded preferred presentation 'Environment, Health, Safety & Government Reporting' for the L1 'EHS & Gov Reporting' is queued in the naming-pass artifact (b42 review); the L1 row is not edited."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-2-5 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Run XPIMS scenarios and finalize the MOP"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.2.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Final Regional Plan"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11222" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11222"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Reconcile actual regional supply, demand, production, inventory, product movements, and commercial outcomes against the approved plan and its contemporaneous assumptions and constraints; quantify and explain variances; and use the findings to improve subsequent regional planning and optimization cycles."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Regional Backcasting"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Regional Backcasting preserves and compares the approved planning baseline, actual outcomes, material assumptions, constraints, decisions, and variance explanations for a completed planning period. It produces commercially meaningful plan-versus-actual analysis — such as margin, price, volume, mix, yield, availability, inventory, logistics, quality, and customer-fulfillment variances — and feeds validated lessons into subsequent regional optimization. It does not replace Finance's statutory or management-close processes, SCM's execution control, or operating functions' technical root-cause investigations."@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-5-6 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-5 ;
    skos:definition "Build and run actuals case stack in XPIMS"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.5.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Evaluate actuals cases"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en .

proc:CM-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1 ;
    skos:definition "Marketing Includes Processes Such As Performing Market Analysis, Developing Marketing Strategy, New Product Development, Managing Retail Marketing Operations. It Includes Processes Required To Take The Products & Services From Strategic Conception, Through Planning To Operational Execution."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Marketing"@en ;
    intake:level "L2"@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10102" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10102"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5 ;
    skos:definition "Plan the marketing communications that deliver the approved communications strategy — from needs and target groups through mix models, budgets, and campaign development to the preparation and controlled release of communications deliverables — handing in-channel program execution to Marketing Plan Execution."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Marketing Communications Planning"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the communications-planning cluster: identifying marketing needs (CM-1-3-5-3-1) and target groups (CM-1-3-5-3-2), developing the marketing mix model (CM-1-3-5-3-3), defining resource allocation and budgets (CM-1-3-5-3-5), defining the communications plan (CM-1-3-5-3-6), developing campaigns (CM-1-3-5-3-4), and coordinating production, approval readiness, controlled release, and handoff of promotional advertising (CM-1-3-5-3-8), internal marketing communications (CM-1-3-5-3-9), and brand advertising/communications deliverables (CM-1-3-5-3-10), with brand-use management (CM-1-3-5-3-7) as a continuous overlay. The split (review): this cluster owns planning, deliverable production/readiness, approval routing, and release coordination; Marketing Plan Execution (CM-1-3-6-5) owns in-channel, in-site, and program activation; media buying and placement commitments follow Procurement and the authorized media-buying process; the accountable approvers authorize releases. Control gates at campaign planning and release (review): claims/substantiation approval where applicable; disclosure/endorsement approval where applicable; brand standards confirmation; privacy/contactability confirmation for direct and digital channels; media/procurement authority confirmation; channel/site readiness confirmation; promotion/supply/inventory alignment; measurement hooks, data sources, and rollback/escalation plan. The b24 control set governs throughout: claims, substantiation, disclosures, and endorsements approved by Legal/Compliance; consent/contactability data consumed, never created; brand use per the CM-1-3-6-7 standards; personal data per the batch 21 statement and batch 23 targeting controls. Performance findings (CM-1-3-5-5) feed the next needs/mix/budget/plan cycle. Excludes communications strategy (CM-1-3-3-5); in-channel program execution and activation (CM-1-3-6-5); media buying/placement commitment (Procurement, authorized buying process); claim/disclosure approval (Legal, Compliance); release authorization (accountable approvers); brand standards ownership (CM-1-3-6-7); corporate and HR communications (Legal & Corporate Communications locked rule, HR).
In scope: Run the communications-planning cluster from needs to campaigns | coordinate deliverable production, approval readiness, and controlled release under the accountable approvers | manage brand use per the owned standards | hand program activation to Marketing Plan Execution
Out of scope: Communications strategy (CM-1-3-3-5) | program execution/activation (CM-1-3-6-5) | media buying commitment (Procurement, authorized process) | claim/disclosure approval (Legal, Compliance) | release authorization (accountable approvers) | brand standards ownership (CM-1-3-6-7) | corporate/HR communications (locked rule, HR)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Integrated communications strategy (CM-1-3-3-5) | offers and propositions (CM-1-3-3-1, CM-1-3-2) | audience findings (CM-1-3-1) | performance findings (CM-1-3-5-5)"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The communications plan, campaign packages, and release-ready deliverables"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Campaigns that arrive at execution planned, funded, approved, and on-brand."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "tactical"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FTC-ADM-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Marketing Communications Strategy | enables: Integrated Marketing Planning (Advertising) | enables: Marketing Plan Execution"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 27 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — planning-through-deliverable-readiness cluster; tactical. Lifecycle with feedback (review): needs → target groups → mix model → budgets → plan → campaigns → prepare/release deliverables → Marketing Plan Execution → Performance Analysis → next cycle; Manage Use Of Brand continuous overlay. Review narrowing verbatim in scope note — prepare/release/handoff, never placement, delivery, channel execution, customer contact, marketing-automation execution, or release approval. Control gates recorded as the review's conditional list. APQC returned NONE at the L4."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20682" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20682"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Counterparty & Contract Administration"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1 ;
    skos:definition "Administer trading counterparties and commercial agreements through their lifecycle — onboarding and maintaining counterparty records; establishing, maintaining, terminating, and novating master agreements, standing agreements, and relevant transaction frameworks; managing specialized agreement types; and monitoring contractual obligations — so trading occurs with authorized counterparties under current, governed terms."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Contract Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the controlled lifecycle administration of trading counterparties (create, maintain, terminate or novate) and trading agreements (create, maintain, terminate or novate), the specialized administration of lease, carrier, and storage contracts, the counterparty and agreement consequences of merger-and-acquisition events, and contract monitoring. Accommodates physical contracts, master agreements such as ISDA-type frameworks, transaction confirmations governed by a master agreement, capacity and lease arrangements, and spot transactions governed by standard terms or market rules rather than a bespoke contract record. Excludes legal drafting, legal review, and legal-risk management, owned by Legal & Corporate Communications under its locked rule; excludes credit reviews, limits, and collateral, owned by Credit Risk Management (CM-1-2-2-1); excludes commercial negotiation of agreements where a negotiating owner exists (for example Negotiate Storage / Throughput Agreements, CM-1-2-7-1-3); excludes deal capture (Trade Capture, CM-1-2-1-3) and deal-level confirmation (CM-1-2-2-2).
In scope: Onboard, maintain, and terminate or novate counterparties | establish, maintain, and terminate or novate agreements and master agreements | administer lease, carrier, and storage contract specializations | handle M&A consequences for counterparties and agreements | monitor contractual obligations
Out of scope: Legal services and legal-risk management (Legal & Corporate Communications) | credit reviews, limits, and collateral (CM-1-2-2-1) | agreement negotiation where separately owned (CM-1-2-7-1) | deal capture and confirmation (CM-1-2-1-3, CM-1-2-2-2)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "counterparty applications and due-diligence results | negotiated agreement terms | corporate event notices | contract obligations and calendars"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "governed counterparty and agreement records through their lifecycle"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Trading occurs with authorized counterparties under current, governed terms."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001 | SRC-ISDA-MA-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Trade Capture | enables: Confirmations Management | governed-by: Credit Risk Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 09 (2026-09-19). Q1 decision (Hamid, Option A): process — lifecycle stages applied to two entity kinds plus type variants and control activities, per the batch 08 variant test. This taxonomy locates the commercial lifecycle-administration process under Trading Management; actual execution may involve Trading, Credit Risk, Regulatory & Compliance, Legal, Finance Operations, or Shared Services according to the enterprise operating model. The negotiate, administer, capture, use/actualize/settle pattern from batch 08 is a taxonomy heuristic, not a universal sequential lifecycle: some agreements are frameworks rather than tradable deals, some deals execute under existing agreements, and some contractual rights are used without a separately captured transaction. APQC 20682 is an unrelated IT candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "14095" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 14095"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Deal Capture"@en,
        "Trade Entry & Reporting"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1 ;
    skos:definition "Perform the controlled pre-trade analysis, authorization, and post-execution recording of trades in the trading system — capturing complete commercial, operational, and regulatory terms by deal type and submitting required creation-data reports — so authorized transactions are available to confirmation, risk, actualization, settlement, and accounting processes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Trade Capture"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the per-transaction lifecycle: analyzing prospective deals, obtaining approval within the delegation of authority, capturing executed physical, exchange, storage, environmental, financial, structured, transportation, and transmission deals after execution, and reporting creation data to swap data repositories where an applicable regime requires it. Trading strategies and approved asset-optimization opportunities inform prospective transactions; the authorized trader or delegated execution role executes a transaction only after required pre-trade controls and approvals have been satisfied — capture is the controlled record-creation activity, not the act of purchasing or selling. Excludes trade confirmation and matching with counterparties, owned by Confirmations Management (CM-1-2-2-2); excludes risk measurement and limits (Market Risk Management, CM-1-2-2-3); excludes actualization (CM-1-2-3-1) and settlement (CM-1-2-4-1); excludes counterparty and contract lifecycle administration (Contract Management, CM-1-2-1-2).
In scope: Analyze prospective deals before execution | obtain trade approval within the delegation of authority | capture executed deals by type with full commercial, operational, and regulatory terms | report creation data where an applicable regime requires it
Out of scope: Trade execution as an act of purchasing or selling (authorized trader or delegated execution role) | strategy definition (CM-1-2-1-4, CM-1-2-1-1-4) | confirmation and matching (CM-1-2-2-2) | risk measurement and limits (CM-1-2-2-3) | actualization and settlement (CM-1-2-3-1, CM-1-2-4-1) | counterparty and contract administration (CM-1-2-1-2)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "approved strategies and recommendations | executed transaction terms | delegation of authority | applicable reporting rules"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "authorized, complete, reported deal records available to downstream processes"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Get every executed trade authorized, completely recorded, and reported where required, once and correctly."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Trading Strategy Management | precedes: Confirmations Management | enables: Market Risk Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 08 (2026-09-19). Q1 decision (Hamid, Option A): process — the children form a per-transaction lifecycle (analyze, authorize, capture by deal type, report) with the eight capture nodes as instrument-specific variants of one controlled deal-entry activity; the Produce Demand Forecast process precedent applies, not the batch 04 capability test. Reworded at Hamid's direction to distinguish trade execution from trade capture: this process controls what happens before, at, and immediately after execution and records the trade; it does not execute it. Whether the enterprise's system of record supports a single deal record or a governed golden-deal model across systems is a follow-up noted in the batch 08 PR. APQC 14095 'Document trade' is a WEAK international-trade candidate; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-7 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12900" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12900"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Inventory"@en,
        "Stock Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "The capability to govern, measure, reconcile, control, position, replenish, and account for petroleum feedstocks, intermediates, and refined products across the downstream network, including their quantity, quality, location, ownership, entitlement, identity, and availability."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Inventory Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers development and maintenance of inventory policies by material class; physical inventory measurement and the volumetric inputs to valuation; book-to-physical reconciliation; replenishment management; and stewardship of inventory held under consignment, commingling, third-party-storage, lot/batch, and re-brand arrangements, including controls over ownership, entitlement, product identity, custody, quality, location, and availability. Excludes setting plan-period finished-product inventory targets inside the regional plan, owned by Plan Finished Goods Inventory (CM-1-1-2-13); excludes physical tank, terminal, and pipeline operation, owned by Midstream; excludes inventory valuation, accounting, and financial reporting, owned by Finance; excludes day-to-day crude and refinery-feedstock position management, owned by Manage Feedstock Inventory (CM-1-2-5-4-2) per the batch 04 decision.
In scope: Inventory policy by material class | physical inventory measurement and volumetric inputs to valuation | book-to-physical reconciliation | replenishment management | consignment, commingled, re-brand, lot/batch, and third-party storage stewardship
Out of scope: Plan-period inventory targets in the regional plan (Plan Finished Goods Inventory, CM-1-1-2-13) | physical storage and movement operation (Midstream) | inventory valuation, accounting, and financial reporting (Finance) | day-to-day feedstock position management (Manage Feedstock Inventory, CM-1-2-5-4-2) | trading inventory risk management (Supply And Trading)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "approved regional and distribution plans | measured tank and meter data | movement and transaction records | storage and consignment agreements"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "governed, reconciled inventory positions and policies for the distribution network"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Ensure the network's stock positions are governed by policy, measured accurately, reconciled, and managed across ownership and storage arrangements."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM32-001 | SRC-API-MPMS-001 | SRC-ASCM-CSCP-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Regional Optimization | enables: Capture Inventory | informed-by: Plan Finished Goods Inventory"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 04 (2026-09-19). Q1 decision (Hamid, Option A): capability, not process — the children are distinct recurring processes that jointly enable inventory stewardship, not stages of one cycle. The bare-noun label can denote the asset/state, a count, or the function; this node denotes the capability. Step 3d naming pass (2026-09-21): renamed to 'Inventory Management' ('Inventory' retained as altLabel); the L4-versus-L5 label collision is resolved — the L5 is now 'Monitor and Control Inventory Positions' ('Manage Inventory' retained as its altLabel). Placement rule: Supply Chain Management answers what should be held, moved, made, or replenished, where and when; this capability answers what inventory exists, under what identity, ownership, and entitlement, in what quantity and location, and whether it is controlled and reconciled; Midstream answers how bulk material is physically received, stored, transferred, and transported. Feedstock boundary constraint (Q3 decision, Hamid, Option A): Manage Feedstock Inventory (CM-1-2-5-4-2) owns the day-to-day monitoring, control, and operational management of crude and refinery-feedstock inventory positions within Crude/Feed Supply Management; this cluster owns cross-material inventory policy, measurement, reconciliation, and specialized inventory-arrangement controls, and does not own day-to-day feedstock position management. A reciprocal note is recorded on CM-1-2-5-4-2. Hydrocarbon loss-control ownership beyond reconciliation reporting has no home in the current tree; playbook step 7 carries that question — deliberately not resolved here. Step 3d naming pass (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Inventory' to 'Inventory Management' — critical semantic-collision resolution. Prior label 'Inventory' retained as altLabel."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10774" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10774"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Product Control"@en,
        "Trading Accounting"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4 ;
    skos:definition "The capability to record, value, reconcile, and report the financial effects of supply and trading activity by maintaining controlled sub-ledger and general-ledger records; booking authorized settlement outcomes, receivables, payables, actuals, accruals, taxes, and interest obligations; reconciling economic P&L to accounting P&L; and producing controlled financial evidence and reporting within the enterprise accounting-policy framework."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Accounting"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the trading and supply accounting capability: transaction-level treatment assessment, sub-ledger and GL recording, AR/AP and actuals booking from authorized settlement outcomes (batch 14 Q3 chain), cash application, accruals, financial production and inventory accounting, royalty and interest disbursement execution, economic-to-accounting P&L reconciliation using the controlled economic inputs delivered by Market Risk at end of month (batch 12), transaction and commodity tax execution, financial documentation and reporting, financial system and ledger-structure administration, and trading-specific accounting procedures. This capability supports Supply & Trading and may be operated by Product Control, Trading Accounting, Finance Operations, Accounting, Shared Services, or another designated accounting function; the taxonomy placement does not prescribe the accountable legal, finance, or organizational owner. Operates within the enterprise accounting policy framework: financial write-off approval, valuation policy, material technical-accounting conclusions, and enterprise accounting policy are owned by Finance under the locked Finance rule; payment release and bank execution by Treasury and banking processes; settlement calculation and invoice validation by Settlements (CM-1-2-4-1); the refinery operational material balance by Perform Production Accounting (CM-1-1-7-1-3); network book-to-physical stock reconciliation by Perform Inventory Reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5).
In scope: Book, value, and reconcile trading activity in sub-ledger and GL | apply cash, accrue, and post actuals from authorized settlement outcomes | reconcile economic P&L to accounting P&L | assess treatments, execute transaction taxes, and produce controlled financial documentation
Out of scope: Enterprise accounting policy, valuation policy, material technical-accounting conclusions, and write-off approval (Finance, locked rule) | payment release and bank execution (Treasury/banking) | settlement calculation and invoicing (CM-1-2-4-1) | refinery material balance (CM-1-1-7-1-3) | network stock reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5) | credit exposure and limits (CM-1-2-2-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Authorized settlement outcomes (CM-1-2-4-1) | controlled economic P&L inputs at EOM (CM-1-2-2-3-5) | operational production and inventory balances (CM-1-1-7-1-3, CM-1-1-3-7-5) | enterprise accounting policy (Finance)"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Complete, reconciled trading financial records, postings, and controlled financial reporting"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Give supply and trading activity a complete, controlled, and reconciled financial record within enterprise policy."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FASB-ASC815-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Settlements | uses-input: Perform End-of-Month (EOM) Process | governed-by: Develop Accounting Policies & Procedures"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: capability per the batch 11/12 test — heterogeneous concurrent record-and-control functions, not one per-transaction lifecycle. Organizational neutrality (Hamid, batch 15): responsible domain recorded as Finance-enabled Supply & Trading — a trading-accounting capability serving Supply & Trading, delivered through Product Control, Trading Accounting, Finance Operations, Accounting, Shared Services, or another designated function per the operating model; taxonomy branch placement under Commercial & Marketing does not prescribe the accountable owner. Execute-within-policy pattern: accounting executes within the Finance-owned policy framework, parallel to the batch 11 limits and batch 12 valuation-control precedents. 'P&L' in prose per the batch 12 convention. APQC 10774 is a WEAK financial-accounting candidate, comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19569" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19569"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Optimize the integrated regional supply, demand, production, inventory, and product-movement plan to maximize commercial margin and reliably fulfill customer commitments within refinery, terminal, transportation, product-quality, and contractual constraints."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Regional Optimization"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Regional Optimization determines the commercially preferred regional supply-and-disposition plan, including demand allocation, supply sourcing, product placement, inventory positioning, exchanges, and inter-regional movements. It does not directly operate refinery units, terminals, pipelines, vessels, trucks, or retail/wholesale delivery activities; those functions provide capabilities, constraints, and execution feedback to the optimization cycle."@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REJECTED"@en ;
    intake:level "L3"@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20711" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20711"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Trading Market Risk Control"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2 ;
    skos:definition "The capability to independently measure, control, monitor, analyze, and report market risk in the trading book by governing risk-book structures, delegated authority, market-data inputs, valuations, limits, control cycles, positions, profit and loss, scenarios, and risk reporting."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Market Risk Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the independent market-risk control over the trading book: control-framework structures (risk-book structure, delegation of authority, risk limits), market-data and valuation ownership (curves, volatilities and correlations, commodity valuations), the daily and monthly control cycles, risk measurement and monitoring, position and P&L control, risk-policy compliance monitoring, scenarios, advanced analytics, and reporting. An independent control function per the approved L3 definition; the delivery model is not prescribed. Excludes enterprise-wide strategic commodity-price risk, corporate hedging policy, capital allocation, and non-trading operating exposure unless those exposures are expressly booked into the governed trading portfolio. Excludes trading decisions and strategy stewardship, which are governed by these controls (CM-1-2-1-4); excludes credit risk (CM-1-2-2-1); excludes regulatory trading compliance (CM-1-2-4-3); excludes accounting P&L and its reconciliation ownership (Accounting, CM-1-2-4-2, with the economic-to-accounting reconciliation at CM-1-2-4-2-6); excludes independent model validation (designated validation function).
In scope: Maintain risk-book structure, delegation of authority, and risk limits | own curves, volatilities, and valuations | run daily and monthly control cycles | measure, monitor, and analyze market risk, positions, and P&L | report the risk picture
Out of scope: Enterprise-wide commodity-price risk, corporate hedging policy, and non-trading exposure not booked into the trading portfolio | trading decisions and strategy stewardship (CM-1-2-1-4) | credit risk (CM-1-2-2-1) | regulatory trading compliance (CM-1-2-4-3) | accounting P&L (CM-1-2-4-2) | independent model validation (designated validation function)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "captured positions | market prices and data | risk appetite and policy | governance decisions"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "measured, bounded, visible trading-book market risk with governed control structures"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Losses from market moves in the trading book are measured, bounded, and visible before they compound."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-002A | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Regulatory & Compliance | enables: Trading Strategy Management | enables: Trade Capture"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Concept type: capability (Hamid confirmed), per the batch 11 reasoning — concurrent heterogeneous control processes, no single lifecycle. Bound explicitly to the trading book at Hamid's direction: refinery-margin, procurement-price, inventory, and corporate hedging exposures are governed here only when expressly booked into the governed trading portfolio; whether a separate corporate hedging program exists is a follow-up noted in the batch 12 PR. Middle-office framing per the approved L3 definition names the control function, not a mandated organization (batch 10 neutrality). APQC 20711 is an IT-risk candidate; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11522" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11522"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6 ;
    skos:definition "A capability that operates loyalty, proprietary-fuel-card, fleet-card, and, where applicable, network-payment-card programs through coordinated but distinct member-program and card-program processes: administering members and cardholders from prospect setup through issuance, servicing, transaction processing, billing coordination, and program benefits, across concurrently running controls for administration, financial coordination, fraud response, compliance, card logistics, and loyalty operation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Loyalty And Cards Management - Marketing"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Classified as a capability because its children are not lifecycle stages of one process but heterogeneous controls operating concurrently over ongoing programs. Internal conceptual split: the loyalty-program scope covers member accounts and tiers, earn/redemption mechanics, rewards fulfillment, loyalty communications under consent, program partner benefits, and loyalty operational records; the card-program scope covers cardholder/customer accounts, card issuance/activation/replacement, card limits and purchase restrictions, transaction authorization and processing coordination, billing and reimbursement coordination, fraud and service response, and secure card production and handling. Program-coordination statement: the programs coordinate member-facing financial touchpoints, but each enterprise control retains its owner — invoicing and billing execute under Manage Customer Invoicing and Billing; customer credit limits under Manage Credit & Risk; collection, disputes, and bad-debt allowance under Manage Collection and Disputes and Finance; payment execution under the payment-authority rule. Where the card program stores, processes, transmits, or can affect the security of payment-card account data within an applicable PCI payment-card environment, PCI DSS and related card-scheme obligations may apply. Closed-loop proprietary fuel-card programs may have different or no PCI DSS scope depending on the payment architecture, payment-card data handled, service providers, and applicable scheme arrangements. Security, with the accountable business, technology, processor, acquirer, and Treasury owners, determines scope and required controls. The program applies Security-defined card-data, authentication, access, encryption, tokenization, retention, vendor, incident-response, and secure-media controls. It does not define PCI scope, security architecture, security control standards, forensic investigation, or breach notification obligations. Personal data handled here is governed under the enterprise personal-data governance framework, with collection, use, retention, and disclosure limited to what the framework and applicable law permit.
In scope: Operate loyalty and card programs through distinct member-program and card-program processes | member/cardholder account administration | program-level financial coordination within owning-process controls | program fraud response and escalation | card logistics and production coordination | loyalty program operation
Out of scope: Enterprise invoicing, credit, collections, and payment execution (their owning processes) | PCI scope determination, security architecture, and forensic investigation (Security) | card scheme rule-setting (external networks) | acquirer/banking relationships (Treasury) | program performance evaluation (Marketing Performance Analysis) | brand standards setting (Brand Standard Management)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "REVIEW LINK"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Program terms and rules | enterprise credit, invoicing, and consent frameworks | Security-defined card-data controls | card scheme specifications where applicable"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Operating card and loyalty programs with governed member, transaction, and program records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Card and loyalty programs operated reliably and compliantly as an ongoing commercial capability."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001; SRC-PCI-DSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Manage Credit & Risk | uses-input: Manage Customer Invoicing and Billing | enables: Customer Program Analysis"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 29 (2026-09-20). Capability per the heterogeneous-concurrent-control test (b21/b26 precedent); review-required loyalty-versus-card internal split stated; revised fact-dependent PCI DSS scope statement adopted verbatim (data handling and payment architecture, not branding alone); Security boundary statement adopted verbatim. Normalized label 'Loyalty and Cards Management (Marketing)' is a case/punctuation-only change — queued for the naming pass, not recorded as an alt label."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Commercial Settlement"@en,
        "Trade Settlement"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4 ;
    skos:definition "Determine and complete the financial settlement of trading transactions — calculating amounts owed and due from actualized quantities, contractual prices, and verified charge determinants; generating and validating invoices; netting where agreements provide; preparing, validating, and submitting authorized settlement instructions or payment requests to the designated Accounts Payable, Accounts Receivable, Treasury, banking, or shared-services process; tracking payment or receipt status; and retaining settlement evidence — so every transaction closes financially on verified actuals and contractual terms."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settlements"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the per-transaction settlement lifecycle across commodity, market-channel, and logistics-service variants: assembling actualized quantities and charge determinants, applying contractual and market prices and rates, calculating settlement amounts, generating outbound and validating inbound invoices and statements, netting under the governing agreements, preparing and submitting authorized settlement instructions and payment requests to the designated finance processes, tracking payment and receipt status, and retaining settlement evidence. Under the batch 10 partition, settlement answers 'what is owed, invoiced, received, paid, netted, or applied'. Consumes actualized records and charge determinants from Actualizations (CM-1-2-3-1) per the batch 13 charge-determinant rule. Where a contract bundles commodity value and logistics charges into one commercial settlement, the settlement process retains the contractual charge components and allocates them to the appropriate commodity and service settlement records where the accounting, tax, operational, or control model requires that distinction. Settlement does not independently approve payment release, initiate an unauthorized bank transfer, post accounting entries, apply cash, determine accounting treatment, or perform treasury cash management unless those authorities are expressly delegated in the enterprise operating model. Excludes actualization of quantities and determinants (CM-1-2-3-1); AR/AP booking, cash application, accruals, sub-ledger, general ledger, and tax, owned by Accounting (CM-1-2-4-2); payment release and bank execution (Treasury and banking processes per the enterprise operating model); financial write-offs and valuation (Finance, locked rule); demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4); credit exposure and limits (CM-1-2-2-1).
In scope: Calculate settlement amounts from actualized quantities, prices, and charge determinants | generate and validate invoices and settlement statements | net obligations under governing agreements | prepare, validate, and submit authorized settlement instructions and payment requests to the designated finance processes | track payment and receipt status and retain settlement evidence
Out of scope: Actualization (CM-1-2-3-1) | AR/AP booking, cash application, accruals, and GL (CM-1-2-4-2) | payment release and bank execution (Treasury/banking processes) | write-offs and valuation (Finance, locked rule) | demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4) | credit exposure and limits (CM-1-2-2-1)"""@en ;
    intake:apqcDecision "NO CANDIDATE"@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Actualized deal, movement, and entitlement records (CM-1-2-3-1) | contractual prices, rates, and netting terms | counterparty, carrier, exchange, broker, ISO, and registry invoices and statements"@en ;
    intake:level "L4"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Settled transactions with validated invoices, netting, and authorized settlement instructions evidenced"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Close every trading transaction financially on verified actuals and contractual terms, with a controlled and evidenced trail from actual to authorized settlement instruction."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001 | SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Actualizations | informed-by: Confirmations Management | enables: Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process per the batch 08/13 variant test — one settlement lifecycle across commodity, channel, and logistics-service variants. Q2 decision (Hamid): commodity-value settlement (value of the commodity or instrument, with trade counterparties) and logistics-service settlement (freight, tariff, charter hire, throughput, storage, handling, with carriers and operators) are distinct; one physical delivery may legitimately produce both; bundled delivered-price contracts are allocated per the scope-note rule. Q3 decision (Hamid): Settlements determines contractual amounts, validates invoices and statements, manages netting, and prepares/submits authorized settlement instructions; Accounting owns booking, cash application, accruals, and GL; Treasury/banking executes payment release and bank movement; Finance owns write-offs and accounting policy (locked rule); the operating model locates these authorities (organizational neutrality, batch 10). Dispute partition (batch 10): settlement disputes answer 'what is owed, invoiced, received, paid, netted, or applied'. APQC 19948 is an unrelated candidate."@en .

src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    dcterms:issued "2025-05-30" ;
    dcterms:publisher "APQC"@en ;
    dcterms:rights "Used with attribution per the APQC/IBM license."@en ;
    dcterms:title "APQC Process Classification Framework (PCF) for Downstream Petroleum, version 7.2.2"@en .

mod:core a skos:ConceptScheme ;
    dcterms:description "SKOS taxonomy of Lagos Specialty Chemicals' downstream petroleum business processes (levels L0-L6), derived from business_architecture/business_process/downstream_process_map.json. The core module namespace serves as the ConceptScheme."@en ;
    dcterms:title "LSC downstream process taxonomy"@en ;
    skos:hasTopConcept proc:L0-downstream-operations,
        proc:L0-enabling-functions .

